perience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. The candidate is a UAE citizen or a UAE residence visa holder. Eligible for an NOC letter from the current employer/sponsor (if applicable) permitting the candidate to teach/work at NYUAD. Required Education, Certifications, Licensing, or Training Bachelor\u2019s degree in Communication, Education, Public Relations, Psychology or other\u00a0 related educational field Public Speaking Certification: Certifications from programs such as but not limited to Toastmasters International (e.g., Competent Communicator or Distinguished Toastmaster) and Dale Carnegie Leadership Development Certification: Leadership-focused certifications, such as but not limited to those from the John Maxwell Team or the Center for Creative Leadership CCL Certificates of training in communication and leadership from a recognized international university Preferred Education, Certifications, Licensing, or Training Advanced degree (Master\u2019s, PhD, or other terminal degree) in Executive Leadership, Professional Communications, Educational Leadership, or a related subject High-school or college-level experience in teaching debating and public speaking Required Work and Related Experience Experience teaching public speaking, communication, leadership, or related courses at a university level Experience teaching a related course at the high School or university level Demonstrated experience in delivering leadership development programs or training in an Academic or Professional setting The ability to demonstrate effective public speaking techniques and mentor students in the art of communication and presentation Ability to teach leadership principles and practices, including team management, decision-making, and strategic thinking in an Academic or Professional manner Experience in designing and developing course curricula focused on public speaking, leadership, or communication skills Proficiency in various teaching methodologies, such as interactive workshops, experiential learning, and assessments tailored to enhance students\u2019 public speaking and leadership abilities Demonstrated ability to provide feedback in a constructive manner Experience supervising high school and/or university students in a co-curricular setting (i.e. school or after-school program) Preferred Work and Related Experience Demonstrated commitment to effective teaching in cross-cultural or global learning environments. Familiarity with online and face-to-face instruction Applicants must submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae in PDF format. For instructions and information on how to apply, please visit our website at https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/about/careers/academic-support.html . If you have any questions, please email nyuad.sa.positions@nyu.edu . For more information about the program, please visit: www.nyuad.nyu.edu/summer.academy . NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195632": {"title": "Adjunct Teaching Assistant, Leadership and Public Speaking", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Academic Programs: AD_Sheikh Mohamed bin Zayed Scholars Program (SMSP) - Summer Academy Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE  Program Overview The NYU Abu Dhabi Summer Academy is an 18-month university preparation program designed to enhance the academic profiles of highly motivated Emirati high school students. The program features intensive instruction in Leadership and Public Speaking, Critical Thinking and Writing, Mathematical Reasoning for the Sciences, College Readiness and Success, and IELTS Preparation over the course of two summers and an intervening academic year. Following a competitive admissions process, up to 90 program participants are selected each year as recipients of the Sheikh Mohamed bin Zayed Scholarships for Outstanding High School Students, which enables them to attend the Summer Academy. Students are exposed to a rich cultural and rigorous academic program which enhances their ability to gain admission to and perform well at top-tier English-language universities. The program begins during the summer following the 10th year of high school and continues for the following 18 months, with two intensive summer sessions of study in Abu Dhabi at the NYUAD Campus. In addition, students participate in online study and academic refresher weekends during the following academic year. University Preparation Students gain the academic, cultural, and social skills necessary for success at top-tier universities. Summer Academy graduates have attended Yale, NYU New York, NYU Abu Dhabi, Princeton, United Arab Emirates University, The London School of Economics, George Washington University, King\u2019s College London, The University of Pennsylvania\u2019s Wharton School, and many others. Academic Preparation We introduce students to accelerated study of mathematics, critical thinking and writing, the humanities and to the value of a liberal arts education. Cultural Awareness Students develop their understanding of the social and cultural norms of universities and build useful intercultural competencies for success in university. Test Preparation We prepare students to excel on the standardized tests necessary for university entry. Leadership and Public Speaking Skills With Leadership and Public Speaking as a required course in the Summer Academy,\u00a0 our program spurs confidence, personal growth, maturity, leadership skills. Social Networking We bring together top students from across the UAE who form a supportive and challenging social network among themselves and with Summer Academy alumni who have gone on to university study, graduate school, professions, and leadership careers. Job Summary The Teaching Assistant for the Leadership and Public Speaking (LPS) course will support course delivery for up to two sections of Summer Academy students - students in their first summer of the program and students in their second summer of the program (Summer I and Summer II). The Teaching Assistant will also support programmatic activities throughout the length of the appointment (including weekends). Length of Appointment Approximately two to three mandatory meetings before the summer session starts for orientation activities Summer Session duration: Five weeks from approximately July 4 to August 6, 2027 Principal Responsibilities Support the delivery of the LPS course for two sections - the Classes of 2027 (Summer II) and 2028 (Summer I) in Abu Dhabi Under the supervision of the LPS specialist(s), meet students individually or in small groups during study halls, meals, breaks, or in the evenings to assist with reviewing classroom instruction and homework assignments Attend all pre-program meetings and/or training sessions Assist the LPS specialist(s) in class with group work and with judging presentations, as necessary Be available to individual students and groups in need of assistance, as well as to specialists undertaking cross-curricular or collaborative projects Provide students with ongoing and regular feedback (oral and written) during the course Participate in faculty meetings by providing detailed feedback on student progress Report any instances of student misconduct, academic performance concerns, or health issues to the Summer Academy administration Contribute to the overall collegial atmosphere of the Summer Academy Attend and provide assistance as needed with co-curricular events and activities throughout the summer session, which will entail leading small groups of students on excursions and trips, or supporting the operations of the program as needed Oversee and support students during assigned day trips, extracurricular evening activities, and events, ensuring a safe and engaging environment that promotes learning and development About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. The candidate is a UAE citizen or a UAE residence visa holder. Eligible for an NOC letter from the current employer/sponsor (if applicable) permitting the candidate to teach/work at NYUAD. Required Education, Certifications, Licensing, or Training Bachelor\u2019s degree Availability to teach in Abu Dhabi during the dates listed above Ability to provide some online instruction during the dates listed above, if necessary Preferred Education, Certifications, Licensing, or Training Bachelor\u2019s degree in Communications, Leadership, or related subject High-school or college-level experience teaching in English Required Work and Related Experience Demonstrated commitment to effective teaching in cross-cultural or global learning environments. Experience supervising high school students in a co-curricular setting (i.e. school or after-school program) Availability to assist with the planning of the program prior to the start of classes Preferred Work and Related Experience Work or internship experience teaching English as an additional language Familiarity with online and face-to-face instruction Applicants must submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae in PDF format. For instructions and information on how to apply, please visit our website at https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/about/careers/academic-support.html . If you have any questions, please email nyuad.sa.positions@nyu.edu . For more information about the program, please visit: www.nyuad.nyu.edu/summer.academy . NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195633": {"title": "Adjunct Specialist, Mathematical Reasoning for the Sciences", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Academic Programs: AD_Sheikh Mohamed bin Zayed Scholars Program (SMSP) - Summer Academy Location: Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates Program Overview The NYU Abu Dhabi Summer Academy is an 18-month university preparation program designed to enhance the academic profiles of highly motivated Emirati high school students. The program features intensive instruction in Leadership and Public Speaking, Critical Thinking and Writing, Mathematical Reasoning for the Sciences, College Readiness and Success, and IELTS Preparation over the course of two summers and an intervening academic year. Following a competitive admissions process, up to 90 program participants are selected each year as recipients of the Sheikh Mohamed bin Zayed Scholarships for Outstanding High School Students, which enables them to attend the Summer Academy. Students are exposed to a rich cultural and rigorous academic program which enhances their ability to gain admission to and perform well at top-tier English-language universities. The program begins during the summer following the 10th year of high school and continues for the following 18 months, with two intensive summer sessions of study in Abu Dhabi at the NYUAD Campus. In addition, students participate in online study and academic refresher weekends during the following academic year. Program Goals University Preparation Students gain the academic, cultural, and social skills necessary for success at top-tier universities. Summer Academy graduates have attended Yale, NYU New York, NYU Abu Dhabi, Princeton, United Arab Emirates University, The London School of Economics, George Washington University, King\u2019s College London, The University of Pennsylvania\u2019s Wharton School, and many others. Academic Preparation We introduce students to accelerated study of mathematics, critical thinking and writing, the humanities, and to the value of a liberal arts education. Cultural Awareness Students develop their understanding of the social and cultural norms of universities and build useful intercultural competencies for success in university. Test Preparation We prepare students to excel on the standardized tests necessary for university entry. Leadership and Public Speaking Skills With Leadership and Public Speaking as a required course in the Summer Academy,\u00a0 our program spurs confidence, personal growth, maturity, and leadership skills. Social Networking We bring together top students from across the UAE who form a supportive and challenging social network among themselves and with Summer Academy alumni who have gone on to university study, graduate school, professions, and leadership careers. Job Summary The Specialist will oversee all academic duties for teaching the Mathematical Reasoning for the Sciences (MTH) course, including lesson planning, instruction, grading, feedback, and office hours for two sections of Summer Academy students - students in their first summer of the program and students in their second summer of the program (Summer I and Summer II). The course will involve approximately 37 contact hours, with the exact hours to be determined during the hiring process. In addition to academic duties, the Specialist will support program-related activities throughout the appointment, including weekends. The Instructor is expected to actively contribute to enriching the educational experience of the students and to play a key role in helping the program achieve its goals. Length of Appointment Approximately two to three mandatory meetings before the summer session starts for orientation activities Summer Session duration: Five weeks from approximately July 4 to August 6, 2027 Principal Responsibilities Teach the MTH course to two sections - the Classes of 2027 (Summer II) and 2028 (Summer I) in Abu Dhabi Work within the existing MTH curriculum, which is focused on math foundations for the sciences. Areas of study include: algebra & functions, system of equations & vectors, Geometry & Trigonometry, introductory probability and statistics, Logic & paradoxes, test practice and other advanced math topics as needed. Attend all pre-program meetings and/or training sessions Work closely with the other MTH specialists to ensure that both cohorts and all sections receive the same material delivered with a similar high-quality approach Supervise and coordinate instruction with teaching assistants(s) Be available to individual students and groups in need of assistance, as well as to other specialists undertaking cross-curricular or collaborative projects Provide students with ongoing and regular feedback (oral and written) during the course Participate in faculty meetings by providing detailed feedback on student progress Before the end of the summer session, submit extensive and detailed written feedback on each student to be incorporated into a recommendation letter from the program Attend and provide assistance as needed with co-curricular events and evening activities throughout the summer session, which will entail leading small groups of students on excursions and trips, or supporting the operations of the program as needed Attend and provide assistance as needed with co-curricular events and activities throughout the summer session, which will entail leading small groups of students on excursions and trips, or supporting the operations of the program as needed Oversee and support students during assigned day trips, extracurricular evening activities, and events, ensuring a safe and engaging environment that promotes learning and development About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. The candidate is a UAE citizen or a UAE residence visa holder. Eligible for an NOC letter from the current employer/sponsor (if applicable) permitting the candidate to teach/work at NYUAD. Required Education, Certifications, Licensing, or Training Advanced degree (Master\u2019s or PhD) in Mathematics, Statistics, or a related subject Availability to teach in Abu Dhabi during the dates listed above Ability to provide some online instruction during the dates listed above, if necessary Preferred Education, Certifications, Licensing, or Training Advanced degree in Mathematics or related subject High-school or college-level experience teaching in English Required Work and Related Experience Demonstrated commitment to effective teaching in cross-cultural or global learning environments. Experience supervising high school students in a co-curricular setting (i.e. school or after-school program) Availability to assist with the planning of the program prior to the start of classes Preferred Work and Related Experience Work or internship experience teaching math to students for whom English is as an additional language Familiarity with online and face-to-face instruction Applicants must submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae in PDF format. For instructions and information on how to apply, please visit our website at https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/about/careers/academic-support.html . If you have any questions, please email nyuad.sa.positions@nyu.edu . For more information about the program, please visit: www.nyuad.nyu.edu/summer.academy . NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195635": {"title": "Adjunct Teaching Assistant, Mathematical Reasoning for the Sciences", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Academic Programs: AD_Sheikh Mohamed bin Zayed Scholars Program (SMSP) - Summer Academy Location: Abu Dhabi  Program Overview The NYU Abu Dhabi Summer Academy is an 18-month university preparation program designed to enhance the academic profiles of highly motivated Emirati high school students. The program features intensive instruction in Leadership and Public Speaking, Critical Thinking and Writing, Mathematical Reasoning for the Sciences, College Readiness and Success, and IELTS Preparation over the course of two summers and an intervening academic year. Following a competitive admissions process, up to 90 program participants are selected each year as recipients of the Sheikh Mohamed bin Zayed Scholarships for Outstanding High School Students, which enables them to attend the Summer Academy. Students are exposed to a rich cultural and rigorous academic program which enhances their ability to gain admission to and perform well at top-tier English-language universities. The program begins during the summer following the 10th year of high school and continues for the following 18 months, with two intensive summer sessions of study in Abu Dhabi at the NYUAD Campus. In addition, students participate in online study and academic refresher weekends during the following academic year. Program Goals University Preparation Students gain the academic, cultural, and social skills necessary for success at top-tier universities. Summer Academy graduates have attended Yale, NYU New York, NYU Abu Dhabi, Princeton, United Arab Emirates University, The London School of Economics, George Washington University, King\u2019s College London, The University of Pennsylvania\u2019s Wharton School, and many others. Academic Preparation We introduce students to accelerated study of mathematics, critical thinking and writing, the humanities, and to the value of a liberal arts education. Cultural Awareness Students develop their understanding of the social and cultural norms of universities and build useful intercultural competencies for success in university. Test Preparation We prepare students to excel on the standardized tests necessary for university entry. Leadership and Public Speaking Skills With Leadership and Public Speaking as a required course in the Summer Academy,\u00a0 our program spurs confidence, personal growth, maturity, and leadership skills. Social Networking We bring together top students from across the UAE who form a supportive and challenging social network among themselves and with Summer Academy alumni who have gone on to university study, graduate school, professions, and leadership careers. Job Summary The Teaching Assistant for the Mathematical Reasoning for the Sciences (MTH) course will support course delivery for up to two sections of Summer Academy students - students in their first summer of the program and students in their second summer of the program (Summer I and Summer II). The Teaching Assistant will also support programmatic activities throughout the length of the appointment (including weekends). Length of Appointment Approximately two to three mandatory meetings before the summer session starts for orientation activities Summer Session duration: Five weeks from approximately July 4 to August 6, 2027 Principal Responsibilities Teach the MTH course to two sections - the Classes of 2026 (Summer II) and 2027 (Summer I) in Abu Dhabi Work within the existing MTH curriculum, which is focused on math foundations for the sciences. Areas of study include: algebra & functions, system of equations & vectors, Geometry & Trigonometry, introductory probability and statistics, Logic & paradoxes, test practice and other advanced math topics as needed. Attend any pre-program meetings and/or training sessions Work closely with the other MTH specialists to ensure that both cohorts and all sections receive the same material delivered with a similar high-quality approach Supervise and coordinate instruction with teaching assistants(s) Be available to individual students and groups in need of assistance, as well as to other specialists undertaking cross-curricular or collaborative projects Provide students with ongoing and regular feedback (oral and written) during the course Participate in faculty meetings by providing detailed feedback on student progress Before the end of the summer session, submit extensive and detailed written feedback on each student to be incorporated into a recommendation letter from the program Attend and provide assistance as needed with co-curricular events and evening activities throughout the summer session, which will entail leading small groups of students on excursions and trips, or supporting the operations of the program as needed Attend and provide assistance as needed with co-curricular events and activities throughout the summer session, which will entail leading small groups of students on excursions and trips, or supporting the operations of the program as needed Oversee and support students during assigned day trips, extracurricular evening activities, and events, ensuring a safe and engaging environment that promotes learning and development. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. The candidate is a UAE citizen or a UAE residence visa holder. Eligible for an NOC letter from the current employer/sponsor (if applicable) permitting the candidate to teach/work at NYUAD. Required Education, Certifications, Licensing, or Training Bachelor\u2019s degree in Mathematics, Statistics, or a related subject Availability to teach in Abu Dhabi during the dates listed above Ability to provide some online instruction during the dates listed above, if necessary Preferred Education, Certifications, Licensing, or Training Advanced degree (Master\u2019s or PhD) in Mathematics or related subject High-school or college-level experience teaching or tutoring in Mathematics Required Work and Related Experience Demonstrated experience in tutoring Mathematics or related subjects Demonstrated commitment to effective teaching in cross-cultural or global learning environments. Experience supervising high school students in a co-curricular setting (i.e. school or after-school program) Availability to assist with the planning of the program prior to the start of classes Preferred Work and Related Experience Work or internship experience teaching math to students for whom English is as an additional language Familiarity with online and face-to-face instruction Applicants must submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae in PDF format. For instructions and information on how to apply, please visit our website at https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/about/careers/academic-support.html . If you have any questions, please email nyuad.sa.positions@nyu.edu . For more information about the program, please visit: www.nyuad.nyu.edu/summer.academy . NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189527": {"title": "Assistant Research Scientist, part-time non-exempt", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Anthropology Department Location: New York, NY The Assistant Research Scientist, part-time non-exempt, will be assisting to develop automated methods for data entry and data collection associated with micro computed tomographic scans from rhesus macaque skulls from\u00a0Cayo Santiago, Puerto Rico housed in the Department of Anthropology in\u00a0the CPRC-NYU skeletal collection. The Assistant Research Scientist will use machine learning and advanced statistical methods to quantify and reconstruct skeletal/dental tissue (and dental tissue loss). This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. The preferred start date is July 27. The position is for 2 months at 20 hours per week. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is\u00a0 $28-$32 per hour. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. The ideal candidate has prior experience in an anthropological research environment, working with CT scans of skeletal material, and skills in machine learning and statistical methods. Education: Bachelor's Degree required; Master's in Data Science preferred Please apply via Interfolio with a resume."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193845": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate, Somatic Health and Aging", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Anthropology Department Location: New York, NY Three positions are available in the New York University Department of Anthropology for a longitudinal study of the rhesus macaques of Cayo Santiago, Puerto Rico, as part of a new U19 award from the National Institute on Aging. All positions are initially for one year, potentially renewable for up to five years. Post-Doctoral Associate 1 (Area: somatic health and aging) A Post-Doctoral Associate will focus on variation in whole-animal health and function across the lifespan, using measures of body composition and mobility to understand how social, environmental, and life-history factors shape the living phenotype. This PDA will work closely with Prof. Cassandra Turcotte, University of Calgary, in addition to Profs. James Higham, Susan Ant\u00f3n and Scott Williams at NYU. The PDA will utilize and further develop the CBRU\u2019s long-term somatoskeletal databank, with particular emphasis on integrating DEXA-based measures of body composition with quantitative assessments of mobility and physical function. Together, these approaches provide an opportunity to investigate how individuals differ in growth, condition, functional capacity, and aging, and how these differences are shaped by their social and ecological environments. The position offers extensive opportunities to lead and collaborate on multiple ongoing and planned research studies, develop a distinctive area of research expertise, and engage in national and international dissemination of findings through publications and conferences. The PDA will be required to spend time at the Cayo Santiago field station supervising the collection of movement data, and at both the Cayo Santiago and Sabana Seca Field Stations during the annual trapping period, during which they may be required handle animals. The position is available immediately, but later start dates are potentially negotiable. The position is for one year, renewable for up to five years. Key Responsibilities Oversee the collection of whole-animal health, body composition, and functional mobility data. Work with existing longitudinal anatomical, morphometric, and life-history data. Establish DEXA imaging as a standardized component of the field research protocol for assessing body composition and somatic condition. Expand quantitative mobility and kinematic data collection to characterize functional variation across age, sex, social status, and environmental context. Integrate body composition, mobility, and existing life-history data to investigate variation in somatic health, functional capacity, and aging. Contribute to the preparation of grant applications and peer-reviewed manuscripts. Present research findings at academic conferences and institutional meetings. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $60,000-65,000 per year. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A PhD in anatomy, zoology, animal biology, biological anthropology, or a related field. Evidence of active outputs, such as peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations, with some success in grant applications also preferred. Experience working with primates or other animal handling preferred. Experience in anatomy is required, and prior experience with digital methods in anatomy preferred. The PDA will be required to make visits to Puerto Rico for components of data collection. Applicants should submit a one-page cover letter, a CV, and the names and addresses of 3 referees, via Interfolio. We will begin reviewing applications immediately on receipt."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193849": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate, Osteology, Health, and Aging", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Anthropology Department Location: New York, NY Three positions are available in the New York University Department of Anthropology for a longitudinal study of the rhesus macaques of Cayo Santiago, Puerto Rico, as part of a new U19 award from the National Institute on Aging. All positions are initially for one year, potentially renewable for up to five years. Post-Doctoral Associate 2 (Area: Osteology, health, and aging) The PDA will focus on variation in skeletal health and morphology across the lifespan, assessing indicators of skeletal health and aging to understand how social, environmental, and life-history factors shape the adult skeletal phenotype. Working closely with Prof. Susan Ant\u00f3n, and in collaboration with Profs. Cassandra Turcotte (University of Calgary), Scott Williams, and James Higham, the PDA will utilize and further develop systematic skeletal collections housed at NYU (the CPRC-NYU collection) and the CBRU\u2019s long-term somatoskeletal databank, with a focus on assessing pathological conditions in gross morphology and imaging including quantitative and qualitative assessments of variation, physical age, and health. Together, these approaches provide an opportunity to investigate how individuals differ in growth, condition, functional capacity, and aging, and how these differences are shaped by their social and ecological environments. The position offers extensive opportunities to lead and collaborate on multiple ongoing and planned research studies, develop a distinctive area of research expertise, and engage in national and international dissemination of findings through publications and conferences. The scholar will also have opportunities to develop independent research questions within the broader theme of skeletal variation in health and function. Based in New York, the scholar will also spend sustained time in Puerto Rico and at imaging facilities in the U.S. where they will collect data and imaging, develop and participate in public science programs, and supervise students and may assist with animal handling and maceration. The position is for one year, renewable for up to five years. Key Responsibilities Oversee the collection, organization and assessment of imaging (surface and microCT scans) of skeletons across the lifespan from the CPRC-NYU collection. Participate in the curation and supervision of curation of the CPRC-NYU collection. Expand and develop gross pathological assessment of bone to characterize health variation across age, sex, social status, and environmental context. Expand quantitative assessment of microCT imaging of bone health and disease to understand changes in skeletal frailty with age and sex. Integrate skeletal data with existing somatic, life-history, and health data to investigate variation in skeletal health, skeletal frailty, mortality, and aging. Establish publicly available training material (virtual imaging and workshops) to support public engagement of science Work with existing longitudinal anatomical, morphometric, and life-history data collected by the CBRU. Contribute to the preparation of grant applications and peer-reviewed manuscripts. Present research findings at academic conferences and institutional meetings. Aid in the training and mentorship of more junior CBRU members. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $60,000-65,000 per year. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. PhD in Biological Anthropology, Anatomy or related field. Expertise in primate osteology and gross visual assessment of pathological conditions in bone. Experience in 2D and 3D methods of collecting data from the skeleton including digital imaging (microCT) required. Background in museum skeletal curation and experience conducting skeletal maceration highly desired. Demonstrated experience with quantitative methods and statistical analysis including working with longitudinal and/or multidimensional biological datasets. Strong record of peer-reviewed publications and scholarly presentations. Enthusiasm for and record of building pathways to science including through publicly engaged science or science outreach required. Ability to work independently, collegially, and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary team required. Applicants should submit a one-page cover letter, a CV, and the names and addresses of 3 referees, via Interfolio. We will begin reviewing applications immediately on receipt."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193851": {"title": "Laboratory Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Anthropology Department Location: New York, NY Three positions are available in the New York University Department of Anthropology for a longitudinal study of the rhesus macaques of Cayo Santiago, Puerto Rico, as part of a new U19 award from the National Institute on Aging. All positions are initially for one year, potentially renewable for up to five years. Laboatory Associate A Laboratory Associate\u00a0is being sought for the lab of Prof. James Higham, to operate the day-to-day operations of our new state-of-the-art wet-lab facilities. They will be responsible for undertaking fecal extractions and running enzyme-immuno assays for the measurement of physiological and immunological analytes. They will also support tissue and sample collections during the annual trapping period on Cayo Santiago, and during animal processing at the Sabana Seca Field Station.\u00a0 The project is part of a large, multi-institutional consortium, and will require working and coordinating with a large (>100 individuals) group of researchers, including shipping and receiving samples. The position is available immediately, but later start dates are potentially negotiable. The position is for one year, renewable for up to five years. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $58,000-62,000 per year. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A bachelor\u2019s degree in zoology, animal biology, biological anthropology, or a related field. Evidence of experience of labwork and research, as well as coordination and management of projects is preferred. The Lab Technician may be required to make visits to Puerto Rico for components of data collection. Applicants should submit a one-page cover letter, a CV, and the names and addresses of 3 referees, via Interfolio. We will begin reviewing applications immediately on receipt."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190706": {"title": "Researcher (Data Reporter)- The SLAPP Back Initiative \u2014 First Amendment Watch", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Arthur L. Carter Journalism Institute Location: New York, NY The Researcher (Data Reporter) will work directly with the project\u2019s senior staff writer and two researchers to identify stories within the Initiative\u2019s dataset and assist in the analysis and fact-checking of alleged anti-SLAPP decisions. This position will assist the team with general research needs and will pitch story ideas for coverage featured on our website, as well as pitches for collaboration with local and national outlets. The ideal candidate will have experience in legal research, can engage in large datasets, and can translate complex issues for a general audience. This position requires creativity and exceptional attention-to-detail. Use our data to identify interesting stories and investigative angles that highlight the harms of this malicious litigation, as well as quick turnaround pieces related to new litigation and proposed legislation about SLAPPs. Produce short and long-form stories focused on strategic lawsuits against public participation and data from The SLAPP Back Initiative. Maintain source and organization relationships to increase our collaborations and online engagement. Collaborate with our team to organize, review and analyze SLAPP decisions consistent with our methodology. This position will require a hybrid/remote work schedule of 2-day per week remote and 3-days per week on campus. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $60,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Required Education A Bachelor\u2019s degree in journalism, communications or another relevant field. Required Experience 1-2 years of experience (can include college internships and reporting work experience if data focused) Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Proficiency in Google Sheets. Experience using PACER and other court records systems. Experience reading complex legal decisions. Knowledge of local and national current events, and issues particularly related to the First Amendment freedoms of speech and press. Familiarity with journalistic ethics. Preferred Education A law degree, or a Master\u2019s degree in journalism, communications or another relevant field. Preferred Experience Data-focused investigative reporting with experience in legal research and constitutional law. Preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Ability to create data visualizations and graphics for article packages preferred. Knowledge of AI tools and software is a plus. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. Please include the following when applying: one-page resume cover letter 3-5 published clips that illustrate ability to perform well in this role. We request that two of the clips should have a data-journalism lens. Please include visualizations, if applicable. If the published clips are behind a paywall, we request an uploaded PDF of the article, or a temporary password to view the article."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193886": {"title": "Associate Research Scientist (Managing Editor)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Arthur L. Carter Journalism Institute Location: New York, NY The SLAPP Back Initiative, a project of First Amendment Watch, a nonprofit newsroom housed at the Arthur L. Carter Journalism Institute, is seeking a part-time managing editor to join our team for 20 hours per week . The managing editor position is a part-time position with NYU employee benefits. It is a one-year grant-funded position with the possibility of an extension based on performance, need and additional funding. The job is based at the Carter Journalism Institute at 20 Cooper Square, New York, N.Y. , and is a fully on-site work. Strong familiarity with data journalism and First Amendment issues is required, as are excellent research and writing skills, analytical ability, and an entrepreneurial mind-set. Responsibilities of the position include: Manage the daily operations of The SLAPP Back Initiative, including workflow management by setting and enforcing deadlines for both short-term projects and long-form investigations. Supervise the work of two full-time reporters and two part-time researchers, plus other contractors and interns as needed. Write and file required update reports to various foundations and individual donors who provide the project withfinancial support. Navigate internal and external budgets as related to The SLAPP Back Initiative, including the tracking of contractor invoices and legal research subscriptions. Manage the Initiative\u2019s collaboration with various First Amendment legal clinics at universities across the country. Spearhead fundraising efforts, including contacts with potential donors, writing fundraising proposals, and coordinating with NYU\u2019s development office. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $50/hr. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . A Bachelor\u2019s degree in journalism, law, communications or another relevant field. A master's degree is a plus. At least 7-10 years of experience in journalism, preferably in investigative and/or data journalism in order to properly support the project. Experience managing a small team. Familiarity with nonprofit financial structures and how to navigate foundational grants and financial deliverables. Please include CV and cover letter. Review of applications will begin immediately. Application deadline is on Wednesday, October 14 th If you have any questions or require additional support to complete this application, please contact The SLAPP Back Initiative team at slappback@nyu.edu . Please do not reach out regarding the status of your application."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181405": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Coruzzi Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Biology and Genomics Location: New York, NY The Coruzzi Lab is currently accepting applications for a Postdoctoral Associate position at NYU\u2019s Center for Genomics and Systems Biology. Plant Systems Biology @ The Coruzzi lab: https://coruzzilab.bio.nyu.edu The Coruzzi lab at NYU\u2019s Center for Genomics & Systems Biology has an open position for a highly motivated and independent individual to work as a Postdoctoral Associate. This position is for a post-PhD trainee preparing for a research scientist career path \u2013 with a future in Academe or Biotech. The position will provide a transition to career independence through the development of professional skills; supervision by senior scientist incorporating individual development plan in support of training goals and those of faculty mentor; and publication of research findings/scholarship during postdoc appointment period. Overview: A major goal of systems biology is to model gene regulatory networks to accurately predict causal interactions between transcription factors (TF) and target genes. Our lab focus is on predictively modeling and validating gene regulatory networks involved in nitrogen (N) use in plants including the model Arabidopsis and crops, including maize and rice. We use time-series transcriptome N-response data and machine learning to learn causal networks using machine learning approaches. For high through put validation, we are using a plant cell-based assay called TARGET scaled up to cover TFs spanning all TF families in Arabidopsis, maize and rice. We aim to identify the master transcription factors in such networks that control NUE/WUE in plants, with a focus on ones conserved across genotypes within species and also across species. We follow-up with validation in planta including green house and field studies. Relevant Papers Arabidopsis:\u00a0 HHO5 & N-dose signaling: https://doi.org/10.1093/plcell/koag201 Maize: NUE Networks: https://academic.oup.com/plcell/article/37/5/koaf093/8129742&#39; Rice: NUE/WUE Networks & Validation:\u00a0 https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2022.1006044 Computational (ML & Bipartite Networks):\u00a0 https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2024.1371607 Location: NYU\u2019s Center for Genomics and Systems Biology is located at 12 Waverly Place, New York, NY 10003, in the historic and vibrant Greenwich Village in downtown Manhattan. NYU is one of the world\u2019s leading research universities and its Center for Genomics and Systems Biology is housed in a new, state-of-the-art facility with 27 faculty members who study Genomics and Systems Biology across all kingdoms of life. For more information on NYUs Genome Center, please go to http://cgsb.as.nyu.edu In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is 65-70K, depending on experience/expertise. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. The successful applicant will hold a Ph.D. and have skills and experience in both Genomics and Bioinformatics. The position will require both experimental and computational skills for the generation and analysis of NextGen datasets \u2013 including RNA-Seq, Chip-Seq and Atac-seq and others. Computational proficiency in R, Perl, Python, or other programming language are strongly preferred. This position also includes training in the writing of grant applications and reports, paper writing and communicating our work in conferences and meetings. Please upload your application materials via Interfolio. Include the following items: 1) CV including a list of publications; 2) a cover letter; 3) three reference letters emailed to gloria.coruzzi@nyu.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194426": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Huang Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Biology and Genomics Location: New York, NY The Huang lab at NYU Biology is looking for a highly motivated and independent individual to work as a Postdoctoral Associate. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. This position is for a post-PhD trainee preparing for a research scientist career path. The planned position will provide a transition to career independence through the development of professional skills; supervision by senior scientist incorporating individual development plan in support of training goals and those of faculty mentor; and publication of research findings/scholarship during postdoc appointment period. Research in the Huang lab combines experimental and computational approaches to study transcriptional regulation in plant systems. Our work primarily uses DAP-seq (DNA affinity purification sequencing) and related methods, together with single-cell genomics approaches. Planned projects will focus on developing novel assays to characterize transcription factor-DNA and protein-protein interactions, as well as applying AI/ML models to predict cis-regulatory elements in plant genomes and uncover the mechanisms underlying transcriptional regulation during plant stress responses and development. Our research primarily uses the model plant Arabidopsis thaliana. Funded projects are supported by NIH grants focused on mechanisms of transcription, as well as collaborative NSF and DOE grants that extend this work to agricultural and bioenergy crops. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $72,000-$75000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. The ideal candidate will hold a PhD in plant biology, genetics/genomics, molecular biology, or a related field; demonstrate research productivity, including first- or co-first-author peer-reviewed publication(s); and have prior research experience in one or more of the following areas: molecular cloning, confocal microscopy, protein expression and purification, biochemical or molecular interaction assays, sequencing-based genomic assays, analysis of high-throughput sequencing data, basic statistics, computer programming/computational analysis, and machine learning. Experience working with Arabidopsis, plant genomic data, and/or single-cell genomics is a strong plus. Candidates with predominantly experimental or computational backgrounds are encouraged to apply. The position is expected to continue for multiple years, contingent upon satisfactory performance and continued funding. Please upload your application materials via Interfolio. Include the following items: 1) CV including a list of publications; 2) a short summary of your present and future research interests; 3) a list of three references and their contact information."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195143": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Biology (Computational/AI)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Biology and Genomics Location: New York New York University\u2019s Department of Biology invites applications for one tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. This search is part of NYU\u2019s Artificial Intelligence Initiative. The successful candidate will establish an independent computational research program in the Department of Biology, complemented by opportunities for collaboration with experimental laboratories. We seek candidates working at the interface of artificial intelligence and biology. We welcome researchers developing new AI and machine learning methods motivated by biological problems, as well as those applying these approaches to advance fundamental biological understanding. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, sequence-to-function modeling, analysis of biological imaging data, generative design of biological molecules and systems, and large-scale modeling and analysis of high-dimensional genomic data. We welcome applications across the full breadth of AI-enabled computational biology, including areas beyond those listed above. The Department of Biology offers a collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment. The successful candidate will have opportunities to collaborate with faculty across the Department, develop new directions that complement its research strengths, and engage with NYU\u2019s broader AI research community. Candidates are expected to develop an externally funded research program and to participate in the teaching mission of the Department. Candidates will supervise scholarly activities of undergraduate and graduate students and participate in administrative activities in the Department of Biology. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html . In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $110,000-$130,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Please direct administrative inquiries to nicky.yeager@nyu.edu. Questions regarding the position may be directed to rahul.satija@nyu.edu . Applicants must provide the following materials: \u25cf Short cover letter addressed to Christine Rushlow, Chair, Department of Biology, NYU \u25cf Curriculum Vitae \u25cf Research statement - up to 3 pages that describe your past and planned research program. \u25cf Teaching statement - 2 pages that describe your past teaching experience and your teaching style and philosophy. \u25cf Names of three (3) referees who will be contacted for reference letters via Interfolio. Applications will be accepted until Nov 15, 2026. All materials should be uploaded via Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195397": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Rocha Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Biology and Genomics Location: New York, NY The Rocha lab at NYU Biology is looking for a highly motivated and independent individual to work as a Postdoctoral Associate. This position is for a post-PhD trainee preparing for a research scientist career path. The planned position will provide a transition to career independence through the development of professional skills; supervision by senior scientist incorporating individual development plan in support of training goals and those of faculty mentor; and publication of research findings/scholarship during postdoc appointment period. Projects in the Rocha lab address the evolutionary history of populations and species of mammals inhabiting extreme terrestrial biomes. Planned projects combine genomics with extensive field work and computational approaches to address the role of genetic and epigenetic variation in adaptation and adaptive potential in natural populations. The Postdoctoral Associate will be expected to lead research projects and publish findings in peer-reviewed journals; apply for external research funding, including independent postdoctoral fellowships and grants; train and mentor students in the lab; and develop and share computational pipelines and workflows for reproducible data analysis. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500-$65,664. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. The ideal candidate will hold a PhD (or equivalent international degree) or enrolled on a PhD program and about to obtain the degree by start-date and a track record of peer-reviewed publications. Preferred qualifications: PhD in the fields of Evolutionary Genetics, Evolutionary Ecology, Computational Biology, Veterinary Sciences or Biology related field(s), and no more than two years of postgraduate experience. Candidates with experience in any of these are strongly encouraged to apply: Experience with genomics, bioinformatics, computer science or related fields. Experience analyzing whole-genome and DNA methylation (epigenomic) sequencing data. Experience with museomics, historical or ancient DNA. Experience analyzing and integrating large-scale genomic and epigenomic data with environmental, ecological and historical datasets. Candidates with experience in any of these are also encouraged to apply: Wet-lab skills for evolutionary research (DNA/RNA extraction, including from museum and degraded samples, Library Preparation, methylation library preparation). Field-work skills (monitoring, capture-release, collar tracking, camera trapping). Please upload your application materials via Interfolio. Include the following items: 1) CV including a list of publications; 2) a short summary of your present and future research interests; 3) a list of three references and their contact information."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175398": {"title": "Laboratory Associate (Fenton Laboratory)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Center for Neural Science Location: New York, NY Professor Andre Fenton\u2019s Lab (Center for Neural Science, NYU; https://as.nyu.edu/cns/people/faculty.andre-fenton.html) Is searching for a full-time Laboratory Associate to join their team. The person hired will be involved in all aspects of animal care and husbandry, routine health care procedures, daily animal evaluations, animal surgeries, general technical assistance to the researcher and animal training. Responsibilities include: - Behavioral training and testing of research animals. - Interacts and coordinates with veterinary services staff to assure health and welfare of animals. - Monitors and cares for pre-operative and post-operative veterinary patients. - Performs sterile surgery on animals. - Histology. - Assists and interacts with investigative staff to meet their research needs. - Trains and orients new employees and research staff, as needed. - Assistance with electrophysiological measurements. - Regularly inspects facility to assure the highest quality of animal care and required sanitation standards. Monitors to assure compliance with all animal care and facility maintenance procedures, standards, and guidelines. - Assists and interacts with investigative staff to meet their research needs. - Inspects facility to assure compliance with USDA, PHS, AAALAC, NYU, and other relevant regulations, guidelines, and policies. Examines operations and procedures to assure efficient utilization of manpower, equipment, and facilities and to assure that relevant regulations, guidelines, policies, and procedures are followed. - Confers with faculty periodically to evaluate new equipment and methods. - Assists investigators with appropriate treatments for research animals. - Performs other job-related duties as assigned. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $58,656- $60,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . Required qualifications: BS or MS in Neuroscience, Biology or related field. Effective oral and writtencommunication skills required. Comfortable with handling animals (in particular mice) is essential. Preferred qualifications: Experience working in a laboratory setting, coordinating research projects, laboratory research support, experience in research methodology and laboratory procedures is desired. Please provide a CV and cover letter."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177800": {"title": "Research Scientist (Alberini Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Center for Neural Science Location: New York, NY Research Scientist position in Neuroscience at New York University to study molecular mechanisms of learning, memory and cognitive impairments. Description Applications are invited for experienced research scientist position in the lab of Dr. Cristina Alberini, in the Center for Neural Science at New York University. The candidate will focus on the identification of molecular and cell biological mechanisms underlying learning-dependent protein metabolism homeostasis regulation. The regulation of protein metabolism is critical for the formation of long-term memories and is altered in several brain diseases, including neurodevelopmental disorders and neurodegenerative diseases. The project will aim to identify novel relevant mechanisms both in healthy brains and in disease. One particular disease that we are focusing on is Angelman syndrome for which we aim to identify neural alterations, biomarkers and therapeutics. The project will employ rodent and/or human systems (iPSC and human brain samples) and multi-level technical approaches to address questions at the molecular, cellular, bioinformatic, and behavioral levels. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $65,000 - $85,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. Qualifications A PhD in Neuroscience, Biology or associated area is preferred. Extensive experience in molecular and cell culture techniques and animal behavior is strongly preferred. Please provide a CV and a cover letter"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180344": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Movshon Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Center for Neural Science Location: New York, NY  Postdoctoral Associate will be working in an experimental lab, performing data collection, analysis,\u00a0and modeling of behavioral and electrophysiological data. Applications are invited to apply for a statistical data analysis postdoctoral position. The position is funded by an ongoing NIH grant and focuses on elucidating the mechanisms of spatial navigation using multi-area recordings in monkeys in the context of VR tasks. as well as a freely moving arena. A significant component of the work focuses on training animals and collecting both behavioral and neural data using an array of tools with a focus on telemetry and wireless recording. The work will also include\u00a0 developing new statistical data analysis tools for behavioral and neural data. More broadly, the postdoc will be part of a large and intellectually vibrant community of researchers from the Center for Neuroscience at NYU. Regular interactions with lab members and experimental collaborators Timely write-ups\u00a0of the results in the form of conference abstracts and manuscripts, as agreed upon with PI and collaborators The initial appointment is one year with the expectation of extension contingent on satisfactory progress. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $70,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. PhD in Neuroscience or related field Submit your CV and cover letter"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183489": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Ma Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Center for Neural Science Location: New York, NY This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. The laboratory of Weiji Ma at New York University ( http://www.cns.nyu.edu/malab ) is looking for a postdoc to study the cognitive and neural mechanisms of multi-step planning across species. Previous work in the lab has developed game-based experimental paradigms for studying multi-step planning and has laid the foundation for the cognitive modeling of human decisions, response times, and eye movements in a variety of planning tasks. The postdoc will build on this work and a) design and run human behavioral experiments on adaptive multi-step planning; b) develop theoretical frameworks for understanding human choices, response times, and eye movements in lab experiments, and human choices and response times in large online data sets; b) perform data analysis and computational modeling for a collaboration with a non-human primate lab that studies the neural basis of planning using a task developed in our lab. The postdoc will be involved in project development, experimental design and data collection (for human experiments only), data analysis, computational modeling, manuscript writing, and dissemination of results. There will likely be opportunities to mentor a Ph.D. student. The postdoc will be part of the lab community and will have access to the broader cognitive science, neuroscience, and data science communities at NYU. The lab cares deeply about mentorship quality, work-life balance, a nurturing culture, a sense of belonging, and being active in the community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $65,000 - $70,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . Ph.D. is required for this position. We are looking for someone with a proven interest in the cognitive mechanisms of human decision-making and deep expertise in the computational modeling of behavioral and/or neural data. Experience in developing experimental paradigms based on games is a plus. Submit CV and 1-page statement of purpose"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195790": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Tian Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Center for Neural Science Location: New York, NY Postdoctoral Associate in the neural basis of cognition. Laboratory of Cognitive Neural Systems (PI: Lucas Tian) Center for Neural Science & Department of Biomedical Engineering | New York University This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. The Laboratory of Cognitive Neural Systems, based in NYU\u2019s Washington Square campus, is seeking a postdoctoral associate to lead a project studying the neural basis of intelligence\u2014focusing on how neural systems learn and generalize by representing the world's abstract, compositional structure and reorganizing it to solve novel problems, in the context of rich motor and cognitive tasks. The successful candidate will join a multidisciplinary team (including neuroscientists, cognitive scientists, and engineers) and lead a project involving one or more of the following approaches: (1) Behavior and electrophysiology in animal models; (2) Computational modeling of behavior and neural activity; (3) Behavior and neural recordings in humans. Responsibilities: Leading the design and execution of projects at the intersection of neuroscience and cognitive science. When appropriate, mentoring students and research assistants. When appropriate, engaging in collaborations within the lab and across labs. Helping to build a lab culture of rigor, curiosity and collegiality. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500- $70,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . Required qualifications: Ph.D. in neuroscience, computational neuroscience, cognitive science, computer science, bioengineering, or a related field. Demonstrated ability to design and execute research projects. Preferred qualifications: Strong quantitative and programming skills (preferably Python). Experience with behavior and electrophysiology in animals or humans. Submit a CV, a brief cover letter describing your experience and interests, and contact information for three references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174106": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Dr. Schlick group)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Chemistry Department Location: New York, NY Applications are solicited for an individual to be appointed as a Postdoctoral Associate under the supervision of Dr. Tamar Schlick\u2019s group. The research will focus on biomolecular dynamics simulations, involving protein and nucleic acid structure and function, including genome structure and function, RNA design, and DNA polymerase mechanisms. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500/yr - $65,000/yr based on experience. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore cannot support STEM OPT extensions. The ideal candidate should have a Ph.D. and will have research experience in chemistry, biophysics, biomedical engineering, or related fields and possess excellent computer and programming skills and molecular simulation experience.\u00a0\u00a0 A solid background in simulation algorithms and experience in advanced scientific computing are highly desired, as is a solid background in chemistry or biophysics.\u00a0 The applicant should be able to work will with a team, including experimentalists, and have excellent writing and communication skills.\u00a0 The terms of employment which would be a year, with a possibility of renewal, include a competitive salary and other benefits. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter summarizing research experience and specifying the interests in this position; a curriculum vitae (including a full list of publications); a statement of research interests; and two letters of reference."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180926": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate (Lupoli lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Chemistry Department Location: New York Applications are solicited for an individual to be appointed as a postdoctoral associate under the supervision of Prof. Tania Lupoli. The research will focus on development of chemical biology probes to study incorporation of sugars into cellular glycans. The project focuses on design, synthesis and use of sugar probes to study incorporation into cellular glycans and interactions that occur on cell surfaces. This position requires expertise in organic synthesis and small molecule chemical analysis (NMR and M/S), with a preference for candidates who have experience in biochemistry, chemical biology and/or cell-based analysis. Candidates should demonstrate a proven ability to conduct independent research, with excellent laboratory skills and experience designing, executing,\u00a0analyzing, and publishing scientific studies. Strong writing, presentation and mentorship skills are also preferred. The terms of employment, which would be a year, with a possibility of renewal, include a competitive salary and other benefits. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, this position's annual base salary range is $62,500.00 \u2013 $64,063. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore cannot support STEM OPT extensions. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Chemistry, Chemical Biology or Biochemistry and no more than 1 year of postdoctoral training . To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter summarizing research experience and specifying their interests in this position (less than two pages), a curriculum vitae (including a complete list of publications), and two contacts to serve as references (previous research mentors are preferred as references)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181911": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Weiss Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Chemistry Department Location: New York, NY The Weiss laboratory at NYU seeks highly motivated and multidisciplinary Postdoctoral Associate to join our team. This role sits at the intersection of fundamental biophysics and advanced instrumentation. You will be responsible for designing, building, and utilizing single-walled carbon nanotube field effect transistor (SWNT FET) sensors to probe biological phenomena at the single-molecule level.\u00a0 See Turvey et al. (2022) Science Advances. 8 : eabl3522, as an example of the type of experiment planned. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500/yr - $62,500/yr based on experience. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore cannot support STEM OPT extensions. Key Responsibilities System Development: Design and iterate custom-built single-molecule measurement platforms SWNT FET biosensors. Hardware Integration: Apply basic electrical engineering principles to interface biological sensors with low-noise, data acquisition hardware. Data Acquisition: Execute complex experiments involving high-bandwidth electrical measurements and advanced microscopy imaging (i.e., AFM and SEM) Signal Processing: Develop pipelines to extract biological signals from noisy data sets using Python, MATLAB, and/or LabVIEW. Collaboration: Work alongside biologists to translate raw sensor data into insights explaining the mechanisms governing how enzymes and cells work. In addition, you will be responsible for maintaining laboratory notebooks, handling confidential data, and following safety regulations. What We Offer Access to state-of-the-art facilities for imaging and fabrication. A highly collaborative environment where chemistry, biology, physics, and engineering are not siloed. Mentorship focused on academic publication, proposal writing, and potential intellectual property/patent development Ph.D. degree or equivalent in a relevant field (e.g., chemistry, electrical or biomedical engineering, etc.) and a minimum of 5 years of relevant research experience. Please provide contact information for three professional references, a CV with a full list of publications, and a cover letter detailing your experience in building versus using instrumentation."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187755": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate (Canary Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Chemistry Department Location: New York, NY Applications are invited for an individual to be appointed as a postdoctoral associate in NYU Structural DNA Nanotechnology Laboratory under the supervision of Professor James Canary. The research will focus on the design, assembly, and high-throughput characterization of multi-strand DNA assemblies (including large-scale multi-component crystals, tiles, brick and origami systems). This role offers a unique opportunity to work at the intersection of DNA structural engineering, synthetic biology, and material science. The postdoctoral researcher will directly interface with existing projects in nanoelectronics, topology and DNA-templated catalysis. Experience with DNA nanotechnology is preferred. The employment terms, which are for one year with a possible renewal, include a competitive salary and other benefits. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, this position's annual base salary range is $62,500.00 \u2013 $62,500.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Chemistry, Biochemistry, Bioengineering, Materials Science, Nanotechnology, or a closely related discipline. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter (less than two pages) summarizing research experience and specifying their interests in this position, a curriculum vitae (including a complete list of publications), and two references (previous research mentors are preferred)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194458": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Chemistry Department Location: New York The Department of Chemistry at New York University (NYU) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in the area of Bioorganic Chemistry/Chemical Biology , broadly defined. Particular attention will be given to candidates whose research applies innovative approaches in organic chemistry to address significant questions at the chemistry-biology interface. \u00a0The successful applicant is anticipated to be appointed at the Assistant Professor level. \u00a0Successful candidates must have a Ph.D. in chemistry or related field and demonstrate the potential to do pioneering research and teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The Department of Chemistry is continuing a significant growth plan, including the recent creation of the Chemical Biology Center, Simons Center for Computational Physical Chemistry, and the addition of numerous senior and junior faculty members. Interested faculty are able to participate in significant interdisciplinary programs within the Washington Square campus, as well as with NYU\u2019s engineering and medical schools, and scholarly activities at its campuses in Shanghai and Abu Dhabi. Candidates should submit a curriculum vita e, a detailed description of research plans and interests, and a statement of teaching experience and interests. \u00a0The teaching statement may reflect plans to create an inclusive teaching and research mentoring environment. The application should also include three letters of recommendation. The deadline for receipt of applications is October 31, 2026, and review of completed applications will begin at that time.\u00a0 The earliest anticipated start date is September 1, 2027 subject to budgetary and administrative approval. Please submit applications through the following: apply.interfolio.com/194458 . Questions about this position can be sent by email to: chemistry.search@nyu.edu In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $110,000 to $130,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194894": {"title": "College Core Visiting Lecturer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): College Core Curriculum Location: New York City, NY The College Core at New York University invites applications for visiting lecturer appointments, with the rank of Lecturer, to begin September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval.\u00a0 We are seeking recent NYU doctoral alumni with strong records of teaching; and candidacy for these positions is limited to NYU graduates within three years of their degree award.\u00a0 These are full-time, non-tenure track, one-year appointments in either the Foundations of Scientific Inquiry or Foundations of Contemporary Culture components of the Core.\u00a0 On good performance and in view of the teaching needs of the College, the positions are potentially renewable for an additional two years:\u00a0 NOTE, however, that incumbents are limited to a total of three years in this rank combined across all schools of the University.\u00a0 The primary instructional responsibilities are leading three sections of Complexities seminars per term, or the equivalent, and other duties to be assigned; and a one-course relief is granted in recognition of these other activities.\u00a0 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until positions are filled, but for best consideration applications should be made prior to November 1, 2026. Compensation: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual salary for this position is $60,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Successful applicants will have demonstrated effectiveness as college-level teachers, and will have completed their dissertation defense by August 1, 2027. Individuals selected for this position will not be allowed to engage in employment outside of the College Core Curriculum. This position is open to recent NYU PhD graduates only (i.e., received an NYU degree within the past 3 years). Applications must be submitted through Interfolio.\u00a0 Applications should include a cover letter, a recent curriculum vitae, and a draft Complexities Seminar course proposal.\u00a0 In your cover letter, please begin by listing your area of specialization and areas of competence, then discuss your teaching record, before turning to details of your research profile.\u00a0 On your CV, please be sure that your educational record appears first, including the actual or expected month and year of your doctoral degree award. For the Complexities Seminar Proposal: Complexities Seminars meet once per week for a double class period (two 75-minute sessions, back-to-back, with a 15 minute break between them).\u00a0 The course is built around a shared library of course materials, which individual instructors are welcome to supplement in their own class sections.\u00a0 A description of the seminar program can be found at this link .\u00a0 It is expected that every seminar will include at least two (2) modules drawn from the natural sciences, (2) from the social sciences, and (2) from the humanities, and that roughly 2/3 of the course will be composed of materials from the shared resources library.\u00a0 Individual instructors are welcome to fill the remaining 1/3 of the course either with other materials from the shared library or of their own design.\u00a0 NOTE:\u00a0 It is neither expected nor required that applicants\u2019 proposals will include new material. For the proposal, please give a weekly schedule of classes.\u00a0 In addition, please give a narrative description, not more than two (2) pages, single-spaced, in which you discuss your reasons for choosing the course modules to include and how you see them connecting over the weeks of the semester.\u00a0 Should you wish to include class excursions, note that they need either to be readily accessible from campus within the assigned class period or to be such that students can complete them on their own schedules.\u00a0 Complexities Seminars typically are designed around a series of integrative course assignments, often with an intellectually culminating individual or group project.\u00a0 NOTE:\u00a0 You are welcome to include proposed course assignments, especially of creative projects; but keep in mind that there is also a collection of course assignments and activities in the wider shared resources library that others have had success with in the past, and that it is neither expected nor required that applicants\u2019 proposals will include new, detailed plans for course requirements."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194801": {"title": "Associate or Full Professor of Sephardic Studies", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Department of Hebrew and Judaic Studies Location: New York The Skirball Department of Hebrew and Judaic Studies of New York University invites applications for a tenured full-time senior faculty position in Sephardic Studies, at the rank of Associate Professor or Full Professor. The appointment will begin on September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. The appointee is expected to have a doctorate in a field related to Sephardic Studies and a significant record of scholarly publications based on original research. Specialization is open, but all candidates should work primarily in the modern period (beginning in 1500) on the study of Jewish communities in or contact with the Mediterranean, North Africa, the geographical regions covered by the Ottoman Empire, the Americas, Europe, and/or Asia. The appointee is expected to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels. We welcome applicants from any discipline with a particular eye toward candidates who connect with and complement the strengths in Hebrew and Judaic Studies and other departments or programs in Arts and Science at NYU. Inquiries about the position may be directed to the chair of the search committee, Prof. Lila Corwin Berman, lila.corwin.berman@nyu.edu In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $110,000 to $170,000 at the rank of Associate Professor and $135,000 to $225,000 at the rank of Full Professor. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here:https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html. Doctorate in a field related to Sephardic Studies. A significant record of scholarly publications based on original research. Work primarily in the modern period (beginning in 1500) on the study of Jewish communities in or contact with the Mediterranean, North Africa, the geographical regions covered by the Ottoman Empire, the Americas, Europe, and/or Asia. Ability to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Applicants should submit the following: (1) Cover letter that describes research and teaching experience and how the applicant would contribute to widening, deepening, and strengthening the scholarly strengths of Hebrew and Judaic Studies and NYU more generally (2) Curriculum vitae (3) One sample syllabus of a previously taught course (4) A writing sample from a recently published work (25-50 pages) Three academic letters of recommendation may be requested later in the process."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194613": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Hebrew Bible", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Department of Hebrew and Judaic Studies Location: New York The Skirball Department of Hebrew and Judaic Studies at New York University seeks to fill a position in Hebrew Bible at the rank of Assistant Professor, tenure track. The successful candidate will be able to teach a range of undergraduate and graduate courses in Hebrew Bible and related areas as well as supervise doctoral students in the field. Preference will be given to candidates with strong philological skills in Hebrew and related ancient languages and training in the cultures of the ancient Near East. Ability to relate to other aspects of a comprehensive program in Jewish Studies, including NYU\u2019s study away site in Tel Aviv and its Archaeology program, is an asset.\u00a0 The appointee is expected to have a doctorate in a field related to the Hebrew Bible. The appointment will begin September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. Doctorate must be in hand by August 31, 2027. Inquiries on the position may be directed to the chair of the search committee, Professor Alex Jassen, apj205@nyu.edu Pay Transparency Statement: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $110,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html Doctorate in a field related to the Hebrew Bible in hand by August 31, 2027. Ability to teach a range of undergraduate and graduate courses in Hebrew Bible and related areas as well as supervise doctoral students in the field. Strong philological skills in Hebrew and related ancient languages and training in the cultures of the ancient Near East. Ability to relate to other aspects of a comprehensive program in Jewish Studies, including NYU\u2019s study away site in Tel Aviv and its Archaeology program. Applicants should submit the following: (1) Cover letter. This should specifically address the applicant\u2019s qualifications for the position. (2) Curriculum vitae. (3) All completed dissertation chapters (for dissertations in progress) or the complete dissertation (including in revised form). (4) Up to three additional publications that have appeared in print or have been accepted for publication Three academic letters of recommendation must be submitted for the candidate directly on Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194029": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor (AY2026-27)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Economics Department Location: New York, NY The Department of Economics at New York University invites applications for a Clinical Assistant position to begin September 1, 2027, pending budgetary and administrative approval. This is a non-tenured teaching position. Ph.D. and substantial evidence of high-quality teaching are required. Clinical Assistant Professors teach six courses per year (3/3) and are expected to participate in departmental service. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate economics courses at the introductory and advanced levels, according to departmental needs. An ability to teach statistics and econometrics is particularly desirable. Applications should be completed by April 7, 2027. Candidates should submit curriculum vita, statement of teaching and teaching evaluations, and the names of three references. Please visit our website at: http://econ.as.nyu.edu/page/home for information on how to apply. In compliance with NYU\u2019s Transparency Act, the annual salary for this position is USD $ $93,730. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as, market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are\u00a0sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds.\u00a0NYU affirms the value of\u00a0differing perspectives\u00a0on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach.\u00a0To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194880": {"title": "Associate Professor or Professor of Economics", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Economics Department Location: New York The Department of Economics in Arts & Science invites applications for a tenured position to begin September 1, 2027, subject to administrative and budgetary approval. Candidates must have outstanding research records. The search is open to all fields of economics. All candidates are expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses. All materials must be received by December 31, 2026. Candidates should submit curriculum vita, statement of research, and one or more recent papers. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are\u00a0sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds.\u00a0NYU affirms the value of\u00a0differing perspectives\u00a0on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach.\u00a0To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html . In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is a minimum of $200,000 at the rank of Associate Professor and a minimum of $250,000 at the rank of full Professor. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194925": {"title": "Faculty Fellow, European and Mediterranean Studies", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): European and Mediterranean Studies Location: New York The Center for European and Mediterranean Studies in Arts and Science at New York University (CEMS/NYU) invites applicants for a Faculty Fellow position in the field of European Studies. The initial appointment will be for one year beginning September 1, 2027, renewable annually for a maximum of three years, pending administrative and budgetary approval. CEMS/NYU seeks a scholar whose research and teaching explores current or recent European affairs and who specializes in one of the following disciplines: comparative European politics, international relations, sociology, or contemporary European history. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research and teaching focus on the European Union; political, economic, and social developments in contemporary Europe; populist politics and democratic backsliding; European foreign affairs or Global Europe; or topics that expand the boundary of European Studies thematically or geographically . Experience advising student research is strongly preferred. Candidates should also demonstrate through their application materials a commitment to interdisciplinary scholarship and excellence in teaching. Responsibilities include teaching a total of three courses per year (graduate and/or undergraduate), often including a graduate research methods seminar. Student advising is a key component of this position, as the faculty fellow will have a significant role in directing theses for master\u2019s students and the possibility of advising undergraduate honors majors. Service to the program includes organizing events that feature Europe (seminars that bring in outside scholars, for instance) and can involve representing the Center at university occasions and committee meetings. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html . In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $60,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Candidates should have completed their Ph.D. no earlier than 2022 and no later than August 1, 2027. To apply, please submit a cover letter (including research interests and teaching experience), CV, three letters of recommendation, and a writing sample. Applications should be submitted through Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195376": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor of Irish Language, Literature, and Culture", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Ireland House Location: New York Glucksman Ireland House, NYU, invites applicants for the position of clinical assistant professor of Irish language, literature, and culture, to begin Fall 2027. This faculty position will be responsible for teaching courses on the intersection of the Irish language with literature and culture in Ireland and in the diaspora, as well as the language itself. This includes the ability to teach regular undergraduate and graduate-level offerings in elementary, intermediate and advanced Irish language. Candidates must have a Ph.D. in hand by the time of appointment. Appointment will begin September 1, 2027 subject to budgetary and administrative approval. Candidates should also be able to teach our required graduate-level course in Irish language culture and history, as well as electives in their area of research and mentor both undergraduate and Masters students. The successful candidate will also coordinate Irish language instruction for the department, organize annual public-facing initiatives such as the department\u2019s Irish-language immersion days and yearly Irish-language lecture. The standard teaching load for clinical faculty is three courses per semester. Preferred qualifications include the ability to lead individualized study in areas such as early and middle Irish, the history of the Irish language, and the contributions of the Irish language to literature. Experience teaching courses in medieval Irish history and literature is also particularly welcome. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, evidence of effective teaching, writing sample, and the names of three references. Review of applications will begin on November 16 th . In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $93,730. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Glucksman Ireland House\u00a0is New York University\u2019s center for the study of Ireland and the Irish diaspora. Renowned for dynamic programming and research initiatives, Glucksman Ireland House is the home of the Glucksman Irish Diaspora Series with NYU Press, The American Journal of Irish Studies , and the Archives of Irish America. NYU undergraduates may pursue a minor in Irish Studies and graduate students may earn an MA in Irish and Irish-American Studies. Our mission at Glucksman Ireland House NYU is to foster excellence in the study of Ireland, Irish America, and the Irish Diaspora in New York and the global communities. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html . Ph.D. and evidence of effective teaching in the Irish language and in related subject areas. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, evidence of effective teaching, writing sample (25 pages of less), and the names of three references. Review of applications will begin on November 16 th ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194929": {"title": "Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor - Global Works & Society", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Liberal Studies Location: New York, New York Liberal Studies at New York University invites applications for a one-year Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor position to begin September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. The Liberal Studies Core is a dynamic liberal arts curriculum that provides a global and interdisciplinary foundation for nearly 100 NYU majors. The Global Liberal Studies Bachelor of Arts is an innovative global studies major grounded in the spatial, conceptual, and temporal understandings of a highly interconnected world, with a program of study that is distinguished by experiential learning, study away, and independent research focused in an interdisciplinary concentration. In both the LS Core and the GLS major, small, seminar-style classes and close faculty-student interaction provide students with the benefits of a liberal arts college within a large urban research university. We are especially interested in hiring qualified candidates who can contribute their research, teaching, and service to the intellectual diversity and excellence of Liberal Studies and NYU. We seek applicants for the following position: GLOBAL WORKS AND SOCIETY PhD in Political Theory, Philosophy, History, or related fields in the social sciences and the humanities. Candidates should have the ability to teach global works that examine enduring questions of philosophy, religion, political and social theory, and history from antiquity to the early 18th century in the Global Works and Society sequence of the Core Curriculum. Candidates must demonstrate the ability to draw on ancient and early modern sources in their teaching with a global emphasis. Candidates must embrace interdisciplinary and intersectional approaches from a variety of global perspectives and must have the ability to examine relationships of power and to interrogate the historical roots of current challenges. Candidates must have the ability to teach upper-division seminars in their areas of expertise. Visiting Clinical Faculty teach three classes per semester, advise undergraduates, and may optionally contribute service to Liberal Studies and to the University. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $75,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Liberal Studies sees equity and inclusion and belonging as essential to education and the development of leadership in a globally interconnected world. These values inform LS as a collegial and respectful environment for students, faculty, and staff\u2014with particular attention to improving the student experience. To learn more about the Liberal Studies commitment to equity and inclusion and belonging, please read here: Global Inclusion . NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: A&S Inclusive Excellence . Minimum qualifications: PhD by the date of appointment; three years of college-level teaching experience; and demonstrated excellence in teaching. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who share Liberal Studies\u2019 commitment to equity and inclusion and belonging, and who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life in LS and NYU. Candidates must present demonstrated experience and commitment to interdisciplinarity, intersectionality, and a teaching approach informed by global perspectives. Publications and evidence of outstanding scholarship and relevant professional activity are strongly encouraged. Applications include ONLY (1) a cover letter and (2) a current CV. Complete applications must be recorded by 11:59 p.m. Eastern Standard Time (US), November 15, 2026 . Applications recorded after this deadline will not be considered."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194940": {"title": "Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor - Global Cultures (East Asia)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Liberal Studies Location: New York, New York Liberal Studies at New York University invites applications for a one year Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor position to begin September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. The Liberal Studies Core is a dynamic liberal arts curriculum that provides a global and interdisciplinary foundation for nearly 100 NYU majors. The Global Liberal Studies Bachelor of Arts is an innovative global studies major grounded in the spatial, conceptual, and temporal understandings of a highly interconnected world, with a program of study that is distinguished by experiential learning, study away, and independent research focused in an interdisciplinary concentration. In both the LS Core and the GLS major, small, seminar-style classes and close faculty student interaction provide students with the benefits of a liberal arts college within a large urban research university. We are especially interested in hiring qualified candidates who can contribute their research, teaching and service to the diversity and excellence of Liberal Studies and NYU. We seek applicants for the following position: GLOBAL CULTURES Ph.D. in East Asian Studies, Cultural Studies, Gender and Sexuality Studies, Comparative Literature, History, Political Science, Anthropology, or related fields in the Social Sciences or Humanities. Candidates must have the ability to teach interdisciplinary, intersectional seminars on East Asia to introduce undergraduates to the politics, history, intellectual thought, arts, culture, and development of the region within a global context, interrogating the historical and interconnected roots of current geopolitical and cultural forces, including but not limited to ethno-nationalism, gender, race, capitalism and neocolonialism. Preference for expertise in Korea and/or Japan. Visiting Clinical Faculty normally teach three classes per semester, advise undergraduates, and may optionally contribute service to Liberal Studies and to the University. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $75,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Liberal Studies sees equity and inclusion and belonging as essential to education and the development of leadership in a globally interconnected world. These values inform LS as a collegial and respectful environment for students, faculty, and staff\u2014with particular attention to improving the student experience. To learn more about the Liberal Studies commitment to equity and inclusion and belonging, please read here: Global Inclusion . NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: A&S Inclusive Excellence . Minimum qualifications: PhD by the date of appointment; three years of college-level teaching experience; and demonstrated excellence in teaching. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who share Liberal Studies\u2019 commitment to equity, inclusion, and belonging, and who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life in LS and NYU. Candidates must present demonstrated experience and commitment to interdisciplinarity, intersectionality, and a teaching approach informed by global perspectives. Publications and evidence of outstanding scholarship and relevant professional activity are strongly encouraged. Applications include ONLY (1) a cover letter and (2) a current CV. Complete applications must be recorded by 11:59 p.m. Eastern Standard Time (US), November 15, 2026. Applications recorded after this deadline will not be considered."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194936": {"title": "Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor - Arts & Cultures", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Liberal Studies Location: New York, New York Liberal Studies at New York University invites applications for a one year Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor position to begin September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. The Liberal Studies Core is a dynamic liberal arts curriculum that provides a global and interdisciplinary foundation for nearly 100 NYU majors. The Global Liberal Studies Bachelor of Arts is an innovative global studies major grounded in the spatial, conceptual, and temporal understandings of a highly interconnected world, with a program of study that is distinguished by experiential learning, study away, and independent research focused in an interdisciplinary concentration. In both the LS Core and the GLS major, small, seminar-style classes and close faculty student interaction provide students with the benefits of a liberal arts college within a large urban research university. We are especially interested in hiring qualified candidates who can contribute their research, teaching and service to the intellectual diversity and excellence of Liberal Studies and NYU. We seek applicants for the following position: ARTS & CULTURES PhD in the humanities, broadly conceived (Classics, Ancient World Studies, Ancient Near Eastern Studies, Egyptology, Indian Ocean or East Asian Studies with specialization in the ancient world, Ancient Art History and Archaeology with literature teaching experience, or other fields related to the study of the ancient and premodern worlds). Preferred candidates will have a specialization in the Ancient World who can also teach the Global Middle Ages and Renaissance. The candidate must be able to teach the first two courses in the Liberal Studies Arts and Cultures Core sequence: \"Arts and Cultures across Antiquity\" & \"Arts and Cultures towards the Crossroads\". This includes the ability to teach both ancient literature from a diverse set of cultures and their visual and material cultural production. Preference will be given to candidates who demonstrate interdisciplinary and cross-cultural competency. Visiting Clinical Faculty teach three classes per semester, advise undergraduates, and may optionally contribute service to Liberal Studies and to the University. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $75,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Liberal Studies sees equity and inclusion and belonging as essential to education and the development of leadership in a globally interconnected world. These values inform LS as a collegial and respectful environment for students, faculty, and staff\u2014with particular attention to improving the student experience. To learn more about the Liberal Studies commitment to equity and inclusion and belonging, please read here: Global Inclusion . NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: A&S Inclusive Excellence . Minimum qualifications: PhD by the date of appointment; three years of college-level teaching experience; and demonstrated excellence in teaching. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who share Liberal Studies\u2019 commitment to equity, inclusion, and belonging, and who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life in LS and NYU. Candidates must present demonstrated experience and commitment to interdisciplinarity, intersectionality, and a teaching approach informed by global perspectives. Publications and evidence of outstanding scholarship and relevant professional activity are strongly encouraged. Applications include ONLY (1) a cover letter (2) and a current CV. Complete applications must be recorded by 11:59 p.m. Eastern Standard Time (US), November 15, 2026. Applications recorded after this deadline will not be considered."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194933": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor - Writing", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Liberal Studies Location: New York, New York Liberal Studies at New York University invites applications for a Clinical Assistant Professor position to begin September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. The Liberal Studies Core is a dynamic liberal arts curriculum that provides a global and interdisciplinary foundation for nearly 100 NYU majors. The Global Liberal Studies Bachelor of Arts is an innovative global studies major grounded in the spatial, conceptual, and temporal understandings of a highly interconnected world, with a program of study that is distinguished by experiential learning, study away, and independent research focused in an interdisciplinary concentration. In both the LS Core and the GLS major, small, seminar-style classes and close faculty-student interaction provide students with the benefits of a liberal arts college within a large urban research university. We are especially interested in hiring qualified candidates who can contribute through their research, teaching, and service to the diversity and excellence of Liberal Studies and NYU. We seek applicants for the following position: WRITING PhD or MFA in Writing Studies, Creative Writing, Nonfiction Writing, Rhetoric, Journalism, Literary Studies, Translation Studies, TESOL, Applied Linguistics, or a closely related field. Candidates must have the ability and demonstrated experience to teach in the first-year writing sequence of the Core Curriculum with a global, interdisciplinary, and intersectional emphasis, as this will be the primary responsibility of the position. Candidates with additional specialization or secondary fields in documentary arts (such as film and/or podcasting), science/environmental writing, and/or qualitative research writing are strongly encouraged to apply. Along with a focus on first-year writing, candidates should be prepared to contribute to upper-level courses in a concentration of the Global Liberal Studies degree with a particular focus on the creative and theoretical implications of their work in a cross-cultural or otherwise global context. All Liberal Studies full-time faculty hold renewable term contracts, initially three years, and later six years in duration. Faculty are eligible for promotion within the Clinical faculty ranks (Assistant, Associate, Full). Faculty normally teach three classes per semester, advise undergraduates, and contribute service to Liberal Studies and to the University. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $93,730. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Liberal Studies sees equity and inclusion and belonging as essential to education and the development of leadership in a globally interconnected world. These values inform LS as a collegial and respectful environment for students, faculty, and staff\u2014with particular attention to improving the student experience. To learn more about the Liberal Studies commitment to equity and inclusion and belonging, please read here: Global Inclusion . NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here . Minimum qualifications: MFA by the date of appointment; three years of college-level teaching experience; and demonstrated excellence in teaching. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who share Liberal Studies\u2019 commitment to equity, inclusion, and belonging, and who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life in LS and NYU. Candidates must present demonstrated experience and commitment to interdisciplinarity, intersectionality, and a teaching approach informed by global perspectives. Publications and evidence of outstanding scholarship and relevant professional activity are strongly encouraged. Applications include ONLY (1) a cover letter and (2) a current CV. Complete applications must be recorded by 11:59 p.m. Eastern Standard Time (US), November 15, 2026 . Applications recorded after this deadline will not be considered."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194934": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor - Economics", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Liberal Studies Location: New York, New York Liberal Studies at New York University invites applications for a Clinical Assistant Professor position to begin September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. The Liberal Studies Core is a dynamic liberal arts curriculum that provides a global and interdisciplinary foundation for nearly 100 NYU majors. The Global Liberal Studies Bachelor of Arts is an innovative global studies major grounded in the spatial, conceptual, and temporal understandings of a highly interconnected world, with a program of study that is distinguished by experiential learning, study away, and independent research focused in an interdisciplinary concentration. In both the LS Core and the GLS major, small, seminar-style classes and close faculty-student interaction provide students with the benefits of a liberal arts college within a large urban research university. We are especially interested in hiring qualified candidates who can contribute their research, teaching, and service to the diversity and excellence of Liberal Studies and NYU. We seek applicants for the following position: ECONOMICS PhD in Economics with a focus on the economics of stratification, inequality, and the political economy of racial capitalism. Candidates should be able to teach introductory microeconomics and macroeconomics in a liberal arts context from both mainstream and heterodox perspectives. We seek colleagues who, as needed, will teach intermediate and advanced courses in the listed fields of specialization in the Economy and Society Concentration (E&S) of Global Liberal Studies. Candidates should emphasize interdisciplinary and global connections in their teaching and research. They must apply theoretical analysis to real-world events in lectures and show a commitment to fostering an inclusive, collegial classroom environment. All Liberal Studies full-time faculty hold renewable term contracts, initially three years, and later six years in duration. Faculty are eligible for promotion within the Clinical faculty ranks (Assistant, Associate, Full). Faculty normally teach three classes per semester, advise undergraduates, and contribute service to Liberal Studies and to the University. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $93,730. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Liberal Studies sees equity and inclusion and belonging as essential to education and the development of leadership in a globally interconnected world. These values inform LS as a collegial and respectful environment for students, faculty, and staff\u2014with particular attention to improving the student experience. To learn more about the Liberal Studies commitment to equity and inclusion and belonging, please read here: Global Inclusion . NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: A&S Inclusive Excellence . Minimum qualifications: PhD by the date of appointment; three years of college-level teaching experience; and demonstrated excellence in teaching. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who share Liberal Studies\u2019 commitment to equity, inclusion, and belonging, and who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life in LS and NYU. Candidates must present demonstrated experience and commitment to interdisciplinarity, intersectionality, and a teaching approach informed by global perspectives. Publications and evidence of outstanding scholarship and relevant professional activity are strongly encouraged. Applications include ONLY (1) a cover letter and (2) a current CV. Complete applications must be recorded by 11:59 p.m. Eastern Standard Time (US), November 15, 2026 . Applications recorded after this deadline will not be considered."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193302": {"title": "Bersoff Faculty Fellow", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Philosophy Department Location: New York, NY The Department of Philosophy of New York University ( http://as.nyu.edu/philosophy.html ) invites applications for up to four Bersoff Faculty Fellowships, areas of specialization open, to begin September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. This is a one-year appointment possibly renewable for a second year. Responsibilities include teaching two undergraduate courses per year; both courses may be taught in the same semester, leaving the other semester free to pursue research. In case of an appointment to a second year, one of the two courses may be a graduate seminar. For a candidate to be eligible, the Ph.D. must be received between September 1, 2022 and August 31, 2027. Applications should include a CV, a writing sample, a research statement, a teaching statement, evidence of teaching ability, and at least three references. Applications must be received by November 15th, 2026 to ensure full consideration. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html . Pay Transparency: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $60,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. For a candidate to be eligible, the Ph.D. must be received between September 1, 2022 and August 31, 2027. Applications should include a CV, a writing sample, a research statement, a teaching statement and evidence of teaching ability, and at least three references. Applicants are welcome, but not required, to submit a statement discussing their commitment to the values of diversity, equity, and inclusion. Applicants who wish to submit such a statement should do so under \u201coptional documents\u201d."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195467": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate/Research Scientist", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Philosophy Department Location: New York University The Department of Philosophy and the Center for Mind, Brain, and Consciousness at New York University invite applications for one or two Post-Doctoral Associate or Research Scientist positions, with areas of specialization in philosophy of artificial intelligence and philosophy of mind, to begin Spring 2027 or Fall of 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. These may be one-year or two-year appointments, possibly renewable depending on funding. These positions are associated with grants to Professor David Chalmers for projects on artificial consciousness and related topics at the intersection of artificial intelligence and the philosophy of mind (possibly including AI mentality, AI interpretability, and AI welfare).\u00a0 Applicants should highlight their interests and strengths in these areas.\u00a0 These positions are research-oriented positions with no teaching duties. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for a Post-Doctoral Associate is $62,500. The annual base salary for a Research Scientist is $62,500. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . These positions are based in New York and the selected candidates will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. For a candidate to be eligible for a Post-Doctoral Associate position, the Ph.D. must be received within five years prior to the appointment. For a Research Scientist position, the Ph.D. must have been received within 5-10 years of the appointment. Applications should include a CV, one or more writing samples, a research statement, and at least three references. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. Applications must be received by November 15, 2026 to ensure full consideration."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174949": {"title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Position in Theoretical Quantum Condensed Matter, Quantum Optics, and Quantum Information Physics", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Physics Department Location: New York, NY Center for Quantum Phenomena, Department of Physics, Arts and Science New York University The Department of Physics at New York University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the Center for Quantum Phenomena (CQP). The start date is September 1, 2026, pending administrative and budgetary approval. A successful candidate will hold a Ph.D. in physics or a related discipline and will be expected to teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels at NYU, while establishing a leading research program in theoretical quantum condensed-matter physics, quantum optics, quantum information sciences, or a related field. Research programs in the CQP include terahertz spectroscopy of correlated materials, quantum optics and ultracold atoms, out-of-equilibrium and strongly driven quantum systems, topological phases, spintronics, Moire materials and color centers in wide-bandgap insulators. We seek candidates whose research will integrate with and enhance these efforts, while also establishing new and complementary directions in quantum science. Qualifications: Successful candidates will have a Ph.D. in physics or a related discipline, and one or more years of postdoctoral research experience is desirable. Candidates are expected to establish a leading research program in their field, as well as teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels. More information about the Physics Department and the Center for Quantum Phenomena can be found at https://as.nyu.edu/departments/physics.html and https://as.nyu.edu/departments/physics/research/center-for-quantum-phenomena.html Pay Transparency Statement: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $$89,000 to $129,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are\u00a0sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds.\u00a0NYU affirms the value of\u00a0differing perspectives\u00a0on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach.\u00a0To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html . Successful candidates will have a Ph.D. in physics or a related discipline, and one or more years of postdoctoral research experience is desirable. Candidates are expected to establish a leading research program in their field, as well as teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Please apply online here at Interfolio with the following: (1) a curriculum vitae including a list of publications, (2) a description of current and planned research activities, (3) a discussion of planned teaching and outreach activities, (4) three confidential letters of recommendation (submitted through Interfolio), (5) a cover letter applying for the position. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2025 and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/176027": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Shabani)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Physics Department Location: New York, NY Project involves deep expertise with microwave and qubit characterization of super/semi materials. The job also requires dilution refrigerator characterization of the quantum circuits. This project involves collaborating with theoretical and experimental groups within and outside NYU. Team work, good communication skills and discipline in summarizing results are necessary. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $68,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. PhD in related field"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177238": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Kent)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Physics Department Location: New York, NY  A postdoctoral position is available in Prof. Andy Kent\u2019s research group in the Center for Quantum Phenomena of the Department of Physics. The research involves the study of spin-torque oscillators based on quantum materials. The aim is to explore oscillators based on transition metal oxides with competing electronic phases (e.g., metal/insulating, magnetic/non-magnetic, etc.) with applications to neuromorphic computing. The successful candidate will characterize spin oscillators using high-speed/frequency electrical test stands and fabricate appropriate samples using electron beam lithography. Experience with electronic transport, magnetic measurements, magnetic materials, microwave techniques, and nanofabrication is desirable. The candidate will work with a multidisciplinary team of experts from multiple universities, specializing in quantum materials and magnetic modeling. Good communication and interpersonal skills are essential. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500.00- $64,000.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . Ph.D. in Physics, Applied Physics, or Electrical Engineering, within 0-5 years of post-PhD training."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180633": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Weiner)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Physics Department Location: New York, NY To study physics beyond the standard model and its phenomenological implications with a focus on theory. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $68,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. PhD in Physics"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180684": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Cuesta-Lazaro)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Physics Department Location: New York, NY Postdoctoral position at NYU's physics department to work on machine learning and applications to\u00a0Cosmology. Candidates\u00a0are expected to carry out an active research program that can be independently directed and/or involve substantial collaboration with other members of NYU. In addition to their own research, candidates are expected to help build the rich scientific community\u00a0on\u00a0cosmology at\u00a0NYU by: participating in seminars, colloquia, and group meetings; developing their software, mathematical, and computational expertise through internal educational opportunities; and sharing their knowledge through scientific publications, presentations, and/or software releases. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500.00 -\u00a0 $71,320.31. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . PhD in Physical Sciences or Computer Science"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188743": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Kostinski)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Physics Department Location: New York, NY A postdoctoral associate position is available at the Department of Physics, New York University under the supervision of Prof. Sarah Kostinski. The research will be focused on theoretical aspects of statistical and biological physics. Candidates for the position must have a PhD in physics or a related discipline, preferably with expertise in stochastic processes, nonlinear dynamics, and biological physics. The intended start date is September 1, 2026, but earlier or later start dates can be arranged. The initial appointment is for one year subject to performance and continued funding, with the possibility of a second and a third year. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. PhD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189154": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Gershow)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Physics Department Location: 726 Broadway New York, NY 10003 Analyze proprioceptive signaling in larval Drosophila using two-photon tracking microscopy developed in the Gershow lab. The postdoctoral researcher will design and conduct imaging experiments, analyze volumetric imaging datasets, and interpret results. There will also be the opportunity for tool development in microscopy and image analysis. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. PhD in Physics, neuroscience, biology or a related field. Experience in optics, programming, and/or Drosophila neurobiology desired but not essential."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194625": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Kostinski)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Physics Department Location: 726 Broadway A postdoctoral associate position is available at the Department of Physics, New York University under the supervision of Prof. Sarah Kostinski. The research will be focused on theoretical aspects of statistical and biological physics. Candidates for the position must have a PhD in physics or a related discipline, preferably with expertise in stochastic processes, nonlinear dynamics, and biological physics. The intended start date is November 2, 2026, but later start dates can be arranged. The initial appointment is for one year subject to performance and continued funding, with the possibility of a second and a third year. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer . PhD To apply, include CV and publications (2) We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195116": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Porrati)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Physics Department Location: 726 Broadway New York, NY 10003 The newly established Carl Feinberg Institute for Theoretical Physics (CFITP) at New York University invites applications for the Feinberg Postdoctoral Prize Fellowship, beginning September 1, 2027. This is a three-year Postdoctoral Associate appointment with a competitive salary, an annual research and travel allowance, and full independence in choice of research direction. About the Institute: CFITP's mission is to advance fundamental research at the intersection of particle theory, condensed matter theory, quantum information, and quantum gravity leveraging computational and AI methods where appropriate. The Postdoctoral Associates will perform research within the scope of the CFITP. They are expected to pursue a vigorous research program, to engage across the Institute's research areas, and to participate in its seminars, workshops, and visitor program. Postdoctoral Associates have access to the full resources of the NYU Department of Physics and to the broader New York theoretical physics community. Postdoctoral Associates are eligible for on-campus subsidized housing, subject to availability Eligibility: Applicants must hold a Ph.D. or equivalent qualifications in physics, mathematics, or a closely related field by the start date and should have received it no more than five years before September 1, 2027. We seek candidates working in any area within the Institute's scope, including quantum field theory, string theory and quantum gravity, particle phenomenology, strongly correlated and topological phases, nonequilibrium quantum dynamics, quantum information, and the application of computational and machine-learning methods to these problems. Candidates whose work bridges two or more of these areas are especially encouraged to apply. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500.00-$90,000.00. \u00a0New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. PhD To appy, include: CV publication research statement letters of recommendation or evaluation (3) Application: Applications received by December 1, 2026 will receive full consideration; the position will remain open until filled. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193724": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Politics Department Location: New York The Wilf Family Department of Politics seeks outstanding and innovative scholars for a position in US politics at the assistant professor level. The position is to commence on September 1, 2027, pending administrative and budgetary approval. Candidates are expected to possess a PhD by September 2027 in political science or a cognate discipline; to have records of excellent research; and to indicate the potential to be future disciplinary leaders in their respective fields of expertise. Candidates should also have demonstrated ability as excellent teachers at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. The Politics Department at NYU is dedicated to the goal of building a diverse and inclusive academic community. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from candidates who can demonstrate through their scholarship, teaching and mentoring, and/or service engagement commitment to these goals. We are particularly interested in hearing about steps you have taken (or plan to take) to foster an open and inclusive intellectual environment in the classroom, in the department and on campus, and/or in your field more generally. Please apply online through Interfolio.\u00a0 Candidates seeking the fullest consideration should submit their applications by October 6, 2026 . Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, research statement, teaching statement, writing sample, and three confidential letters of recommendation. Candidates are strongly encouraged to supply links to their professional websites; this will enhance their applications by allowing the search committee easy access to additional information that might be needed. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $95,000 \u2013 $145,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/166631": {"title": "Visiting Lecturer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Psychology Department Location: New York, New York The Department of Psychology in the College of Arts and Science at New York University seeks applicants for a full-time, non-tenure-track Visiting Lecturer in its undergraduate and graduate programs. This position will start in September 2025, pending final budgetary and administrative approval. The duties of the position include: 1) teaching multiple courses in the undergraduate and graduate psychology programs in personality and social psychology, 2) student advising and research/thesis mentoring, and 3) collaborating with faculty. The ideal candidate will have extensive teaching experience in research focusing on personality and social psychology, with a track record of teaching methods and statistics classes. The candidate will be conducting research with faculty and advising students. Candidates should specify their experience and knowledge of relevant research approaches in personality and social psychology in their cover letter (2 pages maximum) and CV . Any experience with teaching\u2014especially methods and statistics courses--should be described as well. Candidates should express a commitment to working with students from a diverse global population. A Ph.D. in Psychology is required. If the selected candidate will graduate in Summer 2025, the Psychology Department must receive by July 31, 2025 a signed Doctoral Thesis Reader Sheet from the primary adviser approving the dissertation for defense. The dissertation must be successfully defended and deposited to the Graduate School on or before September 10, 2025 in time for the graduation deadlines. Please submit a cover letter (2 pages maximum) and CV . Materials should succinctly provide evidence of teaching excellence (e.g., teaching experience, course evaluations), research excellence (e.g., research interests, publications, awards), and experience mentoring student research in personality and social psychology. Please note the classes you are willing to teach (especially methods and statistics classes at the undergrad and graduate level). To apply, see the following link (LINK). For more information, see the NYU Department of Psychology website https://as.nyu.edu/departments/psychology/current-faculty-searches.html . Review of applications will begin May 1, 2025, and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0Applicants selected as finalists will be asked to arrange for a minimum of three letters of recommendation to be sent followed by virtual interviews. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $70,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. The Faculty of Arts and Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber, who embody the diversity of the United States as well as the global society in which we live.\u00a0Because broad diversity is essential for creating an inclusive climate, we are committed to the fair treatment of and equal access to opportunity and advancement for all, will assess the many qualifications of all applicants.\u00a0We strongly encourage applications from women, racial and ethnic minorities, and other individuals who are under-represented in the profession, across color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, gender and sexual identity, or any other legally protected basis. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the FAS commitment to diversity, equality and inclusion, please see http://as.nyu.edu/facultydiversity.html . To learn more about NYUs commitment to equal opportunity see the Equal Employment Opportunity Statement at EOE/Affirmative Action/Minorities/Females/Vet/Disabled/Sexual Orientation/Gender Identity. For people in the EU see www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr for information on your privacy rights under GDPR"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170656": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (InfoCogLab)", "description": "Posted: Jul 17, 2025 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Psychology Department Location: New York, NY The InfoCogLab at NYU, directed by PI Noga Zaslavsky, is seeking to fill a Postdoctoral Associate position. We are looking for a highly motivated postdoc with strong computational skills and profound interest in understanding the fundamental principles that underlie human and artificial cognition. Research focus areas include, but are not limited to, any of the following: Language learning and development in humans and AI Multi-agent models and evolutionary dynamics of language and cognition Integrative models of language, perception, and action Pragmatic reasoning and theory of mind The position will involve leading cutting-edge research projects at the intersection of cognitive science and AI, while working closely with the PI and other lab members. The InfoCogLab is based in the Psychology Department at NYU and maintains an affiliation with NYU\u2019s Center for Data Science. It provides a unique intellectual environment with many opportunities for collaboration, mentorship, and professional development, as well as state-of-the-art research facilities and resources. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. For full consideration apply by October 17, 2025 . Start date is flexible, but is expected to be in 2026. Questions or inquiries about the position may be directed to Noga Zaslavsky at nogaz@nyu.edu. In compliance with NYC\u2019s\u00a0Pay\u00a0Transparency\u00a0Act, the annual base\u00a0salary\u00a0range for this position is $62,500-$90,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A PhD in computer science, cognitive science, neuroscience, linguistics, or related field by the start date is required. Strong background in working with computational models (such as deep learning models and/or Bayesian models) and strong programing skills. Demonstrated ability to lead research projects and work collaboratively. Background in information theory, cognitive science, or web-based experiments is not required but would be an advantage. Please apply with the following materials: Cover letter CV Research statement, describing the applicant\u2019s broader research agenda and its potential synergies with the position (up to 2-3 pages, one concise page would be fine too) Two samples of published or other written work Three reference letters"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175497": {"title": "Junior Laboratory Associate (Human-AI Trust) (Ho/Sucholutsky Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Psychology Department Location: New York, NY The Computation and Decision-Making Lab (PI: Dr. Mark Ho) and AI Thought Partner Lab (PI: Dr. Ilia Sucholutsky) at NYU invite applications for a Junior Laboratory Associate to work on interdisciplinary projects exploring decision-making in humans and AI systems and the circumstances under which people will trust AI systems to make decisions on their behalf. PIs are seeking two qualified candidates. The position will involve a collaborative project with researchers at Princeton University (Tom Griffiths, Peter Henderson, Tania Lombrozo, Natalia V\u00e9lez), New York University (Mark Ho, Ilia Sucholutsky), Boston University (Josh Peterson), and Cornell University (Nori Jacoby), and will provide opportunities to visit and collaborate with members of these different research groups. Applicants should have a Bachelor\u2019s degree. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a curriculum vitae / resume, and names and contact information for three references. Positions are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. In compliance with NYC\u2019s\u00a0Pay\u00a0Transparency\u00a0Act, the annual base\u00a0salary\u00a0range for this position is $58,656-$60,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. \u00b7 B.A. or B.S. in Cognitive Science, Psychology, or a related field. \u00b7 Strong background in experimental design and data analysis. \u00b7 Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a curriculum vitae / resume, and names and contact information for three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183820": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Levine/Muthukrishna Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Psychology Department Location: New York, NY Dr. Sydney Levine and Dr. Michael Muthukrishna are jointly recruiting a Postdoctoral Associate working across their two labs at NYU starting this summer. You will be working at the intersection of cultural evolution, computational moral cognition, and AI safety. Human societies have solved an extraordinary version of the multi-agent alignment problem, getting millions to billions of individuals to cooperate, coordinate, and enforce shared moral norms. Cultural evolution built these solutions over generations, encoded and transmitted through connected beliefs, values, behaviors, norms, and institutions. The cognitive mechanisms that underpin moral judgment - rule-following, outcome evaluation, bargaining mechanisms - are themselves products of this evolutionary process, shaped to facilitate cooperation under human time, information, and cognitive constraints. Today, we\u2019re building AI systems that navigate the same landscape of pluralistic and often competing human values and incentives, but with new constraints and lack of constraints. Our goal is to bring these three fields together, asking how insights from millennia of cultural evolution and the cognitive science of moral judgment can be brought to bear on the challenge of AI alignment. To offer a glimpse of the kind of questions and approaches you would be working on, consider the question of how do the mechanisms of cooperation operate at scale, how are they made concrete in moral norms, and can analogous mechanisms be engineered into multi-agent AI systems? You would answer this question by building and testing computational models, developing multi-agent simulations where agents, aligned to different users\u2019 values, must coordinate, negotiate, and resolve conflicts, and designing and testing methods for aligning individual AI agents to user values in ways that reflect how humans transmit those values and align to one another. These synchronic models of moral cognition will be complemented by diachronic models of how moral consensus emergence and expands over cultural evolutionary time. The diversity of human values are themselves continually evolving - how might they coevolve with AI agents? This is a collaboration between Levine\u2019s lab (computational moral cognition, AI safety) and Muthukrishna\u2019s lab (cultural evolution, cooperation, cross-cultural and historic variation), and may include collaboration with their respective networks both within and outside academia. The position is housed in the NYU Psychology Department, bridging social and cognitive psychology, with natural links to philosophy, economics, and computer science. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500-$90,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. You have a PhD (or are close to completion) in computer science, computational cognitive science, or any quantitative science, and a genuine interest in working across disciplinary boundaries. We\u2019re especially excited about candidates with backgrounds or serious interest in one or more of: computational modeling of social learning, norms, or moral cognition; cultural evolution and gene-culture coevolution; evolutionary game theory and the evolution of cooperation; mechanism design and institutional design; cooperative AI and multi-agent systems; the study and development of large language models; cross-cultural psychology and large-scale behavioral data; philosophy of morality or social contract theory. You don\u2019t need all of these disciplinary backgrounds, but you should be someone who finds it natural to move between individual cognition, population dynamics, and engineered systems. Please include a CV, a brief research statement describing how your background connects to the themes above, and the names of two references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187252": {"title": "Visiting Lecturer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Psychology Department Location: New York, New York The visiting lecturer will provide individualized and small group research and thesis support for MA candidates in the general psychology program, including research design, quantitative methods, and scientific writing. The visiting lecturer will meet with and assist students with all aspects of thesis development and completion including conceptualization, study design, data collection, data analysis, and writing in compliance with APA publication standards. Note that this position does not typically include formal classroom teaching. The position will begin on September 1, 2026 to August 31, 2027 pending administrative and budgetary approval. The initial appointment is for one year and may be renewed for two additional years. We are seeking to hire a recent doctoral graduate in Psychology with a strong dedication to student mentoring and/or teaching, relevant research experience including demonstrated facility with research design and quantitative methods, familiarity with the Psychology curriculum and student body, and a commitment to questions of equity and diversity. Both internal and external candidates are welcome to apply. Applications should include (1 a cover letter detailing how the candidate\u2019s qualifications fit this particular position and explaining how\u00a0they have addressed issues of equity and inclusion particularly in a classroom and mentoring environment ; and (2) example(s) of research reporting demonstrating competence in research design and best practices, and quantitative methods; and (3) a curriculum vitae. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $70,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are\u00a0sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds.\u00a0NYU affirms the value of\u00a0differing perspectives\u00a0on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about our commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here http://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194608": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Mattar Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Psychology Department Location: New York, NY The Mattar Lab at New York University seeks a Postdoctoral Associate to conduct research on computational models of human learning and decision-making. The research will combine behavioral experiments, computational modeling, and machine learning to develop more general accounts of human cognition across diverse tasks. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500-$90,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Applicants should have a PhD in psychology, neuroscience, cognitive science, computer science, or a related field by the start date, with strong quantitative and computational research skills. Please apply with your CV."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195772": {"title": "Assistant Professor in Brain and Behavior", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Psychology Department Location: New York The Department of Psychology and Center for Neural Science in Arts & Science at New York University invites applications for a joint, tenure-track assistant professor position to study the neural basis of cognition. The appointment is expected to begin September 1, 2027, pending budgetary and administrative approval. We seek applicants with an outstanding record of research in human neuroscience. Examples of research areas include, but are not limited to, attention, perception, decision making, memory, or executive function. Research programs that incorporate computational approaches and use neuroimaging methods (e.g., fMRI) are preferred. Candidates should have a PhD, MD, or equivalent degree, postdoctoral experience, and a strong record of research achievement. Appointees will establish their own independent research program and contribute to the teaching of undergraduate and graduate students. To apply, please follow the Interfolio link (apply.interfolio.com/195772). For more information about the academic environment, please visit each department's website ( http://as.nyu.edu/psychology.html https://as.nyu.edu/cns.html ). For questions or additional information, please contact Professor Catherine Hartley at cate@nyu.edu. Review of applications will begin November 15, 2026. The electronic application should include: \u2022 Cover letter that includes a brief summary of your most significant research accomplishments \u2022 Curriculum vitae \u2022 Concise research statement (no more than three pages) that describes (a) your experience and qualifications and (b) your goals and vision for an independent research program, including possible links to the NYU research community. \u2022 Three recommendation letters, submitted by the referees through Interfolio dossier services \u2022 Copies of at least three representative publications \u2022 Teaching statement (no more than two pages), which includes concrete steps you have taken and that you are planning to take to foster an inclusive intellectual environment for the NYU student population. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html. Pay Transparency In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $110,000 - $130,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189355": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate \u2014 Social Cohesion in Action", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Sociology Department Location: New York Professors Delia Baldassarri, Christopher Barrie, and BK Lee at New York University are seeking a Postdoctoral Associate to join their research project on Social Cohesion in Action: AI-Calibrated Models of Hyperlocal Community Responses to Adverse Events, a project examining how communities respond to disruptive events, and what sustains or erodes social cohesion and collective action in their aftermath. The project analyzes digital trace data from neighborhood-based safety and community platforms, drawing on event alerts, user comments, and engagement patterns, and develops LLM\u2011driven agent-based simulation models of community response, grounded in social scientific theory and empirical data. The postdoc will work closely with the PIs to analyze digital trace data; design, build, and refine simulation models of community response to adverse events; integrate empirical and computational methods; and contribute to the project\u2019s broader research agenda. There will also be opportunities to pursue related independent research. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. The position will ideally begin in September 2026. This is initially a one-year appointment, renewable for up to two years. Review of applications will begin August 1. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual starting base salary for this position is $75,000-$85,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Candidates should hold a PhD in a social science discipline (e.g., sociology, political science, economics) or in computer science or a related field, required at the time of hiring. We are looking for someone with strong quantitative and computational skills, and the ability to work both independently and as part of a collaborative research team. Knowledge of agent-based models (ABMs) or related simulation methods (e.g., Generative ABMs), as well as of LLMs and their application to social science research, is required; software engineering experience is desirable. Submit a CV, cover letter, writing sample, and references Feel free to contact us for more information: Delia Baldassarri (delia.b@nyu.edu), Christopher Barrie (cb5691@nyu.edu), or BK Lee (bklee@nyu.edu)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193450": {"title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Management and Public Service", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Robert F. Wagner Graduate School of Public Service: Academic Affairs Location: New York The NYU Wagner Graduate School of Public Service is seeking highly qualified candidates for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Management and Public Service position with an expected start date of August 2027. The school seeks candidates who have completed their doctoral program or post-doctoral appointment by the start date or are two years or less into an Assistant Professor position. We will consider candidates from a wide range of fields but are especially interested in candidates with a PhD or DBA in Management / Organizational Behavior, Strategy, Healthcare Management, Organizational Sociology, Personnel Economics, and Public Administration. Applications should demonstrate a strong record, or evidence of strong potential, for publishing innovative, rigorous research that is relevant to public service organizations and fields in top-rated academic journals. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $125,000 - $145,000. NYU Wagner NYU Wagner 's mission is to educate and support the next generation of public service leaders and pursue interdisciplinary and impactful research on complex public interest challenges. We seek candidates with demonstrated excellence in research and teaching, and a commitment to institutional service. The candidate would be joining Wagner\u2019s 30+ full-time faculty members who are trained in a wide variety of disciplines including: management, healthcare management, sociology, economics, public policy, social entrepreneurship, financial management and public finance, political science, and urban planning. NYU Wagner confers the following degrees: MPA in Public & Nonprofit Management & Policy, MPA in Health Policy & Management, Master of Urban Planning, Online Master of Health Administration, Master of Science in Health Law and Strategy, Master of Science in Public Policy, Executive MPA, PhD in Public Administration, and a variety of dual degrees for NYU graduates and undergraduates. The candidate will specifically join the current management faculty at Wagner, including tenure-track faculty ( Thomas D\u2019Aunno , Patricia Satterstrom ), clinical faculty ( Martha Stark , Judy Pryor Ramirez , Carla Jackie Sampson , Jacob Victory ) and highly regarded adjunct faculty. Our faculty share a commitment to addressing issues of public importance in their teaching and research and to supporting and mentoring colleagues and students. They collaborate with other schools and departments across the university, including the Leonard N. Stern School of Business, School of Global Public Health, Rory Meyers College of Nursing, School of Law, Tandon School of Engineering, and Departments of Sociology, History, Economics, and Politics, among others. Research & Teaching Areas We seek scholars whose work addresses how organizations function today and how they will function in the years ahead; how new technologies and systems are reshaping the way work gets done and how people relate to one another inside and across organizations; how people lead with particular grounding in public and cross-sector settings \u2014 especially the sectors where many of our students build careers, including healthcare, education, government, advocacy, urban policy, consulting, and impact investing, domestically and internationally. We welcome scholars who do qualitative, quantitative, experimental, and simulation work. Content areas that are particularly important for our school include: Innovation and technology: Research on how to lead, manage, and support innovation and organizational learning amidst accelerated technological changes and new collaboration structures Leading and negotiating wisely and ethically: Research on how people \u2013 particularly in sectors of interest to us \u2013 make decisions, negotiate, and create ethical frameworks in complex, real-world settings. Ideally with an eye toward overcoming systemic and interpersonal biases. Social movements and power: Research on the strategy, management, and power at the heart of organizing and social movements, and community-based organizations \u2014 how organizers build and structure coalitions, mobilize resources and people, exercise power and influence within and across institutions, and translate sustained effort into durable social change. Cross-organization, cross-sector, and cross-national leadership: Research on how people lead, manage, and work between organizations, across sectors, and across countries. Ideally with an eye on how to cultivate sustainable collaborations. Policy implementation and organizational performance: Research on what happens after a policy is passed \u2014 the processes, structures, networks, and jobs that are created or reshaped. This area could also look at how changes and performance are managed, evaluated, and measured. Organizational and human resources: Research on organizational change, organizational structures, understanding difference and creating conditions for inclusive and thriving cultures; career development and talent management are also of interest. We seek candidates who bring a demonstrated commitment to excellence in the classroom. We are particularly interested in candidates who create active-learning classroom cultures that foster student agency and personal investment in the material. We seek candidates who have a commitment to, and experience or evidence of the potential for success in teaching a diverse population of students in one of the most diverse cities in the U.S. A new hire would carry a share of our core management and leadership courses (e.g., Management & Leadership, Strategy, Human Resources and Job Design, Performance Management & Measurement) and electives across our Master\u2019s, mid-career, and executive formats, bringing actual organizational problems into the classroom in support of Wagner's effort to prepare students to lead in real-world settings. Benefits NYU offers a competitive benefits package for full-time employees. This includes comprehensive medical, dental, vision, and life insurance coverage, robust retirement plans, and flexible spending accounts. NYU provides all full-time employees with access to wellness and assistance programs, including free access to health care advocacy. NYU also provides subsidies for back-up child and elder care and commuter benefits. For full-time faculty with eligible dependent children, 90% of NYU tuition is waived for undergraduate degree programs. Portable tuition benefits for eligible children attending other institutions are also available. PhD or DBA in Management / Organizational Behavior, Strategy, Healthcare Management, Organizational Sociology, Personnel Economics, Public Administration, or a related field. Candidates should submit the following information by October 5, 2026 for full consideration on Interfolio: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover letter Job Market Paper Research Statement Teaching Statement, including all teaching evaluations Three letters of recommendation (Optional) Additional Research Papers Note: Placeholder documents are not acceptable and will make your application incomplete. Please do not submit or update material via email. Initial interviews will be conducted over Zoom. Finalists will be invited to visit in the fall semester."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194055": {"title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Urban Planning and Public Service", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Robert F. Wagner Graduate School of Public Service: Academic Affairs Location: New York The NYU Wagner Graduate School of Public Service invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in urban planning, with appointment to begin September 1, 2027. The position will be at the assistant professor level.\u00a0 Successful applicants will have a demonstrated record of research excellence and productivity, a promising research agenda, and a Ph.D. in a relevant field. We encourage more senior candidates to apply for our separate posting for an associate professor in urban planning . NYU Wagner is a multidisciplinary school that prepares public service leaders to translate ideas into actions that have an effective and lasting impact on the public good. Wagner\u2019s 35 full-time faculty members are trained in a wide variety of disciplines and share a commitment to addressing issues of public importance in their teaching, research and service. Wagner faculty members collaborate with other schools and departments across the university, including the Departments of Politics, Economics, and Sociology; School of Law; Stern School of Business; Tandon School of Engineering; and the College of Global Public Health.\u00a0 Wagner faculty members also have opportunities to collaborate with and hold leadership positions at various research centers like the NYU Furman Center, the Rudin Center, and the Marron Institute of Urban Management. Successful candidates will teach in our urban planning program and demonstrate a research interest in cities, both globally and domestically. We welcome applications from scholars with interests in a wide range of urban topics, including infrastructure, housing, climate change, and technology, and who study these issues in different contexts.\u00a0 We are particularly interested in candidates who focus on the physical/spatial dimensions of cities, defined broadly, and those whose work focuses on pressing urban planning challenges more generally. We seek faculty candidates who are excited to teach a diverse population of students motivated to understand and improve urban communities. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for the assistant professor position is $125,000-$145,000. PhD in a relevant field Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, with a final deadline of November 1, 2026. To apply, candidates should submit the following information on Interfolio: Curriculum Vitae Cover letter Job Market Paper Research Statement Three letters of recommendation. They must be submitted by the submission deadline. (Optional) Additional Research Papers"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194083": {"title": "Tenure-Track Associate Professor of Urban Planning and Public Service", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Robert F. Wagner Graduate School of Public Service: Academic Affairs Location: New York The NYU Wagner Graduate School of Public Service invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in urban planning, with appointment to begin September 1, 2027. The position will be at the associate professor level.\u00a0 Successful applicants will have a demonstrated record of research excellence and productivity, a promising research agenda, and a Ph.D. in a relevant field.\u00a0 We encourage more junior candidates to apply for our separate posting for an assistant professor in urban planning . NYU Wagner is a multidisciplinary school that prepares public service leaders to translate ideas into actions that have an effective and lasting impact on the public good. Wagner\u2019s 35 full-time faculty members are trained in a wide variety of disciplines and share a commitment to addressing issues of public importance in their teaching, research and service. Wagner faculty members collaborate with other schools and departments across the university, including the Departments of Politics, Economics, and Sociology; School of Law; Stern School of Business; Tandon School of Engineering; and the College of Global Public Health.\u00a0 Wagner faculty members also have opportunities to collaborate with and hold leadership positions at various research centers like the NYU Furman Center, the Rudin Center, and the Marron Institute of Urban Management. Successful candidates will teach in our urban planning program and demonstrate a research interest in cities, both globally and domestically. We welcome applications from scholars with interests in a wide range of urban topics, including infrastructure, housing, climate change, and technology, and who study these issues in different contexts. We are particularly interested in candidates who focus on the physical/spatial dimensions of cities, defined broadly, and those whose work focuses on pressing urban planning challenges more generally. We seek faculty candidates who are excited to teach a diverse population of students motivated to understand and improve urban communities. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for the position is $145,000-$165,000. PhD in a relevant field Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, with a final deadline of November 1, 2026. To apply, candidates should submit the following information on Interfolio: Curriculum Vitae Cover letter Job Market Paper Research Statement (Optional) Additional Research Papers"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192731": {"title": "Postdoctoral Fellowships in Theoretical and Computational Chemistry", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Arts and Sciences: Chemistry Location: Shanghai, China Applications are invited for postdoctoral fellowships in the research group of Prof. Xiang Sun . The selected individuals will play a leading role in developing and applying new computational methods for chemical dynamics in the condensed phase and simulation of ultrafast nonlinear spectroscopies. Specifically, photoinduced charge and energy transfer dynamics as well as structural dynamics of light-harvesting systems such as organic photovoltaic and luminescent molecules in liquid solutions and disordered condensed phases will be investigated. Classical molecular dynamics, mixed quantum-classical, semiclassical, and path integral approaches will be developed for such systems. Novel dynamical methods will be applied to ultrafast spectroscopies to help reveal important molecular information behind them. More details can be found at https://wp.nyu.edu/xiangsun . Research at NYU Shanghai is supported by the NYU-ECNU Center for Computational Chemistry, a research institute operated jointly by NYU, NYU Shanghai, and East China Normal University (ECNU). The center has a core group of faculty members who are conducting frontier research in various fields of computational chemistry, biology, and material sciences. The postdoc fellow will be also affiliated with the center. More details can be found at https://research.shanghai.nyu.edu/chemistry . Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions. Ph.D. in chemistry, physics, material science, computer science, or related fields (or must be completed before the start date of the appointment) Experience with statistical mechanics, electronic structure theory, quantum dynamical methods, or theoretical ultrafast spectroscopy Experience with coding in C/C++ and Python Excellent communication skills (writing and speaking in English) There are two postdoctoral fellowship positions which are available immediately and will remain open until filled. The term is initially for one year with the possibility of extension. The appointment start date for non-Chinese applicants is subject to immigration regulations. To be considered, applicants must submit: cover letter with a brief description of research accomplishments and interests curriculum vitae with a list of publications 3 recommendation letters (referees will be contacted via Interfolio) Please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment: shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu with any questions regarding the application process. Further information on the advertised position may be obtained by contacting Professor Xiang Sun ( xiang.sun@nyu.edu ) directly. About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194759": {"title": "Tenured/Tenure-Track Positions in Finance", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Business: Finance Location: Shanghai, China NYU Shanghai is currently inviting applications for faculty positions in Finance at all ranks, expected to start in the Fall Semester of 2027. For further details about the research centers and institutes at NYU Shanghai, please visit this webpage . Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions with respect to research start-up funds and compensation, and they include housing subsidies and educational subsidies for children. Faculty may in certain cases have the opportunity to spend time at NYU New York and other sites of the NYU Global Network, engaging in both research and teaching. We seek candidates who have completed a Ph.D. in Finance or a closely related discipline. Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, statement of research, statement of teaching interests, and at least one writing sample via Interfolio. Candidates may opt to submit a cover letter describing their interest in NYU Shanghai. Additionally, applicants (applying at the Assistant or Associate without tenure ranks) will be prompted to enter the names and email addresses of at least three evaluators. Each referee will be contacted to upload a confidential letter of recommendation through Interfolio. Applications may be received through the end of day (11:59PM Eastern Time) on November 20, 2026. If you have any questions, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194862": {"title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Position in Marketing", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Business: Marketing Location: Shanghai, China The Marketing group of NYU Shanghai is currently inviting applications for a faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor, expected to start in the Fall Semester of 2027. For further details about the research centers and institutes at NYU Shanghai, please visit this webpage . Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions with respect to research start-up funds and compensation, and they include housing subsidies and educational subsidies for children. Faculty may in certain cases have the opportunity to spend time at NYU New York and other sites of the NYU Global Network, engaging in both research and teaching. We seek candidates who hold\u2014or expect to receive by Fall 2027\u2014a Ph.D. in Marketing or a related field. We welcome applicants with diverse academic backgrounds and research interests, including consumer behavior, quantitative marketing, economics, computer science, and data science. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of research, statement of teaching interests, and at least one writing sample (e.g., job market paper) via Interfolio. Additionally, applicants will be prompted to enter the names and email addresses of at least three evaluators. Each referee will be contacted to upload a confidential letter of recommendation through Interfolio. Applications may be received until October 31st, 2026. Review of applications will begin in late October and will continue until the position is filled. If you have any questions, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty R ecruitment shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192678": {"title": "Postdoctoral Fellow Positions in Mathematics", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Arts and Sciences: Mathematics Location: Shanghai, China  NYU Shanghai is currently accepting applications for several postdoctoral fellow positions in Mathematics. The fellows will be part of the Mathematics area at NYU Shanghai, which operates in collaboration with the Courant Institute and interacts with East China Normal University. The areas of expertise of possible mentors in the group are mainly Probability Theory, Partial Differential Equations, Fluid Dynamics, and Applied Mathematics. However, exceptional candidates with different research interests will also be considered. Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions. Successful applicants should hold a Ph.D. in Mathematics or a closely related discipline at the time of appointment. Postdoctoral positions are commonly offered for two years, and are potentially renewable for an additional year; they incur a teaching load of two recitations per semester. Applicants will submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a statement on current/future research interests, a statement of teaching interests, and an electronic copy of three selected publications via Interfolio. Additionally, applicants should enter the names and email addresses of at least three referees. Each referee will be contacted to upload a reference letter through Interfolio. It is strongly recommended to submit applications before December 1, 2026 , even though later applications will also be given full consideration in case the positions have not been filled by that date. If you have any questions, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment at shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192681": {"title": "Visiting Faculty Positions in Mathematics", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Arts and Sciences: Mathematics Location: Shanghai, China NYU Shanghai is currently inviting applications for several visiting faculty positions in Mathematics. The Mathematics area at NYU Shanghai operates in collaboration with\u00a0 the Courant Institute and interacts with East China Normal University. Visiting faculty positions are generally for one or two semesters, but longer terms may be discussed; the teaching commitments include teaching two to three courses per year. Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions. We seek candidates who have completed a Ph.D. in Mathematics by the time of appointment. We invite applications from candidates in any domain of mathematics. The search is not restricted to any rank; all levels of seniority will be considered, although in general applicants are expected to have at least three years of experience after their Ph.D. Applicants will submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a statement on current/future research interests, a statement of teaching interests, and an electronic copy of three selected publications via Interfolio. Additionally, applicants at the level of Visiting Assistant and Associate positions should enter the names and email addresses of at least three referees. Each referee will be contacted to upload a reference letter through Interfolio. It is strongly recommended to submit applications before December 1, 2026 , even though later applications will also be given full consideration in case the positions have not been filled by that date. If you have any questions, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment at shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194865": {"title": "Tenured/Tenure-Track Position in Management and Organizations", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Business: Management & Organizations Location: Shanghai, China NYU Shanghai is currently inviting applications for a faculty position in Management and Organizations at all ranks. For further details about the research centers and institutes at NYU Shanghai, please visit this webpage . Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions with respect to research start-up funds and compensation, and they include housing subsidies and educational subsidies for children. Faculty may in certain cases have the opportunity to spend time at NYU New York and other sites of the NYU Global Network, engaging in both research and teaching. We seek candidates who have completed a Ph.D. in Management and Organizations or a closely related discipline. We invite applications from candidates in any field and have a special interest in those with expertise in Organizational Theory, Strategy, and Entrepreneurship. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of research, statement of teaching interests, and at least one writing sample (e.g., job market paper) via Interfolio. Additionally, applicants (applying at the Assistant or Associate without tenure ranks) will be prompted to enter the names and email addresses of at least three evaluators. Each referee will be contacted to upload a confidential letter of recommendation through Interfolio. Applications may be received until October 31st, 2026. Review of applications will begin on October 15th, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. If you have any questions, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194733": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor - Counseling for Mental Health and Wellness MA Program, Non-Tenure Track", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Applied Psychology Location: New York New York University\u2019s Steinhardt School of Culture, Education, and Human Development invites applications for up to four (4) continuing contract (non-tenure track) Clinical Assistant Professors in the Counseling for Mental Health and Wellness Master of Arts Program, to begin on September 1, 2027. The appointed faculty members will be members of the Department of Applied Psychology. The department includes: a large and active undergraduate program with research, theory, and action among its core pillars; 1 accredited MA degree-granting program in Counseling for Mental Health and Wellness; and 3 PhD programs that enroll fully funded doctoral students (5 years of fellowship) in Developmental Psychology, Clinical/Counseling Psychology, and Psychology and Social Intervention. The department has an excellent track record of procuring extramural funding for research and training, and supporting collaborative and transdisciplinary scholarship. The Master of Arts in Counseling for Mental Health and Wellness at NYU Steinhardt is a comprehensive 60-credit program offered in both residential (in-person) and online formats. The program prepares students for careers as licensed mental health counselors through rigorous academic coursework and supervised clinical training. Emphasizing cultural humility, social justice, and responsiveness to diverse client needs, the program develops counseling competencies for working with individuals and communities across the lifespan, promoting social justice, and fostering wellness. Graduates are prepared to deliver evidence-based and culturally informed care in community agencies, hospitals, and private practice settings. We seek applicants with expertise in counseling and counselor education who are able to teach any combination of the following courses: Research and Evaluation Methods in Behavioral Sciences, Program Development and Evaluation, Interpretation and Use of Tests for Adults, Dynamics of Vocational Development, Cognitive Behavior Therapy, Brief Psychodynamic Therapy, Group Dynamics: Theory and Practice . Ideal candidates will demonstrate flexibility in teaching across the curriculum and a commitment to supporting a large and diverse M.A. program, including providing administrative and programmatic support as needed. Experience in teaching and/or mentoring international students is highly desirable. Each position includes teaching six courses across the fall and spring semesters (3 courses per term), with the expectation of availability to teach at least one additional course during the summer term for additional compensation. NYU Steinhardt is a uniquely interdisciplinary school, committed to a holistic understanding of people and human development across the lifespan. NYU Steinhardt has one-of-a-kind integration of education, communication, health, and the arts, with eleven departments, seventeen research centers and institutes, nearly 300 full-time faculty and close to six thousand students. Responsibilities: Successful candidates will demonstrate excellence in teaching, advising, and mentoring graduate students. Responsibilities include contributing to program and curricular development, supporting student professional identity formation, and participating in service to the program, department, School, and University communities. Clinical faculty may also engage in scholarly work, supervision, or creative professional activities relevant to the field of counseling. The expected teaching load is six courses per academic year. NYU's dynamic Global Network University includes NYU Abu Dhabi, NYU Shanghai, and international programs and academic centers around the world. NYU Steinhardt faculty may have the opportunity to engage in teaching at these global sites. Candidates must hold a doctorate in Counseling Psychology, Clinical Psychology, or Counselor Education. Candidates must be eligible for licensure to practice counseling/therapy in the state of New York; current licensure is strongly preferred. Priority will be given to those with a doctorate from an APA or CACREP-approved program in Counseling or Clinical Psychology or Counselor Education . Candidates must have at least two years relevant clinical experience in mental health settings; licensure or progress toward licensure is preferred. At least two semesters of teaching experience are required. Applicants should apply online via Interfolio. Candidates should upload: (a) cover letter providing a short summary of experience and accomplishments as well as fit with the program, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) teaching statement (1-2 pages), and (d) the names and contact information for three referees (letters will be requested for short-listed candidates). Review of applications begins immediately and continues until the search is complete. Questions can be directed to Anil Chacko (Department Chair) at anil.chacko@nyu.edu For more information on the Masters for Counseling for Mental Health and Wellness, visit https://steinhardt.nyu.edu/programs/counseling-mental-health-and-wellness Applications due 12/1/2026. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $94,000 - $108,000 . New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175392": {"title": "Industry Assistant or Associate Professor Department of Applied Physics NYU Tandon School of Engineering", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Applied Physics Location: Brooklyn, NY The Department of Applied Physics at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for a full-time, non-tenured, renewable faculty position at the rank of Industry Assistant or Associate Professor, with a start date of September 1, 2026. While the official start date is Fall 2026, an earlier appointment may be possible depending on departmental needs and candidate availability. The initial appointment is for three years and is renewable contingent on performance. The Department offers BS degrees in Applied Physics and in Mathematics & Physics, a minor in Quantum Technology, and a newly approved MS program in Quantum Science and Technology, launching in Fall 2026. Faculty in the department teach both introductory physics courses for engineering students and upper-level courses for physics majors. An Industry Assistant or Associate Professor\u2019s primary responsibilities include teaching, mentoring, and educational innovation, with additional contributions to curricular development, outreach, and departmental service. While the standard teaching load is three courses per semester, this position carries potential opportunities in program and curriculum development that could reduce this load. In addition to classroom teaching, the position includes a significant role in the development and delivery of a new Quantum Optics laboratory course, to be offered for the first time in Summer 2027. This will involve: Developing experiments and course materials for an advanced optics laboratory (10\u201312 experiments anticipated) Teaching the course in the laboratory setting once it launches Teaching load reductions may be provided for these curricular development activities. We invite applicants with a strong record of teaching and hands-on laboratory development. You should be an excellent teacher in both classroom and instructional laboratory environments. A Ph.D. in experimental physics or a closely related discipline is required, along with substantial on-hand experimental experience in optics, photonics, or quantum optics. A record of effective teaching at the undergraduate or graduate level and/or relevant industrial or professional experience is highly desirable. Please submit the following materials electronically through the \"Apply Now\" button. \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Cover letter \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Current CV \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Teaching statement (with emphasis on approaches to undergraduate physics education, innovation and experiential learning, as well as ideas for curricular development and content delivery at the Undergraduate and Graduate levels both in lecture and laboratory environments) \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Recent teaching evaluations (if available) \u25cf Names and contact information for three references (referees will be automatically contacted to upload confidential letters via Interfolio) Review of applications have begun and will continue until the position is filled. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual (9 month) base salary range for this position is $85,000-$125,000 for the Industry Assistant Professor rank and $95,000-$145,000 for the Industry Associate Professor rank. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/160564": {"title": "Associate Research Scientist", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Associate Research Scientist Part time Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering New York University The Hybrid Nanomaterials Lab in the Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering (CBE) at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Associate Research Scientist to join an exciting project on nanocrystal based photon detectors. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering department is at the center of a high-tech start-up culture where students and faculty are supported and nurtured in New York City, and across the NYU Global Network. Project Focus: Development and fabrication of photodetectors. This project involves the synthesis of colloidal quantum dots and their physical, chemical, structural, optical and electrical characterization followed by fabrication and device characterization of photodetectors. Expectations: Candidates will be responsible for initiating and driving a multi-institute collaborative project and perform work in a\u00a0multidisciplinary\u00a0environment. The successful candidate will be driven, creative and team-oriented with a passion for developing nanomaterials. We are seeking individuals with a strong background in chemistry, physics, engineering, and/or materials\u00a0design, and/or related field. Proficiency in colloidal quantum dots synthesis and/or device fabrication/characterization is required. We welcome applicants from\u00a0recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. It is expected that the candidate will work with others on interdisciplinary research projects, carry out assigned lab and administrative responsibilities, be an efficient experimentalist, and be a good lab and team member. Effective communication skills are required. Duties: Experiment Support: Assist researchers with setting up experiments, preparing solutions, and conducting routine laboratory procedures. Troubleshoot technical issues as they arise and provide guidance to lab members as needed. Data Management: Maintain accurate records of experimental procedures, results, and observations. Organize data in electronic databases and assist with data analysis as required. Laboratory Maintenance: Oversee the day-to-day operations of the laboratory, including equipment maintenance, inventory management, and cleanliness protocols. Ensure compliance with safety regulations and best practices. Procurement and Budgeting: Coordinate the ordering of supplies and reagents, track expenditures, and manage the lab budget effectively. Identify cost-saving opportunities and liaise with vendors to negotiate favorable pricing. Training and Supervision: Provide training to new lab members on laboratory techniques, safety protocols, and equipment usage. Supervise the work of undergraduate students or interns assigned to the lab. Compliance and Documentation: Ensure adherence to institutional guidelines, regulatory requirements, and ethical standards in all laboratory activities. Maintain documentation related to safety training, equipment calibration, and protocol approvals. Salary : In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $34.34 per hour (approximately 21 hours per week),\u00a0 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Duration:\u00a0 6 months with potential of extensions. PhD candidate in physics, chemistry, materials science, chemical engineering, or related field. Previous experience working in a laboratory setting, preferably in a research or academic environment. Familiarity with laboratory techniques, instrumentation, and safety procedures. Strong organizational skills with the ability to multitask and prioritize responsibilities effectively for coordination of administrative and lab responsibilities. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work collaboratively in a team environment. Proficiency in basic computer applications (e.g., Microsoft Office, data analysis software). Familiarity with LabView. Demonstrated attention to detail and a proactive approach to problem-solving. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a current CV and a list of two references with complete contact information. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168125": {"title": "Assistant Research Scientist (Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME ASSISTANT RESEARCH SCIENTIST New York University Tandon School of Engineering The NYU Sustainable Engineering Initiative at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Scientist to work with the Modestino Research Group. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The SEI is an emerging initiative at NYU that aims to develop engineering solutions to avoid, mitigate, and remediate emissions responsible for climate change and environmental contamination, as well as engineering strategies to evaluate the impact of and adapt to these environmental challenges. The SEI research is aimed at building the science and engineering foundation of solutions (i) for decarbonized and sustainable systems for energy generation, transportation, manufacturing, waste treatment and recovery, and agriculture, (ii) the mitigation of CO 2 emissions, and chemical and microbiological pollutants from these systems, (iii) the remediation of natural environments and (iv) adaptation to climate change. Expectations Candidates hired will be working on research projects related with critical materials for energy conversion devices. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in materials science and energy technologies with an understanding of sustainability, economic viability, and policy as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience working in a laboratory setting, preferably in a research or academic environment is necessary. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is: 06/01/2025. This position is expected to be for 3 months. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately 10-21 hours per week. Candidates should have at least a MS in Chemical Engineering, Materials Science, Chemistry or a related field and demonstrated expertise and research experience in one of the areas described above. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for two references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/169345": {"title": "Research Scholar", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Chemical & Biomolecular Engineering Department at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part time Research Scholar to work in the labs of the Hubbell Group. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering department is at the forefront of solving global challenges, from advancing renewable energy technologies to pioneering innovative healthcare solutions. Researchers and students in the department\u00a0work\u00a0collaboratively to address critical issues in energy, healthcare, and the environment, using cutting-edge approaches in molecular science and engineering.\u00a0 The Hubbell Research Group focuses on developing cutting-edge biomaterials and protein engineering to investigate regenerative medicine and immunotherapeutics. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for the design and execution of computational and experimental studies to explore immune system dynamics and therapeutic interventions. Develop and apply biophysical and bioinformatics models to analyze immune responses. Identify and validate novel biomarkers and molecular targets for therapeutic development. Perform high-throughput screening of immune-modulating compounds and their effects. Collaborate with interdisciplinary teams, including clinicians, bioengineers, and computational biologists. Prepare and publish research findings in peer-reviewed journals and present at scientific conferences. Assist in mentoring graduate and undergraduate students in laboratory techniques and research methodologies. Contribute to grant writing and funding applications to support ongoing research initiatives. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in translational immunology with expertise in computational modeling and/or experimental immunoengineering techniques as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on flow cytometry, single-cell sequencing, and immunoassays is preferred. Proficiency in computational tools such as MATLAB, Python, R, or machine learning applications in immunology is desired. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is an hourly rate of $50.00 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is July 14th. This position is expected to be for 12 months. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately 20-35 hours per week. Candidates should have at least a MS in Chemical Engineering, Bioengineering, Immunology or a related field and 2-4 years of experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177168": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Chemical & Biomolecular Engineering Department at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work in the labs of the Hubbell Group. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Hubbell Research Group focuses on developing cutting-edge biomaterials and protein engineering to investigate regenerative medicine and immunotherapeutics. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for the design and execution of computational and experimental studies to explore immune system dynamics and therapeutic interventions. Develop and apply biophysical and bioinformatics models to analyze immune responses. Identify and validate novel biomarkers and molecular targets for therapeutic development. Perform high-throughput screening of immune-modulating compounds and assess their effects. Collaborate with interdisciplinary teams, including clinicians, bioengineers, and computational biologists. Prepare and publish research findings in peer-reviewed journals and present at scientific conferences. Assist in mentoring graduate and undergraduate students in laboratory techniques and research methodologies. Contribute to grant writing and funding applications to support ongoing research initiatives. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $85,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in translational immunology with expertise in computational modeling and/or experimental immunoengineering techniques as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience with flow cytometry, single-cell sequencing, and immunoassays is preferred. Proficiency in computational tools such as MATLAB, Python, R, or machine learning applications in immunology is desired. Candidates should have a PhD in Chemistry, Chemical Engineering, Bioengineering, Immunology or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is November 2025."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185648": {"title": "POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Chemical & Biomolecular Engineering Department at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work in the labs of the Hubbell Group. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Hubbell Research Group focuses on developing cutting-edge biomaterials and protein engineering to investigate regenerative medicine and\u00a0 immunotherapeutics. Responsibilities: Candidates will be responsible for: Design and execute computational and experimental studies to explore immune system dynamics and therapeutic interventions. Develop and apply biophysical and bioinformatics models to analyze immune responses. Identify and validate novel biomarkers and molecular targets for therapeutic development. Perform high-throughput screening of immune-modulating compounds and assess their effects. Collaborate with interdisciplinary teams, including clinicians, bioengineers, and computational biologists. Prepare and publish research findings in peer-reviewed journals and present at scientific conferences. Assist in mentoring graduate and undergraduate students in laboratory techniques and research methodologies. Contribute to grant writing and funding applications to support ongoing research initiatives. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in translational immunology with expertise in computational modeling and/or experimental immunoengineering techniques as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience with flow cytometry, single-cell sequencing, and immunoassays is preferred. Proficiency in computational tools such as MATLAB, Pyt Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $90,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Chemistry, Chemical Engineering, Bioengineering, Immunology or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research rel ates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: May 2026"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185304": {"title": "POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Korley lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire multuple Post Doctoral Associate positions to work in Professor LaShanda Korley\u2019s lab. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Prof. LaShanda\u00a0Korley\u2019s group in the Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering at NYU Tandon and the Center for Plastics Innovation (CPI) strives to\u00a0develop chemical conversion strategies and new sustainable material platforms to transform plastics manufacturing. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for the following: Executing experimental research for this project under the direction of Prof.\u00a0Korley\u00a0focused on the deconstruction and subsequent generation of valorized materials from plastics waste. Perform molecular and macromolecular characterization, thermal and mechanical analysis, and processing experiments as necessary to demonstrate materials and process feasibility. The overall objective is to valorize waste products to enhance polymer sustainability efforts. Collaborate with academic and industrial collaborators as necessary. Provide substantial input in project directions in consultation with Prof.\u00a0Korley. Mentor graduate and undergraduate students as mutually agreed between the candidate and Prof.\u00a0Korley. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $75,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Chemistry, Chemical Engineering, Bioengineering, Immunology or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. The successful candidate will have a strong demonstrated background in strong polymer science (polymer characterization, processing, and physics) as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience in plastics recycling and sustainable materials design is\u00a0preferred."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185342": {"title": "Assistant Research Scientist", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  FULL TIME ASSISTANT RESEARCH SCIENTIST New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Hubbell Research Group in the Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Department at NYU Tandon School of Engineering (https://engineering.nyu.edu/) is seeking to hire a Full Time Assistant Research Scientist with experience in bioengineering research to work on an interdisciplinary project. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Hubbell Research Group focuses on developing cutting-edge biomaterials and protein engineering to investigate regenerative medicine and immunotherapeutics. Responsibilities : Candidates will be responsible for the development of biomaterials and protein engineering. They are expected to have a strong background in biomedical and/or biomolecular engineering, biochemistry, chemistry, chemical engineering and/or materials design. Duties include: Laboratory Maintenance: Oversee the day-to-day operations of the laboratory, including equipment maintenance, inventory management, and cleanliness protocols. Ensure compliance with safety regulations and best practices. Experiment Support: Assist researchers with setting up experiments, preparing solutions, and conducting routine laboratory procedures. Troubleshoot technical issues as they arise and provide guidance to lab members as needed. Data Management: Maintain accurate records of experimental procedures, results, and observations. Organize data in electronic databases and assist with data analysis as required. Procurement and Budgeting: Coordinate the ordering of supplies and reagents, track expenditures, and manage the lab budget effectively. Identify cost-saving opportunities and liaise with vendors to negotiate favorable pricing. Training and Supervision: Provide training to new lab members on laboratory techniques, safety protocols, and equipment usage. Supervise the work of undergraduate students or interns assigned to the lab. Compliance and Documentation: Ensure adherence to institutional guidelines, regulatory requirements, and ethical standards in all laboratory activities. Maintain documentation related to safety training, equipment calibration, and protocol approvals. The successful candidate will be driven, creative and team-oriented with a passion for computationally designed biomaterials and biocatalysts. It is expected that the candidate will work with others on interdisciplinary research projects, carry out assigned lab and administrative responsibilities, be an efficient experimentalist, and be a good lab and team member. Effective communication skills, especially note-taking, are required. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $62,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is : 09/01/26. The position will be 12 months with a potential for extension. Candidates should have at least a BS biomedical and/or biomolecular engineering, biochemistry, chemistry, chemical engineering, or related field and demonstrated expertise and research experience in one of the areas described above. Previous experience working in a laboratory setting, preferably in a research or academic environment. Familiarity with laboratory techniques, instrumentation, and safety procedures. Strong organizational skills with the ability to multitask and prioritize responsibilities effectively for coordination of administrative and lab responsibilities. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work collaboratively in a team environment. Proficiency in basic computer applications (e.g., Microsoft Office, data analysis software). Demonstrated attention to detail and a proactive approach to problem-solving. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a current CV and a list of three references with complete contact information. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185780": {"title": "Research Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Long Island City, NY  FULL TIME RESEARCH ASSOCIATE (Veterinary Technician) New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Hubbell Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Associate to work with preclinical experimentation and animal modeling. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Hubbell lab is known for extensive bioengineering and biomaterials for immunomodulation with a keen eye on translation into clinical settings. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for: Administering injections (intravenous [tail vein], intraperitoneal, and subcutaneous), Routine sampling (blood collection via submandibular or submental puncture), Mouse colony management and husbandry (including breeding and separation of cages), Scoring various autoimmune models (paralysis and arthritis), and Necropsy and organ harvest. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in mouse rearing and treatments as exemplified by a strong publication record and/or references. Previous experience in animal injections, sampling, husbandry, and general care is necessary. Aseptic surgical experience is a plus. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $62,499 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is Sept 1, 2026. This position is expected to be for 12 months. Candidates should have at least a 1 year of experience or 3+ years\u2019 experience in Immunology, Bio- or Biomedical Engineering, Biology or a related field with a BS in those fields preferred Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of known animal techniques and amount of time working with species and publications, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: j.hubbell@nyu.edu , jwr8832@nyu.edu , and a.alpar@nyu.edu . Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188644": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE ON MICROPLASTICS SOLUTIONS New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Sustainable Engineering Initiative at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to develop, manage and contribute to the research on a new Center on Microplastics Solutions. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The NYU Sustainable Engineering Initiative (SEI) at NYU Tandon aims to develop engineering solutions to avoid, mitigate, and remediate emissions responsible for climate change and environmental contamination. Plastics are vital to human prosperity - from the everyday items in our homes to the medical devices that extend our healthspan and the materials that solve our most urgent technological challenges. However, there are drawbacks to the widespread use of plastics. Among the most pressing issues is the proliferation of micro- and nanoplastics (MNPs), which have been recognized as a major problem for the environment and public health. Achieving the combined goals of thriving societies and a thriving planet requires a new generation of polymer materials that maintain or improve the performance of existing materials while minimizing or eliminating the formation of harmful micro- and nanoplastic byproducts throughout their lifecycle. We are looking to engage a postdoctoral associate who will help create, manage, and contribute to the research and growth of a new center focused on microplastics solutions. Responsibilities The candidate will be responsible for establishing the structure of a new center for microplastics solutions, coordinating research activities between multiple research groups, performing research on microplastics solutions as it relates to the molecular design and processing of polymers that avoid their degradation into harmful particles, and assisting the leadership of the new center with fundraising activities. The successful candidate associate will report directly to the SEI Director, Prof. Miguel Modestino, and will likely be co-advised by other faculty members of the center. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $70,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Chemical Engineering, Materials Science, or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in polymer science and engineering, including synthesis and characterization, as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience in research automation, high-throughput experimentation, computational proficiency, experience preparing research proposals, and demonstrated leadership experience are desired. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: August 1 st , 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188648": {"title": "POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Sustainable Energy Transitions (SET) Group, led by Prof. Dharik Mallapragada in the Chemical & Biomolecular Engineering Department at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work on a project focused on developing high fidelity energy system models to study industrial decarbonization pathways. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The SET Group combines process-level analysis with systems modeling and optimization to evaluate emerging low-carbon technologies and their integration into the broader energy economy across diverse policy and market contexts. The project focuses on advancing the spatial, temporal, and technological resolution of energy system models, with emphasis on petrochemical sector representation for decarbonization analysis. Responsibilities The successful candidate will: design and execute research tasks in alignment with project objectives. lead and contribute to collaborative efforts with partner researchers and institutions. Prepare manuscripts for peer-reviewed publication and reports for funding agencies. Present findings at group meetings, conferences, and internal and external events Mentor graduate and undergraduate students engaged in related research. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $70,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in energy systems, process systems engineering, or a closely related area as exemplified by a strong publication record. Strong interpersonal skills and a collaborative research approach with excellent written and oral communication abilities Candidates should have a PhD in a relevant field with a focus on systems modeling and optimization (e.g., Chemical Engineering, Industrial Engineering, Electrical Engineering, or related disciplines) or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: dm6004@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. This is\u00a0 a one-year position, with a start date of August 1, 2026. The position may be renewed for a second and third year based on performance and funding availability."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188890": {"title": "Research Scholar  (Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Modestino Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Scholar to work in electrochemical research focused on redox flow batteries and decoupled water electrolysis. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Modestino Lab, in the Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering, develops electrochemical technologies that integrate renewable energy into chemical manufacturing and energy storage, including redox-mediated decoupled electrolysis systems. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for developing analytical techniques, collecting experimental data on redox flow chemistries, analyzing data, and designing experimental campaigns. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is hourly rate $26.37 for part time depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is : July 16, 2026. This position is expected to be for 2 months . Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately 19 hours per week . Candidates should have at least a BS in Chemical Engineering, Chemistry, or a related field. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in electrochemistry and wet-lab analytical methods (e.g., titration) as exemplified by a strong academic and research record. Previous experience with flow electrochemistry is desired. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to Modestino@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189851": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering The Bui Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to join a new research program centered around the development of scalable electrochemical systems for sustainable chemical synthesis, resource recovery, and wastewater valorization. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Bui Lab\u2019s research approach spans basic and applied science, integrating electrochemical reaction engineering, continuum modeling, and automation for accelerated discovery and optimization of electrochemical reactions and reactor architectures. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for: conducting research in electrochemical reaction engineering, including experimental design and execution providing continuum modeling of electrochemical systems developing automated workflows for accelerated discovery and optimization of electrochemical reactions and reactor architectures . participating in research in an interdisciplinary, collaborative environment Willing to engage in both computational and experimental workflows. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $78,000 \u2013 $85,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Chemical Engineering, Chemistry, Materials Science, Applied Physics/Math , or a related field . We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training . The successful candiate should have a demonstrated background in reaction engineering, electrochemistry, catalysis, separations, modeling of electrochemical systems, and/or machine learning as exemplified by a strong publication record . Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to : justinbui@nyu.edu . Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: Summer 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191885": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering Poudineh Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work in the Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Poudineh Lab focuses on developing new platforms for automating the healthcare via continuous biomarker monitoring and delivering therapeutics. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for developing hydrogel based platforms for health monitoring and therapeutic delivery. performing in vitro and ex vivo characterization of the systems. performing in vivo (pre-clinical) and in human testing training and supervising junior lab members, including PhD, MS, and Undergraduate students. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $ 65,000-$75,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Material Engineering or a related field. The successful candidate should have a demonstrated background in Material and Chemical Engineering as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on polymer synthesis and microneedle fabrication is necessary. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Application instructions Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: mp7923@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is : September/ October 2026"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194620": {"title": "Research Scholar  (Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Modestino Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Scholar to work in electrochemical research focused on redox flow batteries and decoupled water electrolysis. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Modestino Lab, in the Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering, develops electrochemical technologies that integrate renewable energy into chemical manufacturing and energy storage, including redox-mediated decoupled electrolysis systems. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for developing analytical techniques, collecting experimental data on redox flow chemistries, analyzing data, and designing experimental campaigns. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is\u00a0hourly rate $26.37 for part time\u00a0depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is:\u00a0October 16, \u00a02026.\u00a0This position is expected to be for\u00a006\u00a0months.\u00a0Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately\u00a025\u00a0hours per week. Candidates should have at least a BS in Chemical Engineering, Chemistry, or a related field. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in electrochemistry and wet-lab analytical methods (e.g., titration) as exemplified by a strong academic and research record. Previous experience with flow electrochemistry is desired. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf\u00a0Cover letter \u25cf\u00a0up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf\u00a0names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to:\u00a0mam42@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/158453": {"title": "Open Rank Contract Faculty Position in Construction Engineering & Management Department of Civil and Urban Engineering", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Civil and Urban Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY The NYU Tandon School of Engineering Department of Civil and Urban Engineering (CUE) invites applications for a non-tenured industry faculty position in Construction Engineering and Management.\u00a0 The appointment will be at the rank of Associate Industry Professor or Industry Professor depending on the candidate\u2019s education, professional experience, and industry achievements.\u00a0 The anticipated start date is Fall 2025. The position will initially focus on teaching, curriculum development, and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, with opportunities for participating in research. Over time, the successful candidate will take on leadership responsibilities for managing and directing all aspects of the CUE Construction Management programs. Additional duties will include engaging with industry profession organizations and networks, promoting programs within and outside the university, and service to the department, school, and university. The ideal candidate will hold a PhD and have substantial experience as a licensed professional in the construction industry.\u00a0 \u00a0They should possess at least a Master\u2019s degree in a construction-related discipline or a Juris Doctor, along with fifteen or more years of experience managing design or construction programs, drafting and overseeing construction contracts and participating in construction litigation.\u00a0 Prior teaching experience is also highly desirable. Please submit the following materials through the \"Apply Now\" button . \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf Current CV \u25cf Teaching statement \u25cf Recent teaching evaluations (if available) \u25cf Names and contact information for three references. We will review applications beginning on December 1, 2024, and will continue until we fill the position. We encourage you to submit early. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by a historic commitment of $1 billion . The Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. The CUE Construction Management programs offer a range of educational opportunities, including a cross-university undergraduate Minor, Advanced Graduate Certificates and a Master\u2019s in Construction Management, and Master\u2019s and PhD degrees in Civil Engineering with a concentration in Construction Management. These programs are closely connected with industry organizations such as the CMAA, AGC, ASC, and ASCE. With New York City as a testing ground, they equip students to understand and apply current, emerging, and advanced technologies in construction, such as AI, modular construction, robotics, analytics, and innovative project delivery. Central to these efforts is the Institute of Design and Construction (IDC) Innovation Hub\u2014a research center that bridges technology, business, and academia to address industry challenges. The Hub aims to prepare students to develop creative solutions to critical economic, environmental, and social challenges. Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual (9 month) base salary range for this position is $100,000-$150,000 for the Industry Associate Professor rank and $115,000-$175,000 for the Industry Professor rank. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/160082": {"title": "Part-Time Instructor, Department of Civil and Urban Engineering, New York University", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Civil and Urban Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY, United States The Department of Civil and Urban Engineering (CUE) at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for part-time, non-tenured instructors, with a start date of January 21, 2025. The CUE department offers a BS degree in Civil Engineering, MS degrees in Civil Engineering, Construction Management, Environmental Engineering, Environmental Science, and Transportation Systems, and PhD degrees in Civil Engineering and Transportation Systems. We invite applicants to teach one or more graduate or undergraduate courses. In particular, we presently seek instructors to teach laboratory sections of CE-UY 2213 Fluid Mechanics and Hydraulics.\u00a0 The lab sections are currently scheduled to meet on Mondays and Wednesdays from 10:00 AM \u2013 12:00 noon during the spring 2025 semester. Strong candidates will have a PhD in Civil Engineering or a closely related discipline, be an excellent teacher, and have a record of teaching experience in the areas of fluid mechanics and hydraulics. General responsibilities for this position include providing instruction and supervision during weekly laboratory class meetings (four in-class contact hours per week), and assigning and grading in-class assessments and out-of-class individual lab reports.\u00a0 The instructor is expected to do each experiment on his/her own prior to running the class, and to be familiar with the experimental process, data collection, and calculations. Training for NYU Laboratory Safety and NYC Fire Department Lab certification will be provided and are required. Please submit the following materials electronically through the \"Apply Now\" button. Cover letter Current CV Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Teaching statement describing experience and teaching philosophy A statement of your experience with or knowledge of inclusion, diversity, equity, and belonging\u00a0 efforts and your plans for incorporating them into your teaching, research, mentoring, and service Names and contact information for at least two references (if appropriate) We will review applications starting immediately and will continue until we fill the position.\u00a0 We encourage you to submit as soon as possible. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. NYU Tandon is committed to substantially increase the proportion of our faculty from historically underrepresented groups in STEM and we encourage candidates from such groups to apply. We aspire to create a climate where diversity and inclusion are not only appreciated but considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for a culturally diverse environment.\u00a0 We take pride in our high numbers of female students and students who are the first in their family to go to college. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $214.24. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/163574": {"title": "Research Scholar (Part -time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Civil and Urban Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The C2SMARTER Center at NYU Tandon School of Engin eering is seeking to hire a Part Time Scholar to work under the direction of Prof. Eugene Vinitsky . New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The C2SMARTER Center conducts faculty-led research on topics related to urban mobility, reducing congestion, and transportation technologies. C2SMARTER Center, led by NYU Tandon School of Engineering, is a consortium of 7 nationwide universities working on cutting edge research, technology transfer, and education/workforce development. Expectations: Candidates will be responsible for the development of simulated models of human behavior for drivers, pedestrians, and cyclists in a hardware-accelerated simulator developed in Prof. Vinitsky\u2019s group. Candidates will be responsible for extending the simulator, running algorithms to train the agents, and algorithm development to achieve high performance. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in machine learning, software development, or reinforcement learning as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on software development is desired. Salary range: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $34.00 per hour,\u00a0 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualifications. Candidates should have a minimum of a\u00a0 Bachelor\u2019s degree\u00a0 in computer science, applied mathematics, or a related field, plus 1 year of experience. Candidates will be required to present employment eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170220": {"title": "Post Doctoral Researcher", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Civil and Urban Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY POSTDOCTORAL RESEARCHER EMERGE Lab The EMERGE lab at NYU is seeking to hire a postdoc to work on scaling and deploying end-to-end RL planning agents for autonomous vehicles. Based on prior work on creating high performing self-play RL agents , we are interested in understanding the bottlenecks in augmenting the self-driving stack with RL-based planners. Outputs of this project are intended to be significant empirical advances in the design and deployment of end-to-end planners as well as open-source driving policies that can run on a fleet. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States. Tandon faculty and students are at the forefront of the high-tech start-up culture in New York City. The NYU Tandon School of Engineering is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning and fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Expectations An ideal candidate will: Have a prior track record of publications at standard ML or robotics venues Be excited about building and deploying systems. Work with the PI in mentoring PhD and Master students. Be self-motivated and driven, creative, team-oriented, and able to work efficiently in a multidisciplinary environment. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $70,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Experience writing C++ and PyTorch. Training and debugging RL agents. Imitation Learning algorithms for robotics or autonomous vehicles. Prior work combining RL with human data or feedback. A track record of code following good software development practices. Planning algorithms for autonomous systems. The candidate will be part of the new NYU Center for Robotics and Embodied Intelligence at NYU Tandon School of Engineering in the department of Civil and Urban Engineering Transportation Department. We specifically seek candidates excited to be part of a new lab. . We welcome applicants from recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Eligible candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a current CV and a list of three references with complete contact information. All application materials should be submitted electronically at ev2237@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/176526": {"title": "Department Chair & Professor, Civil and Urban Engineering", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Civil and Urban Engineering Location: Brooklyn, New York The Department of Civil and Urban Engineering (CUE) at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for the position of Department Chair and Professor of CUE, with an anticipated start date of September 1, 2026 . We are looking for candidates with strong leadership and a forward-looking vision to guide the department toward the next level of innovation in research, education, and societal impact. The department\u2019s mission is to engage our students, faculty, and researchers to address the critical challenges to our natural and built environments through interdisciplinary education, systems thinking, and advanced research in a collaborative and inclusive environment. The department has 16 tenured and tenure-track faculty members, as well as 8 continuing-contract faculty. The department\u2019s research and teaching portfolio includes transportation, geotechnical, construction, environmental, and structural engineering, with emphasis on smart cities, resiliency, and sustainability. The department promotes a collaborative environment, with a number of faculty having joint appointments or affiliations with other NYU departments and schools. The department offers undergraduate, master\u2019s and doctoral degree programs. For more information about our department and its faculty, please visit our homepage . The Department Chair is expected to shape the department's future, attract the finest faculty, identify areas for investment, pursue and strengthen cross-disciplinary and interdisciplinary efforts in research and education, support the professional development of faculty and staff, and champion the work of the department. Successful candidates should have a PhD degree in Civil Engineering, Environmental Engineering, or a closely related discipline, have demonstrated outstanding professional and research achievements in their field, leadership experience, excellent collaborative, interpersonal, and communication skills, and have a passion for curricular innovation. Please submit the following materials through the \"Apply Now\" button. Cover letter outlining interest in the position and relevant qualifications (one page). Current CV including a section highlighting leadership experience. Strategic vision statement describing perspectives on the future of the discipline and leadership philosophy (2 pages). Research statement (2 pages). Teaching statement (2 pages). Names and contact information for three to five references. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis until the position is filled, with reviews expected to start January 1, 2026 . We encourage interested candidates to submit early. For more information, please contact cue_search@nyu.edu . ABOUT US New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by a historic commitment investment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and new perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. We aspire to create a climate where inclusion is not only appreciated but also considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for, and who possess an interest in, working in broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary (for 9 months) for this position is $200,000 - $300,000 for the Chair & Professor rank. New York University considers factors such as, but not limited to, the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183730": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Civil and Urban Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Hydrologic Systems Group within the Center for Connected Cities for Smart Mobility towards Accessible and Resilient Transportation (C2SMART) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to conduct research\u00a0 in large-scale flood risk analysis. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world.\u00a0 The C2SMART Center works on research related to urban mobility, reducing congestion, and transportation technologies. Responsibilities The successful candidate will have an opportunity to contribute to design and operational decisions related to New York State's culvert infrastructure, which serves thousands of small, mostly headwater, catchments over an area of ~141,000 square kilometers. The postdoc is expected to contribute to the project deliverables, while working collaboratively in a team of PhD and postdoctoral researchers. The postdoctoral associate will be expected to: Conduct statistical and computational analysis Contribute to proposal writing Complete project tasks by assigned deadlines Prepare and publish in peer-reviewed journals Present at conferences Submit computational tasks to NYU\u2019s High Performance Computing (HPC) resources Collaborate in a team setting Coordinate and participate in regular and adhoc meetings with partners and agency sponsors Collaborate with the computational team working on this project, which consists of two PhD researchers and one postdoctoral researcher Mentor students Previous experience on High Performance Computing is desired. The preferred candidate will possess one or all of the following; strong computational and statistical skills, excellent publication record and ability to work collaboratively in teams. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $65,000-$70,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification.This position is supported by a project at NYU's C2SMART Center and is initially available for one year, with the possibility of extension contingent on performance and the availability of funding. Candidates should have a PhD in Civil Engineering or a related field, and a demonstrated background in scalable flood inundation modeling, Impact-based flood forecasting stormwater infrastructure design under uncertainty. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: \u25cf an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, \u25cf copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, \u25cf names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: omarwani@nyu.edu. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: 05/01/2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187881": {"title": "Research Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Civil and Urban Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY FULL TIME RESEARCH ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The C2SMART Center at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Associate to work under the supervision of Professor Eugene Vinitsky. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The C2SMART Center focuses on academic investigations into metropolitan transit, gridlock mitigation, and vehicular innovations. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for creating human behavior simulation models for motorists, walkers, and bikers within a custom hardware-accelerated platform from the research group. Candidates will be responsible for expanding simulator capabilities, executing agent training routines, and refining algorithms to ensure peak system efficiency. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is\u00a0 $48,000 for full time depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is: 7/1/2026. This position is expected to be for 12 months. Candidates should have at least a BS in Computer Science, Applied Mathematics,\u00a0 or a related field and 1 years of experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. The successful candidate should have a demonstrated background in software engineering, reinforcement learning, or machine learning as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on software development is desired. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf names and contact information for three references. Please ensure all materials are provided through the Interfolio digital portal and directed to: ev2237@nyu.edu. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190592": {"title": "Part-Time Instructor, Department of Civil, Urban, and Environmental Engineering", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Civil and Urban Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY Position Description The Department of Civil, Urban, and Environmental Engineering (CUE) at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for part-time, non-tenured instructors, with a start date of January 1, 2027. The CUE department offers BS degrees in Civil Engineering and Environmental Engineering; MS degrees in Civil Engineering, Construction Management, Environmental Engineering, Environmental Science, and Transportation Systems; and PhD degrees in Civil Engineering and Transportation Systems. Qualifications, Responsibilities, and Expectations We invite applicants to teach one or more graduate or undergraduate courses. In particular, we seek instructors to teach sections of CE-GY 7233 Groundwater Hydrology and Pollution and CE-GY 7753 Environmental Systems Management.\u00a0 Graduate courses are generally scheduled in the evenings to accommodate full-time work schedules.\u00a0 Strong candidates will have a PhD in Environmental Engineering or Civil Engineering (or a closely related discipline) and a record of teaching experience in the broad areas of hydrology or environmental systems. General responsibilities for this position include preparing in-person lectures, grading homework and exams, and holding weekly office hours for student support. Additionally, it is expected that instructors will maintain a conducive learning environment, provide timely feedback and assessments, utilize technology effectively, and adhere to all institutional policies and guidelines. Please submit: Cover letter Current CV Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Teaching statement describing experience and teaching philosophy Names and contact information for at least two references Please submit materials electronically in Interfolio. We will review applications starting immediately and will continue until we fill the position. We encourage you to submit as soon as possible. Please reach out to Dr. Andr\u00e9 J. Butler at ajbutler@nyu.edu with any questions regarding your application. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate is $235.61 for this position. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with NYU Adjuncts - ACT-UAW Local 7902. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty who conduct groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. NYU Tandon is committed to substantially increasing the proportion of our faculty from historically underrepresented groups in STEM, and we encourage candidates from such groups to apply. We aspire to create a climate where diversity and inclusion are not only appreciated but considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for a culturally diverse environment.\u00a0 We take pride in our high number of female students and students who are the first in their family to go to college. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life. NYU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and is committed to a policy of equal treatment and opportunity in every aspect of its recruitment and hiring process without regard to age, alienage, caregiver status, childbirth, citizenship status, color, creed, disability, domestic violence victim status, ethnicity, familial status, gender and/or gender identity or expression, marital status, military status, national origin, parental status, partnership status, predisposing genetic characteristics, pregnancy, race, religion, reproductive health decision making, sex, sexual orientation, unemployment status, veteran status, or any other legally protected basis. Women, racial and ethnic minorities, persons of minority sexual orientation or gender identity, individuals with disabilities, and veterans are encouraged to apply for vacant positions at all levels. NYU aims to be among the greenest urban campuses in the country and carbon neutral by 2040. Learn more at nyu.edu/sustainability."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181585": {"title": "Contract Faculty, Department of Computer Science and Engineering", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn The Department of Computer Science and Engineering at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for full time, non-tenured, renewable faculty positions in Computer Science , at the level of Industry Assistant Professor or Industry Associate Professor , with a start date of September 2026 . We invite applicants for classroom teaching in all areas of Computer Science , including a broad range of undergraduate and graduate courses, including, but not limited to algorithms, software engineering, artificial intelligence, machine learning, databases, operating systems, and security. You should be an excellent teacher with substantial experience. At least an MS degree or equivalent in Computer Science or a closely related discipline is required. A Ph.D. degree in Computer Science or a closely related discipline is a strong advantage, as is a record of industrial experience, but neither is necessary. Please submit the following materials electronically through the \"Apply Now\" button Cover letter Current CV Teaching statement Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Names and contact information for three references who are willing to write letters on your behalf. We will review applications beginning on March 23, 2026 and will continue until we fill the position. We encourage you to submit early. Should you have any questions please contact Darryl Reeves at dr3218 [at] nyu.edu. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary (for 9-month) range for this position is $115,000 - $140,000 for the Industry Assistant Professor rank and $110,000 - $160,000 for the Industry Associate Professor rank. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/164939": {"title": "Research Staff (Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Computer Science and Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Scholar to work with Professor Justin Cappos. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Research Scholar will work on location at the NYU Center for Cybersecurity (located in Brooklyn, NY), an interdisciplinary research institute dedicated to training the next generation of cybersecurity professionals and to shaping the public discourse and policy landscape on issues of technology and security. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for developing new methods for isolation, compartmentalization, and virtualization while performing work in a research group advised by Prof. Justin Cappos. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in web assembly, virtualization, and shared object loading as exemplified by a strong implementation track record. Previous experience on implementing systems is necessary. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $39.23 per hour, depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualifications.\u00a0 This is a part-time position and would require a work schedule of 20 hours per week Candidates should have at least an MS in Computer Science or a related field and 1 year of experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf names and contact information for three references. \u25cf A link to your github or other public repository of your work All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to eve.henderson@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/165711": {"title": "Research Scholar (Part -time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Computer Science and Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Scholar to work with Professor Rosanna Bellini . New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the\u00a0 Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Work will take place at the NYU Center for Cybersecurity,\u00a0 an interdisciplinary research institute dedicated to training the next generation of cybersecurity professionals and to shaping the public discourse and policy landscape on issues of technology and security. Expectations The duties and responsibilities of this position involve work on a research project in the area of technology adoption across small businesses. This includes fieldwork, data analysis, writing papers, and preparing presentations. The duties and responsibilities in the position described may be modified as our objectives and mission change. Previous experience in user studies and working with a diverse research group is necessary for success in this position. Salary: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act , the hourly rate for this position is $39.23 (for\u00a0 20 hours per week) depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is: June 16, 2025. This position is expected to end on September 19, 2025. Part-\u00a0 time appointments would require a work schedule of 20 hours per week Candidates should have at least an MS in Computer Science or a related field, OR be currently enrolled in the pursuit of doctoral training for at least one (1) year. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf names and contact information for three references. \u25cf A link to your github or other public repository of your work All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to:\u00a0 eve.henderson@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/173131": {"title": "Research Intern (Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH INTERN New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Computer Science and Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Intern to work with Professor Chinmay Hegde. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The CSE department conducts ongoing research programs in a wide range of areas related to computer science theory, cyber security, analysis, visualization data, computer games, and internet and web research. Expectations The duties and responsibilities of this position involve work on research projects at the intersection of health and machine learning methods, including computer vision and natural language processing. This includes developing and applying advanced analytical techniques, conducting experiments, analyzing data, contributing to research publications, and preparing presentations. The role also involves mentoring and guiding junior students in related projects. As our research objectives evolve, the specific duties and responsibilities of this position may be refined. Previous experience in machine learning, health-related research, and collaboration within a diverse research group is essential for success in this position. Salary: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $30.00 (for 20 hours per week) depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is: September 16th 2025. This position is expected to end at the end of the Fall 2025 semester. Candidates should have at least an MS in Computer Science or a related field, OR be currently enrolled in the pursuit of doctoral training for at least one (1) year. Previous experience in machine learning, health-related research, and collaboration within a diverse research group is essential for success in this position. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf names and contact information for three references. \u25cf A link to your github or other public repository of your work All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: chinmay.h@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177822": {"title": "Adjunct Professor for Offensive Security (Graduate Standing)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY, United States The Department of\u00a0 Computer Science & Engineering at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for part-time, non-tenured instructors, with a start date of Jan 19, 2026 . The department of Computer Science & Engineering offers: BS degrees in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Electrical & Computer Engineering , MS degrees in Computer Science, Cybersecurity, Cybersecurity Risk and Strategy, Emerging Tech, and PhD degrees in Computer Science . We invite applicants to teach one or more graduate courses. In particular, we presently seek instructors for Offensive Security (graduate standing) with a hands-on approach. You should be an excellent experienced instructor with a MS or a Ph.D. in Cybersecurity, Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Electrical & Computer Engineering or a closely related discipline with a record of practical industry experience in this area and a real passion to teach it. Instruction modality is both online and/or in-person at NYU Tandon\u2019s Brooklyn campus. This course teaches Offensive Security in the context of Capture the Flag (CTF) challenges. It covers techniques and methods to reverse x86_64 assembly, exploitation strategies for compiled binaries, and common vulnerabilities in web applications and cryptographic implementations. Upon completion of this course, students should be able to: Assess the logic of compiled code via binary reverse engineering and solve programming challenges based on the functionality of the binary Employ memory corruption tactics and strategies to exploit compiled binaries Recognize web-based vulnerabilities and develop relevant attacks Assess cryptographic implementations to identify vulnerabilities and write-related exploits to demonstrate understanding Apply course-related CTF challenges to real-world software Compete in future CTF competitions Please submit the following materials through the \"Apply Now\" button. Cover letter Current CV Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Teaching statement describing experience and teaching philosophy Names and contact information for at least two references (if appropriate) We will review applications starting immediately and will continue until we fill the position; we encourage you to submit early. If appropriate: Should you have any questions please contact: Abhijit Chitnis at: aac664@nyu.edu About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by a historic commitment investment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and new perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. We aspire to create a climate where inclusion is not only appreciated but also considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for, and who possess an interest in, working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $222.27. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180086": {"title": "Visiting Assistant Professor in Computer Science, Fall 2026", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY The Department of Computer Science and Engineering (CSE) at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for a full time Visiting Assistant Professor position in Computer Science with an anticipated start date of September 1, 2026. The appointment will be for one academic year. We seek strong candidates with research and teaching interests working in any area of computer science. Successful candidates should have a Ph.D. degree in Computer Science or a closely related discipline. We seek individuals with evidence of excellent scholarship and teaching ability, and with an interest in collaborating on research with our current faculty. The visiting faculty member is expected to contribute to both research and teaching in the department. The teaching load will be a maximum of 3 courses for the year. Departmental research areas include algorithms and theory; big data management, analysis, and visualization; cybersecurity, machine learning and artificial intelligence (AI). We also have groups working in interdisciplinary areas such as AI for games, cybercrime, online political communication, public health and social media, responsible AI, sports analytics, and urban computing. Please submit the following materials electronically through the \"Apply Now\" button. \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf Current CV \u25cf Research Statement (up to 3 pages) \u25cf Teaching statement (up to 2 pages) \u25cf Recent teaching evaluations (if available) \u25cf Names and contact information for three references. ( Required f or searches requesting letters from Interfolio directly at the time of application ). Referees will be automatically contacted to upload confidential letters of reference in the Interfolio System. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Applicants are encouraged to apply early. Questions regarding the search may be directed to the search committee chair, Robert Krueger at: rk4815@nyu.edu About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. NYU Tandon is committed to substantially increase the proportion of our faculty from historically underrepresented groups in STEM and we encourage candidates from such groups to apply. We aspire to create a climate where diversity and inclusion are not only appreciated but considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for a culturally diverse environment. We take pride in our high numbers of female students and students who are the first in their family to go to college. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this (9-month) Visiting Assistant Professor position is $100,000-$160,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188541": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering NYU Postdoctoral Appointments Policy The NYU Center for Cybersecurity (NYU CCS) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate with expertise in human-centered security, usable privacy, or technology-facilitated abuse to join an interdisciplinary team focused on defending under-resourced organizations from financial harm and cybercrime. This position provides an opportunity to shape a clinical research program that includes peer-reviewed research on cybersecurity threats. The successful candidate will collaborate with faculty across NYU Tandon School of Engineering and NYU School of Law to generate research from the front lines of community cybersecurity practice. New York University (NYU) is a top private university in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The NYU Center for Cybersecurity, jointly based at NYU School of Law and NYU Tandon School of Engineering, brings together more than 50 faculty members across law, computer science, and policy. The NYU Cybersecurity Clinic , established in 2026 with sustained multi-year funding from Craig Newmark Philanthropies, the Clinic delivers free, faculty-supervised cybersecurity services to nonprofits, community health clinics, shelters, legal aid organizations, K-12 schools, and micro-businesses across all five New York City boroughs. The Clinic is a member of the Consortium of Cybersecurity Clinics and coordinates with NYC Cyber Command, the New York State Chief Cyber Officer, and federal law enforcement partners. Responsibilities The successful candidate will work under the direction of Professor Rosanna Bellini and Professor Damon McCoy to support the development of the NYU Cybersecurity Clinic . The candidate\u2019s primary responsibility is to be an active collaborator within\u00a0 their research group studying cybersecurity, technology risks and how their intersection in under-resourced organizations. The candidate will also help lead new research efforts, contribute to ongoing projects, and be a core member of both the Cybersecurity Clinic leadership team, CCS, including mentoring Ph.D. and MS student researchers as appropriate. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $75,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. ction, information security, or a closely related field (conferred by the start date). They should also possess a demonstrated research record in one or more of the following: usable security and privacy, cybercrime, cyberfraud, technology-facilitated abuse, security for at-risk users, or sociotechnical systems. Strong candidates will possess demonstrable experience translating technical security concepts for non-expert audiences and possess excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to operate across technical, legal, and policy sectors. Field experience with nonprofits, civil society organizations, or under-resourced groups is preferred. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: Prof. Rosanna Bellini (bellini@nyu.edu) Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: Fall 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189684": {"title": "Research Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART-TIME RESEARCH ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Computer Science and Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Associate to work with Professor Erdem Varol and Professor Shy Shoham in the Department of Biomedical Engineering. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The CSE department conducts ongoing research programs in a wide range of areas related to computer science theory, cyber security, analysis, visualization data, computer games, and internet and web research. Responsibiities: The Research Associate will be working on developing next-generation signal processing, machine learning and artificial intelligence techniques for analyzing large scale neural data. Specific duties include but not limited to: Working on research projects at the intersection of health and machine learning methods, including computer vision and natural language processing. Developing and applying advanced analytical techniques, conducting experiments, analyzing data, contributing to research publications, and preparing presentations. Mentoring and guiding junior students in related projects. Other duties as needed for the project as needed. Salary : In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $26.37 for 20 hours per week depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is asap and the position is available until\u00a0 8/31/2027, subject to available funding and performance. Candidates should have at least an MS in Computer Science or a related field, OR be currently enrolled in the pursuit of doctoral training for at least one (1) year. Previous experience in machine learning, health-related research, and collaboration within a diverse research group is essential for success in this position. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf names and contact information for three references. \u25cf A link to your github or other public repository of your work All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194842": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Visualization Imaging and Data Analysis Center (VIDA) at New York University invites applications for a Postdoctoral Associate. The successful candidate will join a research group working at the intersection of artificial intelligence, data management, visualization, and scientific discovery, with a focus on developing AI-driven methods and open-source systems to address challenges in data discovery, integration, and agentic systems. New York University (NYU) is a top private university in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in research, teaching, and learning. Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. VIDA offers a collaborative, interdisciplinary research environment with strong connections to NYU's broader strengths in AI/ML and partnerships spanning the natural sciences and biomedical research. New York City provides unmatched access to world-class academic institutions, leading industry research laboratories, and a thriving startup ecosystem \u2014 making it an exceptional setting for postdoctoral career development. Responsibilities The candidate will help lead new research efforts, contribute to ongoing projects, and be a core member of VIDA, including mentoring Ph.D. and MS student researchers as appropriate. Conduct original research on AI-driven methods for discovery, integration, and agentic systems Design and implement open-source tools and systems Publish and present research at leading venues Pursue independent collaborations across disciplines Participate in teaching and mentorship Salary range The position comes with a competitive salary, full benefits, and strong support for career development, including teaching opportunities for those who wish to pursue them. The initial appointment is for two years, with the possibility of an extension. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $75,000 \u2013 $110,000. Ideal candidates will have a Ph.D. in Computer Science, Data Science, Information Science, or a related field, along with experience in one or more of the following: data management and integration, machine learning and AI, information visualization, and open-source software development. Please submit the following materials via Interfolio: Cover letter describing your research interests and fit for this position Curriculum vitae (CV) Names and contact information for three professional references Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. Those received by October 16, 2026th will receive priority consideration, though we will continue to accept applications until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195500": {"title": "Faculty Fellow, Department of Computer Science & Engineering", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY The Department of Computer Science and Engineering at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for multiple full-time Faculty Fellow positions, with an anticipated start date of Fall 2027 . Job Description The Faculty Fellows Program aims to attract and support promising early career scholars with expertise across computer science. The program provides Faculty Fellows with mentoring, professional development, and academic networking opportunities, to prepare them for faculty positions in computer science at top universities. Faculty Fellows will do research, both independently and with an NYU faculty mentor. They will also teach 1 course per semester. The initial appointment is for one year, beginning in September 2027, and is renewable for a second year. Fellowship benefits include an annual housing allowance (up to $20,000) and an annual allowance for research expenditure of ($5,000). An additional summer month is also covered by the department, with the option for the designated Faculty Mentor to provide additional two months from grants or discretionary funds. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in Computer Science, Information Science, or a closely related discipline by the time of appointment. We will consider factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Please submit the following materials: Cover letter Current CV Teaching statement Research statement or writing sample Names and contact information for three references. Process We will review applications beginning on January 4, 2027, and will continue until the position is filled. We strongly encourage you to submit early. Please submit your questions about this position to Assistant Professor Rosanna Bellini via email @bellini@nyu.edu. Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary (9-month) is $90,000 for the Faculty Fellow position. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment investment of $1 billion and under the leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is recruiting world-class faculty, and conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier and safer real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; entrepreneurial thinking, academic and technological innovation and the creation of accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195587": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POST DOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering Computer Science and Engineering Secure Systems Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Postdoctoral Associate to work with Justin Cappos. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Computers and connected systems are central to modern life, but they also expose substantial security risks. The Secure Systems Laboratory (SSL) at New York University addresses these challenges by designing practical, open-source solutions that improve the security of real-world systems at scale. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for: Performing cutting-edge cybersecurity research. Transitioning research work products into widespread production use. Writing peer-reviewed journals and conferences, and disseminating findings through publications, conference presentations, and technical talks. Mentoring lab members and working collaboratively. Designing and drafting grant proposals for federal and private funding sources. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is ranging from $64,063 to $100,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Computer Science or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in Software Supply Chain Security and Forensics as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on contributing to real-world open-source software is desired but not required Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: \u25cf an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, \u25cf copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, \u25cf names and contact information for three references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195647": {"title": "Research Engineer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, New York FULL TIME RESEARCH ENGINEER New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Visualization Imaging and Data Analysis Center (VIDA) at NYU's Tandon School of Engineering is a multidisciplinary research hub focused on developing innovative solutions to complex data challenges. We collaborate with academic, industry, and government partners to advance the state of the art in computer and data science to support data-driven exploration. VIDA is seeking talented Research Engineers to join our dynamic team. This position involves research and development in data science and engineering areas relevant to ongoing projects in the VIDA Center. Our projects span implementation-focused and method-focused directions, as well as multidisciplinary collaborations across domains. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Beyond VIDA, NYU offers a unique environment for pursuing data science and engineering research, and New York City provides unmatched access to top academic institutions, industry research labs, and startups. Responsibilities The ideal candidates will contribute to projects involving: Design and development of novel visualization, AI, data management and data analysis methods Creation of systems for managing, analyzing, and visualizing large datasets Application of machine learning and AI techniques to domain-specific challenges Collaboration with domain experts from fields such as urban computing, biomedical research, and beyond Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $60,000-$80,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific funding source and the terms of the research grant contracts when extending an offer. The position would come with a competitive salary, full benefits, and opportunities for career development, including teaching opportunities if those are desired. Appointment lengths are negotiable. Expected start date and period of employment We are looking for candidates that can start as soon as possible. This position is expected to be for 1 year with the possibility of renewalv subject to available funding and job performance. MS or BS degree in Computer Science, Data Science, or a related field, with practical software engineering experience. Strong programming and a track record of building and optimizing complex codebases. Experience contributing to open-source communities and deploying software is a strong plus. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in the following: Strong programming skills and experience with data analysis Database systems and big data management Information integration and data engineering Data visualization and visual analytics Machine learning and artificial intelligence Software development and system design Experience with open-source software development Domain expertise in urban computing, biomedical research, or other applied fields Experience collaborating on multidisciplinary research teams Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: CV/resume Cover letter describing your research interests and experience Names and contact information for 2-3 professional references (Optional) Links to relevant publications, projects, or GitHub repositories All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until positions are filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195649": {"title": "Research Scientist", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Computer Science & Engineering Location: Brooklyn, New York FULL TIME RESEARCH SCIENTIST New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Visualization Imaging and Data Analysis Center (VIDA) at NYU's Tandon School of Engineering is a multidisciplinary research hub focused on developing innovative solutions to complex data challenges. We collaborate with academic, industry, and government partners to advance the state of the art in computer and data science to support data-driven exploration. VIDA is seeking talented Research Scientists to join our dynamic team. These positions involve research and development in data science and engineering areas relevant to ongoing projects in the VIDA Center. Our projects span implementation-focused and method-focused directions, as well as multidisciplinary collaborations across domains. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Beyond VIDA, NYU offers a unique environment for pursuing data science and engineering research, and New York City provides unmatched access to top academic institutions, industry research labs, and startups. Responsibilities The ideal candidates will contribute to projects involving: Design and development of novel visualization, AI, data management and data analysis methods Creation of systems for managing, analyzing, and visualizing large datasets Application of machine learning and AI techniques to domain-specific challenges Collaboration with domain experts from fields such as urban computing, biomedical research, and beyond Provide technical leadership and day-to-day project management, including coordinating and mentoring team members. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $80,000-$100,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific funding source and the terms of the research grant contracts when extending an offer. The position would come with a competitive salary, full benefits, and opportunities for career development, including teaching opportunities if those are desired. Appointment lengths are negotiable. Expected start date and period of employment We are looking to have the candidates start as soon as possible.\u00a0 This position is expected to be for 1 year with the possibility of renewal, subject to available funding and job performance. PhD in Computer Science, Data Science, or a related field, or an MS degree with extensive practical and research experience. Proven track record of leading complex technical projects or research initiatives from concept to execution. Experience publishing peer-reviewed research, contributing to open-source communities, or presenting at technical conferences is a strong plus. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in the following: Strong programming skills and experience with data analysis Demonstrated experience in one or more of the following areas: Database systems and big data management Information integration and data engineering Data visualization and visual analytics Machine learning and artificial intelligence Software development and system design Experience with open-source software development Domain expertise in urban computing, biomedical research, or other applied fields Experience collaborating on multidisciplinary research teams Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: A brief cover letter, explaining your interest, background with developing production software, and fit for the position; an up-to-date resume that includes a link to code samples (such as your github), 3 reference letters, submitted by recommenders (not by the candidate). All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Period of performance: Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is January 1st, 2026. This position is for one year, with an opportunity to renew subject to performance and availability of funds."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/145114": {"title": "POSTDOCTORAL RESEARCHER", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL RESEARCHER Machines in Motion Laboratory New York University The Machines in Motion laboratory at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a\u00a0Post Doctoral Researcher/Post Doctoral Associate to join an exciting project on model predictive control with force feedback. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, hasthe distinct history of having been known previously as Brooklyn Poly and the NYU Polytechnic School of Engineering. Candidates will be responsible for conducting scholarly research, writing papers, help mentor students and perform work in a multidisciplinary environment. The successful candidate will conduct research on high frequency model predictive control using force feedback. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 to $75,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Tandon faculty and students are at the forefront of the high-tech start-up culture in New York City and have access to NYU\u2019s Global Network University. The NYU Tandon School of Engineering is\u00a0deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty\u00a0innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. We are seeking individuals with a strong background in model-predictive control and a publication track record in top robotics conferences and journals. Previous experience of numerical\u00a0implementations of optimal control solvers for high frequency control of complex robots is necessary. We welcome applicants from recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Eligible candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit a complete CV, a research statement describing your interests, goals\u00a0and how they related to our research, a complete list of publications, copies of up to three\u00a0relevant scientific papers, and the names and contact information of three references (all as pdf).\u00a0All application materials should be submitted electronically via interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/149170": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The NYU Nanolab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work on the development of innovative biosensors for wearable applications. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The opportunities this position offer include: Affiliation with the groups led by Prof. Davood Shahrjerdi ( NYU Nanolab ) and Prof. Elisa Riedo ( PicoForce lab ) Close collaboration with scientists at NYU in the NanoBioX Initiative Opportunity to engage with biotech startups in the Future Labs Incubator Opportunity to live in vibrant New York City One-year contract with an opportunity to extend Expectations: The successful candidate will primarily work on fabricating and characterizing field-effect transistor biosensors for sensing target analytes in sweat. The candidate will work closely with Prof. Davood Shahrjerdi and Prof. Elisa Riedo in accomplishing this goal.\u00a0 The ideal candidate must have extensive experience with nanofabrication of electronic devices and their electrical characterization. This position is at Tandon School of Engineering (Brooklyn, NY) at New York University. Salary Range: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $62,500 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have: PhD degree in physics, electrical engineering, or a nanotechnology-related field. Creative, independent, motivated, and solution-oriented researcherStrong understanding of the physics of field-effect transistors Extensive experience with cleanroom fabrication techniques, including e-beam lithography, electron-beam deposition, etching, and metrology techniques such as AFM Strong communication skills and willingness to interact and collaborate closely with internal and external researchers Hardworking Candidates should have: PhD degree in physics, electrical engineering, or a nanotechnology-related field. Creative, independent, motivated, and solution-oriented researcherStrong understanding of the physics of field-effect transistors Extensive experience with cleanroom fabrication techniques, including e-beam lithography, electron-beam deposition, etching, and metrology techniques such as AFM Strong communication skills and willingness to interact and collaborate closely with internal and external researchers Hardworking"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/162993": {"title": "Research Intern", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  RESEARCH INTERNSHIP Department of Computer Science and Engineering New York University The Department of Computer Science and Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a short-term (6 month) paid Research Intern to work with Prof. Nasir Memon. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, a nd the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Expectations: Candidates will be responsible for developing new methodologies for secure, robust and transparent biometric recognition algorithms and perform work in a multidisciplinary environment and a diverse research group advised by Prof. Nasir Memon. The successful candidate will be driven, creative and team-oriented with a passion for developing explainable authentication frameworks. Salary Range: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this full time position is $48,000 - $62,499. Candidate should have a M.S. degree in Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, Science, or a related field. They should have a strong background in AI/ML, biometric algorithm design and evaluation, and interests in Generative AI. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit a complete CV, cover letter, and the names and contact information of three references (all as pdf). All application materials should be submitted electronically at via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/164631": {"title": "Research Scientist", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Full-Time Research Scientist New York University Tandon School of Engineering Description : The Research in Advanced Integrated Circuits and Systems (RAISE) Lab in the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Research Scientist. The RAISE lab focuses on developing scalable and spectrally agile software-defined radios with innovative RFIC-based solutions for the upper-mid band (FR3) spectrum. This project aims to enable the use of these bands across cellular and satellite systems, contributing to the commercialization of the FR3 band. This position is for 12 months, with an opportunity to renew subject to availability of funds. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching, research, and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Expectations Candidates will engage in original interdisciplinary research. They will be expected to take on a leadership roles in ongoing projects, and disseminate research (publications, conferences). They will engage in other research activities of interest within the scope of NYU's RAISE Lab. The candidate will also work closely with Prof. Hamed Rahmani on broadband and energy-efficient RF Front-end building blocks as well as custom Integrated circuits and systems for emerging sensing and connectivity paradigms. Salary Requirements In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this full-time position is $90,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. This position is for 12 months, with the possibility of renewal, subject to availability of funds. Qualifications Masters or PhD (preferred) in Electrical and Computer Engineering or related field; Excellent communication skills; Demonstrated program coordination experience, ideally in an academic setting. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Masters or PhD (preferred) in Electrical and Computer Engineering or related field; Excellent communication skills; Demonstrated program coordination experience, ideally in an academic setting. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, Brief cover letter, explaining your interest and fit for the position.; Names and contact information for three references (submitted by recommenders (not by the candidate). copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: [ ece-announcements@nyu.edu ] Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is March 31, 2025."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/165088": {"title": "Research Scientist (Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Research Scientist (Part-time) NYU Tandon School of Engineering Description : The System & Artificial Intelligence (SAI) Lab in the Electrical and Computer Engineering department at NYU Tandon focuses on exploring the field where deep learning and hardware systems intersect. Our primary goal is to co-optimize AI algorithms and the underlying hardware architecture to enhance the implementation of AI. Additionally, we leverage AI as a powerful tool to streamline the hardware design process. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Expectations The research scientist will engage in original interdisciplinary research. In addition to taking on a leadership role in ongoing projects, they will be able to engage in the research activities of their choice, within the scope of NYU's SAI Lab, within the Electrical and Computer Engineering Department. They will also work closely with Prof. Sai Qian Zhang on efficient designs for machine learning models. Salary: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the salary for this position is $27.50 an hour with a commitment of at least 10 hours per week.\u00a0 This position is for 4 months, with an opportunity to renew subject to availability of funds. MA or PhD (preferred) with expertise in Electrical and Computer Engineering or related field; Excellent communication skills; Demonstrated program coordination experience, ideally in an academic setting. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position Brief cover letter, explaining your interest and fit for the position names and contact information for three references (submitted by recommenders (not by the candidate) copies of up to three relevant scientific papers All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: [ ece-announcements@nyu.edu ] Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is April 1, 2025 ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/166057": {"title": "POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire one or more Postdoctoral Associates to work in the area of machine learning and data analytics for smart grids in Prof. Yuzhang Lin\u2019s research group at NYU Tandon School of Engineering. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Prof. Yuzhang Lin\u2019s research group in the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering conducts cutting-edge research in modeling, situation awareness, and cyber-physical resilience of smart power grids with machine learning applications. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for: Conducting advanced research in machine learning and data analytics for power system operation and planning; Support externally funded projects by developing algorithms and software, performing validation and demonstration using real-world datasets, and delivering project milestones and deliverables; Publish research outcomes in top-tier journals and conferences, prepare project reports and presentations, and assist in grant proposal development; Supervise and mentor graduate students in their research activities. Successful candidates will have a demonstrated background in the following areas, as exemplified by a strong publication record: Modeling, monitoring, control, and simulation of power systems; Innovation and application of deep learning in power systems; Strong academic writing, oral communication, and team collaboration skills. In addition, previous experience in any of the following areas is a plus but not required: Cyber-physical power systems Detection and estimation theory Optimization algorithms Federated learning Multimodal machine learning Large language models Power system visualization and software development Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for these positions\u00a0 will be $62,500 - $67,500 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualifications Candidates should have a PhD in Electrical Engineering or a related field by the start date of the position. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, research experiences, work experiences, honors and awards, and skills related to coding, simulation, software, and hardware. a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, transcripts from all degrees earned or to be earned, a Ph.D. certificate (if the degree has already been awarded) copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: yuzhang.lin@nyu.edu. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled. The start date for this position s January 1st, 2026 but an earlier start date may be considered as well."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/166734": {"title": "NYU ECE Faculty Fellow | Fall 2025", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at the Tandon School of Engineering at New York University invites applications for multiple full-time Faculty Fellow positions, beginning in Fall 2025. The Faculty Fellowship Program welcomes candidates with research interests spanning the broad spectrum of ECE, including (but not limited to): -Communications, networking, and information theory -Signal processing, computer vision, and machine learning -Cybersecurity, privacy, and cryptography -Robotics, control systems, and cyber-physical systems -Power systems, energy harvesting, and sustainable electronics -Semiconductor devices, photonics, and nanoelectronics -Quantum computing, hardware security, and embedded systems -Biomedical devices, neural engineering, and wearable systems -RF/microwave circuits and terahertz technologies -Computer architecture, VLSI design, and hardware-software co-design Faculty Fellows will engage in high-quality research, work closely with ECE faculty mentors, and contribute to the department\u2019s teaching and mentoring missions. The program offers mentoring, professional development, and academic networking to prepare Fellows for future faculty positions. Appointments are for one year initially, starting in September 2025, and are renewable for a second year based on performance and funding availability. Responsibilities Conduct high-quality independent research and collaborate with an ECE faculty mentor. Co-advise Ph.D. students with the faculty mentor. Teach one course per semester, with class sizes capped at 50 students. Faculty Fellows will be assigned to courses with established materials or parallel sections with faculty support. Participate in grant writing and serve as Co-Principal Investigator on successful grants. Engage in professional development activities and contribute to NYU campus life. Fellowship Benefits An annual research allowance of $5,000 to support conference travel, publication fees, and research expenses. Comprehensive benefits package, including medical and dental coverage. Office space at NYU\u2019s Engineering buildings, Brooklyn, NY. Access to networking and professional development events, and structured support in the faculty job search process during the final year of the program A Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering, Computer Science, or a closely related field is required at the time of appointment. Demonstrated ability to pursue an innovative research agenda and strong potential for teaching excellence. Please submit the following materials through the \"Apply Now\" button. Curriculum vitae (CV) Cover letter Research statement Teaching statement Names and contact information for at least three references (to be uploaded as a separate document). All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. For full consideration, applications should be submitted by May 15, 2025 , though we will continue to review applications past this date as needed. For any questions, please email us at : ece-announcements@nyu.edu About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. NYU Tandon is committed to substantially increase the proportion of our faculty from historically underrepresented groups in STEM and we encourage candidates from such groups to apply. We aspire to create a climate where diversity and inclusion are not only appreciated but considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for a culturally diverse environment. We take pride in our high numbers of female students and students who are the first in their family to go to college. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life. Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual 9 month salary range is $70,000 - $110,000 for the Faculty Fellowship position .\u00a0 New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168806": {"title": "Research Scientist \u2013 Radio Frequency (RF) Propagation Channel Development", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  RESEARCH SCIENTIST NYU Wireless LAB New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Electrical and Computer Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire two (2) Research Scientists to join an exciting project to commercialize next generation wireless data and analytics. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, has a distinct history of having been known previously as Brooklyn Poly and the NYU Polytechnic School of Engineering. It is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship among students and faculty who make a difference in the world. Research Scientist \u2013 Radio Frequency (RF) Propagation Channel Development Position Overview: NYU, with its world class industrial affiliates program and extensive research expertise in wireless communications, is partnering with one of the world\u2019s largest professional engineering organizations to create unique research and development offerings for the wireless communications industry. In fulfillment of this mission, NYU is seeking a talented, motivated and energetic Radio Frequency (RF) Propagation Channel Researcher to lead efforts in RF propagation measurements and data processing. Program Description: The collaboration will serve the wireless communications industry with much needed propagation data and analytics from new environments and frequency bands never explored. NYU is a vibrant, dynamic and fast paced creative leader in this field. This collaboration will operate much like a start-up within NYU for an initial period but rapidly become self-sustaining with anticipated growth and will provide vast research publication and teaching opportunities while building a global user base. The RF Propagation Channel Researcher has an opportunity to play a key role in this success and play a vital role in NYU\u2019s wireless communications research community, as well as provide fundamental knowledge on the world stage. Position: Research Scientist who wishes to continue research in the field of wireless communications with advanced channel modeling, data analysis and simulation. Initial two-year appointment with potential extension based on program success. Duties and Responsibilities: The RF Propagation Channel Researcher will conduct, manage and oversee RF propagation measurements, channel modeling, data normalization, analysis and simulation working with Professors Theodore S. Rappaport and Daniel Abraham in the NYU WIRELESS center, and NYU\u2019s Technology and Opportunity Ventures office. Specific duties are to plan, manage and carry out extensive and accurate channel measurements in emerging wireless bands; procure and calibrate equipment, FCC licenses, coordinate measurements, and lead a team of students to carry out measurement campaigns, as well as arrange for all logistics for the measurement campaigns. Process data and document all measurements procedures, create raw normalized database sets and statistical and site-specific channel models based on empirical data, analyze and explore new methods for characterizing wireless channels, while leading a team of graduate and undergraduate students in all of the above activities, publish results in peer reviewed literature, present data\u00a0and papers in IEEE and industry/3GPP standard bodies, and provide data in specified data formats to our partner. Specific analytics responsibilities will entail analyzing radio wave propagation data for the creation and improvement of statistical and site-specific channel models, including the ability to represent the raw measurement data in large-scale formats that may be pipelined and built upon in massive data sets working with our Data Analytics Researcher. Salary and Benefits: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position will range from $100K to $140K, depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. NYU employee benefits: Medical/Dental Insurance, PTO Application instructions: Applicants should submit, as one PDF, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interests and goals and how their research relates to this position, brief cover letter explaining your interest and fit for the position; names and contact information for three references (submitted by recommenders, not by the candidate); copies of up to three relevant scientific papers. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio and sent to: [ ece-announcements@nyu.edu ] and directly to D.Abraham@nyu.edu with the subject line identifying NYU Wireless Initiative and the position. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled. The expected start date is February 1, 2026. Requirements and Skills: Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in electrical engineering, more specifically, RF engineering, wireless propagation measurements and channel modeling. Desired Requirements: Previous experience in channel analysis and modeling, RF hardware design, propagation experiment design, equipment maintenance, large database management, data analytics and python simulation coding. Excellent communication skills. Demonstrated program coordination experience, ideally in an academic setting. Candidates must present proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one PDF, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interests and goals and how their research relates to this position, brief cover letter explaining your interest and fit for the position; names and contact information for three references (submitted by recommenders, not by the candidate); copies of up to three relevant scientific papers. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio and sent to: [ ece-announcements@nyu.edu ] and directly to D.Abraham@nyu.edu with the subject line identifying NYU Wireless Initiative and the position. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168810": {"title": "Research Scientist \u2013 Channel Simulation and Data Analytics", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Research Scientist NYU Wireless LAB New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Electrical and Computer Engineering Department at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire two (2) Postdoctoral Associates to join an exciting project to commercialize next generation wireless data and analytics. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, has\u00a0a distinct history of having been known previously as Brooklyn Poly and the NYU Polytechnic School of Engineering.\u00a0It is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship among students and faculty who make a difference in the world. Research Scientist\u2013 Channel Simulation and Data Analytics Position Overview: NYU, with its world-class industrial affiliates program and extensive research expertise in wireless communications, is partnering with one of the world\u2019s largest professional engineering organizations to create unique research and development offerings for the wireless communications industry. In fulfillment of this mission, NYU is seeking a talented, motivated and energetic Channel Simulation and Data Analytics Researcher to lead efforts in creating state of the art RF channel simulators, software, and data analytics tools for statistical and site-specific radio frequency (RF) propagation prediction products in collaboration with our partner. Program Description: The collaboration will serve the wireless communications industry with much needed propagation data and analytics from new environments and frequency bands never explored. NYU is a vibrant, dynamic and fast paced creative leader in this field. This collaboration will operate much like a start-up within NYU for an initial period but rapidly become self-sustaining with anticipated growth and will provide vast research publication and teaching opportunities while building a global user base. The Channel Simulation and Data Analytics Researcher has an opportunity to play a key role in this success and play a vital role in NYU\u2019s wireless communications research community, as well as provide fundamental knowledge on the world stage. Position: A Research Scientist\u00a0 who wishes to continue research in wireless communications with advanced AI/ML approaches. Initial three-year appointment with potential extension based on program success. Duties and Responsibilities: The Channel Simulation and Data Analytics Research Scientist will plan, manage, oversee and carry out extensive data processing and coding to implement existing data sets and channel modeling simulators in Python, and to build AI-based software tools to create new propagation prediction software, working with Professors Theodore S. Rappaport and Daniel Abraham in the NYU WIRELESS center, and NYU\u2019s Technology and Opportunity Ventures office. Implement statistical and site specific and channel models based on past and future empirical databases. Implement AI and ML techniques to enhance and improve the creation of statistical and site-specific propagation simulators. Coordinate and lead data science activities related to channel measurements and channel modeling, while leading a team of graduate and undergraduate students. Master and improve the code base for all measurement systems, data processing approaches, and resulting models and simulators. Implement all logistics and software development tools and systems for best practices for the on-going coding for measurement campaigns, and for raw database sets and statistical and site-specific channel models based on empirical data. Provide data analytics that explore new methods for characterizing wireless channels: Statistical and site-specific models based on empirical data Use AI/ML techniques to enhance propagation simulators Lead graduate students in all the above activities, publish results in peer reviewed literature, present data and papers in IEEE and industry/3GPP standard bodies, and provide data in specified data formats to our partner. Salary and Benefits : In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position will range from $100K to $120 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. NYU employee benefits: Medical/Dental Insurance, PTO Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in electrical engineering, more specifically, RF engineering. Desired Requirements: Previous experience in RF Channel modeling and coding for wireless propagation analytics, channel analysis and simulators. Strong experience building and implementing AI/ML models, creating algorithms, running simulations. Experience with Python and data science libraries. Experience in AI/ML applications for RF channel modeling a plus. In-depth knowledge of state-of-the-art machine-learning algorithms, tools, and technologies for large-scale data. Strong research background or publication record in solving complex computational problems using data analytics methods. Hand-on experience using complex machine-learning models to resolve large-scale data analytics challenges, deploy, fine-tune and execute machine learning models in production. Strong coding skills in Python, Spark, SQL, TensorFlow, Pytorch, or similar machine-learning and data engineering frameworks. Preferred: experience with wireless data. Excellent communication skills. Demonstrated program coordination experience, ideally in an academic setting. Candidates must present proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one PDF, the following information: \u25cf an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf a research statement that describes their interests and goals and how their research relates to this position, \u25cf brief cover letter explaining your interest and fit for the position; \u25cf names and contact information for three references (submitted by recommenders, not by the candidate); \u25cf copies of up to three relevant scientific papers. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio and sent to: [ ece-announcements@nyu.edu ] and directly to D.Abraham@nyu.edu with the subject line identifying NYU Wireless Initiative and the position. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled. The expected start date is July 1, 2025, or sooner."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168892": {"title": "Research Scholar ( Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Electrical and Computer Engineering Department at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part-Time Research Scholar in Electrical Engineering to join an exciting project on \u201cNonlinear and Time-Dependent Systems\u201d. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship among students and faculty who make a difference in the world. Expectations Candidates will engage in original interdisciplinary research. In addition to taking on a leadership role in ongoing projects, they will have opportunities to pursue research activities of their choice within the scope of NYU's Electrical and Computer Engineering Department. The candidate will also work closely with Professor Unnikrishna Pillai on solutions for nonlinear and time-dependent systems\u201d . Salary: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position begins at $29.50 -\u00a0 $34.34 per hour,\u00a0 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately 20 to 27 hours per week. Minimum Masters\u00a0 Degree ( PhD preferred) with expertise in Electrical and Computer Engineering or related field; Excellent communication skills; Demonstrated program coordination experience, ideally in an academic setting. Candidates must present proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one PDF, the following information: \u25cf an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf a research statement that describes their interests and goals and how their research relates to this position, \u25cf brief cover letter explaining your interest and fit for the position; \u25cf names and contact information for three references (submitted by recommenders, not by the candidate); \u25cf copies of up to three relevant scientific papers. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio and sent to: [ ece-announcements@nyu.edu ] Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled. The expected start date is July 1st 2025. This position duration is for approximately 10 weeks."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/169263": {"title": "Research Scholar ( Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Electrical and Computer Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part-Time Research Scholar in Electrical Engineering to join an exciting project on \u201cthe use of AI to design hardware\u201d. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world Expectations: Candidates will have the unique opportunity to engage in original interdisciplinary research and take on a leadership role in ongoing projects. They will also have the intellectual freedom to pursue research activities of their choice within the scope of NYU's Electrical and Computer Engineering Department. The candidate will work closely with Professors Siddharth Garg and Ludovic Righetti on solutions to use RL-based solutions for optimizing hardware design. Salary range: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $26.27 to\u00a0 $30 per hour depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. The position requires at least 27 hours per week. MS (preferred) with expertise in Electrical and Computer Engineering or related field; Excellent communication skills; Demonstrated program coordination experience, ideally in an academic setting. Candidates must present proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one PDF, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications a research statement that describes their interests and goals, and how their research relates to this position brief cover letter explaining your interest and fit for the position names and contact information for three references (submitted by recommenders, not by the candidate) copies of up to three relevant scientific papers. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Please ensure that your application is complete and includes all the required documents. Incomplete applications may not be considered. The application should be sent to: [ ece-announcements@nyu.edu ]. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled. The expected start date is July 1, 2025."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170534": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE in Bioelectronics New York University Tandon School of Engineering The NYU Tandon School of Engineering Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work on the development of innovative bioelectronic devices for wearable applications. The successful candidate will primarily work on design, fabrication, and characterization of electronic chips for sensing application, including brain interfaces. The successful candidate will work closely with Prof. Davood Shahrjerdi in accomplishing this goal. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The opportunities this position offer include: Expectations: The ideal candidate must have extensive experience with CMOS (complementary metal-oxide semiconductor) chips design, fabrication of electronic devices, and their electrical characterization. This position is located at Tandon School of Engineering at New York University. Salary: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $67,500, depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering, Physics, Materials Science, or a related field Expertise in one or more of the following areas: CMOS analog/mixed-signal circuit design Semiconductor device fabrication and cleanroom processing Electrical/optical characterization of electronic or bioelectronic devices Strong problem-solving skills and the ability to work independently Excellent communication skills and a collaborative mindset Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: Cover letter Curriculum vitae (CV) Contact information for two references All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to Prof. Davood Shahrjerdi ( davood@nyu.edu ) Review of applications\u00a0 will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. The initial appointment is for one year, with the possibility of renewal based on performance and funding availability. The expected start date is: August 1, 2025."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/171008": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY Post-Doctoral Positions in Robotics, Controls, AI, Computer Vision, and Hardware Security The Control/Robotics Research Laboratory at NYU invites qualified applicants with a doctorate degree in the areas of electrical, or computer, or mechanical engineering, or related field to apply. A strong publication record is encouraged and previous experience in areas such as Neural network vulnerabilities and defenses, Anomaly detection, Adversarial machine learning, Computer vision, Swarms, Autonomous Robots, hardware security, and Embedded systems development is desired. The successful applicant will work on various projects on robotics and computer vision, controls, cyber-physical systems and their security, hardware security, and machine learning and their security. The work will include algorithm design, prototype implementation (e.g., in Matlab/Python), deployment (to the agents&#39; onboard computational hardware using the Robot Operating System), and experimental testing (on unmanned vehicle swarms). A solid competency with the standard implementation tools used in autonomous robots and embedded systems (e.g., C, C++, Linux, MATLAB, Python, Robot Operating System, Gazebo Physics Engine Simulator) is required. In compliance with NYC\u2019s\u00a0Pay\u00a0Transparency\u00a0Act, the annual base\u00a0salary\u00a0range for this position is $60,000 - $80,000. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, has the distinct history of having been known previously as Brooklyn Poly. The position is open to PhDs with background in robotics, controls, AI, and/or computer vision and hardware security. The candidate is expected to work in a highly collaborative multi-disciplinary environment with other post-doctoral associates, PhD and undergraduate students, as well as faculty members. It is expected that applicants have a strong publication record in the aforementioned areas.\u00a0\u00a0The applicants are also expected to have strong analytical, communication, and writing skills. The terms of employment are very competitive. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with full publication list, degree transcript, statement of research interests, and at least two letters of reference, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email Farshad Khorrami at\u00a0khorrami@nyu.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/172864": {"title": "Research Scholar ( Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Research Scholar to join an exciting project on accelerating cryptographic and privacy-preserving computation using modern computer architectures. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Expectations: Candidates will have the unique opportunity to engage in original interdisciplinary research and take on a leadership role in ongoing projects. They will also have the intellectual freedom to pursue research activities of their choice within the scope of NYU's Electrical and Computer Engineering Department. The candidate will work closely with Prof. Brandon Reagen on exploring system-level and architectural solutions for next-generation cryptographic and other relevant projects. Salary range: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $26.27 to\u00a0 $30 per hour depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. The position requires at least 20 hours per week. MS (preferred) with expertise in Electrical and Computer Engineering or related field. Excellent communication skills. Demonstrated program coordination experience, ideally in an academic setting. Candidates must present proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one PDF, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications. a research statement that describes their interests and goals, and how their research relates to this position brief cover letter explaining your interest and fit for the position. names and contact information for three references (submitted by recommenders, not by the candidate) copies of up to three relevant scientific papers. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Please ensure that your application is complete and includes all the required documents. Incomplete applications may not be considered. The application should be sent to: [ ece-announcements@nyu.edu ]. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled. The expected start date is September 2025"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177377": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Post Doctoral Associate NYU Tandon School of Engineering Post-Doctoral Positions in Robotics, Controls, AI, Computer Vision, and Hardware Security The Control/Robotics Research Laboratory at NYU Tandon Electrical & Computer Engineering department invites qualified applicants with a doctorate degree in the areas of electrical, computer, or mechanical engineering, or related fields to apply. A strong publication record is encouraged and previous experience in areas such as neural network vulnerabilities and defenses, anomaly detection, adversarial machine learning, computer vision, swarms, autonomous robots, hardware security, and embedded systems development is desired. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Expectations: The successful applicant will work on various projects related to robotics and computer vision, controls, cyber-physical systems and their security, hardware security, and machine learning and their security. The expected work includes: algorithm design prototype implementation (e.g., in Matlab/Python) deployment (to the agents & onboard computational hardware using the Robot Operating System), and experimental testing (on unmanned vehicle swarms). A solid competency with the standard implementation tools used in autonomous robots and embedded systems (e.g., C, C++, Linux, MATLAB, Python, Robot Operating System, Gazebo Physics Engine Simulator) is required. Salary: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $80,000. The position is open to PhDs with backgrounds in robotics, controls, AI, and/or computer vision and hardware security. The candidate is expected to work in a highly collaborative multi-disciplinary environment with other post-doctoral associates, PhD and undergraduate students, as well as faculty members. It is expected that applicants have a strong publication record in the aforementioned areas. The applicants are also expected to have strong analytical, communication, and writing skills. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with full publication list, degree transcript, statement of research interests, and at least two letters of reference, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email Farshad Khorrami at khorrami@nyu.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182085": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The NYU Nanolab at New York University Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Postdoctoral Associate to work in quantum information science and engineering, with a focus on superconducting and superconductor\u2013semiconductor hybrid quantum devices. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Electrical and Computer Engineering Department and NYU Nanolab host a growing interdisciplinary research program in superconducting and semiconductor-based quantum technologies, with strong ties to national laboratories and industry partners. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for conducting experimental research in superconducting quantum hardware and quantum materials, contributing to sponsored research projects in quantum information science and engineering. Responsibilities include nanofabrication, materials synthesis, cryogenic transport and microwave characterization of quantum devices, data analysis, and dissemination of results through publications and presentations. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in experimental quantum materials, superconductivity, or semiconductor device physics, as evidenced by a strong publication record. Previous experience in superconducting devices, cryogenic measurements, or microwave characterization is necessary. The postdoctoral researcher will contribute to one or more of the following research directions: 1. Superconductor\u2013semiconductor hybrid quantum devices 2. Superconducting quantum circuits Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $65,000 to $70,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Physics, Electrical Engineering, Materials Science, or a closely related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Required qualifications include: Strong experimental background in superconductivity, quantum transport, semiconductor device physics, or cryogenic measurements Experience in at least one of the following: superconducting device fabrication, cryogenic transport measurements, or RF/microwave measurements Preferred qualifications include: Cleanroom nanofabrication experience (e-beam lithography, UHV thin-film deposition, dry/wet etching, materials and surface metrologies) Experience operating dilution refrigerators Expertise in superconducting microwave circuit design and measurement Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three representative scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to davood@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is open."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187320": {"title": "Research Engineer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH ENGINEER Nanofabrication Cleanroom New York University Tandon School of Engineering The NYU Nanofabrication Facility (NYU Nanofab) provides open-access semiconductor fabrication services to the research community at NYU and external institutions. NYU Nanofab has accumulated fabrication expertise in the broad area of quantum devices made of superconducting and semiconductor materials. To establish these activities into accessible documents and support the underlying infrastructure, NYU Nanofab is seeking to hire a part-time research engineer to assist with these ongoing activities. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States. The Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship among students and faculty, making a difference in the world. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be responsible for the following activities: Updating existing Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) documents for fabrication and characterization tools Validating updated SOPs in collaboration with cognizant Nanofab staff Updating the tool management and infrastructure tracking programs Supporting process documentation for superconducting and semiconductor quantum device fabrication workflows Applicants should possess a proven track record in nanofabrication, process documentation, and scientific writing. A publication record demonstrating hands-on research experience is necessary. Applicants should possess a proven track record in nanofabrication, process documentation, and scientific writing. A publication record demonstrating hands-on research experience is necessary. SALARY RANGE In accordance with the NYC Pay Transparency Act, the part time base salary for this role is $26.37 per hour, commensurate with the candidate\u2019s professional experience and qualifications. EXPECTED START DATE AND PERIOD OF EMPLOYMENT The anticipated start date is June 16, 2026.\u00a0 This appointment is for a duration of up to 3 months, contingent upon the availability of funds.The part-time schedule requires a commitment of approximately 10 hours per week. Candidates are expected to hold at least a MS in Materials Engineering, Electrical Engineering or a related discipline for at least 3 years of hands-on experience. Proof of eligibility to work in the United States is required. Interested applicants should provide the following documentation: \u25cf\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 A cover letter \u25cf\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 A current CV, including a comprehensive list of publications All materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio and sent to: davood @nyu.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187335": {"title": "Summer Research Scholar", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART\u00a0 TIME SUMMER RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Research in Advanced Integrated Systems and Circuits (RAISE) Lab at the Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) department at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Associate to work on communication and sensing integrated solutions across RF/mmWave frequency ranges. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The ECE department at Tandon, with a track record of pioneering advanced research in wireless technology, is dedicated to the advancement of future integrated solutions for a variety of communication and sensing applications. In particular, the RAISE lab\u2019s mission under the supervision of Prof. Hamed Rahmani is to research advanced electronics, applied Electromagnetic and integration technologies to further advance this vision. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for working independently on research projects related to the focus areas of the lab. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in RF/Analog circuit, microwave components, and physics, as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on transistor-level design, high-frequency electromagnetic simulation, and Electronic Design Automation (EDA) tools is desired. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $27 per hour, depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. The candidate would be expected to work 27 hours per week. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is: June 18 th , 2026. This position is expected to be for 2.5 months, ending on August 31 th , 2026. Candidates should have at least a MS in Electrical and Computer Engineering or a related field. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: hamedrahmani@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187878": {"title": "Research Scientist (Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCIENTIST Center for Cyber Security New York University Tandon School of Engineering The NYU Center for Cybersecurity (CCS) represents an interdisciplinary collaboration between the NYU School of Law, the Tandon School of Engineering, the Steinhardt School of Culture, Education, and Human Development, and various other university departments. Maintaining a physical presence in both New York City and Abu Dhabi, the Center operates alongside a global network of scholars and practitioners spanning Europe, Asia, the Middle East, and North America. The research and educational programs led by CCS are defining the frontiers of cybersecurity, cultivating resilience against emerging threats to security , safety , privacy , software , and hardware integrity, alongside the infrastructure of global modern life. Furthermore, CCS educational initiatives, spanning certificate and online degree programs, facilitate the efficient adoption of important new technologies across various organizations. These initiatives have also received formal recognition in Cyber Defense, Cyber Operations, and Cyber Research from the National Centers of Academic Excellence in Cybersecurity . NYU CCS is seeking to hire a part-time research scientist to assist with these ongoing activities. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States. The Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship among students and faculty, making a difference in the world. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be: Expected to process documentation and contribute to scientific writing for projects related to nanofabrication. Responsible for researching new techniques for privacy-preserving machine learning. Expected to contribute to advances in fully homomorphic encryption compiler infrastructure. Expected to contribute to new methods for developing neural network architectures that are more amenable to AI. Candidates are expected to hold at least a PhD in Engineering, Electrical Engineering or a related discipline for at least 3 years of hands-on computer engineering experience. Candidates will be required to provide proof of eligibility to work in the United States upon hire. They should also have a proven track record in nanofabrication and scientific writing.\u00a0 A publication record demonstrating hands-on research experience is necessary. SALARY RANGE In accordance with the NYC Pay Transparency Act, the part time base salary for this role is $34.34 per hour, commensurate with the candidate\u2019s professional experience and qualifications. EXPECTED START DATE AND PERIOD OF EMPLOYMENT The anticipated start date is July 1,\u00a0 2026. This appointment is for a duration of up to 3 months, contingent upon the availability of funds. The part-time schedule requires a commitment of approximately 25 hours per week. Interested applicants should provide the following documentation: \u25cf\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 A cover letter \u25cf\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 A current CV, including a comprehensive list of publications All materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio and sent to: bjr5@nyu.edu Review of applications will commence immediately and continue until the position is successfully filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188744": {"title": "Research Engineer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH ENGINEER Center for Cyber Security New York University Tandon School of Engineering The NYU Center for Cybersecurity (CCS) represents an interdisciplinary collaboration between the NYU School of Law, the Tandon School of Engineering, the Steinhardt School of Culture, Education, and Human Development, and various other university departments. Maintaining a physical presence in both New York City and Abu Dhabi, the Center operates alongside a global network of scholars and practitioners spanning Europe, Asia, the Middle East, and North America. The research and educational programs led by CCS are defining the frontiers of cybersecurity, cultivating resilience against emerging threats to security , safety , privacy , software , and hardware integrity, alongside the infrastructure of global modern life. Furthermore, CCS educational initiatives, spanning certificate and online degree programs, facilitate the efficient adoption of important new technologies across various organizations. These initiatives have also received formal recognition in Cyber Defense, Cyber Operations, and Cyber Research from the National Centers of Academic Excellence in Cybersecurity . NYU CCS is seeking to hire a part-time research scientist to assist with these ongoing activities. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States. The Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship among students and faculty, making a difference in the world. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be: Expected to process documentation and contribute to scientific writing for projects related to nanofabrication. Responsible for researching new techniques for privacy-preserving machine learning. Expected to contribute to advances in fully homomorphic encryption compiler infrastructure Expected to contribute to new methods for developing neural network architectures that are more amenable to AI. SALARY RANGE In accordance with the NYC Pay Transparency Act, the part time base salary for this role is $34.34 per hour, commensurate with the candidate\u2019s professional experience and qualifications. EXPECTED START DATE AND PERIOD OF EMPLOYMENT The anticipated start date is July 1,\u00a0 2026. This appointment is for a duration of up to 3 months, contingent upon the availability of funds. The part-time schedule requires a commitment of approximately 25 hours per week. Candidates are expected to hold at least a PhD in Engineering, Electrical Engineering or a related discipline for at least 3 years of hands-on computer engineering experience. Candidates will be required to provide proof of eligibility to work in the United States upon hire. They should also have a proven track record in nanofabrication and scientific writing.\u00a0 A publication record demonstrating hands-on research experience is necessary Interested applicants should provide the following documentation: \u25cf\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 A cover letter \u25cf\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 A current CV, including a comprehensive list of publications All materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio and sent to: bjr5@nyu.edu Review of applications will commence immediately and continue until the position is successfully filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195160": {"title": "Research Scholar ( Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR Human Movement Analysis with Inverse Reinforcement Learning New York University Tandon School of Engineering The NYU Tandon School of Engineering Electrical and Computer Engineering Department and NYU Center for Robotics and Embodied Intelligence are investigating advanced methods for autonomous robots, championing innovations that enhance quality of life and promote the development of just and equitable global societies. They are seeking to hire a part-time research scholar to assist with these ongoing activities in the field of inverse reinforcement learning.This position is for up to 4 months. The research will be conducted in the Machines in Motion Laboratory , which conducts research in movement planning and control for autonomous robotics, particularly within legged locomotion and manipulation. Its broader research interests lie at the nexus of automatic control, decision-making, and machine learning as applied to physical systems. Furthermore, it is dedicated to examining the societal impact of technology, championing innovations that enhance quality of life and promote the development of just and equitable global societies. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States. The Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship among students and faculty, making a difference in the world. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be responsible for the following activities: Conduct research on inverse reinforcement learning Conduct real robot experiments Analyze data and write publications Conduct data collection through robotic experiments Writing software to support experiments SALARY RANGE In accordance with the NYC Pay Transparency Act, the part time base salary for this role is $34.34 per hour, commensurate with the candidate\u2019s professional experience and qualifications. EXPECTED START DATE AND PERIOD OF EMPLOYMENT The anticipated start date is October 16, 2026. This appointment is for a duration of up to 6 months with possible extension or up to 1 year ,\u00a0 contingent upon the availability of funds. The part-time schedule requires a commitment of approximately 20 hours per week. Candidates are expected to hold at least a PhD in Engineering, Electrical Engineering or a related discipline. They should possess a proven track record in inverse reinforcement learning, including publications in top venues. Candidates will be required to provide proof of eligibility to work in the United States upon hire. Interested applicants should provide the following documentation: \u25cf\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 A cover letter \u25cf\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 A current CV, including a comprehensive list of publications All materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio. Review of applications will commence immediately and continue until the position is successfully filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195164": {"title": "Research Engineer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Research Engineer New York University Tandon School of Engineering Electrical and Computer Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a part-time Research Engineer to work with Ramesh Karri. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The NYU Tandon School of Engineering Electrical and Computer Engineering Department is\u00a0 highlighting the frontiers of stability and control of robust, sustainable, and smart power grids. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for: Using prototype research frameworks to model and analyze chiplet interconnects. Model full chiplet systems by implementing digital chip designs and interfacing them with the research framework. Working on publications with other team members Attending regular meetings with PI and providing feedback on the quality and effectiveness of the research frameworks Qualifications Candidates should have at least a Master's in Electrical Engineering or a related field and at least 3 years of hands-on computer engineering experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Required Experience A proven track record in control theory, process documentation, and scientific writing. A proven track record in nanofabrication, process documentation, and scientific writing. A publication record demonstrating hands-on research experience is necessary. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly base salary for this position is $26.37-$34.34 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment The anticipated start date is 11/01/26 with an targeted end date of 05/31/2027. This part-time position would require a work schedule of approximately 20 hours per week . Candidates should have at least a Master's in Electrical Engineering or a related field and at least 3 years of hands-on computer engineering experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: ece-announcements@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195339": {"title": "Research Intern", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  RESEARCH INTERN New York University Tandon School of Engineering Electrical and Computer Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a part-time Research Intern to work with Ramesh Karri. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The NYU Center for Cybersecurity serves as an interdisciplinary initiative focused on advancing impactful technological solutions and policy frameworks through innovative research, instruction, and scholarship. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for: Coordinating the day-to-day operations of the NRT program, including scheduling, trainee cohort activities, seminars, and workshops. Serving as the primary point of contact for NRT trainees, faculty participants, and university administrative staff. Tracking program milestones and expenditures, and assisting the PI with required reporting and documentation. Organizing trainee recruitment, onboarding, and program communications (including the program website and event announcements). Preparing meeting agendas and minutes and tracking action items across the program team. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly base salary for this position is $30.00-$45.00 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment The expected start date is November 1, 2026 with an intended end date of appointment to run through March 31, 2027. The part-time position would require a work schedule of approximately 20 hours per week . Candidates should have at least a Master's in Electrical and Computer Engineering or a related field and at least 3 years of hands-on computer engineering experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit a complete CV, cover letter, and the names and contact information of three references (all as pdf). All application materials should be submitted electronically at via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/161819": {"title": "Research Scholar (Part -time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Finance & Risk Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Finance and Risk Engineering Department at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking\u00a0 to hire a Part Time Research Scholar to work with Professor Renyuan Xu. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the\u00a0 Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Finance and Risk Engineering Department furnishes students with foundational knowledge in financial concepts to engineer the future of finance and transform financial theory into practice. Expectations Candidates will work with Dr. Xu\u2019s group in conducting research in the area of mathematical\u00a0 foundations of generative AI and applications in finance. The successful candidate will have a\u00a0 demonstrated background in stochastic analysis and machine learning theory as exemplified by a\u00a0 strong publication record. Previous experience in financial data analysis is desired but not\u00a0 required. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $40\u00a0 per hour depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is: February 1st. This position is expected to be for 3 months. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately 20 hours per week Candidates should have at least a BS or MS in applied mathematics or a related field. Candidates\u00a0 will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf cover letter \u25cf up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf research statement (up to 3 pages) \u25cf names and contact information for three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/165596": {"title": "Research Scholar", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Finance & Risk Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  FULL TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Finance and Risk Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full Time Scholar to work with Professor Nizar Touzi. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Department of Finance and Risk Engineering develops research in the most advanced technical methods for decision modeling in economics and finance, and the latest statistical and computational developments related to this area. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for project on model risk hedging. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in stochastic analysis, optimal transport and the related numerical approximation and implementation methods as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on computer programming in Python is desired. Salary: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $50,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have at least a MS in Mathematics or a related field and a strong research experience comparable to a PhD degree. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. research outputs and research statement All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: Nizar.touzi@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/166294": {"title": "Financial Engineering Faculty Fellow", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Finance & Risk Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY The Department of Finance and Risk Engineering at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering i nvites applications for a one year Faculty Fellow position to start on September 1, 2025. We are seeking candidates in all areas of mathematics applied to financial engineering and decision modeling within the context of economics and finance. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, stochastic analysis, optimization, control and game theory, statistics, and data science. Candidates should hold a Ph.D. in applied mathematics or a closely related field, with demonstrated knowledge of contemporary challenges in financial engineering and decision modeling. The position includes a light teaching load, offering valuable teaching experience within the department. Applicants are expected to show strong potential for excellence in both research and teaching. Please submit the following materials through the \"Apply Now\" button. Cover letter Current CV Research Statement Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Names and contact information for three references. (For searches that request letters from Interfolio directly at application time) Referees will be automatically contacted to upload confidential letters of reference in the Interfolio System. We will review applications beginning on April 20 and will continue until we fill the position. We encourage you to submit early. Should you have any questions please contact Professor Nizar Touzi, Nizar.touzi@nyu.edu . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual salary range is $70,000 - $110,000 for the Faculty Fellowship position. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/178065": {"title": "Research Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Finance & Risk Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  FULL TIME RESEARCH ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering Quantum Geometric Intelligence (QGI) Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Associate to work with Prof. Amine Mohamed Aboussalah. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. The Department of Finance and Risk Engineering (FRE) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is a leader in quantitative finance and applied mathematics education and research, offering innovative courses and fostering strong industry connections. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for developing a machine learning prototype model using C++. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in machine learning research as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on C++ is necessary. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the monthly base salary for this full-time position is $2,850-$3,850 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date is April 1, 2026 This position is expected to be for 4 months. A MS in computer science, engineering, or a related field 3 or more years of experience in machine learning. Experience on C++ required. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf Up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf Names and contact information for three references. \u25cf Applicants may upload up to two publications or technical reports relevant to their machine learning research experience. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: amine.aboussalah@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184398": {"title": "Research Scholar", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Finance & Risk Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering Quantum Geometric Intelligence (QGI) Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Scholar to work with Prof. Amine Mohamed Aboussalah.\u00a0 New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Department of Finance and Risk Engineering (FRE) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is a leader in quantitative finance and applied mathematics education and research, offering innovative courses and fostering strong industry connections. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for\u00a0developing and implementing machine learning and C++ prototype models. Responsibilities include coding and running machine learning experiments, analyzing and interpreting experimental results, and contributing to the design and evaluation of research ideas and methodologies. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in machine learning research, as evidenced by a strong publication record. \u00a0Previous experience with C++ is required. Experience with experimental research workflows, reproducible implementation, and scientific communication is highly desirable. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $27.77 per hour depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is 5/1/2026. This position is expected to be fo r 6 months with possibility of extension, depending on available funding and performance.\u00a0 This is a p art time appointment and\u00a0 would require a work schedule of approximately 27 hours per week. Candidates should have at least a MS in computer science, engineering, or a related field and 3 or more years of experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. applicants may upload up to two publications relevant to their machine learning research experience All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to amine.aboussalah@nyu.edu . Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195158": {"title": "Research Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Finance & Risk Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  FULL TIME RESEARCH ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Quantum Geometric Intelligence (QGI) Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Associate to work with Prof. Nizar Touzi. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Department of Finance and Risk Engineering (FRE) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is a leader in applied mathematics education and research, offering innovative courses and fostering strong industry connections. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for developing a machine learning prototype model using Python and C++. Working under the supervision of Prof. Nizar Touzi the Research Associate will perform the following duties: Review relevant scientific literature and identify machine learning methods and algorithms suitable for the project\u2019s research objectives. Translate research methods into functional Python and C++ code, including implementing model components, training procedures, and evaluation routines. Prepare and process datasets for model development, including data cleaning, transformation, and the creation of appropriate training, validation, and testing sets. Design and conduct computational experiments to train and validate the prototype, compare its performance against baseline methods, and analyze its accuracy, robustness, and computational efficiency. Test, debug, and optimize the implementation to improve reliability, execution speed, and memory usage, and refine the prototype based on experimental findings. Maintain organized, version-controlled source code and technical documentation describing the implementation, experimental procedures, results, and instructions for reproducing the work. Participate in regular research meetings, present progress and findings, identify technical challenges, and coordinate next steps. Contribute to technical reports, research manuscripts, presentations, and prototype demonstrations that communicate the project\u2019s methods and results. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in machine learning research as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience with Python and C++ is necessary. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $48,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is : December 1, 2026 This position is expected to be for 8 months . Candidates should have at least a MS in computer science, engineering, or a related field and 2 or more years of experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in machine learning research as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on C++ is necessary. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. Other additional information needed (e.g., research statement, publications) All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: nizar.touzi@nyu.edu . Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/155586": {"title": "Continuing Contract Faculty in Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY The Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications among the robotics community for two full-time, teaching-oriented, non-tenured, continuing contract faculty positions in Mechanical Engineering , at the rank of Industry Assistant Professor, with a start date as early as January 1, 2025. The primary responsibilities of the positions are classroom and laboratory teaching and project/thesis mentoring in Robotics and Embodied Artificial Intelligence (AI). The successful candidate should be an excellent teacher and have research experience in robotics and embodied AI fields. The candidate must be able to mentor projects/theses in support of our MS program in Robotics and undergraduate courses in our Robotics minor. Candidates should have a Ph.D. degree in engineering or science closely related to Robotics, such as mechanical engineering, electrical engineering, computer engineering, computer science, aerospace engineering, industrial engineering, civil engineering, or a related discipline. Candidates must demonstrate a solid understanding of the basics of one or multiple fundamental areas in robotics (perception & sensing, planning & control, learning, and design). Strong hands-on experience in robotics theories and software tools is a plus. Candidates must demonstrate clear potential for scholarship, leadership, and curricular innovation. All candidates must demonstrate or show potential for excellence in teaching and mentoring. Requirement The expectations for the Industry Assistant Professor primarily involve teaching robotics courses per semester. This position is not required to conduct research, although if the candidate wishes to, the teaching assignment can be arranged to allow the candidate to engage in funded research, and the candidate is welcome to leverage the Robotics and AI research networks and resources within the department, the school, and the whole university. The initial appointment may take 2-3 years, and the position is renewable afterward. Ideal candidates for the role could include postdocs and research fellows who are actively involved in robotic research in either academia or industry R&D labs. Application Materials We request applicants who are interested in this position to submit the following documents by November 15 to receive full consideration from the search committee. The application will be reviewed on a rolling basis. We encourage candidates to submit their applications as soon as possible. Cover Letter Current CV Transcripts in Graduate School especially for the courses related to robotics Teaching Statement (up to 2 pages) We are looking for descriptions of teaching experience, philosophy and vision A statement of your plans for incorporating inclusion, diversity, equity, and belonging into your teaching, research, and service (up to 2 pages). Research Statement (optional) Teaching Evaluation (required if you have prior teaching experience) Sample Teaching Materials (optional) We are looking for syllabi, slides, or video recordings from the candidate's past teaching experience Names and contact information for three references We will review applications beginning on October 21 and will continue until we fill the position. For any questions, please contact Katsuo Kurabayashi katsuo.k@nyu.edu About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. The Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering at NYU Tandon educates future engineers who develop the physical systems and devices that modern society demands, from automobiles to air conditioning, prosthetic limbs to autonomous machines, and rocket engines to satellites. Our faculty is breaking new ground in controls and robotic systems, computer-integrated mechanical and electromechanical systems, the modeling and simulation of complex systems, and fluid and thermal systems, among other areas. Recent research has led to breakthroughs in fast trajectory planning for legged robots and robot manipulators; accurate full-body proprioception for soft robotics; robust robot localization and navigation in urban environments; robots that aid in the education of children with autism; lightweight new protective materials with applications in national defense, sports medicine, and transportation; technological methods of leading wildlife away from danger areas; aspects of complex urban systems that impact how people live and work in dense urban environments, etc. NYU Tandon is committed to substantially increase the proportion of our faculty from historically underrepresented groups in STEM and we encourage candidates from such groups to apply. We aspire to create a climate where diversity and inclusion are not only appreciated but considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for a culturally diverse environment.\u00a0 We take pride in our high numbers of female students and students who are the first in their family to go to college. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual (9 month) base salary range for this position is $85,000-$125,000 for the Industry Assistant Professor rank . New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/162701": {"title": "Research  Technician ( Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH TECHNICIAN New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Mechatronics Laboratory at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Technician to work with Prof. Vikram Kapila. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Research in the Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering includes robotics and automation, additive manufacturing, nanotechnology and materials engineering, computational engineering, biomechanics, and healthcare applications. Expectations: Equipment testing Assessing the applicability of equipment for future research work Overseeing the transfer of equipment to a new location Supervising and mentoring at least one graduate student assistant Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is February 1, 2025. This position is expected to be for 2 months. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately 10 hours per week. Salary range: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate will be $30/hour depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Mechanical Engineering or related field At least 2 years of experience in mechatronics as demonstrated by a publication record Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter Up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications Name and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio to: Sarah Maslov (sarah.maslov@nyu.edu). Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/169824": {"title": "Research Scientist (part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Part-Time Reseaech Scientist NYU Tandon School of Engineering The Climate, Energy, and Risk Analytics Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Scientist to work with Prof. Yuki Miura. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. At the Climate, Energy, and Risk Analytics Lab (CERA), we drive innovation in understanding and managing climate risks and energy transitions using data-driven approaches with collaborations with stakeholders. Our cutting-edge research focuses on identifying, quantifying, and mitigating various climate risks including floods, heatwaves, and transition risks to build resilient communities, protect assets, and support institutions. The Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering\u2019s research is breaking new ground in robotics and automation, additive manufacturing, nanotechnology and materials engineering, computational engineering, biomechanics, and healthcare applications. The Climate, Energy, and Risk Analytics Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Scientist to work with Prof. Yuki Miura. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. At the Climate, Energy, and Risk Analytics Lab (CERA), we drive innovation in understanding and managing climate risks and energy transitions using data-driven approaches with collaborations with stakeholders. Our cutting-edge research focuses on identifying, quantifying, and mitigating various climate risks including floods, heatwaves, and transition risks to build resilient communities, protect assets, and support institutions. The Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering\u2019s research is breaking new ground in robotics and automation, additive manufacturing, nanotechnology and materials engineering, computational engineering, biomechanics, and healthcare applications. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for conducting research on identifying and measuring climate risks coupling with machine learning techniques and physics models. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in computer science, statistics, and engineering as exemplified by a strong publication record. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the salary for this position is $30/hour depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is July 1. This position is expected to be for 2 months. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately 27 hours per week. Applicants should submit the following information: \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: Sarah Maslov (sarah.maslov@nyu.edu)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/176977": {"title": "Professor in Robotics / Embodied AI (Open Rank)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for a full-time professor (open rank) in Robotics and Embodied AI, with a start date of September 1, 2026 . We seek creative, ambitious candidates who are passionate about both research and education in robotics and embodied AI. This faculty search is led by the Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering (MAE) , and appointments will be in MAE, with the possibility of a joint appointment in other departments depending on the candidate\u2019s interests and experience. The successful candidate will be affiliated with the NYU Center for Robotics and Embodied Intelligence (CREO), and we expect the candidate to contribute to the center's activities and help transform it into a leading hub for interdisciplinary research, education, and innovation Artificial Intelligence (AI) has revolutionized many fields, but remains largely confined to the virtual world inside computers. The next technological revolution will occur as AI steps into the physical world. When robots, vehicles, and cities interact intelligently with the environment, new capabilities and societal impacts follow. NYU CREO strives to pioneer this next era of Intelligent Machines. Our goal is to lead the embodied intelligence revolution at the convergence of robotics, AI, and systems engineering. Areas of Interest: We welcome excellent applicants across the broad spectrum of robotics or embodied AI, spanning theory, computation, hardware, and application domains (including but not limited to manufacturing, construction, infrastructure, mobility, healthcare, or urban environments). Interdisciplinary research that leverages NYU\u2019s strengths across engineering, computer science, and the broader university is strongly encouraged. Ph.D. in Robotics, Engineering, Computer Science, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Demonstrated potential for excellence in research (publications, scholarship, plans for external funding) and for high-quality teaching and mentoring. Evidence of the ability to develop an independent research program and to collaborate broadly across disciplines. Please submit the following materials through the \"Apply Now\" button . Cover letter describing research and teaching interests and career goals. Curriculum vitae. Research statement. Teaching statement including teaching experience and interests. Up to three representative publications (PDFs). Contact information for at least three references (letters may be requested at a later stage). Any additional documents (optional). Review of applications will begin December 15, 2025 and will continue until the position is filled. Anticipated start date is Fall 2026 (flexible). If appropriate: Should you have any questions please contact: Chen Feng at: cfeng@nyu.edu , Ludovic Righetti at : ludovic.righetti@nyu.edu About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by a historic commitment investment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. We aspire to create a climate where inclusion is not only appreciated but also considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for, and who possess an interest in, working in broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . The newly established NYU Center for Robotics and Embodied Intelligence (CREO) unites leading researchers from across NYU to advance cutting-edge research and education in robotics and embodied AI. CREO fosters cross-disciplinary collaboration across perception, learning, planning, hardware, systems, and real-world applications, with a strong emphasis on urban environments and infrastructure. The center features approximately 10,000 square feet of state-of-the-art experimental robotics facilities. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary (for 9-month) range for this position is $130,000 - $200,000 for the Assistant Professor rank, $150,000 - $240,000 for the Associate Professor rank and $175,000 - $275,000 for the Professor rank. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177167": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work with Professor Katepalli R. Sreenivasan. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The research group where the position resides is internationally known for its work in fluid dynamics, one of its recent activities being quantum computation of fluid dynamics (QCFD). Expectations Candidates will be responsible for the QCFD work. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in fluid dynamics, quantum physics, and machine learning as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on applied and computational mathematics is desired. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the initial annual base salary for this position is $65,000. Candidates should have a PhD in Engineering or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional Physics postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to krs3@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 The earliest expected start date is January 5, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180612": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Postdoctoral Associate to work with Professor Joo H. Kim. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. This individual hired in this position will be working with faculty at NYU Tandon and NYU Langone. Expectations The Postdoctoral Associate will conduct research in the area of shoulder arthroplasty (joint replacement) design, biomechanical analysis, and experiments. Research activities will include design and analysis of mechanisms for shoulder joint replacements; biomechanical modeling and its integration with shoulder joint replacement mechanisms; kinematic and dynamic analysis; development and validation of testing apparatus; and experimental testing of mechanism prototypes. The Postdoctoral Associate is expected to publish their work in high-impact journals and conferences and mentor graduate students. The position is immediately available for the duration of one year with a flexible start date and can be renewed based on performance and availability of funds. Salary: Salary range - In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the initial annual base salary for this position i s $64,063 Candidates should have a PhD in mechanical engineering or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Ideal candidates should have a strong background and/or experience with mechanisms and mechanical design, computational modeling (including finite element analysis and multibody system dynamics), kinematic and dynamic modeling and analysis of mechanical systems, biomechanics and biomechanical systems modeling, robotics, fabrication and testing of mechanisms, and applications of machine learning and AI. A strong record of publications and communication skills are also required. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one PDF, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to joo.h.kim@nyu.edu and jie.yao@nyulangone.org. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Earliest expected start date is March 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187917": {"title": "Research Scholar", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a full time Research Scholar . New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for: Python Programming: Write, test, and maintain Python scripts and modules to support robot control applications. Hands on robotics: Plan and conduct experiments with real robot systems, log data, track data, visualize data. ML Model Training: Use NYU\u2019s high performance computing (HPC) to train large models (4B parameters). ML Model Inference: Deploy trained models for real robot experiments. Robot Simulation: Train and deploy models within a simulated robot environment. Exceptional communication and time management skills. Ability to work both independently and within a team. Demonstrated proficiency in Python and ML model training is required. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the minimum salary for this position is $48,000 to $62,500 per year, depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification . Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is June 18th , 2026. This position is expected to be for 2.5 months through August 31, 2026. Candidates should have a Masters in Mechanical Engineering or a degree in a related STEM field. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in robotics, vision language action models, behavior cloning techniques, underwater robot systems, and autonomous systems, as exemplified by a strong publication record. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter, up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references, Other additional information needed (e.g., research statement, publications). All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188643": {"title": "Research Scholar ( Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Scholar to work with Prof Vikram Kapila. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for: Expand existing \u201czero-code\u201d framework based on large-language models (LLM), visual-language models (VLM), and a local perception pipeline Create, implement, and test uses cases for robotics teaching and learning based on LLM, VLM, and perception models Develop a tool based on mobile devices (tablets, smartphones, etc.) to simulate physical circuits with on-device sensors and Unity framework to create test modules for education Working knowledge of modern ML frameworks, development, and testing pipeline Exceptional communication and time management skills Ability to work both independently and within a team Demonstrated proficiency in C, C++, Python, and CUDA ML model training is required. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in large language models, vision language model, robotics, and various programming environments listed above. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is: July 1, 2026. This position is expected to be for Summer 2026 from July to August. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of 20 hours per week. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the salary for this position is $30 per hour. Candidates should have at least a Masters in Mechanical Engineering or a related field. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Application instructions Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. Other additional information needed (e.g., research statement, publications) All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194744": {"title": "Research Scientist ( Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  PART TIME RESEARCH SCIENTIST New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Tim e Research Scientist to work with Prof Katsuo Kurabayashi. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for the following: Validate the microfluidic leukemia chip model. Test CAR T cells on the microfluidic chip. Fabricate and assemble PDMS microfluidic devices for bone marrow-on-a-chip studies. Prepare and maintain endothelial, stromal, immune, and leukemia cell cultures. Load cells into fibrin hydrogels within the devices and maintain on-chip cultures. Validate the leukemia chip model by assessing cell viability, vascular network formation, and tissue organization. Evaluate CAR T-cell responses on the chip, including leukemia cell killing, T-cell activation, and migration. Perform immunofluorescence staining, confocal microscopy, and live-cell imaging. Measure cytokine secretion using ELISA and quantify cellular and tissue-level responses through image analysis. Maintain experimental records, analyze results, and prepare figures and research summaries for discussions with the supervisor. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the salary for this position is $30 per hour. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is October 16, 2026 ,for appointments for up to\u00a0 three to six months, based on available funding and job performance. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of 23 hours per week. Candidates should have at least a Masters in computer science, mechanical engineering, data science, statics, and applied mathematics or a related field and at least 2 years of experience in computer science. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. Other additional information needed (e.g., research statement, publications) All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/104653": {"title": "Part-Time Instructor, Department of Tandon Online", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Technology Based Learning Location: Brooklyn, New York The Department of Tandon Online at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for a part-time, non-tenured instructors, with a start date of September 1, 2022. We invite applicants to teach one or more graduate courses. In particular, we presently seek instructors for the courses listed below . Foundations of Computer Science (Discrete Math) Design & Analysis of Algorithms I Principles of Database Systems Introduction to Operating Systems Penetration Testing and Vulnerability Analysis Information Systems Security Engineering & Management Information Security & Privacy Network Security Computer Networking Applied Cryptography Machine Learning Digital Forensics Application Security Mobile Security Cloud Security Operational Technology Security Offensive Security You should be an excellent teacher. You should have a Master\u2019s or Ph.D. in Cybersecurity or a closely related discipline, demonstrated industry experience, and a record of teaching experience in these areas. You should submit electronically: Cover letter Current CV Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Teaching statement describing experience and teaching philosophy Names and contact information for at least two references. We will review applications starting immediately and will continue until we fill the position; we encourage you to submit early. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and\u00a0 NYU Tandon School of Engineering traces its roots back to 1854, building on an illustrious past as Brooklyn Poly and the NYU Polytechnic School of Engineering. Our mission is to excel in research, teaching, and entrepreneurship and inspire and educate engineers for the 21st century \u2014 backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion to recruit new faculty, fuel groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas, and expand the Downtown Brooklyn campus. NYU Tandon faculty are world renowned leaders in science and technology, with a strong commitment to research, innovation, and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. We lead and have ties to multidisciplinary centers in wireless technology, cybersecurity, urban informatics, data sciences, artificial intelligence, renewable energy, and health, among others. Our faculty and students are part of the high-tech start-up culture in New York City and in downtown Brooklyn, and we support three \u201cfuture lab\u201d business incubators that connect our students and faculty to today\u2019s innovation economy. We are deeply committed to teaching and learning, and with NYU's unrivaled global network of campuses, we promote a truly global engineering education. NYU Tandon is committed to substantially increase the proportion of our faculty from historically underrepresented groups in STEM and we encourage candidates from such groups to apply. We aspire to create a climate where diversity and inclusion are not only appreciated but considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for a culturally diverse environment.\u00a0 We take pride in our high numbers of female students and students who are the first in their family to go to college. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $149.25. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177165": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Technology Management and Innovation Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Center for Urban Science and Progress (CUSP) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work with Dr Graham Dove and Dr Takahiro Yabe developing and implementing protocols for understanding and mitigating community concerns around trust and transparency in smart cities through generating appropriate ideas for data generation and use. The project\u2019s focus is a new smart city development in Vietnam, and the project will require travel. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Center for Urban Science + Progress (CUSP) at NYU Tandon is an interdisciplinary center dedicated to the application of science, technology, engineering, math, and social sciences in service of urban communities across the globe. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for: helping to build and maintain a network of local collaborators in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam (e.g., researchers, practitioners, community-based organizations, and municipal officials); developing and implementing a framework for collaborative workshops and focus groups to identify site-specific concerns and translate principles from prior work in other locations and contexts; coordinating with faculty contacts in NYC to arrange knowledge sharing with Vietnamese project partners; conducting case study research on the design and implementation of the project; collaborating with a team of faculty and external partners, including visits and workshops in Ho Chi Minh City; and performing other research related tasks as required. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in: (1) digital civics and public interest technology research, as it relates to smart city technologies and open data platforms; (2) planning, conducting, and analyzing participatory workshops and co-design activities; and (3) community and civic engagement and working with local government and community-based organizations, especially in urban contexts; as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on cross-cultural collaborations, working across languages, geographies, and institutional settings, and in project managing multi-stakeholder research or policy projects with timelines and deliverables is desired. Vietnamese language skills are helpful but not required. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $62,500 - 65,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates must have a PhD in urban planning, human-computer interaction, design, information science, urban science or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to gd64@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: January 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192134": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Technology Management and Innovation Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Department of Technology Management and Innovation at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work at the intersection of technology, innovation, operations, and people. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Department of Technology Management and Innovation is an acknowledged pioneer and leader in offering courses and programs about these increasingly critical arenas. The department serves a diverse and broad range of professionals, and its faculty and students compose a vital and forward-thinking research and learning community. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for working on interdisciplinary projects to contribute to both methods and foundational knowledge in their core disciplines, as well as to novel data- and technology-driven applications. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in human-technology interaction, computational social science, operations research, and operations management as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience and/or interest in teaching and mentoring is desired. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $75,000 \u2013 $85,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Complex Systems, Social Computing, Urban Science, or related fields. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: \u25cf an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position (up to 2 pages), \u25cf copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, \u25cf names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: takahiroyabe@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled. Expected start date is: October \u00a01, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/100021": {"title": "Part-Time Instructor, IDM", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Technology, Culture and Society Location: Brooklyn, New York The Integrated Design & Media (IDM) Program in the Department of Technology, Culture and Society at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering accepts applications on a rolling basis for part-time, non-tenured (adjunct) instructors. We invite applicants to teach one or more graduate or undergraduate courses for our core and elective classes . Please refer to the NYU Bulletin for a list of our classes at the undergraduate and graduate levels . We welcome applicants with ideas for special topic courses to submit proposals in their cover letter. You should be an excellent teacher with a student-focused pedagogical style. Ony applicants with a graduate degree (Masters, PhD) or a college degree (BS, BA, BFA) and ten years of relevant professional experience will be considered. Applicants with a hybrid academic or professional background (e.g. art/design and engineering/science) are especially encouraged to apply. You must be authorized to work for any employer in the United States, NYU is not an E-verified employer . Compensation is in accordance with NYU\u2019s bargaining agreement with ACT-UAW Local 7902. Please submit the following materials electronically: A cover letter introducing yourself and identifying which course(s) you are applying to teach A current CV or resume Teaching evaluations (if available) Names and contact information for at least two references We review applications on a rolling basis. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and\u00a0 NYU Tandon School of Engineering traces its roots back to 1854, building on an illustrious past as Brooklyn Poly and the NYU Polytechnic School of Engineering. Our mission is to excel in research, teaching, and entrepreneurship and inspire and educate engineers for the 21st century \u2014 backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion to recruit new faculty, fuel groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas, and expand the Downtown Brooklyn campus. NYU Tandon faculty are world renowned leaders in science and technology, with a strong commitment to research, innovation, and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. We lead and have ties to multidisciplinary centers in wireless technology, cybersecurity, urban informatics, data sciences, artificial intelligence, renewable energy, and health, among others. Our faculty and students are part of the high-tech start-up culture in New York City and in downtown Brooklyn, and we support three \u201cfuture lab\u201d business incubators that connect our students and faculty to today\u2019s innovation economy. We are deeply committed to teaching and learning, and with NYU's unrivaled global network of campuses, we promote a truly global engineering education. NYU Tandon is committed to substantially increase the proportion of our faculty from historically underrepresented groups in STEM and we encourage candidates from such groups to apply. We aspire to create a climate where diversity and inclusion are not only appreciated but considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for a culturally diverse environment.\u00a0 We take pride in our high numbers of female students and students who are the first in their family to go to college. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $222.27. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194437": {"title": "Cinema Studies, Assistant Professor, Global Media Industries", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tisch School of the Arts: Cinema Studies Location: New York The Martin Scorsese Department of Cinema Studies at New York University\u2019s Tisch School of the Arts is accepting applications\u00a0 for a tenure-track assistant professor position in global media industries. The position begins Fall 2027. Full-time faculty teach our core courses as well as topical seminars and small lecture classes. The core includes both undergraduate and graduate versions of three courses: Film Theory, Film History, and Television: History and Culture. Undergraduates must also take Introduction to Cinema Studies; MA students also take Film Form/Film Sense. We offer American Cinema: Origins to 1960 and International Cinema: Origins to 1960 in fall semesters and American Cinema: 1960 to Present and International Cinema: 1960 to Present in spring semesters. Beyond this core, faculty teach a variety of courses of their own design. The department is part of the NYU Tisch School of the Arts and its new Institute for Critical Studies in the Arts, which is home to three departments: Cinema Studies, Performance Studies, and Art & Public Policy. The department\u2019s ongoing initiatives include the Asian Film & Media Initiative and its Reel China Biennial, as well as the Orphan Film Symposium and the Audiovisual Preservation Exchange (APEX). In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $105,000 - $125,000. This represents base pay only (for 9 months) and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, clinical compensation, or other items. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We value applicants whose research on material and conceptual infrastructures of moving images intersects with any of the following: platform studies; production studies; environmental media studies; South-South collaboration, exchange, and competition ; cinema and media of South and Southeast Asia and the Middle East; labor and political economy. We value the study of both contemporary and historical media. Successful candidates will be willing and able to teach courses in our undergraduate and graduate core curricula. Apply by October 23, 2026 . Review of applications will begin at the time of submission and continue up to the final deadline. Applicants are required to submit: a letter of application, a CV, a sample of your scholarly writing (preferably a publication), and the names and emails/phone numbers of three referees we may contact."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194434": {"title": "ITP/IMA, Assistant Professor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tisch School of the Arts: Interactive Telecommunications Program Location: New York Tisch's ITP/IMA (Interactive Telecommunications and Interactive Media Arts) department is seeking applications for individuals to join its faculty in a tenure track, Assistant Professor position beginning in Fall 2027. ITP/IMA encompasses three individual programs: ITP, a two-year graduate program, IMA, a four year undergraduate program, and IMA Low Residency, a one year low residency graduate program in partnership with NYU Shanghai. ITP/IMA is located in the Tisch School of the Arts and its mission is to explore the imaginative uses of emerging technologies \u2014 to make people's lives safer, more just, more beautiful, more meaningful and more fun. At ITP/IMA we believe that working with people of different disciplines, backgrounds and experiences is key to generating new ideas. Our department is pluralistic in its methodologies, from art, science, engineering, design and the humanities, to methodologies that have yet to be defined. We nurture skepticism of them all in the hopes of developing new ways of thinking. We are not looking particularly from any one of those disciplines but rather for someone who can help us craft an environment where students can benefit from all of them. Service The greatest project out of ITP/IMA is ITP/IMA. The creative application of new technology is ever changing and we seek an individual with experience in crafting environments, institutions, programs, systems, and culture that can help to continue to evolve ITP/IMA in the future. All faculty are expected to further the experiment of ITP/IMA. Faculty members should always be on the lookout for appropriate students, better facilities, activities, events and customs for conducting our mission and building the culture of the department. Service may include work with admissions, advisement, community development, classrooms, shops, labs, enrichment topics, showcases, guest visitors and professional opportunities. Faculty are expected to attend faculty and committee meetings to discuss these ideas and to do the legwork of executing them in coordination with the staff. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for an Assistant Professor position is $100,000 - $120,000. This pay range represents base pay only (for 9 months paid over 12 months) and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, clinical compensation, or other items. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Professional/Artistic Work/Research Traditional measures of individual achievements in artistic and professional work such as exhibits at prestigious institutions and publications with significant impact in popular or professional spheres are valued. However, interactive media is not just about those who use tools, but also about those who make them. Therefore, the production of technology, tools and environments that enable the achievement of others are also very highly valued. Professional impact may be measured by the reflection of work ultimately produced by others who use such tools, examples, frameworks, and tutorials. Additionally, work creating and guiding open source communities of creative practice is also highly valued. In all cases, professional work that crosses disciplines as well as work that helps disadvantaged or underrepresented people is particularly valued. Teaching and Mentorship Central to this role is a deep and sustained commitment to teaching and mentoring rising artists and practitioners. The successful candidate will be an exceptional educator who inspires students through intellectual generosity, rigorous critique, and a belief in the transformative power of interactive media and technology. They will guide students in the development of artistic voice, technical mastery, critical thinking, and collaborative practice. The faculty member will play a significant role in mentoring students as well as fostering an inclusive, challenging, and supportive learning environment that reflects NYU Tisch\u2019s global mission and values. We especially invite applicants whose work can help us expand and strengthen our offerings in design and storytelling in interactive media, including areas such as time-based media, XR, immersive experiences, the emerging applications (and complications) of AI/ML, visual design, interaction design, product design, and UI/UX. All qualified internal candidates are strongly encouraged to apply for this position. Candidates should provide: Cover Letter that specifies areas of expertise particularly relevant to the position; Curriculum Vitae; Contact information for three letters of reference accompanied by a bio or CV from each Referrer; and Work samples that best represent areas of expertise outlined in the Cover Letter. Samples may include recordings, videos, published materials, etc. The application deadline is November 1, 2026"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194818": {"title": "Drama, Assistant Professor/Faculty Fellow, Theatre Studies", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tisch School of the Arts: Undergraduate Drama Location: New York Assistant Professor Faculty Fellow in Theatre Studies The Department of Drama at NYU's Tisch School of the Arts invites applications for an Assistant Professor Faculty Fellow, to begin in September 2027. This is a one-year teaching and postdoctoral position with a course load of four courses per year. Renewal is possible for two subsequent years. The Department of Drama combines rigorous professional training with an expansive theatre studies curriculum that includes theatre history, dramatic literature, and performance theory. The curriculum also includes Honors Seminars, and minors in Applied Theatre and Dramaturgy. Responsibilities: The Fellow will teach one introductory, writing-intensive theatre studies course and three advanced undergraduate courses each year, and will serve on committees. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $85,000 - $105,000. This represents base pay only (for 9 months) and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, clinical compensation, or other items. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We seek candidates with a demonstrated record of excellence in teaching who completed the Ph.D. no earlier than 2022 and have it in hand at the time of appointment. We welcome candidates working across a range of areas in theatre and performance studies, and we are particularly interested in scholars whose research and teaching engage digital theatre and performance, emerging technologies, artificial intelligence, or the relationship between technology and performance. To apply: Please submit the following: Letter of application CV Three letters of recommendation Writing sample of no more than 6,000 words Applications should be addressed to Professor Alisa Zhulina, Chair of the Theatre Studies Search Committee, Department of Drama. Questions may be sent to Professor Zhulina at oiz203@nyu.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194707": {"title": "UGFTV, Assistant Professor, TV Studio Production", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tisch School of the Arts: Undergraduate Film and Television Location: New York NYU Tisch School of the Arts, Kanbar Institute of Film and Television, Undergraduate Division is searching for a full-time Assistant Professor (tenure-track) in Television Studio Production to begin in September 2027. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act , the annual base salary range for this position is $100,000 - $120,000. This pay range represents base pay only (for 9 months) and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, clinical compensation, or other items. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Salary, to be negotiated, includes an excellent benefits package. Candidates must have a significant, proven track record directing scripted television series. The ideal candidate will focus on teaching production and directing classes, but would also be able to teach other areas of the curriculum such as series writing. Evidence of teaching experience at the university level is preferred, as well as demonstrated experience in a creative/collaborative environment. We are actively seeking applicants who have a commitment to equity-forward teaching practices and who recognize and embrace the ongoing development of a community where everyone is respected and will be able to contribute their unique skills and perspectives . Duties include teaching 5 courses per year, mentoring undergraduate students, and participating in departmental and/or school-wide committees and events. Ongoing professional work is encouraged and expected. All qualified internal candidates are strongly encouraged to apply for this position. You will need to submit a cover letter stating your interest and expertise, a curriculum vitae and the names and contact information of three references that highlight both academic and industry experience. Candidates may be asked to submit a creative reel of professionally produced work and/or produced writing samples. APPLICATIONS ARE DUE NO LATER THAN 5PM ET on Wednesday, November 4. The committee will be reviewing applications on a rolling basis and encourages the submission of applications as early as possible"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193635": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate; Center for Mind, Ethics, and Policy", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Environmental Studies Location: New York, NY The Center for Mind, Ethics, and Policy (CMEP) at New York University invites applications for a full-time Postdoctoral Associate in relevant disciplines including philosophy, neuroscience, computer science, and economics. The position is intended for an early-career scholar who will contribute to the Center's research on digital minds and broader activities while also developing an independent research program within the Center's areas of focus. The Postdoctoral Associate will join an interdisciplinary community working on foundational research on the nature and intrinsic value of nonhuman minds, including both biological and digital minds (AI). Recent projects include : A report on taking AI welfare seriously; A report on studying AI welfare empirically; A perspective on evaluating animal consciousness; A paper about AI safety, AI welfare, and potential tensions between them; and A paper about social perceptions of AI consciousness. The position is designed to combine close collaboration with Center faculty and staff, meaningful opportunities to contribute to the development of the Center and its field, and substantial protected time for independently led research. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. The appointment will be for one year beginning Fall 2027, renewable annually for up to three years total, subject to performance and funding. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. If you are an international applicant, please review NYU\u2019s Office of Global Services (OGS) website . We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $65,000 - $75,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Mentorship and Research Environment The Postdoctoral Associate will be part of a thriving and growing research community that includes: an internal team of researchers and postdocs, a diverse set of junior and senior collaborators from both within and outside NYU, regular mentorship from our research manager, and high-level guidance from the Director. The Center aims to provide an environment in which postdoctoral researchers can both contribute meaningfully to collaborative projects and develop the ideas, publications, relationships, and research agenda needed for the next stage of their careers. Research plans and workload allocations will be discussed regularly to ensure that Center responsibilities do not systematically displace the Associate's protected independent research and professional-development time. Job Summary The Postdoctoral Associate's time will ordinarily be allocated approximately as follows. These percentages are intended as guidelines rather than rigid timesheet categories and may vary across the appointment by mutual agreement. 50% \u2014 Center-initiated and collaborative research The Postdoctoral Associate will contribute to research projects developed or prioritized by the Center. Depending on the candidate's expertise and the Center's needs, this work may include: Conducting original research, including academic papers, reports, and other publications, both individually and collaboratively; Assisting with grant proposals and the development of new research projects and partnerships Helping evaluate and recruit research collaborators; and Helping plan, host, and attend meetings among research teams and collaborators. The specific portfolio of collaborative projects will be developed in consultation with the Postdoctoral Associate and may evolve over the course of the appointment. 30% \u2014 Independently led research The Postdoctoral Associate will have substantial protected time to develop and lead original research of their own design. This work should fall within, or bear a clear relationship to, the Center's mission and research priorities regarding digital minds, while allowing the Associate to develop a distinctive scholarly agenda and establish an independent research profile. At the beginning of the appointment, and periodically thereafter, the Postdoctoral Associate will develop an independent research plan in consultation with their manager and the Center Director. Projects will be reviewed for their fit with the Center's mission, rigor, originality, feasibility, and responsible research practices. Within those parameters, the Postdoctoral Associate will retain substantial intellectual autonomy over the questions they pursue, the approaches they take, and the conclusions they reach. Consultation and feedback from Center faculty are intended to strengthen the work rather than predetermine its substantive conclusions. The Center may apply additional review and editorial standards to work published as a Center report, published on the Center website, or otherwise clearly associated with the Center. Independently authored academic work need not represent the views of the Center and can be published without this extra review. 10% \u2014 Center-led outreach and field-building activities The Postdoctoral Associate will also contribute to the broader intellectual and institutional work of the Center. Activities may include: Helping organize and host workshops, conferences, seminars, and other events; Participating in outreach to researchers, policymakers, industry actors, funders, the public, and other stakeholders; Reviewing funding proposals from external researchers; and Participating in other Center activities. 10% \u2014 Professional development The Postdoctoral Associate will have protected time for activities that support their longer-term development as an independent scholar. These may include: Attending and presenting at academic conferences and workshops; Developing new substantive or methodological expertise; Preparing grant and fellowship applications; Participating in mentoring and professional-development activities; Developing public-engagement or other relevant professional skills; and Preparing for future academic or research positions. Applicants should have completed, or expect to complete before the start date, a PhD or equivalent terminal degree in a field relevant to digital minds. Successful candidates will demonstrate: An excellent record of research appropriate to their career stage; Expertise relevant to nonhuman consciousness and welfare; The ability to conduct rigorous and original independent research; The ability to collaborate effectively across disciplines and perspectives; Strong written and oral communication skills; Interest in contributing to the intellectual and institutional development of a growing research center; and Broad alignment with the Center's mission and commitment to rigorous, open-minded, and responsible inquiry. Experience with any of the following is welcome but not required: Empirical methods for studying AI systems (such as model evaluations or interpretability); Consciousness science or welfare science; Moral philosophy or philosophy of mind; Policy engagement or public communication; and Interdisciplinary research collaboration. Applications should include: A curriculum vitae A one-page cover letter addressing why you are interested in and well-suited to this role A 1-3 page statement describing your research background and research plans A writing sample of no more than 10,000 words or 25 pages, such as a publication, dissertation chapter, or working paper Names and email addresses of three academic or professional references. Letters will be requested only for shortlisted candidates. Applications received by December 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. Please contact toni.sims@nyu.edu with questions related to the role."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195421": {"title": "Researcher - NYU Center for Mind, Ethics, and Policy", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Environmental Studies Location: New York, NY The Researcher will focus on the consciousness, sentience, moral status, or welfare of AI systems for the Center for Mind, Ethics, and Policy ( CMEP ). CMEP conducts and supports foundational research on the nature and intrinsic value of nonhuman minds, including biological and digital minds (AI). Recent projects include: The Moral Circle , a book by Director Jeff Sebo A report on taking AI welfare seriously; A perspective on evaluating animal consciousness; A paper about AI safety, AI welfare, and potential tensions between them; and A paper about social perceptions of AI consciousness. The Researcher will be responsible for maintaining the highest scholarly standards while working collaboratively with a range of stakeholders both within and outside of academia. Successful candidates will have a background in research, project management, or both. Responsibilities 1. Identifying opportunities for high-impact research 2. Engaging, when relevant, with external partners such as researchers or policymakers 3. Conducting or facilitating rigorous research and ensuring that the project meets the highest scholarly standards 4. Producing\u2014or facilitating the production of\u2014clear, concise, and actionable reports tailored to field leaders, policymakers, and funders 5. Participating in report launch events and coordinating with external stakeholders to increase each project\u2019s impact 6. Staying up to date on current research and field-level developments 7. Providing strategic input to ensure that our centers\u2019 research stays mission aligned Position expectations The start date is on or around January 4, 2027. This is a 12-month position with the possibility of extension. This is an onsite position. The successful candidate should anticipate regular meetings with CMEP Director Jeff Sebo and the research team. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $78,358.20. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Required Strong research, analytical, and communication skills A bachelor\u2019s degree Ability to write clearly and precisely Ability to edit documents thoughtfully for content and clarity Excellent time and project management skills, with demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities Ability to work independently and on a team Discretion, professionalism, and a commitment to ethical research practices Preferred Advanced degree in a related field, such as philosophy, neuroscience, or public policy Experience leading an interdisciplinary team or project Experience producing actionable research for policy makers, philanthropists, or other decisionmakers Commitment to producing socially impactful research For consideration, applicants need to submit the following via Interfolio: A curriculum vitae A cover letter addressing the following questions: Why are you interested in this role? What is your relevant experience in research or project management? Briefly describe the tools or systems you use to track projects, tasks, and deadlines. Three academic or professional references (contact information only) Please contact Audrey Becker (audrey.lynn.becker@nyu.edu) with questions."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/178637": {"title": "Part-time Research Assistant, Level  1 (Winter 2025) - Dr. Yaguang Zheng", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Rory Meyers College of Nursing Location: NYU Rory Meyers College of Nursing; NYU Langone Health CTSI This position primarily assists with clinical trial data collection and coordination. Project Title: Adaptive Dietary Intervention for Asian Americans with Type 2 Diabetes Position Details: Approximate Start and End Dates: January 2026 to August 31, 2028 Expected Work Hours: Part-time, Fixed-term Position with 10 - 20 hours per week. Summary of study: The overall objective of the study is to evaluate the feasibility of using CGM for dietary management for Asian Americans with type 2 diabetes. MAIN DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Coordinate, manage, and assist with document preparation and data collection efforts related to participant activities (e.g., recruitment, screening, communications, scheduling assessments, administering questionnaire surveys, guiding participants to the appropriate use of continuous glucose monitor [CGM], individual interviews, etc.). Assist participants to properly put on and take off the CGM, have expertise to ensure CGM use safety, have ability to assess adverse effect of wearing CGM, and have the knowledge and skills of downloading CGM data and report. Assist with data collection, e.g., using ASA 24 for nutrition assessment. Coordinate team meetings. Maintain confidentiality of participants and storage of confidential data. Performs other tasks as needed. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $30. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Required Skills: \u00b7 Experience and knowledge with informed consent procedures. \u00b7 Experience with clinical research and study operating procedures. \u00b7 Experience with using ASA 24 for nutrition assessment. \u00b7 Strong computer and technical skills, including MS Office software and statistical software programs (e.g., RedCap, SPSS, ATLAS.ti preferred). \u00b7 Ability to review and understand study protocols and other clinically related materials. \u00b7 Experience in collecting research data. \u00b7 Must be self-motivated, flexible, organized, detailed-oriented, have strong verbal and written communication skills, be able to work independently and on a team, and handle multiple priorities. \u00b7 Experience of taking care of persons with diabetes preferred. Experience of using CGM in clinical work is preferred. Required Education Bachelor\u2019s degree Required Experience Registered Nurse (RN) Preferred Qualifications Registered Nursing License in New York State Bilingual language skills in Chinese, Vietnamese, and/or English (verbal and written) Priority given to candidates who can speak, read, and write Cantonese, Mandarin, and Vietnamese Experience in diabetes management For consideration, please u pload a resume/CV & cover letter to Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183245": {"title": "Research Associate, Level 2 (Dr. Haeok Lee), Spring 2026", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Rory Meyers College of Nursing Location: New York, NY Project Title:\u00a0Asian Cohort for Alzheimer\u2019s Disease (ACAD) Project Details and Goal The overall goal of the ACAD study is to recruit\u00a05,081 Asian older adults\u00a0across the United States and Canada. The study will collect DNA and plasma biomarkers, genotypes, social determinants, and clinical diagnostic information. At the NYU site, the study will recruit approximately\u00a0800 Korean American older adults\u00a0aged 60 years and older, including both cognitively healthy individuals and those with cognitive impairment, from community settings in the Northeast United States. Position Summary The Research Associate Level 2 will be responsible for the standardized implementation of study protocols, including cognitive assessment batteries, blood sampling, biospecimen shipment, and data entry. The position requires handling all data collected in Korean and active participation in manuscript preparation.\u00a0This role supports recruitment and data collection among approximately 800 Korean American participants aged 60 years and older, utilizing validated and translated instruments, clinical/diagnostic protocols, DNA and plasma biomarker collection, and SNP genotyping. Duties and Responsibilities 1.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Demonstrate extensive experience working with Korean older adults in community settings, ensuring culturally appropriate engagement and high-quality data collection. 2.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Apply language, clinical, and cultural expertise essential for blood sampling and cognitive assessments at New Jersey and New York\u00a0data collection sites. 3.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Develop and maintain a surveillance registry of potential participants; schedule and coordinate data collection; monitor enrollment and retention. 4.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Organize community outreach activities and assist the PI in developing educational manuals in Korean and English. 5.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Demonstrate excellence in professionalism, work performance, and adherence to research ethics and confidentiality standards. Position Details Approximate Start and End Dates: This is a full-time, fixed-term, grant-funded position expected to run from end of May 2026 through June 20, 2027, subject to continuation and possible extension based on the availability of continued grant funding. Appointment Type: Full-time, fixed-term, grant-funded position. Expected Work Hours: 35 hours per week; The role follows a Monday\u2013Friday, 9:00 a.m.\u20135:00 p.m. schedule with some flexibility. Location: Hybrid. The position requires at least three days of in-person work per week, including field-based research activities and College-designated common in-person days. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $83,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Required Skills and Qualifications 1.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Education: BSN and MSN (earned in Korea or the United States) 2.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Licensure: Active RN licenses in New Jersey and New York 3.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Language: Bilingual in Korean and English; ability to work with Korean-language dementia health surveys 4.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Clinical Experience: Minimum of three years of blood sampling experience and shows confidence performing blood draws in community settings 5.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Experience interacting with high-level executives and diverse stakeholders 6.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Ability to work with confidential and sensitive information 7.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Experience with data collection, evaluation/reporting, and tracking project deliverables and timelines Preferred Qualifications Experience working with Korean or Korean American older adults in community-based settings \u00b7 Strong communication skills and ability to engage effectively with Korean American older adults \u00b7 Formal nursing education completed in both Korea and the United States \u00b7 Experience working with individuals with Alzheimer\u2019s disease or dementia and/or their family members \u00b7 Strong problem-solving skills, ability to follow through on tasks, and excellent organizational skills Upload a resume/CV & cover letter to Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184850": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate - 2027 (Dr. Han Chen)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Rory Meyers College of Nursing Location: New York, NY New York University Rory Meyers College of Nursing is committed to the development of the next generation of nurse scientists and is seeking to fill a P ostdoctoral Associate role for Summer 2026 . This position is initially awarded as a one\u2010year appointment, but renewable annually based on performance . The position involves postdoctoral work in developing efficient methods and tools for analyzing large-scale biomedical data with complex designs, and conducting big data analyses using advanced statistical models. Experience in the use of various datasets, e.g., TOPMed, UK Biobank, All of Us, and/or digital health data, is also desirable. The postdoctoral associate will engage in developing and implementing methods and tools, conducting and running analysis, reporting the study results, publishing, presenting, and grant-writing, in addition to advancing their own area of research. The postdoctoral associate will be under the mentorship and direction of Dr. Han Chen and receive professional development and academic networking opportunities. The position is available immediately and will be open until filled. Position Details: Approximate Start and End Dates: This is a full-time, fixed-term position expected to run for one -year, starting in January 2027, subject to continuation and possible extension based on the availability of continued grant funding and performance. Appointment Type: Full-time, fixed-term, grant-funded position for one-year. Expected Work Hours: 35 hours per week; The role follows a Monday\u2013Friday, 9:00 a.m.\u20135:00 p.m. schedule with some flexibility. Location: Hybrid. The position requires at least three days of in-person work per week, including field-based research activities and College-designated common in-person days. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500-$65,000 (based on years of relevant postgraduate experience, both at NYU and elsewhere). New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. 1. Dissertation completed in the last 3 years.\u00a0 If you have not yet completed your dissertation at the time of application, please provide verification in writing from your dissertation chair confirming you will complete it before Sep 1 st , 2026 . Please upload verification as an additional document in the document upload section. 2. Earned PhD or equivalent in statistics, biostatistics, epidemiology, bioinformatics, computational biology, nursing, or other health-related fields . 3. Qualified candidates must be eligible to work in the US at the time of hire and for the duration of this appointment. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. 4. As part of its selection process, our selection committee looks for evidence of a strong track record of scholarly achievement (publications , honors, awards), and grant writing experience, and promise of continuing achievement towards independence as a researcher. 1. Curriculum vitae and cover letter 2 . A research statement/proposal detailing the reasons for applying for the position and how the proposed research fits with the mentor Dr. Han Chen , including interests and goals and how their research relates to this position. (maximum 1 page single spaced) 3 . Copies of up to three relevant research scientific papers that are most representative of the candidate\u2019s work (preferred first-authored by candidate) 4 . Names and contact information of three references familiar with your scholarly and scientific work 5. A dissertation abstract and reprints of at least 3 publications The position is available immediately and will be open until filled. Applications will be reviewed as they are received. Eligible applicants will be invited for an interview. Incomplete submissions will not be accepted."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192041": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate - Digital Health and Implementation Science (Fall 2026 - Dr. Jasmine Travers Altizer)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Rory Meyers College of Nursing Location: New York, NY - Hybrid (NYU Rory Meyers College of Nursing) Position Summary: Join the NYU Meyers Research Team as a Post-Doctoral Associate and contribute to advancing innovative approaches to improve the health and well-being of older adults. This position offers the opportunity to lead and collaborate on projects focused on the development, implementation and evaluation of evidence-based tools in long-term care and community settings, the development and testing of digital health applications, and the analysis of survey and other research data. The role provides opportunities to conduct high-impact research, work within a multidisciplinary research team, and translate research into practical solutions that improve care delivery and support healthy aging. The Postdoctoral Associate will engage in a range of research projects and activities, primarily focused on: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0The Supporting Staffing Stability and Resident Wellbeing in Nursing Homes project aims to develop a toolkit to help nursing home administrators implement practical, evidence-based strategies that support staffing retention and resident wellbeing. The Post-Doctoral Associate will lead the development, implementation, and evaluation of the toolkit alongside a multidisciplinary advisory board of experts familiar with organizational change in long-term care settings. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0The Bridging Research in Aging Equity: Advancing Community-Based Tools and Digital Innovation for Aging Well project aims to develop, test, and implement a digital app that advances health equity and improves outcomes for older adults. The Post-Doctoral Associate will lead the design, development, and iterative refinement of a mobile/web-based health application. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0A NIH funded mixed-methods R01 study examining the effects of the Paycheck Protection Program (PPP) on staffing patterns and health outcomes among nursing home residents with Alzheimer's disease and related dementias (ADRD). The Post-Doctoral Associate will support and advise on survey data collection, analyses, and interpretation of findings. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a010% - Protected time to conduct and disseminate independent research. Mentorship will be provided to foster the scientist's professional growth and further prepare them for a successful research career. Responsibilities: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Support day-to-day project administration, including scheduling, IRB communications, and coordinating with collaborators and nursing homes. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Assist in managing project timelines, deliverables, and compliance with funding requirements. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Help facilitate team meetings, maintain study documentation, and ensure adherence to research protocols and project deadlines. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Supervise and mentor undergraduate and graduate research assistants \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Apply implementation frameworks like CFIR or RE-AIM to projects. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Partner and collaborate with advisory boards, nursing home leadership, and community groups to ensure successful implementation of digital tools and evidence-based strategies \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Lead the design, development, and iterative refinement of mobile and web-based health applications. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Travel, if needed, to nursing homes to pilot and evaluate nursing home toolkit implementation. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Manage and analyze research data using appropriate statistical software and methodologies. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Contribute to the writing of conference abstracts and manuscripts for peer-reviewed publications both through lead authorship and co-authorship. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Present research at scientific conferences and professional meetings. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Collaborate on the development of external grant proposals. Position Details: Approximate Start and End Dates: This is a full-time, fixed-term, grant-funded position expected to run from September 1, 2026 (or when position is filled) through August 31, 2027, based on the availability of continued grant funding and performance. Appointment Type: Full-time, fixed-term, grant-funded position. Expected Work Hours: 35 hours per week; The role follows a Monday\u2013Friday, 9:00 a.m.\u20135:00 p.m. schedule with some flexibility. Location: Hybrid. The position requires at least three days of in-person work per week, including field-based research activities and College-designated common in-person days. Contract and Salary: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $70,000 (based on years of relevant postgraduate experience and discipline). New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. This is a full-time fixed-term position for 1 year with potential for renewal based on performance and continued grant funding. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0A Ph.D. in public health or equivalent doctoral degree in nursing, behavioral science, computer science, software engineering, information science, or a related field by the start date. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Strong quantitative or implementation science background in research methodology, including study design, data collection, and statistical analysis \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Knowledge of human or user-centered design principles and usability testing. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Experience developing a digital tool or new application. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Experience analyzing quantitative data using statistical software packages such as R, STATA, SPSS or SAS. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Proficiency in modern programming languages (e.g., Swift, Kotlin, Dart, JavaScript/TypeScript, Python). \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0A background in aging, gerontology, older adults, long-term care, post-acute care, or community-based aging services is strongly preferred. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Excellent scientific writing and oral communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to prepare manuscripts and present research findings clearly and concisely. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0A track record of scholarly productivity, including publications in peer-reviewed journals and conference presentations. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Ability to work independently, take initiative, and manage multiple tasks effectively. Strong interpersonal and collaborative skills to work effectively within a research team. Interested applicants are invited to submit the following application materials: 1. Cover Letter: A detailed letter outlining your research interests, relevant qualifications, and specific skills that align with the requirements of this position. 2. Curriculum Vitae (CV): A comprehensive CV that includes your educational history, research experience, publications, presentations, software development experience (if applicable), and any other relevant professional activities. 3. Representative Writing Sample: A sample of your scholarly writing, such as a published peer-reviewed manuscript that reflects your research and writing abilities and is representative of the work described in this position. 4. Software portfolio: A portfolio showcasing relevant software or app development projects (e.g., GitHub repository, deployed applications, or other examples of technical work). 5. List of Three References Application Deadline: Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Contact Information: For any questions regarding this position, please feel free to contact Jasmine Travers at jt129@nyu.edu or Aasha Raval at ar6937@nyu.edu . Work Authorization: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time employment begins. Employment is contingent upon completion of the applicable employment eligibility verification requirements."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193019": {"title": "NYU Rory Meyers College of Nursing - Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Position_AY 2026-2027", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Rory Meyers College of Nursing Location: New York, NY The NYU Rory Meyers College of Nursing invites applications for tenure track or tenured positions, at the level of Assistant/Associate/Full Professor rank, with a start date of September 1, 2027. Tenured/tenure-track faculty contribute to the College\u2019s research and educational strengths with the vision and experience to advance our global mission. Tenured/tenure-track faculty at Meyers grow a rigorous and impactful\u00a0 extramurally funded program of research; teach across our academic programs; advocate for policies to advance health; and perform service activities at NYU, with the communities we serve, and as leaders within local, national, and global professional organizations. We seek faculty with emerging or established research programs grounded in: Innovative clinical applications for technology and artificial intelligence Precision health, biomarkers, omic-based approaches, or other translational and biobehavioral research Community participatory methods Palliative care research and practice And applied through one of the established NYU Meyers research pillars: Aging & Serious Illness: Research on aging, palliative and end-of-life care, dementia, and caregiving Health Services Research (HSR): Research on healthcare delivery, nursing workforce, quality and safety, and digital health innovation. Maternal, Reproductive, and Child Health: Research to improve the health and well-being of women, children, adolescents, and families across the life course. Population Precision Health & Chronic Conditions: Research that integrates biomolecular, behavioral, symptom, and social science to advance precision health, chronic condition research, and chronic disease prevention About Us Since its founding in 1831, NYU has been an innovator in higher education, reaching out to an emerging middle class, embracing an urban identity and professional focus, and promoting a global vision that informs its 20 schools and colleges. Today, that trailblazing spirit makes NYU one of the most prominent and respected research universities in the world. NYU students come from nearly every state and 133 countries, and the university draws upon the diverse backgrounds of our faculty, staff, and students, ensuring its scholarship and teaching benefit from a wide range of perspectives. NYU takes seriously its role as an engine of social mobility, and stands out among the top US universities in its representation of low-income and first-generation students within its community. The largest private research university in the US, NYU provides a rigorous, demanding education to more than 65,000 students and undertakes $1.27 billion in research annually. Thriving beyond borders and across academic disciplines, NYU has emerged as one of the most networked and extensive worldwide platforms for learning, teaching, researching, building knowledge, and inventing new ways to meet humanity\u2019s challenges. Rory Meyers College of Nursing At the NYU Rory Meyers College of Nursing, we are committed to creating an inclusive community, bringing a diversity of perspectives to our work, promoting equity of opportunity, and engaging in scholarship and education to build a more just society . We generate knowledge through research in nursing, health, and interdisciplinary science; we educate leaders in nursing to advance healthcare locally and globally; we provide innovative and exemplary healthcare; and we shape the future of nursing through leadership in healthcare policy. Learn more about NYU Meyers 2020-2025 Strategic Plan. Our Vision is to be a global leader in advancing health for all people where they live, play, learn, and work. For more information about New York University Rory Meyers College of Nursing, please visit our website: https://nursing.nyu.edu . The Meyers Biological Laboratory, a Biosafety Level 2 facility, processes human biospecimens (blood, saliva) and offers DNA and RNA extraction, genotyping using PCR and qPCR techniques, and secure long-term sample storage (-20\u00b0C and -80\u00b0C). The laboratory coordinates specimen transportation and facilitates collaborations with NYU core facilities for advanced molecular analyses. In compliance with NYC\u2019s\u00a0Pay\u00a0Transparency\u00a0Act, the annual base\u00a0salary\u00a0range for this position is: Assistant: $107,228.00 - $167,228.00 Associate: $128,000.00 - $198,000.00 Professor: $139,735.00 - $229,735.00 New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity,\u00a0as well as\u00a0market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Requirements PhD or equivalent research-focused doctoral degree Assistant Professor Rank Postdoctoral fellowship training or equivalent research or policy experience Associate or Full Professor Rank Active program of extramurally funded research Demonstrated history of strong interprofessional and/or transdisciplinary collaboration Demonstrated evidence of successful mentoring of early career faculty, doctoral students, and post-doctoral fellows Preferred University teaching experience Track-record of successful extramural funding (Assistant Professor) Advanced Practice Registered Nursing license (NP, CNM, CNS, CRNA) Clinical expertise in palliative care (1 position) Applicants should submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae via Interfolio.\u00a0 Finalists will be asked to provide references.\u00a0 If you have any questions email: nursing.hr@nyu.edu .\u00a0 All application materials and correspondence will be kept con\ufb01dential. For those applying at the assistant professor rank, submit your application by September 30, 2026 for best consideration. Application Deadline: 11:59 p.m. ET on November 20, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/179150": {"title": "SMART IT Coordinator", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work Location: New York, NY Position Title: SMART IT Coordinator Schedule: Part-Time ( Hours: 10\u201315 hrs/week during development; 5\u201310 hrs/week post-launch (immediate start - August 2026). ) Timeline: 8 Months (with possible extension) Location: New York, NY Project Overview The Silver Mental Health & Support Resource Network (SMART) is launching a digital platform to connect NYU Silver students, alumni, and licensed professionals. Development is active and aligns with NYU enterprise architecture. Additional part-time IT capacity is required to ensure continuity, institutional compliance, technical alignment, and vendor oversight. The IT Coordinator will serve as the technical liaison between SMART leadership, NYU IT stakeholders, and the external vendor, ensuring smooth platform development, deployment, and post-launch operations.\u00a0Works with Silver leadership and project team for SMART Primary Responsibilities 1. Requirements & Technical Alignment Validate and clarify technical and functional requirements Ensure alignment with NYU architecture, identity, security, and data standard Confirm appropriate role-based access, security design, and data flows Participate in design sessions and provide technical review 2. Vendor Management & Liaison Work Serve as main technical Point of Contact for vendor Respond to and resolve vendor technical questions Ensure timely delivery of product milestones and issue resolution 3. Development & Testing Support testing and QA cycles Participate in technical sign-off checkpoints Identify integration and interoperability risks Review data migration plans and validate data models 4. Operational Support Post-Launch Serve as escalation point for platform support Manage change requests and update cycles Monitor vendor SLA performance and review service metrics Support future roadmap planning and enhancements 5. Knowledge Transfer & Documentation Ensure vendor provides technical documentation Coordinate knowledge hand-off to business and IT stakeholders Bachelor\u2019s degree with 5 years of relevant experience Strong knowledge of NYU systems, governance, and security policies Experience working with enterprise vendors and web/app platforms Familiarity with CRM workflows, identity/authentication, and data security This position is classified under the professional research staff category at NYU and does not qualify for visa sponsorship including STEM OPT extension. Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States for the duration of employment. Apply via Interfolio In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly\u00a0 rate for this position is $45-$65 . New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194200": {"title": "Research Assistant (Part-Time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work Location: Los Angeles, CA Position: Research Assistant (Part-Time) Schedule: In-Person - 20-27 hours a week Location: Los Angeles, CA Start Date: October 26, 2026 Principal Investigators: Dr. Rohini Pahwa and Dr. Erin Kelly Study title: Social Networks Engage (SNEngage): A longitudinal examination of factors predicting the social networks and mental health services of individuals with serious mental illnesses. Duties and Responsibilities \u25cf Collecting Quantitative survey data \u25cf Conduct social network, social cognition and life history interviews \u25cf Enter data into various platforms including RedCap, excel, and box \u25cf Attend regular check-in and team meetings \u25cf Perform other research related activities as required This position is classified under the professional research staff category at NYU and does not qualify for visa sponsorship including STEM OPT extension. Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States for the duration of employment Required Education, Experience, and Skills Masters in social work, psychology, public health, and/or other allied social/behavioral science 1-2 years experience working on quantitative research studies, including experience working with large datasets and entering data in RedCap/Quantrics Be able to drive to interview sites in Los Angeles to conduct in-person interviews Interest in working with individuals with serious mental illnesses Excellent data collection, data entry and data management skills High level of attention to detail, flexibility, ability to work on a team Commitment to social equity and services to marginalized and vulnerable populations Performance evaluation procedure The team member will meet with the Principal Investigator and other team members, as needed, on a regular basis to review completed tasks and plan next activities. During these contacts the student will discuss professional and academic development goals. Apply Via Interfolio In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $20-$30 . New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195214": {"title": "Research Coordinator", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work Location: Los Angeles, CA Position: Research Coordinator Schedule: Full Time Location: Los Angeles, CA Dates: October 26, 2026 - October 25, 2027 Principal Investigators: Dr. Rohini Pahwa and Dr. Erin Kelly Project Title: Social Networks Engage (SNEngage): A longitudinal examination of factors predicting the social networks and mental health services of individuals with serious mental illnesses Duties and Responsibilities Coordinate a 5-year longitudinal research study Implement recruitment plans Screen participants for eligibility Obtain participant consent and schedule assessments Oversee participant compensation, reconcile petty cash and participant compensation logs Refine and implement tracking protocols to maintain high follow-up and retention rates Conduct assessments with participants as needed Perform other related activities as required Required Education, Experience, and Skills Master\u2019s degree or higher in social work, public health, psychology, nursing, or other social/behavioral science disciplines Experience with research and participant tracking Excellent organizational skills Access to a care and willing to drive within Los Angeles for project activities Must have a high level of attention to detail, flexibility, ability to work on a team, and punctuality Preferred Education, Experience, and Skills Bilingual in English and Spanish Familiarity with institutional review boards, HIPAA regulations, and clinical research standards. Prior experience working with research protocols Experience with working with adults with serious mental illnesses Experience with quantitative methods Experience managing a team and/or supervising staff Apply Via Interfolio In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the salary range for this position ($70,000 - $75,000) . New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193612": {"title": "Program Manager / Associate Research Scholar", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the Provost: Marron Institute Location: Brooklyn, NY The Higher Education Design Lab invites applications for a Program Manager / Associate Research Scholar at the NYU Marron Institute starting in November 2026. The Program Manager / Associate Research Scholar position is focused on research evaluation, project management, stakeholder engagement, and communications. About the Higher Education Design Lab: Launched in January 2026, the Higher Education Design Lab (HEDL) is a landmark research partnership between New York University and the State University of New York to evaluate and accelerate innovations in higher education that shape student outcomes. HEDL is a research and evaluation platform focused on measurement, evidence, and learning that is grounded in self-reflection and open inquiry, designed to inform decisions, and build towards long-term sector-wide learning.\u00a0 The HEDL is committed to evaluating what works \u2013 for whom, in what context, and why \u2013 and translating evidence into actionable insight for institutional leaders with the aim of strengthening student readiness for leadership in a rapidly changing world. The Program Manager / Associate Research Scholar can expect to focus on the following: Evaluation, Research, and Data Management Conduct and continually refresh relevant environmental scans and literature reviews, synthesize research, and summarize learning. Draft, update and support implementation of evaluation frameworks for pre-evaluation activities, evaluation design and execution. Interface with evaluation pilots, advisors, and HEDL leadership regarding quantitative and qualitative data collection and analysis. Manage and analyze large data sets in service of evaluation pilots. Project Management and Stakeholder Engagement Assist with facilitating communication and coordination among HEDL leaders and interest holders including relevant faculty, institutes, and external partners. Help develop, manage, and participate in meetings, peer-to-peer learning engagements, and special convenings, both virtual and in-person. Develop internal and external facing project deliverables including briefs, progress reports, strategic executive memos, and presentations. Collaboration, Communication, and Development Provide project planning and project management support. Serve as a point of contact across teams and stakeholders, supporting efficient internal and external communications. Support development through grant writing and creation of collateral materials.. Minimum Required Qualifications: Bachelor\u2019s degree and 2+ years work experience in education, applied statistics, social sciences or a related field. Project and performance management experience overseeing complex projects. Experience monitoring and evaluating intervention programs, especially related to education. Experience managing and analyzing large datasets, and partnering to merge quantitative with qualitative findings. Experience using programming (e.g., R) and data visualization tools (e.g., Tableau) to transform complex data into navigable dashboards, research briefs, decks and reports. Demonstrated ability to be flexible and responsive to change and to problem solve. Highly organized and detail oriented while not losing sight of the big picture. Able to thrive in a fast-paced work environment. Curiosity and willingness to learn new skills. Preferred Qualifications Advanced degree and/or additional experience working in related fields. Interest in the role higher education plays in shaping future leaders and communities. Familiarity with cloud-based productivity tools like Google Docs and Google Drive. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the compensation for this position is $80,000- $90,000 USD. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. This position will be based in NY, NY. To apply, please submit the following: CV and cover letter Contact information for three (3) professional references To apply, please submit the following: CV and cover letter Contact information for three (3) professional references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185068": {"title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Tax Law in the Graduate Tax Program", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law Location: New York, NY Position: Visiting Assistant Professor of Tax Law in the Graduate Tax Program.\u00a0 One-year term appointment with possibility of renewal for a second year. \u00a0The position is not a tenure-track appointment. Compensation and Benefits: Tax VAPs receive an annual salary ( $65,000 to $75,000 ) and benefits, including health insurance. Tax VAPs may also apply for subsidized faculty housing.\u00a0 Each Tax VAP is provided with a faculty office, administrative support, and a research budget. Requirements: A J.D. degree, a strong record of academic achievement, and an interest in pursuing an academic career.\u00a0 Tax VAPs will be selected based on their scholarly potential and their teaching ability. Application Materials: Curriculum vitae; Copies of law school transcripts (JD and, if applicable, LLM); brief statement of research interests; list of 3 references. The position will remain open until filled, but the selection committee will begin consideration of candidates based on applications received by November 1, 2026. Requirements: A J.D. degree, a strong record of academic achievement, and an interest in pursuing an academic career.\u00a0\u00a0 Tax VAPs will be selected based on their scholarly potential and their teaching ability. Application Materials: Curriculum vitae; Copies of law school transcripts (JD and, if applicable, LLM); brief statement of research interests; list of 3 references. Applications are accepted exclusively through Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189573": {"title": "Acting Assistant Professor of Lawyering", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law Location: New York LAWYERING PROGRAM FACULTY, New York University School of Law, Academic Year 2027-2028. Seeking applicants for several full-time, non-tenure-track Acting Assistant Professor positions to teach a two-semester course. The Lawyering Program provides support for faculty scholarship and preparation for entering the law teaching market as well as opportunities for reflection on practice. The position offers an excellent opportunity to work with students in a cutting-edge simulation-based curriculum. Lawyering introduces first-year law students to legal analysis, fact development, legal research, legal writing, oral advocacy, and client counseling in litigation and transactional settings. Most Lawyering professors transition into tenure-track clinical or doctrinal teaching positions. Some choose to transition into new areas in the legal profession or other positions in academia. Qualifications include a JD degree, excellent academic record, bar admission, significant practice experience (generally three years or two years plus a clerkship), and demonstrated ability in the capacities the course is designed to develop. Starting salary is $75,000. The\u00a0appointment is for one year (June 1, 2027-May 31, 2028) with the possibility of up to two subsequent one-year renewals. In compliance with New York City's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $75,000 - $75,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. All applications must include resume, law school transcript (an unofficial copy is fine), one writing sample, three references (letters are preferred, but names and current e-mail and/or telephone contact information are acceptable), and cover letter. Applications are accepted exclusively through Interfolio. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Candidates are strongly encouraged to apply before October 1, 2026; however, applications will be accepted until all positions are filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194175": {"title": "Fellow", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law Location: New York Trust in American institutions\u2014government, courts, and even civil society\u2014has steadily eroded and now stands at historic lows. In October 2025, Vanita Gupta launched the Center for Law and Public Trust at NYU School of Law to confront this crisis in confidence and reimagine US institutions so they are more effective and accountable to the American people, more resilient to corruption and abuse, and therefore more deserving of public trust. The Center works to rebuild trust and advance effectiveness in government and other institutions by: developing issue briefs, roadmaps, and concrete blueprints for institutional reform and reconstruction; designing structures, processes, and policies that are more responsive to community needs, efficient, resilient, and accountable to the public; and training the next generation of lawyers to lead in public service, armed with the skills to deploy the levers of government and leadership, advance justice, and deliver results for the American people. Position Summary: The Fellow will work closely with the Center\u2019s Director, Deputy Director, Senior Fellows, and Distinguished Scholars in Residence to contribute to rigorous, high-impact legal work in the public interest. For this particular position, the Center is seeking someone who will work on our accountability and anti-corruption portfolio. The Center is therefore particularly interested in candidates with a background in these areas. The Fellow will perform a wide range of research, writing, and project management tasks, including, but not limited to: Conducting research on matters relevant to the Center\u2019s projects and initiatives Supporting and coordinating outreach, meetings and convenings with subject matter experts and partner organizations Generating scholarship around the Center\u2019s areas of expertise Writing all types of materials, from internal research memos and talking points to public-facing reports Building tools and templates, analyzing and synthesizing inputs and information, and helping to create and refine key deliverables for Center projects. Managing lines of effort and providing logistical support to various projects to ensure milestones are met and deliverables are completed on time. Advising Center leadership, staff, and fellows and acting as a thought partner based on the scholar\u2019s expertise and experience. As needed, supervising student researchers to produce deliverables and complete tasks, ensuring quality and timeliness. Representing the Center at coalition meetings, conferences, convenings, and other public events, as appropriate Building and maintaining strong relationships with senior practitioners, academics, experts, and counterparts across the University and at partner organizations. Salary and Benefits In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary is $150,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. You will be an employee of New York University, with access to an excellent, comprehensive benefits package including generous paid time off and retirement contributions. The Center has physical office space at NYU School of Law in New York, but also offers hybrid or remote work arrangements from within the US. Required Qualifications: JD 5 - 10 years post-graduate work experience Prior experience working on accountability is a strong plus but not required. This could include experience as a prosecutor, investigator, on an oversight committee, at an organization that works to promote accountability, or as a participant or member of a government or community-led initiative or commission focused on truth, transparency or accountability for prior harms. Familiarity or experience with issues relating to corruption, public integrity, or ethics is a strong plus but not required. Required Skills: Excellent research and writing skills, including the ability to express precise, complex information in a way that is clear and compelling A commitment to the mission of the Center Excellent interpersonal skills, including a demonstrated ability to work well with different types of individuals and teams to achieve results Experience working successfully in coalition or collaborative spaces with partners Strong project management skills and a proven track record of executing complex tasks in a timely fashion Highly self-motivated and able to take initiative Please submit a cover letter, resume, writing sample, and three references on Interfolio. Applications are rolling, with a final submission deadline at the end of the day on October 19th, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183108": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor | Biostatistician", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry Location: New York, NY NYU College of Dentistry Center for Oral Health Policy & Management's Office of Data Management invites applications for a faculty position in biostatistics at the clinical assistant professor level. The individual will develop and lead an extramurally funded research program in the theory, methods, and/or applications of statistics in health-related fields. Additionally, the incumbent will develop collaborations with Translational Research Center, department of Molecular Pathobiology, department of Epidemiology & Health Promotion and/or clinician scientists and enhance these projects using innovative designs and methods. The faculty member will teach graduate level courses and advise master\u2019s and doctoral students.\u00a0 This is an annually-renewing appointment. Responsibilities include conducting research, teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses, participating in university, college and departmental governance, and professional and public service. A majority of effort is expected to derive from collaborating on research projects and teaching courses. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $120K - $180K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree Experience in developing and implementing novel statistical methods, experience collaborating with health scientists, and a passion for teaching and mentoring. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and scholarly activity appropriate to rank.\u00a0 Strong communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively in an interdisciplinary academic environment. Candidates should apply by submitting a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, a statement of current and future research interests, and contact information for three professional references to Dr. Richard Valachovic, Search Committee Chair via Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183198": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor | Biostatistician", "description": "Posted: Mar 19, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry Location: New York, NY NYU College of Dentistry Center for Oral Health Policy & Management's Office of Data Management invites applications for a faculty position in biostatistics at the clinical assistant professor level. The individual will develop and lead an extramurally funded research program in the theory, methods, and/or applications of statistics in health-related fields. Additionally, the incumbent will develop collaborations with Translational Research Center, department of Molecular Pathobiology, department of Epidemiology & Health Promotion and/or clinician scientists and enhance these projects using innovative designs and methods. The faculty member will teach graduate level courses and advise master\u2019s and doctoral students.\u00a0 This is an annually-renewing appointment. Responsibilities include conducting research, teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses, participating in university, college and departmental governance, and professional and public service. A majority of effort is expected to derive from collaborating on research projects and teaching courses. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $120K - $180K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree Experience in developing and implementing novel statistical methods, experience collaborating with health scientists, and a passion for teaching and mentoring. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and scholarly activity appropriate to rank.\u00a0 Strong communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively in an interdisciplinary academic environment. Candidates should apply by submitting a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, a statement of current and future research interests, and contact information for three professional references to Dr. Richard Valachovic, Search Committee Chair via Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195512": {"title": "Clinical Instructor | Dentists | Clinical Faculty | Brooklyn", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry Location: Brooklyn, NY NYU College of Dentistry is seeking applications for a full-time Clinical Instructor to join our modern, state-of-the-art dental facility in Downtown Brooklyn. The successful candidate will be responsible for providing high-quality clinical instruction and direct patient care while mentoring dental students in a supportive, hands-on learning environment. Key responsibilities include supervising students in clinical settings, delivering comprehensive general dental care, and contributing to curriculum development and student assessment. This position offers the opportunity to be part of a dynamic team committed to clinical excellence and educational innovation. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $100K - $150K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Candidates must hold a D.D.S. or D.M.D. degree from an accredited institution and possess current licensure in New York State. Prior experience in general dentistry practice is required, along with demonstrated clinical proficiency in operative dentistry, endodontics, fixed and removable prosthodontics, and periodontics. Preferred qualifications include prior academic or clinical teaching experience, additional proficiency in oral surgery and esthetic dentistry, and familiarity with digital dentistry tools such as CAD/CAM systems and intraoral scanners. Candidates must be sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. Academic rank will be commensurate with experience. NYU offers competitive compensation, including excellent benefits and incentives based on growth and production. Salary and academic rank are commensurate with credentials and experience. Applicants should submit a letter of interest and curriculum vitae to Dr. Richard Valachovic by clicking the \"Apply Now\" button."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190102": {"title": "POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: School of Engineering Academic Deans Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering Human Factors & Urban Ergonomics Lab (HumanFuel) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work with Dr. Linda Boyle. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Human Factors and Urban Ergonomics Lab (Human FUEL) is dedicated to designing systems centered on the end user.\u00a0 Our research focuses on understanding and enhancing human performance and behavior as people interact with complex systems, with the ultimate goal of improving operator safety, productivity, and comfort. Responsibilities The successful candidate is expected to be responsible for the following: Conduct literature reviews and synthesize findings, Design and conduct experiments on driver/pedestrian/user behavior, Collect and analyze human performance data (reaction times, workload, situational awareness, eye tracking, etc.), Develop and validate computational models of human behavior in driving scenarios, Collaborate on peer-reviewed journal articles and conference papers, Contribute to grant proposals and progress reports, Supervise or co-mentor graduate and undergraduate students, Coordinate with external sponsors as needed, Manage IRB protocols and ensure ethical research compliance, Maintain research equipment (driving simulators, VR headsets, eyetracking glasses, motion capture inside out/outside in devices, heart rate sensors), Contribute to lab operations. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $75,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Engineering, Cognitive Psychology, or a related field. Additional Qualifications include: Required Record of peer-reviewed publications and strong scientific writing and communication skills. Experience designing and conducting human-subjects experiments, including IRB protocol development and ethical research compliance. Proficiency in statistical modeling and analysis of human performance data (reaction time, workload, situation awareness), including mixed-effects/multilevel models. Programming proficiency in Python, R, and/or MATLAB. Preferred Experience designing studies in vehicle/driving simulators (fixed-base or VR-based). Unity3D development (C#), including multiplayer and networking (e.g., Netcode for GameObjects, Mirror, or Photon). Experience with human-factors instrumentation and data streams: eye tracking, physiological sensors, and motion capture. Familiarity with data/video coding tools and computer vision (e.g., OpenCV, scikit-learn). We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: TandonVDR@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: September 1, 2026. This is a one-year position with an opportunity for an additional year."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170218": {"title": "[EOI] Postdoctoral Researcher - NYU Courant - Benjamin Peherstorfer's Group", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science Location: New York, NY The Postdoctoral Researcher will work in Professor Benjamin Peherstorfer\u2019s group ( https://cims.nyu.edu/~pehersto/ ) on scientific machine learning at the Courant Institute of Mathematical Sciences where they will help to develop, implement, and apply novel computational techniques to problems of scientific importance, in collaboration with application scientists and engineers. Topics of interest include data-driven modeling via nonlinear parametrizations such as deep networks, dynamical systems and control, Bayesian inference and generative modeling, and randomized linear algebra. Applications of interest are transport-dominated parametrized problems, control, and uncertainty quantification. The start date is flexible and the initial appointment will be for one year with yearly extensions depending on performance and funding. NYU Courant is a highly respected institute for cutting-edge research and advanced training in applied mathematics and computer science.\u00a0 NYU Courant is in the very heart of the world's leading artistic and financial center in downtown Manhattan. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $75,000 - $84,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, and internal peer equity, as well as market and organization considerations. Candidates should have a Ph.D. degree and past work in applied and computational mathematics, with a solid background in machine learning and/or numerical analysis. The candidate should have an interest in science and engineering applications. Please follow instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter explaining interests and goals Up-to-date curriculum vitae with publications Two confidential (2) reference letters."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193426": {"title": "Courant Instructor Positions in Mathematics for Fall 2027", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science Location: New York The NYU Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science is the center for advanced training and research in the mathematical sciences. Each year a limited number of Courant Instructorships in the Department of Mathematics are offered to postdoctoral scientists. All areas of Mathematics are considered. These appointments are for a maximum of three (3) years and carry a light teaching load of one course per semester. They are primarily for recent Ph.D.'s, and candidates must have a degree in mathematics or an affiliated field. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary of this position is a set amount of $101,766. Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Mathematics or a related field. Please submit the following via Interfolio: Cover letter CV with Publication List (as one document) Research Statement Teaching Statement Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers). One of the reference letters must address your teaching ability. If you wish to upload additional reference letters, click on \"Additional Documents -> Add File -> Request a recommendation\". The final application deadline is November 15, 2026. Due to the competitiveness of these positions and the high volume of applicants, we urge you to apply as early as possible."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/163629": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate - (P.I.: Dr. Steve Davidson) 2 of 2", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York The Davidson Lab at the New York University Pain Research Center (New York, NY) seeks a Postdoctoral Associate to investigate the neurobiology of pain. The lab uses a combination of physiology, cellular, molecular, and functional imaging techniques. The qualified candidate will assist with tissue acquisition and processing nerve and dorsal root ganglia from organ donors, induced pluripotent stem cell origin, and mouse models. Methods in the lab include cell culture, cell physiology, protein and gene assays, and fluorescent time-lapse microscopy. This position requires excellent communication, bench skills, high integrity recordkeeping, data analysis including statistical reasoning, and scientific writing ability. This successful candidate will build scientific relationships with members of the Pain Research Center and the scientific community at NYU and assist with training new lab members. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $78,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. The position is for a highly motivated researcher who holds a doctoral degree in biology or related fields. Strong laboratory and analytical skills, ability to follow protocol, effective written and oral communication, and efficient time management are essential. Experience with tissue and cellular methods, cell physiology, wet bench lab skills including under sterile conditions, microscopy, histology, imaging, molecular biology, and a scientific knowledge or an interest in somatosensation and pain are highly desirable. The position requires some irregular scheduling due to donor tissue timing. Interested candidates should apply with a CV, complete contact information for three references and a short cover letter. To access the application, please click the \"Apply Now\" button located on the right."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168137": {"title": "Research Scientist (PI: Dr. Halil Aydin)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York  The Aydin Lab in the Department of Molecular Pathobiology at New York University College of Dentistry (New York, NY) seeks a Research Scientist who will investigate the mechanisms governing thylakoid membrane biogenesis in cyanobacteria. The lab uses a combination of physiology, biochemistry, structural biology tools, and molecular and functional imaging techniques. The qualified candidate will also utilize state-of-the-art electron cryo-tomography (cryo-ET) and computational approaches to study the atomic-scale details of membrane-associated processes. In addition, the candidate will engage in bacterial culturing, including maintaining cell lines, growing adherent and suspension cells, and working with various cyanobacterial strains. This position requires excellent communication, high-quality recordkeeping, and the ability to analyze data. The successful individual will earn opportunities to disseminate research with authorship credit. This position will also maintain general lab functions and assist with training new lab members. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $75,000 to $85,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. We seek a highly motivated researcher who holds a Ph.D. in Biological Sciences, Biophysics or related fields. Strong laboratory and analytical skills, ability to follow protocol, effective written and oral communication, and efficient time management are essential. Experience with bacteriology, wet bench lab skills including genetic manipulation of bacteria, molecular biology, structural cell biology, and biophysics and a scientific knowledge or an interest in membrane biology and molecular mechanisms of cellular dynamics are highly desirable. Interested candidates should apply with a CV, complete contact information for three references and a short cover letter via Interfolio link."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168153": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate (PI: Dr. Halil Aydin) # 5", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York  The Aydin Lab in the Department of Molecular Pathobiology at New York University College of Dentistry (New York, NY) seeks a Postdoctoral Scholar who will investigate the underlying mechanisms of intercellular communication and its relationship to human health and disease. The lab uses a combination of physiology, biochemistry, cell biology tools, and molecular and functional imaging techniques. The qualified candidate will also utilize state-of-the-art electron cryo-microscopy (cryo-EM) and computational approaches to study the atomic-scale details of macromolecules. In addition, the candidate will engage in tissue culture experiments, including maintaining cell lines, growing adherent and suspension cells, and working with cell co-culture systems. This position requires excellent communication, high-quality recordkeeping, and the ability to analyze data. The successful individual will earn opportunities to disseminate research with authorship credit. This position will also maintain general lab function and assist with training new lab members. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 to $70,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. We seek a highly motivated researcher who holds a Ph.D. in Biological Sciences or related fields. Strong laboratory and analytical skills, ability to follow protocol, effective written and oral communication, and efficient time management are essential. Experience with tissue and cellular methods, wet bench lab skills including molecular imaging, cell biology and dynamics, molecular biology, and tissue culture and a scientific knowledge or an interest in neurobiology and molecular mechanisms of cell-cell communication are highly desirable. Interested candidates should apply with a CV, complete contact information for three references and a short cover letter via Interfolio link."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/169152": {"title": "Assistant Research Scientist - AI-Linked Cytology  (P.I.: Dr. John McDevitt)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York The McDevitt Laboratory within the Bioengineering Institute and the Department of Molecular Pathobiology at New York University is seeking a full-time\u00a0Assistant Research Scientist to support the development of AI-linked cytology diagnostic technologies. Background The McDevitt Lab has led innovations in the creation and validation of lab-on-a-chip diagnostic platforms that integrate microfluidics, cytology, and artificial intelligence. These technologies are designed to enable early disease detection and precision oncology care by digitizing biological information at the single-cell level. We are now expanding our efforts to build large clinical databases essential for AI-based diagnostic model development. The successful candidate will work closely with an interdisciplinary team of scientists, clinicians, and engineers to support cytology testing, method development, and data generation critical to advancing these next-generation diagnostic tools. Responsibilities Design and carry out hypothesis-driven experiments to support technology and product development Execute and validate cytology-based assays, focusing on the processing and analysis of clinical samples Assist in building a large, high-quality clinical database for training AI diagnostic models Generate technical documents, including requirements, specifications, test plans, and experimental reports Validate test methods, ensuring robustness, reproducibility, and compliance with established protocols Perform cell staining, imaging, and image-based cytology measurements Analyze cell-based data using software tools (e.g., ImageJ, CellProfiler) Maintain detailed lab records, manage sample inventories, and contribute to data integrity initiatives Work independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research environment NYU offers excellent benefits. Join our dynamic team at NYU and contribute to the development of groundbreaking technologies that are transforming early disease detection and precision diagnostics. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $55,000 - $65,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Required: B.S. or M.S. degree in a life science or engineering discipline (e.g., Biology, Biochemistry, Bioengineering, Biomedical Engineering, Chemical Engineering) Strong hands-on experience in experimental design and laboratory research Direct experience with cytology techniques, including cell staining, sample preparation, and imaging Familiarity with image analysis software for cell-based data quantification Strong technical writing skills to prepare study protocols, test plans, and reports Excellent organizational and communication skills Ability to work both independently and as part of a collaborative team Preferred: Experience handling human clinical samples under biosafety guidelines Experience with microfluidics or single-cell analysis platforms Knowledge of AI/machine learning concepts related to biomedical data (a plus, but not required) Applicants must submit: A current CV or resume A cover letter highlighting your interest in the role and relevant experience A list of\u00a0 at least three professional references (highly recommended) To access the application, please click the \"Apply Now\" button located on the right."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/171397": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist  (PI: Dr. Kara Margolis)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York, New York Position available for one full-time junior research scientist to join my laboratory, studying the biology of the enteric nervous system (ENS) and the role of the gut and gut to brain signaling in brain-gut axis disorders.\u00a0 The candidates will work closely with the PI and other lab members in the design and implementation of experiments that use mouse genetic models to investigate the role of ENS and epithelial development in gastrointestinal function, motility, inflammatory bowel disease and mood and neurobehavioral disorders.\u00a0 This position requires a bright, self-motivated and organized individual who is quick to learn new techniques, and feels comfortable working independently as well as with others. \u00a0Some evening and weekend work will be necessary. A minimum of a two-year commitment required. Primary job responsibilities include performing experiments and maintaining the mouse colony (breeding, genotyping, and administering treatments to genetically-modified mice). Experiments will involve mouse dissection, molecular biology, immunohistochemistry, qPCR, immunoblotting , FACS, confocal microscopy, GI motility studies, studies to assess for nociception and behavioral studies. Other responsibilities will include ordering supplies, maintenance of stock reagents, and management of lab expenditures. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $40,000 to $47,500. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor\u2019s or Master\u2019s degree. The ideal candidate will have spent some time in a basic science laboratory in the past and worked with mouse models or has a distinct desire to work with mice. Position-Specific Minimum Qualifications: Facility with Microsoft Word, Excel and Powerpoint Exceptional organizational skills Proficiency with written and spoken English Excellent team player Candidate must feel comfortable with the handling and dissection of laboratory mice Preferred Qualifications: Experience in study of the physiology of the enteric and/or central nervous systems, experience with IPSCs, skills in AI. Special Indications: This position works with chemicals and laboratory animals. Special instructions: N/A Interested candidates should apply using the \"Apply Now\" button located on the right with a current curriculum vitae, cover letter indicating interest and goals, and contact information for two professional references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174150": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist (PI: Dr. Kenny L. Chan)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York, New York A junior research scientist position is available in the laboratory of Kenny Chan, PhD in the Department of Molecular Pathobiology at the NYU College of Dentistry. The Chan Lab studies bi-directional gut-brain communication during psychosocial stress, with an emphasis on neuroimmune interactions. We integrate techniques from the fields of neuroscience, immunology, and gastroenterology to identify circuits, cell types and secreted factors that modulate behavior and gastrointestinal physiology. Duties and Responsibilities We are seeking a highly-organized applicant with experience in animal work, cell culture, and biochemistry laboratory techniques. The junior research associate will work alongside a team of postdoctoral researchers in performing experiments, and be responsible for lab organization and animal colony maintenance. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $40,000.00 -$47,500.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grand funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A Bachelor\u2019s or Master\u2019s degree in a relevant field Experience working in a basic science laboratory, with experience working with rodents in research preferred Interpersonal skills to work as part of a team and participate in departmental events Applicants are invited to submit their applications through Interfolio by clicking the \"Apply Now\" button. Application should include: a c over letter, Curriculum vitae and c ontact information for three professional references. Additional questions regarding this posting can be directed to Dr. Kenny Chan at kc6439@nyu.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/176454": {"title": "Tenure Track Faculty Position: Pain Research Center", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York, NY The NYU Pain Research Center (PRC) and Department of Molecular Pathobiology at the New York University (NYU) Dentistry seeks applications at the level of Assistant or Associate Professor for tenure track research-focused positions in the areas of chronic pain mechanisms, therapeutic management of pain, and related disciplines. The Department of Molecular Pathobiology has a strong and rapidly expanding research program with substantial support from the National Institutes of Health, Department of Defense, and Foundations. The Department is located in state-of-the-art laboratories in close proximity to the broader NYU research community. Research in the Department focuses on understanding normal human biology and disease through studies of molecules, cells, tissues and systems as well as the engineering aspects of life science and medicine. Current interests include: molecular mechanisms and treatment of chronic pain; metabolic and inflammatory diseases; gut-brain signaling; and regenerative and developmental biology, tissue engineering, biomaterials and AI-linked diagnostics. The members of the Pain Research Center investigate the structure and function of receptors and ion channels that signal pain, pain signaling mechanisms and pathways, as well as mechanisms of neuropathic, inflammatory and cancer pain, abdominal pain, oral sensation and itch. The Department wishes to recruit highly creative scientists who will lead and maintain cutting-edge and extramurally funded research programs that will complement and synergize with current research activities in the Department and the Pain Research Center. The ideal candidate will collaborate with members of the Pain Research Center and Department of Molecular Pathobiology, faculty of NYU College of Dentistry, NYU School of Medicine-Langone Medical Center, NYU Tandon School of Engineering and NYU College of Arts and Sciences, as well as foster collaborations with the greater New York research community. Individuals studying pain mechanisms using state-of-the-art approaches of biomedical and chemical engineering, stem cell biology and tissue regeneration, modeling painful diseases using human-focused experimental models, and developing novel approaches to treat chronic pain are encouraged to apply. Qualified candidates should hold a PhD, DDS, MD or equivalent higher degree, and have a demonstrated record of scholarly excellence illustrated by the publication of influential research papers and a record of substantial extramural funding. Candidates with K99/R00 or similar awards are strongly encouraged to apply. Successful candidates will be expected to maintain a robust externally funded research program and to contribute to the intellectual life of the Department by mentoring and instructing students and postdoctoral fellows and by contributing university and community service. Teaching requirements will be commensurate with the Department\u2019s needs and the applicant\u2019s qualifications. Successful candidates will receive a competitive start-up package. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for the rank of Assistant Professor on the tenure track is $140K - $210K & for the rank of Associate Professor on the tenure track is $135K - $225K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. The Department of Molecular Pathobiology provides a respectful, equitable and inclusive community that fosters creativity, innovation and collaboration. NYU is committed to building a culturally inclusive, intellectual community, and strongly encourages women and minorities to apply. For inquiries, contact: Dr. Kara Margolis, PRC Director (km5994@nyu.edu) or Dr. Steve Davison, PRC Associate Director (sd6290@nyu.edu) NYU Dentistry. To apply, please send: 1) cover letter, (addressed to: Pain Research Center Faculty Search Committee, including names, title and contact information for 3 referees); 2) CV; 3) description of current research interests (no more than 3 pages);and 4) PDFs of recent impactful publications (no more than 5). Please apply via Interfolio by clicking the \u201capply now\u201d button."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189341": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist (PI: Dr. Yalda Moayedi  )", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York The Moayedi lab in the Pain Research Center at New York University College of Dentistry (New York, NY) is seeking a Junior Research Scientist to study mechanisms of mechanotransduction and functional outcomes related to loss of mechanosensation. The lab uses a combination of molecular biology, advanced microscopy, in vivo functional imaging, physiology, and behavior in animal models and humans. The researcher will assist with rodent husbandry (colony maintenance, genotyping, performing injections, assessing animal health, maintaining records), somatosensory behavior, histology (whole mount and sliced preparations, immunohistochemistry), data analysis, and preparing figures. Responsibilities will include carrying out experiments, analyzing and presenting data, rodent colony maintenance, ordering, general lab upkeep and assisting with training new lab members. Strong laboratory and analytical skills, effective written and oral communication, and efficient project management are essential. Experience with rodent somatosensory behavior and imaging are highly desirable. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $47,500. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. We seek a highly motivated and creative individual a Bachelor\u2019s degree in biology, neurobiology, or a related field to join our team. Qualified candidates must apply through Interfolio. Applicants must submit: 1) Cover letter / Letter of Interest; 2) Curriculum Vitae; and 3) Names and contact information of at least three professional references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194159": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor (One of Two Positions)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York, New York The Department of Molecular Pathobiology at the New York University (NYU) College of Dentistry seeks applications at the level of Assistant or Associate Professor for two tenure track research-focused positions. The Department of Molecular Pathobiology has a strong and rapidly expanding research program with substantial support from the National Institutes of Health, Department of Defense, and Foundations. The Department is located in state-of-the-art laboratories in within the broader NYU research community. Research in the Department focuses on understanding normal human biology and disease through studies of molecules, cells, tissues and systems. The Department houses the NYU Pain Research Center. Current interests include: mechanism and treatment of chronic pain; metabolic and inflammatory mechanisms and disease; receptors and signal transduction;\u00a0 and biomaterials, development and regenerative biology. For more information see the departmental website: https://dental.nyu.edu/faculty/molecular-pathobiology.html?challenge=d06e90d7-4d8f-4b88-9d8c-10b73beb60f1 The Department wishes to recruit highly creative scientists who will lead and maintain cutting-edge and extramurally funded research programs that will complement and synergize with current research activities in the Department of Molecular Pathobiology. The ideal candidates will interact with faculty of NYU Dentistry, NYU Langone School of Medicine, NYU Tandon School of Engineering, as well as foster collaborations with the greater NYU and New York research communities. Successful candidates will receive a competitive start-up package. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for the rank of Assistant Professor on the tenure track is $140K - $210K & for the rank of Associate Professor on the tenure track is $135K - $225K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. The Department of Molecular Pathobiology provides a respectful, equitable and inclusive community that fosters creativity, innovation and collaboration. The successful candidate will be encouraged to build collaborative linkages to other science, engineering, medical programs at NYU through affiliated positions in other synergistic departments. NYU is committed to building a culturally diverse and inclusive intellectual community, and strongly encourages women and minorities to apply. Qualified candidates should hold a PhD, DDS, MD or equivalent higher degree, and have a demonstrated record of scholarly excellence illustrated by the publication of influential research papers and a record of substantial extramural funding. Junior candidates with K99/R00 or similar awards are strongly encouraged to apply. Senior candidates are expected to have a sustained record of extramural research funding. Successful candidates will be expected to maintain a robust externally funded research program and to contribute to the intellectual life of the Department by mentoring and instructing students and postdoctoral fellows and by contributing university and community service. Teaching requirements will be commensurate with the Department\u2019s needs and the applicant\u2019s qualifications. Please submit your application to one of the two available tenure-track faculty positions. The positions have identical qualifications, responsibilities, and expectations. Candidates should not submit separate applications to both postings.\u00a0\u00a0Candidates who apply to one posting will be considered for the available tenure-track faculty opportunities. If a candidate submits applications to both postings, the applications will be consolidated for review. To apply, please send: Cover letter, including names, title and contact information for 3 referees; CV; Statement of current and future research program (no more than 3 pages); and PDFs of no more than five recent impactful publications. Please apply via Interfolio by clicking the \u201capply now\u201d button."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194170": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor (Two of Two Positions)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Molecular Pathobiology Location: New York The Department of Molecular Pathobiology at the New York University (NYU) College of Dentistry seeks applications at the level of Assistant or Associate Professor for two tenure track research-focused positions. The Department of Molecular Pathobiology has a strong and rapidly expanding research program with substantial support from the National Institutes of Health, Department of Defense, and Foundations. The Department is located in state-of-the-art laboratories in within the broader NYU research community. Research in the Department focuses on understanding normal human biology and disease through studies of molecules, cells, tissues and systems. The Department houses the NYU Pain Research Center. Current interests include: mechanism and treatment of chronic pain; metabolic and inflammatory mechanisms and disease; receptors and signal transduction;\u00a0 and biomaterials, development and regenerative biology. For more information see the departmental website: https://dental.nyu.edu/faculty/molecular-pathobiology.html?challenge=d06e90d7-4d8f-4b88-9d8c-10b73beb60f1 The Department wishes to recruit highly creative scientists who will lead and maintain cutting-edge and extramurally funded research programs that will complement and synergize with current research activities in the Department of Molecular Pathobiology. The ideal candidates will interact with faculty of NYU Dentistry, NYU Langone School of Medicine, NYU Tandon School of Engineering, as well as foster collaborations with the greater NYU and New York research communities. Successful candidates will receive a competitive start-up package. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for the rank of Assistant Professor on the tenure track is $140K - $210K & for the rank of Associate Professor on the tenure track is $135K - $225K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. The Department of Molecular Pathobiology provides a respectful, equitable and inclusive community that fosters creativity, innovation and collaboration. The successful candidate will be encouraged to build collaborative linkages to other science, engineering, medical programs at NYU through affiliated positions in other synergistic departments. NYU is committed to building a culturally diverse and inclusive intellectual community, and strongly encourages women and minorities to apply. Qualified candidates should hold a PhD, DDS, MD or equivalent higher degree, and have a demonstrated record of scholarly excellence illustrated by the publication of influential research papers and a record of substantial extramural funding. Junior candidates with K99/R00 or similar awards are strongly encouraged to apply. Senior candidates are expected to have a sustained record of extramural research funding. Successful candidates will be expected to maintain a robust externally funded research program and to contribute to the intellectual life of the Department by mentoring and instructing students and postdoctoral fellows and by contributing university and community service. Teaching requirements will be commensurate with the Department\u2019s needs and the applicant\u2019s qualifications. Please submit your application to one of the two available tenure-track faculty positions. The positions have identical qualifications, responsibilities, and expectations. Candidates should not submit separate applications to both postings.\u00a0 Candidates who apply to one posting will be considered for the available tenure-track faculty opportunities. If a candidate submits applications to both postings, the applications will be consolidated for review. To apply, please send: Cover letter, including names, title and contact information for 3 referees; CV; Statement of current and future research program (no more than 3 pages); and PDFs of no more than five recent impactful publications. Please apply via Interfolio by clicking the \u201capply now\u201d button."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193533": {"title": "Professor (COHPM)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care Location: New York, New York The Center for Oral Health Policy and Management (COHPM) at NYU College of Dentistry seeks a senior academic leader to advance the Center\u2019s global oral health policy, international dental education, accreditation, and leadership development initiatives. Reporting to the Executive Vice Dean/Co-Executive Director of COHPM, the successful candidate will provide strategic and scholarly leadership across the Center\u2019s domestic and international portfolio. Key responsibilities include: Lead and advise international dental education and institutional development initiatives, including program design, governance, financing, quality assurance, and leadership development. Serve as a senior advisor on dental accreditation and quality assurance, including accreditation standards, readiness assessments, consultation, training, and competency frameworks. Advance COHPM\u2019s oral health policy scholarship and dissemination through peer-reviewed publications, policy briefs, white papers, convenings, and other scholarly activities. Develop and strengthen partnerships with governmental, multilateral, philanthropic, academic, professional, and industry organizations, including opportunities with organizations such as WHO and PAHO. Contribute to the development of extramural funding, contracts, and sponsored initiatives that advance COHPM\u2019s mission. Teach and mentor within COHPM\u2019s leadership education and academic programs, and mentor faculty, fellows, and students in oral health policy, global health, and related areas. Fulfill the teaching, scholarship, service, and other responsibilities associated with a Professor-level faculty appointment in an academic department of NYU College of Dentistry. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $200K - $300K. New York University considers factors such as scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate experience, education and training, key skills, internal peer equity, and market conditions when extending an offer. The successful candidate should possess the following qualifications and experience: Earned terminal degree (DDS/DMD, PhD, or equivalent) and academic credentials appropriate for appointment at the rank of Professor. Substantial senior academic leadership experience, such as serving as a dean, center director, or comparable executive leader within a dental school or academic health center. Current, substantial experience with dental accreditation, including service as an accreditation site visitor, consultant, commissioner, or comparable role. Established record of peer-reviewed scholarship and demonstrated success obtaining and managing extramural grants, contracts, or sponsored programs. National or international leadership in oral health policy, dental education, accreditation, quality measurement, testing and assessment, or related areas. Experience leading or advising international dental education, institutional development, or global oral health initiatives. Excellent interpersonal, diplomatic, written, and oral communication skills, with demonstrated ability to engage effectively with academic, governmental, international, and philanthropic stakeholders. Interested applicants should submit: Curriculum vitae Cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position Statement of research/scholarly interests Statement of teaching and/or leadership experience Contact information for three professional references Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195071": {"title": "Open Rank - Clinical Faculty (Three of Three)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care Location: New York, New York The NYU College of Dentistry, Department of General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care, is seeking three full-time, five-day clinical faculty members to join its faculty. The Department is responsible for the education and clinical development of predoctoral dental students across all four years of the curriculum (D1\u2013D4), with a focus on dental anatomy, cariology, operative and aesthetic dentistry, communication skills, community-based dentistry, and comprehensive patient care. An important goal of the Department is to prepare students to provide comprehensive dental care in a modern group practice environment. Students throughout all four years of the predoctoral program are integrated into group practices, where they provide patient care under the guidance and supervision of group practice directors and clinical faculty. We are seeking Clinical (non-tenure) Track Faculty with credentials and teaching experience in general dentistry and comprehensive care. Successful candidates will contribute to the education and development of predoctoral students through teaching in clinical and simulation settings, participation in the University community, and engagement in scholarly and professional activities. Responsibilities will include approximately 20% teaching foundational clinical skills through state-of-the-art simulation technology and hands-on instruction in preparation for patient care, 60% supervision of students providing clinical care , and 20% professional development and scholarly activity . Faculty will be expected to foster a supportive and engaging learning environment while helping students develop the clinical knowledge, technical skills, and professional judgment necessary for successful dental practice. Key responsibilities include: Instruct predoctoral dental students in preclinical simulation laboratory courses Provide direct chairside instruction and evaluation of student performance Assess student competency through practical examinations and skill evaluations Mentor students in developing proper ergonomics, infection control, and clinical techniques Participate in curriculum development and educational innovation initiatives Collaborate with faculty colleagues in interdisciplinary education Contribute to faculty meetings, committees, and institutional service Other related duties as required In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position for the rank of Clinical Instructor is $90K - $130K & for the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor is $100K - $150K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Required Qualifications Doctor of Dental Surgery (DDS) or Doctor of Dental Medicine (DMD) degree or equivalent. Minimum of 2 years of clinical practice experience in general dentistry Strong verbal and written communication abilities Proficiency in dental terminology and clinical documentation Understanding of infection control protocols and safety procedures Knowledge of dental materials and their application Successful candidates should demonstrate patience and enthusiasm for working with students, a professional demeanor, strong ethical standards, and the ability to provide constructive feedback and mentorship. Candidates should also demonstrate a commitment to lifelong learning and professional development, as well as a collaborative approach to team-based education. Prior teaching experience in dental education, whether formal or informal, is preferred. Experience with simulation-based learning or virtual reality training, familiarity with competency-based education models, and knowledge of digital dentistry and CAD/CAM systems are also desirable. Proficiency with educational technology platforms and an interest in curriculum innovation and educational research are encouraged. New York State dental licensure is required for this position, and candidates must be eligible for institutional employment authorization. Appointment is contingent upon successful completion of required background checks and credential verification. The position requires the ability to stand for extended periods during laboratory instruction, perform fine motor activities necessary for supervising clinical procedures, and maintain sufficient visual acuity for detailed clinical work and instruction. Faculty must be able to move throughout simulation laboratory spaces and dental clinics as needed to support student learning and clinical activities. Faculty are expected to work during scheduled laboratory and clinical sessions and be available for student consultation and office hours as appropriate. Responsibilities also include participation in faculty meetings and institutional events, with potential evening or weekend commitments associated with special programs or departmental activities. Interested applicants should submit: Curriculum vitae Cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position Contact information for three professional references Please submit your application to one of the three available full time faculty positions. The positions have identical qualifications, responsibilities, and expectations. Candidates should not submit separate applications to both postings.\u00a0 Candidates who apply to one posting will be considered for the available tenure-track faculty opportunities. If a candidate submits applications to both postings, the applications will be consolidated for review."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194945": {"title": "Open Rank - Clinical Faculty (Two of Three)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care Location: New York The NYU College of Dentistry, Department of General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care, is seeking three full-time, five-day clinical faculty members to join its faculty. The Department is responsible for the education and clinical development of predoctoral dental students across all four years of the curriculum (D1\u2013D4), with a focus on dental anatomy, cariology, operative and aesthetic dentistry, communication skills, community-based dentistry, and comprehensive patient care. An important goal of the Department is to prepare students to provide comprehensive dental care in a modern group practice environment. Students throughout all four years of the predoctoral program are integrated into group practices, where they provide patient care under the guidance and supervision of group practice directors and clinical faculty. We are seeking Clinical (non-tenure) Track Faculty with credentials and teaching experience in general dentistry and comprehensive care. Successful candidates will contribute to the education and development of predoctoral students through teaching in clinical and simulation settings, participation in the University community, and engagement in scholarly and professional activities. Responsibilities will include approximately 20% teaching foundational clinical skills through state-of-the-art simulation technology and hands-on instruction in preparation for patient care, 60% supervision of students providing clinical care , and 20% professional development and scholarly activity . Faculty will be expected to foster a supportive and engaging learning environment while helping students develop the clinical knowledge, technical skills, and professional judgment necessary for successful dental practice. Key responsibilities include: Instruct predoctoral dental students in preclinical simulation laboratory courses Provide direct chairside instruction and evaluation of student performance Assess student competency through practical examinations and skill evaluations Mentor students in developing proper ergonomics, infection control, and clinical techniques Participate in curriculum development and educational innovation initiatives Collaborate with faculty colleagues in interdisciplinary education Contribute to faculty meetings, committees, and institutional service Other related duties as required In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position for the rank of Clinical Instructor is $90K - $130K & for the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor is $100K - $150K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Required Qualifications Doctor of Dental Surgery (DDS) or Doctor of Dental Medicine (DMD) degree or equivalent. Minimum of 2 years of clinical practice experience in general dentistry Strong verbal and written communication abilities Proficiency in dental terminology and clinical documentation Understanding of infection control protocols and safety procedures Knowledge of dental materials and their application Successful candidates should demonstrate patience and enthusiasm for working with students, a professional demeanor, strong ethical standards, and the ability to provide constructive feedback and mentorship. Candidates should also demonstrate a commitment to lifelong learning and professional development, as well as a collaborative approach to team-based education. Prior teaching experience in dental education, whether formal or informal, is preferred. Experience with simulation-based learning or virtual reality training, familiarity with competency-based education models, and knowledge of digital dentistry and CAD/CAM systems are also desirable. Proficiency with educational technology platforms and an interest in curriculum innovation and educational research are encouraged. New York State dental licensure is required for this position, and candidates must be eligible for institutional employment authorization. Appointment is contingent upon successful completion of required background checks and credential verification. The position requires the ability to stand for extended periods during laboratory instruction, perform fine motor activities necessary for supervising clinical procedures, and maintain sufficient visual acuity for detailed clinical work and instruction. Faculty must be able to move throughout simulation laboratory spaces and dental clinics as needed to support student learning and clinical activities. Faculty are expected to work during scheduled laboratory and clinical sessions and be available for student consultation and office hours as appropriate. Responsibilities also include participation in faculty meetings and institutional events, with potential evening or weekend commitments associated with special programs or departmental activities. Interested applicants should submit: Curriculum vitae Cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position Contact information for three professional references Please submit your application to one of the three available full time faculty positions. The positions have identical qualifications, responsibilities, and expectations. Candidates should not submit separate applications to both postings.\u00a0 Candidates who apply to one posting will be considered for the available tenure-track faculty opportunities. If a candidate submits applications to both postings, the applications will be consolidated for review."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194941": {"title": "Open Rank - Clinical Faculty (One of Three)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care Location: New York The NYU College of Dentistry, Department of General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care, is seeking three full-time, five-day clinical faculty members to join its faculty. The Department is responsible for the education and clinical development of predoctoral dental students across all four years of the curriculum (D1\u2013D4), with a focus on dental anatomy, cariology, operative and aesthetic dentistry, communication skills, community-based dentistry, and comprehensive patient care. An important goal of the Department is to prepare students to provide comprehensive dental care in a modern group practice environment. Students throughout all four years of the predoctoral program are integrated into group practices, where they provide patient care under the guidance and supervision of group practice directors and clinical faculty. We are seeking Clinical (non-tenure) Track Faculty with credentials and teaching experience in general dentistry and comprehensive care. Successful candidates will contribute to the education and development of predoctoral students through teaching in clinical and simulation settings, participation in the University community, and engagement in scholarly and professional activities. Responsibilities will include approximately 20% teaching foundational clinical skills through state-of-the-art simulation technology and hands-on instruction in preparation for patient care, 60% supervision of students providing clinical care , and 20% professional development and scholarly activity . Faculty will be expected to foster a supportive and engaging learning environment while helping students develop the clinical knowledge, technical skills, and professional judgment necessary for successful dental practice. Key responsibilities include: Instruct predoctoral dental students in preclinical simulation laboratory courses Provide direct chairside instruction and evaluation of student performance Assess student competency through practical examinations and skill evaluations Mentor students in developing proper ergonomics, infection control, and clinical techniques Participate in curriculum development and educational innovation initiatives Collaborate with faculty colleagues in interdisciplinary education Contribute to faculty meetings, committees, and institutional service Other related duties as required In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position for the rank of Clinical Instructor is $90K - $130K & for the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor is $100K - $150K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Required Qualifications Doctor of Dental Surgery (DDS) or Doctor of Dental Medicine (DMD) degree or equivalent. Minimum of 2 years of clinical practice experience in general dentistry Strong verbal and written communication abilities Proficiency in dental terminology and clinical documentation Understanding of infection control protocols and safety procedures Knowledge of dental materials and their applications Successful candidates should demonstrate patience and enthusiasm for working with students, a professional demeanor, strong ethical standards, and the ability to provide constructive feedback and mentorship. Candidates should also demonstrate a commitment to lifelong learning and professional development, as well as a collaborative approach to team-based education. Prior teaching experience in dental education, whether formal or informal, is preferred. Experience with simulation-based learning or virtual reality training, familiarity with competency-based education models, and knowledge of digital dentistry and CAD/CAM systems are also desirable. Proficiency with educational technology platforms and an interest in curriculum innovation and educational research are encouraged. New York State dental licensure is required for this position, and candidates must be eligible for institutional employment authorization. Appointment is contingent upon successful completion of required background checks and credential verification. The position requires the ability to stand for extended periods during laboratory instruction, perform fine motor activities necessary for supervising clinical procedures, and maintain sufficient visual acuity for detailed clinical work and instruction. Faculty must be able to move throughout simulation laboratory spaces and dental clinics as needed to support student learning and clinical activities. Faculty are expected to work during scheduled laboratory and clinical sessions and be available for student consultation and office hours as appropriate. Responsibilities also include participation in faculty meetings and institutional events, with potential evening or weekend commitments associated with special programs or departmental activities. Interested applicants should submit: Curriculum vitae Cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position Contact information for three professional references Please submit your application to one of the three available full time faculty positions. The positions have identical qualifications, responsibilities, and expectations. Candidates should not submit separate applications to both postings.\u00a0 Candidates who apply to one posting will be considered for the available tenure-track faculty opportunities. If a candidate submits applications to both postings, the applications will be consolidated for review."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195146": {"title": "Adjunct Clinical Faculty", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care Location: New York, New York We invite you to consider a rewarding opportunity to make a lasting impact on dental education while enhancing your own professional fulfillment. The NYU Dentistry Department of General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care is seeking multiple dedicated practitioners to join our adjunct clinical faculty team, supervising dental students as they learn in the simulation laboratory and provide patient care in our state-of-the-art facilities. As an adjunct clinical faculty member, you'll discover the unique satisfaction that comes from mentoring the next generation of dentists. Our current adjunct faculty consistently share that working with students reinvigorates their passion for dentistry, challenges them to stay current with best practices, and provides a welcome change of pace from private practice. We understand the demands of practice and family life. Several adjunct clinical faculty positions are available, with opportunities requiring as little as four hours per week. Scheduling is designed to provide flexibility and accommodate your availability.\u00a0\u00a0Whether you're a recent graduate looking to give back or an experienced practitioner seeking new challenges, we welcome your interest. Join NYU Dentistry and help shape the next generation of dental professionals while finding a rewarding and flexible way to remain engaged in clinical education. What You'll Contribute: Supervise dental students during patient care in our comprehensive care clinics Provide guidance on treatment planning, clinical procedures, and patient management Mentor students in developing professional judgment and chairside manner Share your real-world experience and clinical expertise What We Offer: Faculty privileges and resources The satisfaction of investing in your profession's future Connection with a vibrant academic community We welcome applications from recent alumni eager to stay connected to their alma mater, as well as established practitioners throughout the metropolitan area. We value diverse clinical backgrounds and practice experiences that enrich our students' learning. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the salary for this part-time is $11K-15K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as, market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. New York State License is required. Minimum of 5 years post residency training or 3 years with 2 letters of recommendation. GPR preferred. Experience with the use and application of digital dentistry, as well as implant restoration and removable prosthodontics, is highly desired. Candidates must be sensitive to the needs of, and possess an interest in, working in an academic community that is diverse with regard to gender, race, ethnicity, religion, nationality, sexual orientation or identity, disability status, age, and protected veteran status. Please send your cover letter, curriculum vitae and list of three professional references to: Dr. Angela De Bartolo, Department of General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care via Interfolio by clicking the \"apply now\" button."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185718": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor | Modified Full Time", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Endodontics Location: New York, New York New York University College of Dentistry seeks applicants for a full-time faculty position on the clinical educator track (non-tenure) in the Dr. Ignatius N. and Sally Quartararo Department of Endodontics. The faculty in this position will primarily provide laboratory and clinical instruction to the pre-doctoral program as well as clinical and seminar instructions to post-graduate endodontic students. Scholarly effort will be expected as a part of the employment, by participation in research efforts of the Department and/or other scholarly work in consultation with the Chair. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this modified full time Clinical Assistant Professor position is $125K - $195K. New York University considers factors such as scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate experience, education and training, key skills, internal peer equity, and market conditions when extending an offer. Applicants must have completed an accredited postdoctoral program in endodontics and be either board-eligible or a Diplomate of the American Board of Endodontics. Candidates must also possess, or be eligible to obtain, a New York State dental license or a New York Dental Faculty license issued by the New York State Department of Education. Preferred qualifications include an M.S. and/or Ph.D., a demonstrated record of scholarly publications, and experience in clinical teaching. For consideration, qualified applicants should send a letter of interest, CV and names and contact information for three references to Dr. Asgeir Sigurdsson, Department Chair. The position will be available until filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/151319": {"title": "Adjunct Professor/Instructor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Epidemiology and Health Promotion Location: New York, New York The department of Epidemiology & Health Promotion at the NYU College of Dentistry is seeking an adjunct professor/instructor for the upcoming Fall 2024 semester, with opportunity for renewal. The course is \u201cBiostatistics\u201d and is offered as part of the MS program in Clinical Research. Biostatistics is a 3 credit course and is scheduled for one evening a week from 5:00PM to 730:PM. Alternative scheduling options are potentially available. This course covers basic probability, descriptive and inferential statistics, and the role of biostatistics in the practice of clinical research. Specific attention will be given to common probability distributions in clinical research and medicine, t-tests, Analysis of Variance, multiple linear and logistic regression, categorical data analysis, and survival analysis. Course enrollment is expected to be between 15-25 students. Materials (e.g., syllabus, presentations, assignments) used in previous offerings of the course are available, but faculty have freedom to develop and use new and additional materials as desired. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate salary range for this position is $75-$83. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as, market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. An earned doctorate in a related field is required. Preference is given to interested adjuncts with a demonstrated history of clinical research and the clinical trials process. Interested applicants please contact Dr. Ryan Richard Ruff, Associate Professor and Director of the MS program in Clinical Research. Salary is commensurate with faculty appointment level in keeping with a 3-credit class."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/128032": {"title": "Adjunct Clinical Faculty (Open Rank)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Oral and Maxillofacial Pathology, Radiology & Medicine Location: New York, NY NYU College of Dentistry seeks applicants available Monday through Friday (mornings, and/or afternoons) for a part-time clinical faculty position in the Department of Oral and Maxillofacial Pathology, Radiology, and Medicine. These positions are available immediately. Responsibilities include clinical teaching, patient care, and student evaluation, with a focus on the medical risk assessment of patients, soft tissue and radiographic evaluation, and the dental management of medically complex patients. Candidates must possess a DDS or DMD degree and must be eligible for licensure in New York State. General practice residency training and at least five years of clinical practice are required, prior teaching experience is preferred. * Academic rank will be commenstrate with credentials and experience. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the rate for this position is $41,250 Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest and curriculum vitae to Isabella Pelllcciari, Department Manager, OMPRM by clicking the \"Apply Now\" button."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187736": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professor (Special Needs Dentistry)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Oral and Maxillofacial Pathology, Radiology & Medicine Location: New York, NY The NYU College of Dentistry invites applications for a full-time, clinical non-tenure track faculty position at the level of Clinical Assistant or Clinical Associate Professor in the Department of Oral & Maxillofacial Pathology, Radiology & Medicine. This diverse and collaborative department brings together faculty from multiple specialties in a collegial, scholarly environment dedicated to excellence in patient care, education, and research. This faculty role is integral to advancing the College\u2019s mission of delivering exceptional, compassionate dental care to individuals with disabilities and complex medical conditions. Because expertise in special care dentistry is not widespread within general practice, faculty with advanced training and experience in caring for patients with specialized healthcare needs play a particularly valuable role within the institution. Primary responsibilities include providing patient care in a state-of-the-art facility; supervising and mentoring predoctoral and postgraduate students in both didactic and clinical settings; teaching within the department\u2019s various clinical services; engaging in scholarly activity in oral medicine and/or special needs dentistry; providing clinical consultations; and overseeing care in the College\u2019s Oral Health Center for People with Disabilities. The faculty member will help ensure that graduates are well prepared to deliver inclusive, patient-centered care to individuals with intellectual, developmental, physical, cognitive, behavioral, and medically complex conditions, including patients undergoing cancer therapy, organ transplantation, and other high-risk medical treatments requiring dental clearance. Faculty in this role may also participate in the College of Dentistry faculty practice, providing specialized diagnostic and clinical care while contributing to the academic and educational mission of the institution. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $120,000 \u2013 $180,000. New York University considers factors such as scope of responsibilities, experience, education and training, key skills, internal equity, and market considerations when extending an offer. Candidates must be sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. Successful candidates will demonstrate a commitment to delivering compassionate, patient-centered care. Candidates must possess a DDS or DMD degree from an accredited dental program and demonstrate a strong commitment to providing comprehensive oral healthcare for patients with specialized healthcare needs, including individuals with developmental or physical disabilities and medically complex conditions. Advanced training and meaningful clinical experience in Special Needs Dentistry are required. Applicants should have at least five years of recent clinical experience in general dentistry and experience managing patients with medical complexity. Board certification by the American Board of Oral Medicine or the American Board of Special Care Dentistry is preferred but not required. Eligibility for licensure to practice dentistry in the state of New York is required. Experience or interest in treating patients with complex medical conditions, including dental oncology, is valued. Experience working in collaborative, interprofessional environments such as hospitals or academic settings is also appreciated. Applicants who are enthusiastic about growing in these areas are encouraged to apply, even if they do not meet every preferred qualification listed above. NYU offers an excellent benefits package. Salary and academic rank will be commensurate with credentials and experience. Interested candidates should submit a statement of academic objectives, curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information of four references to:\u00a0Dr. Denise A. Trochesset |\u00a0Chair, Department of Oral and Maxillofacial Pathology, Radiology and Medicine"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189485": {"title": "HRSA CC Junior Research Scientist - # 2 (P.I.: Dr. Courtney Chinn)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Pediatric Dentistry Location: New York, NY New York University Department of Pediatric Dentistry has established a one year Connecting Care (CC) training program focused specifically on oral health care for persons with disabilities and complex health conditions (PWDCMC).\u00a0 This position is funded through a five-year postdoctoral HRSA training grant: D88HP37550. The purpose of the Connecting Care training program is to create a sustainable primary care dental workforce that is equipped and empowered to increase the quality, acceptability, and timeliness of oral health care services for\u00a0 PWDCMC.\u00a0 This includes increased knowledge in the prevalent medical conditions, physical and neurodevelopmental disabilities that impact this population. This position will involve researching and training in approaches that help achieve optimal oral health for these underserved populations. Principal Duties: The CC Junior Research Scientist will working closely with the program PI to meet the grant goals of providing comprehensive dental services at the NYU Dentistry Oral Health Center for Persons with Disabilities (OHCPD) and Bringing Smiles Comprehensive Care program (Bringing Smiles). The CC Junior Research Scientist will engage in didactic instruction which includes curriculum focused on special needs populations, cultural competence in oral health care delivery for PWDCMC, quality improvement in community-based oral health programs, interprofessionalism, advocacy for vulnerable populations, teledentistry, and dental public health. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $47,500- $75,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Required Education: Bachelor\u2019s Degree Required Licensure: Must have active NYS dental hygiene license by July 1st Required Experience: US accredited dental hygiene program Required Knowledge, Skills, Abilities: Excellent communication skills, strong command of dental procedures/treatments, desire to work with special needs and/or underserved populations, organized and highly motivated, works well with teams Preferred Education: Training in behavior management Preferred Experience: Community-based or school-based care, work with children or adults with special needs Preferred Knowledge, Skills, Abilities: Knowledge of EHR systems and teledentistry, Spanish fluency HRSA Requirements: Associate must be a US citizen or permanent resident Qualified candidates must apply through Interfolio. Applicants must submit: 1) Cover letter / Letter of Interest; 2) Curriculum Vitae; and 3) Names and contact information of at least three professional references. To access the application, please click the \"Apply Now\" button located on the right."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/157384": {"title": "Adjunct Clinical Faculty - Prosthodontics", "description": "Location: New York, NY The Department of Prosthodontics at NYU Dentistry is currently seeking a highly motivated candidate with Dental Laboratory Technology experience for a part-time (Adjunct) position. This part-time faculty interacts with the predoctoral DDS and/or the Advanced Education Program in Prosthodontics students. This position requires a minimum of one day to 2.5 days of time commitment per week. As the third oldest and the largest dental school in the United States, NYU Dentistry educates nearly 10 percent of our nation\u2019s dentists and endeavors through its faculty, students, and alumni to improve the health of the highly diverse populations in New York City and around the world. Adjunct faculty at NYU Dentistry benefit from: Affiliation with NYU. Regular interaction with top dental professionals in NYC. Contributions to the future of the dental profession using the latest technology. Opportunities to receive continuing education credit while learning from renowned faculty and researchers. Candidates must be sensitive to the needs of, and possess an interest in, working in an academic community that is diverse with regard to gender, race, ethnicity, religion, nationality, sexual orientation or identity, disability status, age, and protected veteran status. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the salary range for this position is $12K-$45K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as, market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Candidates must be a Certified Dental Technician (CDT). Knowledge of prosthodontics laboratory and digital procedures, evidence of previous teaching experience (Academia or industry), as well as laboratory management, are required. The successful applicant will join a department committed to the highest quality educational programs and a broad spectrum of patient care using digital technology for optimal patient care efficiency. Extensive experience with the use and application of digital dentistry, software designs, and manufacturing of prostheses as related to Prosthodontics and dental prostheses fabrication is highly desirable. Interested candidates please send your cover letter, curriculum vitae and two (2) letters of professional recommendation to: Dr. Leila Jahangiri, Chair, Department of Prosthodontics via Interfolio by clicking the \"apply now\" button."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/179145": {"title": "Adjunct Clinical Faculty - Prosthodontics", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Prosthodontics Location: New York, NY The Department of Prosthodontics at NYU Dentistry is currently seeking a highly motivated candidate for a part-time (Adjunct) position. This part-time faculty interacts with the predoctoral DDS and/or the Advanced Education Program in Prosthodontics students. This position requires a minimum of one day to 2.5 days of time commitment per week. As the third oldest and the largest dental school in the United States, NYU Dentistry educates nearly 10 percent of our nation\u2019s dentists and endeavors through its faculty, students, and alumni to improve the health of the highly diverse populations in New York City and around the world. Adjunct faculty at NYU Dentistry benefit from: Affiliation with NYU. Regular interaction with top dental professionals in NYC. Contributions to the future of the dental profession using the latest technology. Opportunities to receive continuing education credit while learning from renowned faculty and researchers. Candidates must be sensitive to the needs of, and possess an interest in, working in an academic community that is diverse with regard to gender, race, ethnicity, religion, nationality, sexual orientation or identity, disability status, age, and protected veteran status. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the salary range for this position is $14K-$45K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as, market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Qualified candidates must possess a D.D.S./D.M.D. degree, or international equivalent and have successfully completed the NBDE Parts I & II or the INBDE. By the position start date, the candidate must have completed a CODA-accredited graduate program in Prosthodontics; must be Board Certified, or eligible for Board Certification, as a Diplomate of the American Board of Prosthodontics; and must have active NYS Dental licensure. Interested candidates please send your cover letter, curriculum vitae and two (2) letters of professional recommendation to: Dr. Leila Jahangiri, Chair, Department of Prosthodontics via Interfolio by clicking the \"apply now\" button."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/162734": {"title": "Part-time lecturer in Web Design", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU London Location: NYU London Position Description: NYU London is currently recruiting for a part-time lecturer to teach Introduction to Web Design and Computer Principles, a course sponsored by the College of Arts & Science at New York University. Teaching commences in the autumn term (Sept-Dec 2025). Course Description: This course introduces students to both the practice of web design and the basic principles of computer science. The practice component of the course covers not only web design but also current graphics and software tools. The principles section includes an overview of hardware and software, the history of computers, and a discussion of the impact of computers and the internet. Upon completion of this course, students will: Students will develop foundational coding skills in web design, including HTML, CSS, and an introduction to JavaScript Students will be able to utilize open-source graphics and software tools as part of the web development process Students will gain a broad understanding of the Internet in general and the web in particular, along with applied knowledge of digital accessibility Expectation of Hours: This is a 4-credit class, which calls for total contact hours equaling about 35 hours. The semester is 15 weeks (14 weeks of teaching plus an exam week) and there are 2.5 hours of classroom instruction each week. In addition to the time required for course preparation, classroom instruction, and marking, the instructor is required to hold a minimum of one set 60-minute \u201coffice hour\u201d at NYU London per week, during which time they will be available to meet with students. Teaching information: Instructors are encouraged to use a variety of pedagogical techniques. This typically includes a combination of lecture- and discussion-based class sessions, as well as the use of student activities, such as debate exercises, flipped classroom environments, and experimental simulations. In addition to exams, instructors may use a variety of assignments to assess student comprehension of the course material. These may include online weekly homework, writing assignments, short student presentations, and in-class multiple-choice quizzes. Instructors may select the mix that is more appropriate to their teaching methodology. Instructors should be prepared to teach in-person and simultaneously to be prepared for remote instruction should circumstances require it. NYU uses Zoom as its primary platform for remote instruction. About NYU and NYU London: Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 14 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU London is an undergraduate study abroad program of exceptional quality with teaching strengths across the curriculum. Our UK faculty, drawn mainly from local universities, has an excellent record in teaching and research. Classes are held at the NYU London centre at 265 Srand, London, WC2R 1BH. If you would like to learn more about our program please visit NYU London . We are seeking candidates with a PhD in Computer Science or a closely aligned specialist area. It is expected that the post-holder will ideally have 2 to 3 years of experience teaching at the university level; experience teaching undergraduates studying at a US university is helpful. The successful candidate should have the right to live and work in the UK prior to appointment. All candidates must submit the following: (1) a cover letter; and (2) a current C.V. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at http://apply.interfolio.com/162734. If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Applications, in the form of a C.V. and covering letter, should arrive by 14 March 2025. Competitive salary. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. The successful candidate should have the right to live and work in the UK prior to appointment. NYU London is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from under-represented individuals in the profession, across color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, and gender and sexual identity.\u00a0NYU London affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188131": {"title": "Part-time lecturer in Writing for English Language Learners", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU London Location: NYU London Position Description: NYU London is currently recruiting for a part-time lecturer to teach Academic English for Liberal Studies sponsored by NYU's Liberal Studies Program. Teaching commences in the Autumn term (Sept-Dec 2026). Course Description This class will focus on reading, writing, speaking and listening for academic purposes and is designed to provide advanced English support to students who are enrolled in degree programs full-time. Students will work with authentic sources and receive individualized feedback on accuracy, pronunciation, and both productive and receptive language skills. Learning Objectives By the end of this course, students will have learned to: Produce a range of texts that follow academic rhetorical structures (i.e., development, organization, unity, and coherence) typical for Liberal Studies assignments Work with sources by summarizing, paraphrasing, and quoting different types of academic texts Expand academic vocabulary and syntactic sophistication Synthesize academic sources for argumentation Deliver clear and effective academic presentations with PowerPoint slides Participate in classroom discussion (e.g., expressing agreement/disagreement) Speak with appropriate volume, pace, rhythm, eye contact, body language, and clear pronunciation Expectation of Hours: This is a 2-credit class, which calls for total contact hours equaling about 17.5 hours. The semester is 15 weeks (14 weeks of teaching plus an exam week) and there are 1.25 hours (1 session of 75 minutes) of classroom instruction each week. In addition to the time required for course preparation, classroom instruction, and marking, the instructor is required to hold a minimum of one set 60-minute \u201coffice hour\u201d at NYU London per week, during which time they will be available to meet with students. Teaching information: Instructors are encouraged to use a variety of pedagogical techniques. This typically includes a combination of lecture- and discussion-based class sessions, as well as the use of student activities, such as debate exercises, flipped classroom environments, and experimental simulations. In addition to exams, instructors may use a variety of assignments to assess student comprehension of the course material. These may include online weekly homework, writing assignments, short student presentations, and in-class multiple-choice quizzes. Instructors may select the mix that is more appropriate to their teaching methodology. Instructors should be prepared to teach in-person and simultaneously to be prepared for remote instruction should circumstances require it. NYU uses Zoom as its primary platform for remote instruction. About NYU and NYU London: Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 14 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU London is an undergraduate study abroad program of exceptional quality with teaching strengths across the curriculum. Our UK faculty, drawn mainly from local universities, has an excellent record in teaching and research. Classes are held at the NYU London centre at 265 Srand, London, WC2R 1BH. If you would like to learn more about our program please visit NYU London . Applicants must minimally have a PhD or MFA. Related fields include Writing Studies and Creative Writing, among others. Candidates must have the ability and experience to teach in the first-year writing sequence of the Core Curriculum with a global, interdisciplinary, and intersectional emphasis; experience teaching American undergraduates is helpful. We welcome teachers driven by a sense of writing community and collective inquiry. The successful candidate should have the right to live and work in the UK prior to appointment. All candidates must submit the following: (1) a cover letter; and (2) a current C.V. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at https://apply.interfolio.com/188131 . If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Applications, in the form of a C.V. and covering letter, should arrive by 6 July. Competitive salary. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. The successful candidate should have the right to live and work in the UK prior to appointment."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193475": {"title": "Sessional Lecturer in Principles of Biology II", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Sydney Location: NYU Sydney (Classes held at the University of Sydney, Camperdown/Darlington Campus) NYU Sydney is seeking a sessional lecturer to teach the undergraduate course 'Principles of Biology II' during the NYU semester of February-June 2026. Class is scheduled to meet twice a week with lectures and recitations tentatively scheduled on Tuesdays from 12:30-2:00pm and Thursdays 12-3pm, though final schedules will be discussed with the chosen candidate. Responsibilities of the instructor include preparing and leading the weekly class sessions and recitations, course coordination, writing assignments, and grading. Lecturers are paid at competitive casual academic rates informed by local market practices and applicable employment frameworks. In addition to their teaching duties, they will be paid to attend periodic meetings and training, including an induction meeting during the orientation week prior to the start of the semester. Classes take place at the University of Sydney's Camperdown/Darlington Campus. Course Description This is a continuation of BIOL-UA 11, an introductory course for Science majors and pre-health students, designed to acquaint the student with the fundamental principles and processes of biological systems. Subjects include the basics of chemistry pertinent to biology, biochemistry and cell biology, genetics and molecular biology, anatomy and physiology, neurobiology, ecology, population genetics and history and classification of life forms and evolution. A sample syllabus is available on the NYU London website. About NYU and NYU Sydney Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university\u00a0in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 11 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. New York University sends more students to study abroad than any other U.S. college or university. Since opening in 2012, NYU Sydney has run\u00a0an undergraduate\u00a0study away program of exceptional quality with teaching strengths across the curriculum.\u00a0 Our lecturers, drawn from local universities, have\u00a0a strong track record\u00a0in teaching and research. To learn more about our program, please visit NYU Sydney . NYU's Global Programs is committed to being a welcoming campus community that reflects and enacts the values of inclusion, diversity, belonging, equity, and accessibility that inform academic excellence. Employees in this organization are expected to contribute to diverse, equitable, inclusive, and accessible learning and working environments for our students, staff, and faculty. Applicants must be based in Sydney, and should hold a doctorate in Biology, or a related field, and have demonstrated experience teaching university students at the undergraduate level. Candidates with experience teaching Introductory level Biology classes is a plus. Applications must include a CV and a letter of interest outlining their suitability to teach this particular class. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at apply.interfolio.com/193475 . If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Please direct any questions to Yuri Ogura, Associate Director, Academics and Administration at NYU Sydney via email: yuri.ogura@nyu.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193476": {"title": "Sessional Lecturer in Principles of Biology laboratory", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Sydney Location: NYU Sydney (Classes held at the University of Sydney, Camperdown/Darlington Campus) NYU Sydney is seeking a lecturer to teach the course 'Principles of Biology lab' to undergraduate students during the NYU semester of February-June 2026. The lab is scheduled to meet weekly and tentatively scheduled on Tuesday evenings from 6-9pm. Responsibilities of the instructor include leading the weekly laboratory, as well as course coordination, writing assignments, and grading. Lecturers are paid at competitive casual academic rates informed by local market practices and applicable employment frameworks. In addition to their teaching duties, they will be paid to attend periodic meetings and training, including an induction meeting during the orientation week prior to the start of the semester. Classes take place at the University of Sydney's Camperdown/Darlington Campus. Course Description In this introductory lab, you will be exposed to different approaches in modern biology. Each may introduce a model system or a field of study. This lab can help frame your interests and your further study within the Biology Department at NYU. You\u2019ll be pushed to study living organisms at all levels, from their genes and molecules, to their organismal phenotypes, to how organisms interact in communities and with abiotic factors. Because of the scope of the lab, two framing principles are the levels of organization \u2013 from the molecular to the ecological \u2013 and the techniques used to study each level, and model organisms that are widely used in research labs to understand particular systems. A sample syllabus is available on the NYU London website. About NYU and NYU Sydney Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university\u00a0in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 11 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. New York University sends more students to study abroad than any other U.S. college or university. Since opening in 2012, NYU Sydney has run\u00a0an undergraduate\u00a0study away program of exceptional quality with teaching strengths across the curriculum.\u00a0 Our lecturers, drawn from local universities, have\u00a0a strong track record\u00a0in teaching and research. To learn more about our program, please visit NYU Sydney . NYU's Global Programs is committed to being a welcoming campus community that reflects and enacts the values of inclusion, diversity, belonging, equity, and accessibility that inform academic excellence. Employees in this organization are expected to contribute to diverse, equitable, inclusive, and accessible learning and working environments for our students, staff, and faculty. Applicants must be based in Sydney, and should hold a doctorate in Biology, or a related field, and have demonstrated experience teaching university students at the undergraduate level. Candidates with experience teaching Introductory level Biology classes is a plus. Applications must include a CV and a letter of interest outlining their suitability to teach this particular class. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at apply.interfolio.com/193476 . If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Please direct any questions to Yuri Ogura, Associate Director, Academics and Administration at NYU Sydney via email: yuri.ogura@nyu.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/165229": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer, Causal Inference", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Paris Location: Paris, France NYU Paris is seeking a part-time lecturer to teach an expected undergraduate course in Data Science, Causal Inference. The course is held at NYU academic center in Paris and is offered to undergraduate students. Language of instruction: English Teaching commences in January 2026; courses meet for 2.5 hours per week for a 14.5 week term.Lecturers will be expected to teach an additional recitation session for 1.5 hours per week. Instructors should be prepared to teach in-person and simultaneously to be prepared for remote instruction should circumstances require it. NYU uses Zoom as its primary platform for remote instruction. Causal Inference Prerequisite: Intro to Data Science Teaches the fundamentals of how to reason about causality and make causal determinations using empirical data. Introduces the counterfactual framework of causal inference and then considers a variety of approaches, starting with the most basic experimental designs to more complex observational methods, for making inferences about causal relationships from the data.\u00a0Addresses questions about data generation and interpretation and provides hands-on implementation of causal inference techniques with real data sets. Problem sets will contain a mixture of both theoretical and applied questions in order to reinforce key concepts. Examines theoretical concepts and their applications; uses R or Python\u00a0programming language. About NYU and NYU Paris Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university\u00a0in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 11 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU Paris is one of 11 study away academic centers in NYU's global network.\u00a0Students at NYU Paris participate in an immersive\u00a0study away program while earning credits towards their degree. To learn more, please visit NYU Paris . Description du poste de travail NYU Paris est \u00e0 la recherche d'un enseignant \u00a0\u00e0 temps partiel pour enseigner le cours anticip\u00e9\u00a0de premier cycle en Data Science: Causal Inference Langue d'enseignement: Anglais L'enseignement commence en janvier 2026 ; les cours se d\u00e9roulent \u00e0 raison de 2,5 heures par semaine pour un trimestre de 14,5 semaines. L'enseignant enseignera \u00e9galement un TD \u00e0 raison de 1,5 heure par semaine. Les instructeurs doivent \u00eatre pr\u00eats \u00e0 enseigner en personne et simultan\u00e9ment \u00e0 \u00eatre pr\u00eats \u00e0 enseigner \u00e0 distance si les circonstances l'exigent. NYU utilise Zoom comme plateforme principale pour l'enseignement \u00e0 distance. Causal Inference Pr\u00e9requis: Intro to Data Science Le cours traite les principes fondamentaux du raisonnement sur la causalit\u00e9 et des d\u00e9terminations causales \u00e0 l'aide de donn\u00e9es empiriques. Il commence avec\u00a0le cadre contrefactuel de l'inf\u00e9rence causale, puis passe \u00e0 l'exploration d'une vari\u00e9t\u00e9 d'approches, en commen\u00e7ant par les plans exp\u00e9rimentaux les plus basiques jusqu'aux m\u00e9thodes d'observation plus complexes, pour faire des inf\u00e9rences sur les relations causales \u00e0 partir des donn\u00e9es. Il aborde les questions relatives \u00e0 la g\u00e9n\u00e9ration et \u00e0 l'interpr\u00e9tation des donn\u00e9es et propose une mise en \u0153uvre pratique des techniques d'inf\u00e9rence causale avec des ensembles de donn\u00e9es r\u00e9elles. Les ensembles de probl\u00e8mes contiendront un m\u00e9lange de questions th\u00e9oriques et appliqu\u00e9es afin de renforcer les concepts cl\u00e9s.Examine les concepts th\u00e9oriques et leurs applications; utilise le langage de programmation R ou Python. Sur NYU et NYU Paris Fond\u00e9e en 1831, l\u2019universit\u00e9 de New York (NYU) est la plus grande universit\u00e9 priv\u00e9e aux Etats Unis. L'universit\u00e9 dispose des campus d\u00e9livrant des dipl\u00f4mes \u00e0 New York, Abu Dhabi et Shanghai et g\u00e8re 11 centres universitaires et programmes de recherche dans plus de 25 pays. NYU Paris est l'un des 11 centres acad\u00e9miques \u00e0 l'\u00e9tranger du r\u00e9seau global NYU. Les \u00e9tudiants de NYU Paris participent \u00e0 un programme d'\u00e9tudes immersives tout en obtenant des cr\u00e9dits pour leur dipl\u00f4me. Pour en savoir plus sur NYU Paris, veuillez visiter le site Web \u00e0 l'adresse https://www.nyu.edu/paris.html . Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Data Science or a related field,\u00a0and two to three years relevant teaching experience. The appointed person will have full responsibility for teaching and coordinating the course. The course is taught to a small group and is offered to undergraduate students, mostly in their second or third year of study. All students are NYU students enrolled in NYU degrees in New York, Abu Dhabi or Shanghai who come to study at NYU Paris for one semester. Classes take place at our Academic Centre in Paris. Candidates must be eligible to work in France. Qualifications d'instructeur Les candidats doivent avoir un doctorat en Sciences des donn\u00e9es ou une domaine proche,\u00a0et deux \u00e0 trois ann\u00e9es d'exp\u00e9rience pertinente en enseignement. La personne nomm\u00e9e aura l'enti\u00e8re responsabilit\u00e9 d'enseigner et de coordonner le cours.\u00a0 Le cours\u00a0est enseign\u00e9 a un petit groupe et est offert aux \u00e9tudiants de premier cycle, pour la plupart dans leur deuxi\u00e8me ou troisi\u00e8me ann\u00e9e d'\u00e9tudes. Les \u00e9tudiants sont en premier cycle et viennent des campus de NYU \u00e0 New York, Shanghai et Abu Dhabi afin d'\u00e9tudier \u00e0 NYUParis pour un semestre. Le cours a lieu dans notre centre acad\u00e9mique \u00e0 Paris. Les candidats doivent \u00eatre \u00e9ligibles pour travailler en France. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at apply.interfolio.com/165229 .\u00a0If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Applications must include: (1) a cover letter, (2) C.V., and (3) course outline or synopsis; please be sure to note in particular any relevant teaching experience. Current NYU Paris faculty are encouraged to apply. NYU Paris is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from under-represented individuals in the profession, across color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, and gender and sexual identity. NYU Paris affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. NYU Paris\u00a0 s'engage \u00e0 respecter l'\u00e9galit\u00e9 des chances, la diversit\u00e9 et l'inclusion sociale. Nous encourageons fortement les candidatures de personnes sous-repr\u00e9sent\u00e9es dans la profession, sans distinction de couleur, de croyance, de race, d'origine ethnique et nationale, de capacit\u00e9 physique, de genre et d'identit\u00e9 sexuelle. NYU Paris affirme la valeur de diff\u00e9rentes perspectives afin de s'\u00e9fforcer de construire l'universit\u00e9 la plus forte possible avec la port\u00e9e la plus large. Toutes les demandes de candidature doivent \u00eatre faites en ligne par le biais du Interfolio ByCommittee \u00e0 l'adresse: . Pour plus d'information sur l'utilisation d' Interfolio Faculty Search veuillez consulter Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Tout dossier doit\u00a0comprendre (1) une lettre de pr\u00e9sentation, (2) un CV, et (3) un plan de cours ou un synopsis; veillez \u00e0 noter en particulier toute exp\u00e9rience d'enseignement pertinente. Les professeurs actuels de NYU Paris sont encourag\u00e9s \u00e0 postuler. Collapse Instructions"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/176418": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer, Adaptive Reuse of Buildings: Successes and Failures II", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Paris Location: Paris, France NYU Paris is seeking a part-time lecturer in Architectural History to teach in a Master of Arts program in Historical and Sustainable Architecture. Teaching takes place in Paris, from January 19th to April 15th; classes meet for approximately 4 hours per week ; the instructor will also hold weekly office hours. The course is taught in English. Adaptive Reuse of Buildings: Successes and Failures II The overarching goal of this course is to see the ways architects and developers reuse older buildings today for modern purposes. This is the second half of a two-part course, that balances readings against case studies and visits. Two key theoretical issues are explored. The first is the role of memory in the significance of historic buildings. Can public memory withstand radical changes of use? Are some building types more likely to lose their burden of public memories more easily than others? The desired outcome is to develop an understanding of the many challenges of adaptive re-use, through the study of building types and their inherent physical and social characteristics. The second issue is that of the relationship between the built environment and \u201cheritage,\u201d using Paris as a case study.\u00a0 Visits illuminate these issues by practical example. By the end of the course students should have a broad understanding of the place of historic architecture in France today, based on a range of contrasting designs and building types. NYU Paris recherche un professeur \u00e0 temps partiel en Histoire de l'architecture pour enseigner dans le cadre d'un programme de niveau Master en Histoire de l'architecture et le d\u00e9veloppement durable. L'enseignement a lieu \u00e0 Paris, du 19 janvier au 15 avril ; les cours ont lieu pendant 4 heures une fois par semaine ; le professeur tiendra \u00e9galement des heures de bureau hebdomadaire.\u00a0 Le cours sera enseign\u00e9 en anglais. R\u00e9utilisation adaptative des b\u00e2timents : r\u00e9ussites et \u00e9checs II Deuxi\u00e8me partie d'un cours en deux volets, bas\u00e9 sur les lectures, les \u00e9tudes de cas et les visites. Deux questions th\u00e9oriques cl\u00e9s sont explor\u00e9es. La premi\u00e8re concerne le r\u00f4le de la m\u00e9moire dans l'importance des b\u00e2timents historiques. La m\u00e9moire collective peut-elle r\u00e9sister \u00e0 des changements radicaux d'utilisation ? Certains types de b\u00e2timents sont-ils plus susceptibles que d'autres de perdre plus facilement le poids des souvenirs collectifs ? L'objectif est de d\u00e9velopper une compr\u00e9hension des nombreux d\u00e9fis li\u00e9s \u00e0 la r\u00e9utilisation adaptative, \u00e0 travers l'\u00e9tude des types de b\u00e2timents et de leurs caract\u00e9ristiques physiques et sociales inh\u00e9rentes. La deuxi\u00e8me question est celle de la relation entre le modernisme et le \u00ab patrimoine \u00bb, en prenant Paris comme \u00e9tude de cas. Les visites illustrent ces questions par des exemples concrets. \u00c0 la fin du cours, les \u00e9tudiants devraient avoir une compr\u00e9hension globale de la place de l'architecture moderne en France aujourd'hui, sur la base d'une s\u00e9rie de conceptions et de types de b\u00e2timents contrast\u00e9s. About NYU and NYU Paris Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university\u00a0in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 11 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU Paris is one of 11 study away academic centers in NYU's global network.\u00a0Students at NYU Paris participate in an immersive\u00a0study away program while earning credits towards their degree. To learn more, please visit NYU Paris . Sur NYU et NYU Paris Fond\u00e9e en 1831, l\u2019universit\u00e9 de New York (NYU) est la plus grande universit\u00e9 priv\u00e9e aux Etats Unis. L'universit\u00e9 dispose des campus d\u00e9livrant des dipl\u00f4mes \u00e0 New York, Abu Dhabi et Shanghai et g\u00e8re 11 centres universitaires et programmes de recherche dans plus de 25 pays. NYU Paris est l'un des 11 centres acad\u00e9miques \u00e0 l'\u00e9tranger du r\u00e9seau global NYU. Les \u00e9tudiants de NYU Paris participent \u00e0 un programme d'\u00e9tudes immersives tout en obtenant des cr\u00e9dits pour leur dipl\u00f4me. Pour en savoir plus sur NYU Paris, veuillez visiter le site Web \u00e0 l'adresse https://www.nyu.edu/paris.html . Eligible candidates must have a Ph.D. in Architectural History or related field. Classes take place at the NYU Academic Centre in Paris. Qualifications d'instructeur Les candidats doivent \u00eatre titulaires d'un doctorat en Histoire de l'architecture ou une discipline proche. Les cours ont lieu au centre acad\u00e9mique de NYU \u00e0 Paris. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at apply.interfolio.com/176418 . If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Applications must include: (1) a cover letter, (2) C.V., and (3) course outline or synopsis; please be sure to note in particular any relevant teaching experience. Candidates must be eligible to work in France. Current NYU Paris faculty are encouraged to apply. Toutes les demandes de candidature doivent \u00eatre faites en ligne par le biais du Interfolio ByCommittee \u00e0\u00a0 l'adresse apply.interfolio.com/176418 . Pour plus d'information sur l'utilisation d' Interfolio Faculty Search veuillez consulter Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Tout dossier doit\u00a0comprendre (1) une lettre de pr\u00e9sentation, (2)un CV, et (3) un plan de cours ou un synopsis; veillez \u00e0 noter en particulier toute exp\u00e9rience d'enseignement pertinente. Les candidats doivent \u00eatre \u00e9ligibles pour travailler en France. Les professeurs actuels de NYU Paris sont encourag\u00e9s \u00e0 postuler."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181674": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer, Advanced Topics in Data Science", "description": "Posted: Feb 19, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Paris Location: Paris, France NYU Paris is seeking a part-time lecturer to teach an undergraduate course in Data Science, Advanced Topics in Data Science: Economic Analysis with Data. The course is held at NYU academic center in Paris and is offered to undergraduate students. Language of instruction: English Teaching commences in September 2026; courses meet for 2.5 hours per week for a 14.5 week term. Lecturers will be expected to teach an additional recitation session for 1.5 hours per week. Advanced Topics in Data Science: Economic Analysis with Data Prerequisite: Introduction to Data Science This course offers a comprehensive introduction to applied data science through the lens of economic analysis. Students will master the end-to-end research pipeline: from automated data acquisition and advanced visualization to the implementation of modern causal inference frameworks. This course bridges contemporary data science with classical economic theory, emphasizing the transition from raw digital datasets to validated econometric methods. It prioritizes the application of quasi-experimental designs to disentangle complex market endogeneity, culminating in a formal defense of empirical findings. About NYU and NYU Paris Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university\u00a0in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 11 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU Paris is one of 11 study away academic centers in NYU's global network.\u00a0Students at NYU Paris participate in an immersive\u00a0study away program while earning credits towards their degree. To learn more, please visit NYU Paris . Description du poste de travail NYU Paris est \u00e0 la recherche d'un enseignant \u00a0\u00e0 temps partiel pour enseigner le cours anticip\u00e9\u00a0de premier cycle en Data Science: Advanced Topics in Data Science: Economic Analysis with Data Langue d'enseignement: Anglais L'enseignement commence en septembre 2026 ; les cours se d\u00e9roulent \u00e0 raison de 2,5 heures par semaine pour un trimestre de 14,5 semaines. L'enseignant enseignera \u00e9galement un TD \u00e0 raison de 1,5 heure par semaine. Advanced Topics in Data Science: Economic Analysis with Data Pr\u00e9requis: Introduction to Data Science Ce cours offre une introduction compl\u00e8te \u00e0 l'applied data science \u00e0 travers le prisme de l'analyse \u00e9conomique. Les \u00e9tudiants ma\u00eetriseront le processus de recherche de bout en bout : de l'acquisition automatis\u00e9e de donn\u00e9es et la visualisation avanc\u00e9e \u00e0 la mise en \u0153uvre de cadres modernes d'inf\u00e9rence causale. Ce cours fait le lien entre la science des donn\u00e9es contemporaine et la th\u00e9orie \u00e9conomique classique, en mettant l'accent sur la transition entre les ensembles de donn\u00e9es num\u00e9riques brutes et les m\u00e9thodes \u00e9conom\u00e9triques valid\u00e9es. Il privil\u00e9gie l'application de mod\u00e8les quasi exp\u00e9rimentaux pour d\u00e9m\u00ealer l'endog\u00e9n\u00e9it\u00e9 complexe du march\u00e9, aboutissant \u00e0 une d\u00e9fense formelle des r\u00e9sultats empiriques. Sur NYU et NYU Paris Fond\u00e9e en 1831, l\u2019universit\u00e9 de New York (NYU) est la plus grande universit\u00e9 priv\u00e9e aux Etats Unis. L'universit\u00e9 dispose des campus d\u00e9livrant des dipl\u00f4mes \u00e0 New York, Abu Dhabi et Shanghai et g\u00e8re 11 centres universitaires et programmes de recherche dans plus de 25 pays. NYU Paris est l'un des 11 centres acad\u00e9miques \u00e0 l'\u00e9tranger du r\u00e9seau global NYU. Les \u00e9tudiants de NYU Paris participent \u00e0 un programme d'\u00e9tudes immersives tout en obtenant des cr\u00e9dits pour leur dipl\u00f4me. Pour en savoir plus sur NYU Paris, veuillez visiter le site Web \u00e0 l'adresse https://www.nyu.edu/paris.html . Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Applied Quantitative Science (e.g. Economics, Statistics, Data Science, etc.) and two to three years relevant teaching experience. The appointed person will have full responsibility for teaching and coordinating the course. The course is taught to a small group and is offered to undergraduate students, mostly in their second or third year of study. All students are NYU students enrolled in NYU degrees in New York, Abu Dhabi or Shanghai who come to study at NYU Paris for one semester. Classes take place at our Academic Centre in Paris. Candidates must be eligible to work in France. Qualifications d'instructeur Les candidats doivent avoir un doctorat en\u00a0Sciences quantitatives appliqu\u00e9es (par exemple, \u00e9conomie, statistiques, science des donn\u00e9es...)\u00a0et deux \u00e0 trois ann\u00e9es d'exp\u00e9rience pertinente en enseignement. La personne nomm\u00e9e aura l'enti\u00e8re responsabilit\u00e9 d'enseigner et de coordonner le cours.\u00a0 Le cours\u00a0est enseign\u00e9 a un petit groupe et est offert aux \u00e9tudiants de premier cycle, pour la plupart dans leur deuxi\u00e8me ou troisi\u00e8me ann\u00e9e d'\u00e9tudes. Les \u00e9tudiants sont en premier cycle et viennent des campus de NYU \u00e0 New York, Shanghai et Abu Dhabi afin d'\u00e9tudier \u00e0 NYUParis pour un semestre. Le cours a lieu dans notre centre acad\u00e9mique \u00e0 Paris. Les candidats doivent \u00eatre \u00e9ligibles pour travailler en France. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at apply.interfolio.com/181674 .\u00a0If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Applications must include: (1) a cover letter, (2) C.V., and (3) course outline or synopsis; please be sure to note in particular any relevant teaching experience. Current NYU Paris faculty are encouraged to apply. NYU Paris is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from under-represented individuals in the profession, across color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, and gender and sexual identity. NYU Paris affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. NYU Paris\u00a0 s'engage \u00e0 respecter l'\u00e9galit\u00e9 des chances, la diversit\u00e9 et l'inclusion sociale. Nous encourageons fortement les candidatures de personnes sous-repr\u00e9sent\u00e9es dans la profession, sans distinction de couleur, de croyance, de race, d'origine ethnique et nationale, de capacit\u00e9 physique, de genre et d'identit\u00e9 sexuelle. NYU Paris affirme la valeur de diff\u00e9rentes perspectives afin de s'\u00e9fforcer de construire l'universit\u00e9 la plus forte possible avec la port\u00e9e la plus large. Toutes les demandes de candidature doivent \u00eatre faites en ligne par le biais du Interfolio ByCommittee \u00e0 l'adresse: apply.interfolio.com/181674 . Pour plus d'information sur l'utilisation d' Interfolio Faculty Search veuillez consulter Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Tout dossier doit\u00a0comprendre (1) une lettre de pr\u00e9sentation, (2) un CV, et (3) un plan de cours ou un synopsis; veillez \u00e0 noter en particulier toute exp\u00e9rience d'enseignement pertinente. Les professeurs actuels de NYU Paris sont encourag\u00e9s \u00e0 postuler."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182546": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer, Analytical Statistics", "description": "Posted: Mar 04, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Paris Location: Paris, France NYU Paris is seeking a part-time lecturer to teach the following undergraduate course in Economics: Analytical Statistics Introduction to statistical reasoning. Topics covered include descriptive statistics, calculation of moments, probability theory, an introduction to distribution theory, and an introduction to inference. Lab sessions enable the student to run a wide variety of computer experiments and to simulate all distributions that are discussed as well as to experiment with a wide variety of statistical procedures. Language of instruction: English Teaching commences in September 2026; courses meet for 3 hours a week for a 14.5 week term. NYU Paris is one of 11 study away academic centers in NYU's global network.\u00a0Students at NYU Paris participate in an immersive\u00a0study away program while earning credits towards their degree. To learn more about NYU Paris please visit the website at https://www.nyu.edu/paris.html NYU Paris est \u00e0 la recherche d'un professeur \u00e0 temps partiel pour enseigner le cours de premier cycle en \u00e9conomiques: Analytical Statistics Introduction au raisonnement statistique. Les sujets abord\u00e9s comprennent les statistiques descriptives, le calcul des moments, la th\u00e9orie des probabilit\u00e9s, une introduction \u00e0 la th\u00e9orie de la distribution et une introduction \u00e0 l'inf\u00e9rence. Les s\u00e9ances de laboratoire permettent \u00e0 l'\u00e9tudiant de r\u00e9aliser une grande vari\u00e9t\u00e9 d'exp\u00e9riences sur ordinateur et de simuler toutes les distributions abord\u00e9es, ainsi que d'exp\u00e9rimenter une grande vari\u00e9t\u00e9 de proc\u00e9dures statistiques. Langue d'enseignement: Anglais L'enseignement commence en septembre 2026; les cours se rencontrent 3 heures par semaine pour une dur\u00e9e de 14,5 semaines. NYU Paris est l'un des 11 centres acad\u00e9miques \u00e0 l'\u00e9tranger du r\u00e9seau global NYU. Les \u00e9tudiants de NYU Paris participent \u00e0 un programme d'\u00e9tudes immersives tout en obtenant des cr\u00e9dits pour leur dipl\u00f4me. Pour en savoir plus sur NYU Paris, veuillez visiter le site Web \u00e0 l'adresse https://www.nyu.edu/paris.html . Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Economics, and two or three semesters of teaching or comparable experience. The appointed person will have full responsibility for teaching and coordinating the course. The course is taught in a seminar, discussion-based style and offered to undergraduate students, mostly in their second or third year of study. All students are NYU students enrolled in NYU degrees in New York, Abu Dhabi or Shanghai who come to study at NYU Paris for one semester. Classes take place at our Academic Centre in Paris. Les candidats doivent avoir un doctorat en Sciences Economiques plus deux ou trois semestres d'enseignement ou d'exp\u00e9rience comparable. La personne nomm\u00e9e aura l'enti\u00e8re responsabilit\u00e9 d'enseigner et de coordonner le cours.\u00a0 Le cours\u00a0est enseign\u00e9 en forme de s\u00e9minaire et est offert aux \u00e9tudiants de premier cycle, pour la plupart dans leur deuxi\u00e8me ou troisi\u00e8me ann\u00e9e d'\u00e9tudes. Les \u00e9tudiants sont en premier cycle et viennent des campus de NYU \u00e0 New York, Shanghai et Abu Dhabi afin d'\u00e9tudier \u00e0 NYU Paris pour un semestre. Le cours a lieu dans notre centre acad\u00e9mique \u00e0 Paris. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at apply.interfolio.com/182546 If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Applications must include: (1) a cover letter, (2) C.V., and (3) course outline or synopsis; please be sure to note in particular any relevant teaching experience. Candidates must be eligible to work in France. Current NYU Paris faculty are encouraged to apply. NYU Paris\u00a0is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from under-represented individuals in the profession, across color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, and gender and sexual identity. NYU Paris\u00a0affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. NYU Paris s'engage \u00e0 respecter l'\u00e9galit\u00e9 des chances, la diversit\u00e9 et l'inclusion sociale. Nous encourageons fortement les candidatures de personnes sous-repr\u00e9sent\u00e9es dans la profession, sans distinction de couleur, de croyance, de race, d'origine ethnique et nationale, de capacit\u00e9 physique, de genre et d'identit\u00e9 sexuelle. NYU Paris affirme la valeur de diff\u00e9rentes perspectives afin de s'\u00e9fforcer de construire l'universit\u00e9 la plus forte possible avec la port\u00e9e la plus large. Toutes les demandes de candidature doivent \u00eatre faites en ligne par le biais du Interfolio ByCommittee \u00e0 l'adresse Pour plus d'information sur l'utilisation d' Interfolio Faculty Search veuillez consulter Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Tout dossier doit\u00a0comprendre (1) une lettre de pr\u00e9sentation, (2)un CV, et (3) un plan de cours ou un synopsis; veillez \u00e0 noter en particulier toute exp\u00e9rience d'enseignement pertinente. Les candidats doivent \u00eatre \u00e9ligibles pour travailler en France. Les professeurs actuels de NYU Paris sont encourag\u00e9s \u00e0 postuler."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185513": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer, Causal Inference", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Paris Location: Paris, France NYU Paris is seeking a part-time lecturer to teach an expected undergraduate course in Data Science, Causal Inference. The course is held at NYU academic center in Paris and is offered to undergraduate students. Language of instruction: English Teaching commences in September 2026; courses meet for 2.5 hours per week for a 14.5 week term.Lecturers will be expected to teach an additional recitation session for 1.5 hours per week. Causal Inference Prerequisite: Intro to Data Science Teaches the fundamentals of how to reason about causality and make causal determinations using empirical data. Introduces the counterfactual framework of causal inference and then considers a variety of approaches, starting with the most basic experimental designs to more complex observational methods, for making inferences about causal relationships from the data.\u00a0Addresses questions about data generation and interpretation and provides hands-on implementation of causal inference techniques with real data sets. Problem sets will contain a mixture of both theoretical and applied questions in order to reinforce key concepts. Examines theoretical concepts and their applications; uses R or Python\u00a0programming language. About NYU and NYU Paris Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university\u00a0in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 11 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU Paris is one of 11 study away academic centers in NYU's global network.\u00a0Students at NYU Paris participate in an immersive\u00a0study away program while earning credits towards their degree. To learn more, please visit NYU Paris . Description du poste de travail NYU Paris est \u00e0 la recherche d'un enseignant \u00a0\u00e0 temps partiel pour enseigner le cours anticip\u00e9\u00a0de premier cycle en Data Science: Causal Inference Langue d'enseignement: Anglais L'enseignement commence en septembre 2026 ; les cours se d\u00e9roulent \u00e0 raison de 2,5 heures par semaine pour un trimestre de 14,5 semaines. L'enseignant enseignera \u00e9galement un TD \u00e0 raison de 1,5 heure par semaine. Causal Inference Pr\u00e9requis: Intro to Data Science Le cours traite les principes fondamentaux du raisonnement sur la causalit\u00e9 et des d\u00e9terminations causales \u00e0 l'aide de donn\u00e9es empiriques. Il commence avec\u00a0le cadre contrefactuel de l'inf\u00e9rence causale, puis passe \u00e0 l'exploration d'une vari\u00e9t\u00e9 d'approches, en commen\u00e7ant par les plans exp\u00e9rimentaux les plus basiques jusqu'aux m\u00e9thodes d'observation plus complexes, pour faire des inf\u00e9rences sur les relations causales \u00e0 partir des donn\u00e9es. Il aborde les questions relatives \u00e0 la g\u00e9n\u00e9ration et \u00e0 l'interpr\u00e9tation des donn\u00e9es et propose une mise en \u0153uvre pratique des techniques d'inf\u00e9rence causale avec des ensembles de donn\u00e9es r\u00e9elles. Les ensembles de probl\u00e8mes contiendront un m\u00e9lange de questions th\u00e9oriques et appliqu\u00e9es afin de renforcer les concepts cl\u00e9s.Examine les concepts th\u00e9oriques et leurs applications; utilise le langage de programmation R ou Python. Sur NYU et NYU Paris Fond\u00e9e en 1831, l\u2019universit\u00e9 de New York (NYU) est la plus grande universit\u00e9 priv\u00e9e aux Etats Unis. L'universit\u00e9 dispose des campus d\u00e9livrant des dipl\u00f4mes \u00e0 New York, Abu Dhabi et Shanghai et g\u00e8re 11 centres universitaires et programmes de recherche dans plus de 25 pays. NYU Paris est l'un des 11 centres acad\u00e9miques \u00e0 l'\u00e9tranger du r\u00e9seau global NYU. Les \u00e9tudiants de NYU Paris participent \u00e0 un programme d'\u00e9tudes immersives tout en obtenant des cr\u00e9dits pour leur dipl\u00f4me. Pour en savoir plus sur NYU Paris, veuillez visiter le site Web \u00e0 l'adresse https://www.nyu.edu/paris.html . Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Data Science or a related field,\u00a0and two to three years relevant teaching experience. The appointed person will have full responsibility for teaching and coordinating the course. The course is taught to a small group and is offered to undergraduate students, mostly in their second or third year of study. All students are NYU students enrolled in NYU degrees in New York, Abu Dhabi or Shanghai who come to study at NYU Paris for one semester. Classes take place at our Academic Centre in Paris. Candidates must be eligible to work in France. Qualifications d'instructeur Les candidats doivent avoir un doctorat en Sciences des donn\u00e9es ou une domaine proche,\u00a0et deux \u00e0 trois ann\u00e9es d'exp\u00e9rience pertinente en enseignement. La personne nomm\u00e9e aura l'enti\u00e8re responsabilit\u00e9 d'enseigner et de coordonner le cours.\u00a0 Le cours\u00a0est enseign\u00e9 a un petit groupe et est offert aux \u00e9tudiants de premier cycle, pour la plupart dans leur deuxi\u00e8me ou troisi\u00e8me ann\u00e9e d'\u00e9tudes. Les \u00e9tudiants sont en premier cycle et viennent des campus de NYU \u00e0 New York, Shanghai et Abu Dhabi afin d'\u00e9tudier \u00e0 NYUParis pour un semestre. Le cours a lieu dans notre centre acad\u00e9mique \u00e0 Paris. Les candidats doivent \u00eatre \u00e9ligibles pour travailler en France. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at\u00a0.\u00a0If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Applications must include: (1) a cover letter, (2) C.V., and (3) course outline or synopsis; please be sure to note in particular any relevant teaching experience. Current NYU Paris faculty are encouraged to apply. NYU Paris is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from under-represented individuals in the profession, across color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, and gender and sexual identity. NYU Paris affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. NYU Paris\u00a0 s'engage \u00e0 respecter l'\u00e9galit\u00e9 des chances, la diversit\u00e9 et l'inclusion sociale. Nous encourageons fortement les candidatures de personnes sous-repr\u00e9sent\u00e9es dans la profession, sans distinction de couleur, de croyance, de race, d'origine ethnique et nationale, de capacit\u00e9 physique, de genre et d'identit\u00e9 sexuelle. NYU Paris affirme la valeur de diff\u00e9rentes perspectives afin de s'\u00e9fforcer de construire l'universit\u00e9 la plus forte possible avec la port\u00e9e la plus large. Toutes les demandes de candidature doivent \u00eatre faites en ligne par le biais du Interfolio ByCommittee \u00e0 l'adresse: . Pour plus d'information sur l'utilisation d' Interfolio Faculty Search veuillez consulter Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Tout dossier doit\u00a0comprendre (1) une lettre de pr\u00e9sentation, (2) un CV, et (3) un plan de cours ou un synopsis; veillez \u00e0 noter en particulier toute exp\u00e9rience d'enseignement pertinente. Les professeurs actuels de NYU Paris sont encourag\u00e9s \u00e0 postuler. Collapse Instructions"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193176": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer, US-China Relations", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: NYU Washington, DC Location: Washington, DC NYU\u2019s Washington, DC Academic Center is seeking a local, part-time lecturer to teach an undergraduate social sciences course, \u201cUS-China Relations,\" both semesters of the academic year, starting Spring 2027. Classes run for two hours and forty minutes once a week. The class meeting pattern will be discussed with qualified applicants during the interview process. In addition to the time required for course preparation, classroom instruction, and assessment, the instructor is required to hold a minimum of one set 60-minute office hour per instruction day, during which the instructor will be available to meet with students. The instructor role includes all preparation, delivery, and grading. Using Washington, DC as a learning tool (e.g., a guest speaker, an excursion) is an expectation for the course. Courses are to be intellectually challenging in content, and rigorous student assessment is required. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who share NYU Washington, DC\u2019s commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion, and who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life at our site. NYU is committed to providing a campus community that reflects and enacts the values of inclusion, diversity, belonging, and equity that inform academic excellence. Course Description: This course examines the complexities of the bilateral relationship between the People\u2019s Republic of\u00a0China\u00a0(China) and the United States (US), focusing on the states' historical rapport, major debates, and current relations. Topics include Sino-US economic relations, media reporting, variation in political systems, global politics, climate/energy issues, military affairs, and contested territories. About NYU and NYU Washington, DC: Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 12 global academic centers, along with research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU Washington, DC is one of NYU\u2019s global academic centers where students can participate in study away while earning credits towards their degree. To learn more about NYU Washington, DC please visit the website: https://www.nyu.edu/washington-dc.html Qualified applicants will ideally have 2-3 years of experience teaching at the university level, as well as a PhD or other terminal degree in a relevant field. We are also interested in candidates with relevant professional expertise or practical experience. All applications must be made online through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/193176 . Applications must include a cover letter and an updated CV. In the cover letter, please highlight any relevant experience you have in teaching. Consideration for applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed jointly by NYU Washington, DC and NYU New York. The salary will be based on equivalent and appropriate rates for the course. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. Please direct any questions to Kimberly Cheng, Assistant Director for Academic Affairs, at kac780@nyu.edu. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193177": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer, Social Entrepreneurship", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: NYU Washington, DC Location: Washington, DC NYU\u2019s Washington, DC Academic Center is seeking a local, part-time lecturer to teach a three-credit undergraduate course, \u201cSocial Entrepreneurship,\u201d every Spring semester starting Spring 2027. The Spring semester runs from mid-January to early May. Classes meet once a week, for 2 hours and forty minutes. In addition to the time required for course preparation, classroom instruction, and assessment, the instructor is required to hold a minimum of one set 60-minute office hour per instruction day, during which the instructor will be available to meet with students. The instructor role includes all preparation, delivery, and grading. Leveraging Washington, DC as a learning environment (e.g., a guest speakers, excursions) is an expectation for the course. Courses are to be intellectually challenging in content, and rigorous student assessment is required. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who share NYU Washington, DC\u2019s commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion, and who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life at our site. Course Description Social Entrepreneurship is an emerging and rapidly changing business field that examines the practice of identifying, starting and growing successful mission-driven for-profit and nonprofit ventures\u2014that is, organizations that strive to advance social change through innovative solutions. This course, rooted in stakeholder theory, is designed to provide a socially relevant academic experience in order to help students gain in-depth insights into economic and social value creation across a number of areas including poverty alleviation, energy, health and sustainability. Students will have the opportunity to find and test new ideas and solutions to social problems, create sustainable business models, identify funding options and alternatives, learn about evolving legal and governance structures, learn how to measure social impact and scale a social enterprise to name a few. This course will provide students with a toolkit and frameworks that can be used to start a social venture, applied within a social venture or within other types of organizations to influence social change. About NYU and NYU Washington, DC Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 12 global academic centers, along with research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU Washington, DC is one of NYU\u2019s global academic centers where students can participate in study away while earning credits towards their degree. To learn more about NYU Washington, DC please visit the website: https://www.nyu.edu/washington-dc.html We are seeking candidates with a mix of education/training and professional practice, as well as experience teaching courses at the undergraduate level, in a degree-granting program. Industry experience and terminal degree (PhD, JD, MFA, MPS, MBA) would be a plus factor for qualified candidates.The salary will be based on equivalent and appropriate part-time faculty rates for the course and location. All applications must be made online through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/193177 . Applications must include a cover letter and an updated CV. In the cover letter, please highlight any relevant experience you have in teaching. Please also let us know if you are interested in teaching one specific semester, both semesters, and/or the summer term. Consideration for applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed jointly by NYU Washington, DC and NYU New York. The salary will be based on equivalent and appropriate rates for the course. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. Please direct any questions to Kimberly Cheng, Assistant Director for Academic Affairs, at kac780@nyu.edu. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193304": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer, Politics", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: NYU Washington, DC Location: Washington, DC NYU\u2019s Washington, DC Academic Center is seeking a local, part-time lecturer to teach a four credit undergraduate course, \u201cPrivate Influence in Public Policy,\u201d to teach every Spring starting Spring 2027. The Spring semester runs mid-January 20 to mid-May. Classes meet once a week, for 2 hours and forty minutes. In addition to the time required for course preparation, classroom instruction, and assessment, the instructor is required to hold a minimum of one set 60-minute office hour per instruction day, during which the instructor will be available to meet with students. The instructor role includes all preparation, delivery, and grading. Using Washington, DC as a learning tool (e.g., a guest speaker, an excursion) is an expectation for the course. Courses are to be intellectually challenging in content, and rigorous student assessment is required. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who share NYU Washington, DC\u2019s commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion, and who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life at our site. Course Description In this course, we will examine the role of private influence in public policy from a variety of theoretical and empirical perspectives. Course topics will include in-depth analysis of mechanisms of influence (e.g., selection of sympathetic incumbents, the provision of incentives for public officials, and the provision of information), objects of influence (voter choices, legislative behavior, bureaucratic decisions), collective action, and organizational maintenance. Special attention will be paid to overcoming the challenges associated with demonstrating influence empirically. About NYU and NYU Washington, DC Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 12 global academic centers, along with research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU Washington, DC is one of NYU\u2019s global academic centers where students can participate in study away while earning credits towards their degree. To learn more about NYU Washington, DC please visit the website: https://www.nyu.edu/washington-dc.html All part-time instructors are expected to have a PhD or a terminal degree in the area of their teaching (advanced ABD doctoral candidates may also apply.) Qualified applicants will ideally have 2-3 years of experience teaching at the university level. In some instances, significant professional expertise and experience in a relevant field will be considered a suitable qualification. The salary will be based on equivalent and appropriate part-time faculty rates for the course and location. All applications must be made online through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/193304 . Applications must include a cover letter and an updated CV. In the cover letter, please highlight any relevant experience you have in teaching. Please also let us know if you are interested in teaching one specific semester, both semesters, and/or the summer term. Consideration for applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed jointly by NYU Washington, DC and NYU New York. The salary will be based on equivalent and appropriate rates for the course. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. Please direct any questions to Kimberly Cheng, Assistant Director for Academic Affairs, at kac780@nyu.edu. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193306": {"error": "RuntimeError: interfolio position api status=400"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192784": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer in Business of Film", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Berlin Location: Berlin, Germany New York University (NYU) Berlin is seeking a part-time lecturer to teach Craft and Commerce: Berlin International Film Festival . Utilizing the Berlin International Film Festival as its primary case study, this course investigates the contemporary film industry through the lens of creativity, capital, and global market forces. Students will explore the entire lifecycle of a motion picture, from early development and financing to international sales and distribution, while examining how digital technology, streaming platforms, and evolving consumer habits are reshaping the entertainment economy. At the heart of the curriculum is the Berlinale\u2014a pivotal junction where cinema and commerce converge\u2014providing a rich environment for classroom learning and direct field observation. Through festival screenings, industry sessions, and exposure to professional platforms such as the European Film Market and the Co-Production Market, students from across disciplines will learn to assess film projects by balancing artistic identity with commercial potential. The language of instruction is English, and the course is planned to be offered in Spring 2027. The spring semester runs from January 18 to May 5, 2027, with a week-long break from March 15-21. In this term, the class meets six times for a total of 19 contact hours, with a flexible schedule to allow for participation in the Berlinale, where possible. NYU is committed to providing a campus community that reflects and enacts the values of inclusion, diversity, belonging, and equity, which inform academic excellence. The lecturer is expected to support and promote this commitment both in and outside of the classroom through critical approaches to the formation of social difference. NYU Berlin offers you: An inspiring and rewarding teaching position in an innovative university setting with opportunities to co-design and co-shape our processes. Opportunities for personal and professional development, including access to NYU's Global Faculty Fund, participation in NYU Berlin's IDBEA (Identity, Diversity, Belonging, Equity, Accessibility) Council, as well as training in topics such as, for example, anti-racist teaching and syllabus design. A diverse, engaged and welcoming team. About New York University (NYU) and NYU Berlin Founded in 1831, New York University is one of the largest and most prestigious private universities in the United States. NYU is based in New York but also operates branch campuses and research programs in other parts of the U.S. and around the world. NYU Berlin is one of NYU\u2019s global study-away sites and provides NYU students with the opportunity to spend a semester studying in Berlin. The program is fully integrated into NYU\u2019s curriculum and designed for BA students in the social sciences, arts and humanities who want to earn credit in their major \u2013 including art, art history, drama, environmental studies, European studies, German, history, music, politics, psychology, and sociology \u2013 while having a transformative experience abroad. Seminars and lectures take place at the academic center located within the complex of the Kulturbrauerei in Prenzlauer Berg and at NYU Berlin\u2019s art and drama studio in Kreuzberg. The program size is 120 students per semester, as well as various January Term and Summer Schools. NYU aims to be among the greenest urban campuses in the U.S. and carbon neutral by 2040. Learn more at nyu.edu/nyugreen. A Ph.D., MFA, MBA, MPS or equivalent training in a relevant field, as well as experience teaching courses at the undergraduate level within a degree-granting program Industry experience in film, TV, or music required A minimum of three years teaching experience at the university level Fluency in English and teaching experience in English German work-permit and tax number at the time of application, covering the entire period of the employment Preference will be given to candidates who have been students or postdoctoral fellows in the US and/or have international teaching experience and who possess a record of research and publications in the field Women, ethnic minorities, persons of diverse sexual orientations and/or fluid gender identities, and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. All applications must be made online through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/191051 . If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the Job Applicant's Guide useful. The deadline is October 23, 2026. In your cover letter, please address your approach to teaching and learning, how your particular specialization may influence the teaching of the course, what you believe to be the most important learning outcomes from the course for the students, and what particular strategies you are using to empower your students to thrive. Please also upload either a list of at least two referees under \u201cContact Reference\u201d or upload two finished letters of recommendation under \u201cNon-Confidential Letter of Recommendation or Evaluation\u201d. If available, please submit past syllabi/ workshop programs that illustrate your teaching philosophy. NYU Berlin is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from under-represented individuals in the profession, across color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, and gender and sexual identity. NYU Berlin affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. Applications will be reviewed jointly by NYU Berlin and NYU New York. Interviews of short-listed candidates will be held online or in person at NYU Berlin and with faculty from NYU New York. Please direct any inquiries to berlin.academics@nyu.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193289": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer in Interactive Media Arts", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Berlin Location: Berlin, Germany New York University (NYU) Berlin is seeking a part-time lecturer for a course called \u201cAugmenting the Gallery. \u201d The course is offered by NYU Berlin in conjunction with the ITP/IMA Department of the Tisch School of the Arts. The course is taught in English and runs approximately from January 18 to May 5, 2027. The course meets once per week for three-and-a-half hours on NYU Berlin\u2019s arts campus at St. Agnes. The sessions are a mixture of lectures, discussions, practical exercises, and guest visits and excursions. Within the physical walls of GLAMs (galleries, libraries, archives, and museums), we\u2019re used to seeing traditional wall labels, audio guides, and maps that don\u2019t move. As cultural institutions confront the impacts of shifting 21st-century audience demands and the reach of artificial intelligence, those staples of the past are no longer enough. How can creative technologists design digital experiences that extend works of art without turning a traditional physical exhibition space into a tech demo, or abandoning it all together? Thinking further, how can digital tools expand upon the power and importance of a collection of artworks without distracting from it, or even purposefully challenge a collection\u2019s problematic aspects? And how can these tools make collections more accessible to a wider audience? This course mobilizes resources from media studies, museography, art history, sociology, interaction design, and web design and development to answer these questions. The course is open to students from a variety of academic backgrounds interested in gaining hands-on experience in applying new technologies to virtual exhibitions and cultural heritage, and participants complete the course with a portfolio-ready project that they\u2019ll present to industry representatives in Berlin. Candidates should be passionate about teaching, fluent in English, and have an academic background that reflects the course themes. Important is a broad academic outlook and the ability to cater to the individual needs of the students. Ideal candidates should be excellent teachers, who can demonstrate an ability to lead rigorous class discussion, develop assignments that lead students through critical analysis of complex and multi-layered investigations, offer constructive criticism of student projects, and understand the importance of student-led inquiry in the classroom. You should be an excellent teacher with a student-focused pedagogical style. Only applicants with a graduate degree (Masters, PhD) OR a college degree (BS, BA, BFA, etc.) and ten years of relevant professional experience will be considered. Applicants with a hybrid academic or professional background (e.g. art/design and engineering/science) are especially encouraged to apply.Ideal candidates should be excellent teachers and accomplished professional artists in their own right, who can demonstrate an ability to connect theory and practice with young artists with a varying degree of experience with course themes, and encourage student-led inquiry in the classroom. Additional Qualifications: Professional experience in an international environment. A minimum of 3 years teaching experience at university level. Fluency in English and experience teaching in English. German work-permit and tax number at the time of application, covering the entire period of the employment. Preference will be given to candidates who have international teaching experience. Women, ethnic minorities, persons of minority sexual orientation or gender identity, and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. All applications must be made online through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/193289 . The deadline is October 20, 2026. In your cover letter, please address your approach to teaching and learning, how your particular specialization may influence the teaching of the course, what you believe to be the most important learning outcomes from the course for the students, and what particular strategies you are using to empower your students to thrive. Please also upload either a list of at least two referees under \u201cContact Reference\u201d or upload two finished letters of recommendation under \u201cNon-Confidential Letter of Recommendation or Evaluation\u201d. If available, please submit past syllabi/ workshop programs that illustrate your teaching philosophy. NYU is committed to providing a campus community that reflects and enacts the values of inclusion, diversity, belonging, and equity that inform academic excellence. NYU Berlin\u00a0is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from all interested persons including without limitation under-represented individuals in the profession, across religions, color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, and gender and sexual identity. NYU Berlin\u00a0affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. Applications will be reviewed jointly by NYU Berlin and NYU New York. Interviews of short-listed candidates will be held online or in person. Please direct any inquiries to berlin.academics@nyu.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr NYU is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193567": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer in Marketing", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Berlin Location: Berlin, Germany New York University (NYU) Berlin is seeking a part-time lecturer for a course called \u201cArts Marketing. \u201d The course is offered by NYU Berlin in conjunction with the Stern School of Business, and counts towards the BEMT minor . The arts sector is in the midst of a rapid structural shift, placing new and evolving demands not just on artists, producers, venue directors, and marketers alike. As audience engagement and promotional channels change just as quickly, intuitive outreach is no longer sufficient. Navigating this climate requires analytical rigor and tactical precision. To remain viable and impactful, cultural marketers must adapt by pairing operational efficiency with genuine creative resourcefulness. This course is designed to be a self-contained introduction to marketing in the arts. It will focus primarily on live performing arts, particularly those encompassed by the term Tanztheater, and can also include museums, galleries, and other performance venues. The language of instruction is English, and the course is planned to be offered in Spring 2027. The spring semester runs from January 18 to May 5, 2027, with a week-long break from March 15-21. In this term, the class meets eight times for a total of 24 contact hours. NYU Berlin offers you: An inspiring and rewarding teaching position in an innovative university setting with opportunities to co-design and co-shape our processes. Opportunities for personal and professional development, including access to NYU's Global Faculty Fund, participation in NYU Berlin's IDBEA (Identity, Diversity, Belonging, Equity, Accessibility) Council, as well as training in topics such as, for example, anti-racist teaching and syllabus design. A diverse, engaged and welcoming team. About New York University (NYU) and NYU Berlin Founded in 1831, New York University is one of the largest and most prestigious private universities in the United States. NYU is based in New York but also operates branch campuses and research programs in other parts of the U.S. and around the world. NYU Berlin is one of NYU\u2019s global study-away sites and provides NYU students with the opportunity to spend a semester studying in Berlin. The program is fully integrated into NYU\u2019s curriculum and designed for BA students in the social sciences, arts and humanities who want to earn credit in their major \u2013 including art, art history, drama, environmental studies, European studies, German, history, music, politics, psychology, and sociology \u2013 while having a transformative experience abroad. Seminars and lectures take place at the academic center located within the complex of the Kulturbrauerei in Prenzlauer Berg and at NYU Berlin\u2019s art and drama studio in Kreuzberg. The program size is 120 students per semester, as well as various January Term and Summer Schools. NYU aims to be among the greenest urban campuses in the U.S. and carbon neutral by 2040. Learn more at nyu.edu/nyugreen. A Ph.D., MFA, MBA, MPS or equivalent training in a relevant field, as well as experience teaching courses at the undergraduate level within a degree-granting program. Industry experience in film, TV, or music would be a plus factor for qualified candidates. A minimum of three years teaching experience at the university level. Fluency in English and teaching experience in English. German work-permit and tax number at the time of application, covering the entire period of the employment. Preference will be given to candidates who have been students or postdoctoral fellows in the US and/or have international teaching experience and who possess a record of research and publications in the field. Women, ethnic minorities, persons of diverse sexual orientations and/or fluid gender identities, and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. All applications must be made online through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/193567 . The deadline is TBD. If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the Job Applicant's Guide useful. In your cover letter, please address your approach to teaching and learning, how your particular specialization may influence the teaching of the course, what you believe to be the most important learning outcomes from the course for the students, and what particular strategies you are using to empower your students to thrive. Please also upload either a list of at least two referees under \u201cContact Reference\u201d or upload two finished letters of recommendation under \u201cNon-Confidential Letter of Recommendation or Evaluation\u201d. If available, please submit past syllabi/ workshop programs that illustrate your teaching philosophy. NYU Berlin is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from under-represented individuals in the profession, across color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, and gender and sexual identity. NYU Berlin affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. Applications will be reviewed jointly by NYU Berlin and NYU New York. Interviews of short-listed candidates will be held online or in person at NYU Berlin and with faculty from NYU New York. Please direct any inquiries to berlin.academics@nyu.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/103817": {"title": "Part Time Lecturer - History of the Israeli-Palestinian Conflict", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Tel Aviv Location: Tel Aviv NYU Tel Aviv is seeking a part-time lecturer to co-teach a course on History of the Israeli-Palestinian Conflict , under the sponsorship of the History Department at New York University. The course will be taught in the Fall 2023 semester (August 31 - December 11, 2023).\u00a0 For further course content information, please review the sample syllabus . Course Description In History of the Israeli-Palestinian Conflict , students will study the main political junctions in the Palestinian-Israeli conflict and will connect them to their reincarnations in contemporary times in Israel, the West Bank, and Gaza (the Occupied Palestinian Territories). This will include analysis and study of the theoretical frameworks and approaches to the study of the conflict, the history of Ottoman and Mandate Palestine, Zionism, the politics, popular culture and daily life of Israelis and Palestinians. Among the themes that will be studied are the history of Zionism and its various streams, Arab and Palestinian nationalism, the Arab-Jewish identity, the Nakba and the founding of the State of Israel, Israel and the memory of the Holocaust, the Arab-Israeli wars, the Israeli political and legal systems, the Oslo accords and peace negotiations, and contemporary political developments. The course highlights the way current political and cultural elements in the life of Israelis and Palestinians are offshoots of previous processes that shaped the Palestinian and Israeli identity, the sense of belonging, the collective memory and horizons of expectations. About NYU and NYU Tel Aviv Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university\u00a0in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 11 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries.\u00a0NYU Tel Aviv is an undergraduate\u00a0study away program of exceptional quality with teaching strengths across a number of academic clusters, including Hebrew and Judaic studies; Middle Eastern and Islamic studies; business and innovation; biology and chemistry; and social science. Our faculty, drawn from local universities, has an excellent record in teaching and research.\u00a0NYU Tel Aviv\u2019s academic center is located at 17 Brandeis Street in Tel Aviv, just a few minutes walk from HaYarkon Park.\u00a0For more information, please visit our website . Qualified\u00a0candidates should hold a PhD in history, middle eastern studies\u00a0or related field and have a strong record of research as well as experience\u00a0teaching. Candidates who have experience\u00a0in teaching within an American university system to a diverse English speaking undergraduate student body are strongly encouraged to apply.\u00a0Preference will be given to those who have a minimum of 2-3 years' experience teaching at the university level in English.\u00a0Applicants are required to have a legal right to work in Israel. NYU Tel Aviv\u00a0is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from under-represented individuals in the profession, across color, creed, race, ethnic and national origin, physical ability, and gender and sexual identity. NYU Tel Aviv\u00a0affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. All applications must be made online through Interfolio By Committee at http://apply.interfolio.com/103817 . If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search,\u00a0you may find the Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search useful. Applications in the form of a current C.V., a letter of interest, a sample syllabus, and student course evaluations (if available) should be submitted online to Interfolio no later than August 1, 2023.\u00a0 We regret that we are unable to notify unsuccessful candidates."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/163116": {"title": "Part Time Lecturer - Introduction to Marketing", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Tel Aviv Location: Tel Aviv NYU Tel Aviv is seeking a part-time lecturer to teach a course on Introduction to Marketing , under the sponsorship of the Marketing Department in the Stern School of Business of New York University. The course is taught in both the Fall (late August-mid-December) and Spring (late January-mid-May) semesters . The course provides an overview of the fundamentals of marketing. This includes an in-depth analysis of the customer, the company, and the competitive environment in which they interact (the 3C\u2019s); an understanding of marketing strategy (the STP\u2014segmentation, targeting, and positioning); and tactics that define the marketing mix (the 4P\u2019s-- pricing, promotion, product, and place). For further course content information, please review the sample syllabus . About NYU and NYU Tel Aviv Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university\u00a0in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 11 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries.\u00a0NYU Tel Aviv is an undergraduate\u00a0study away program of exceptional quality with teaching strengths across a number of academic clusters, including hebrew and judaic studies; middle eastern and islamic studies; business and innovation; biology and chemistry; and social science. Our faculty, drawn from local universities, has an excellent record in teaching and research.\u00a0NYU Tel Aviv\u2019s academic center is located at 17 Brandeis Street in Tel Aviv, just a few minutes walk from HaYarkon Park.\u00a0For more information, please visit our website . Qualified candidates should hold a PhD in marketing, business, economics, or a related field and have a strong record of research as well as experience teaching. Candidates who have experience in teaching within an American university or international system to a diverse English speaking undergraduate student body are strongly encouraged to apply. Preference will be given to those who have a minimum of 2-3 years' experience teaching at the university level in English. Applicants are required to have a legal right to work in Israel. All applications must be made online through Interfolio By Committee at apply.interfolio.com/163116 . If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search,\u00a0you may find the Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search useful. Applications in the form of a current C.V., a letter of interest, a sample syllabus, and student course evaluations (if available) should be submitted online to Interfolio.\u00a0 We regret that we are unable to notify unsuccessful candidates."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/173661": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer: Law, Business, and Society", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Prague Location: NYU Prague NYU Prague is seeking a Lecturer to teach Law, Business & Society, beginning in the Spring 2026 semester (mid-January-mid-May). Course Description This course is an introductory course that provides students who have no previous knowledge with a basic understanding of the American legal system. The instructor will present legal cases and hypotheticals from the perspective of multiple stakeholders while focusing on the connection between law, business, and society. The instructor will also demonstrate how laws may reflect societal norms, promote material well-being and reflect economic/historical conditions. In addition, the instructor will illustrate the inherent difficulty in predicting exactly how laws will be applied. The course consists of a common core of customized course readings drawn upon judicial decisions, statutes, scholarly works and multimedia. This course emphasizes analytical writing of the type required in business and the written assignments are designed to help students learn how to engage in thorough analysis, address arguments made by both sides, and write clearly, concisely and convincingly. Teaching and Learning Expectations Instructors are expected to participate in the drafting, grading and administration of a variety of assignments throughout the course, including weekly homework, short paper assignments, debates and exams. Each class session may include a variety of activities, including discussion, writing, debates, and other participatory exercises. These activities are designed to promote a reflective dialogue about course themes, and the instructor\u2019s teaching style should be highly interactive. The instructor is also responsible for: Reviewing course material and generally adhering to a common syllabus Creating a classroom environment where students feel comfortable participating and learning Providing extra support to students through office hours, when requested Keeping up-to-date with advancements in the field and integrating new knowledge into lessons Candidates should have teaching experience in law, business ethics, or related disciplines, a strong knowledge of U.S. legal frameworks with openness to global perspectives, and the ability to engage a diverse, international student body in a dynamic learning environment. Please submit a Cover Letter and your CV via Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/173661 . Recommendation letters may be included with the application and will be considered as part of the review process."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/173664": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer: Economics", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Prague Location: NYU Prague NYU Prague is seeking a Lecturer to teach Economics of Global Business , an in-person, core Stern School of Business course, beginning in Spring 2026. This course provides students with a framework for understanding the international macroeconomic environment in which firms operate. The course explores topics such as economic growth, exchange rates, international capital flows, fiscal and monetary policy, trade balances, and the impact of globalization on business strategy and performance. The lecturer will be responsible for delivering rigorous and engaging lectures, guiding class discussions, designing and grading exams and assignments, and supporting students in developing analytical tools to evaluate economic conditions across different countries and regions. Responsibilities also include holding office hours, fostering an inclusive and intellectually challenging classroom environment, and maintaining NYU\u2019s high academic standards. Candidates should hold an advanced degree in economics or a related field, have demonstrated experience teaching macroeconomics or international economics at the undergraduate level, and possess the ability to engage a diverse and international student body. Recommendation letters are welcome in support of applications. Please submit a Cover Letter and your CV via interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/173664 . Recommendation letters may be included with the application andd will be considered as part of the review process."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/173666": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer: Philosophy", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Prague Location: NYU Prague NYU Prague is seeking a part-time lecturer to teach a Philosophy course: \" Existentialism and Phenomenology ,\" beginning in the Spring 2027 semester (mid-January through mid-May). This course provides a survey of two of the most influential movements of twentieth-century European philosophy, which each attempted to articulate a \u201cnew beginning\u201d to philosophical reflection. Key themes such as freedom, responsibility, authenticity, consciousness, and intentionality will be explored, as well as the interactions and mutual influences between these two currents. The course should give some attention to the particular contributions of Central European thinkers, as well as the fruitful dialogue with other disciplines such as literary and political science. Major figures to be discussed could include Kafke, Kierkegaard, Nietzsche, Brentano, Husserl, Heidegger, Sartre, Camus, Merleau-Ponty, De Beauvoir, Arendt, Ingarden and Pato\u010dka. The instructor will be responsible for preparing and delivering intellectually engaging lectures, leading seminar-style class discussions encouraging critical reflection and debate, and guiding students in close reading and interpretation of complex philosophical texts. Additional duties include designing, administering and grading written assignments and exams, providing constructive feedback, holding office hours, and fostering an inclusive classroom environment that fosters diverse perspectives and open exchange in line with NYU practice. Candidates should hold an advanced degree in philosophy, have demonstrated teaching experience in modern European philosophy in English and be able to engage a diverse, international cohort of undergraduate students in rigorous and reflective study. Recommendation letters are welcome in support of applications. Please submit a Cover Letter and your CV. Recommendation letters may be included with the application and will be considered as part of the review process."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/173667": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer: Finance", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Prague Location: NYU Prague NYU Prague is seeking a Lecturer to teach Topics in European Finance , an introductory course on European banking and finance that provides students with a comprehensive perspective on international finance in historical and contemporary context. The course examines the fundamental purpose of financial systems, the reasons for regional differences in European practice, and the diverse approaches to further development and integration within the EU. Students will engage with critical issues such as the role of financial markets in supporting businesses and households, the EU\u2019s goals of environmental sustainability and competitiveness, and the challenges posed by productivity growth, regulatory frameworks, and integration efforts. The lecturer will be responsible for preparing and delivering lectures, facilitating discussions, assigning and evaluating coursework, and supporting student learning through feedback and office hours, while upholding NYU\u2019s standards of academic rigor and integrity. Candidates should hold an advanced degree in finance, economics, or a related field, have demonstrated teaching or professional experience in European or international finance, and be able to engage an international undergraduate student body. Recommendation letters are welcome in support of applications. Please submit a Cover Letter and your CV via Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/173667. Recommendation letters may be included with the application and will be considered as part of the review process."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177652": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer: Modern Jewish History", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Prague Location: NYU Prague NYU Prague is seeking a lecturer to teach Modern Jewish History , an in-person course for undergraduate students of New York University, beginning in Spring 2026. This course provides students with a framework for understanding the cultural, political, religious and legal contours of Jewish life in Europe. The main focus of the class is on Central Europe. Candidates should hold an advanced degree in modern history or\u00a0 a related field, have demonstrated experience teaching the topic of modern jewish history at the undergraduate level, and possess the ability to engage a diverse and international student body. Recommendation letters are welcome in support of applications. Please submit a Cover Letter and your CV via Interfolio. Recommendation letters may be included with the application and will be considered as part of the review process."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192230": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Public Health Research Center (PHRC) - Dr. Youssef Idaghdour", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Public Health Research Center (PHRC) Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE  The Public Health Research Center at New York University Abu Dhabi is seeking a Post-Doctoral Associate in Population Genomics to work with the UAE Healthy Future Study, a cohort of 15,000 Emirati nationals investigating genetic, environmental, and lifestyle determinants of complex diseases. The successful candidate will lead and contribute to large-scale population genomic analyses, with a focus on whole-genome sequencing and multi-omics data and the genetic architecture of complex traits. The PHRC offers a multidisciplinary environment with access to genomic, clinical and lifestyle phenotypes, and high-performance computing infrastructure. Applicants must hold a PhD in human genetics, genetic epidemiology, biostatistics, computational biology, or a related field, with a strong publication record and experience analyzing large-scale genomic datasets in HPC environments. Applicants must be within 5 years post receipt of their PhD. Terms of employment include a competitive salary and benefits. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree/ copy of highest degree, research statement, and contact information for at least two professional references who can provide letters of recommendation. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. If you have any questions, please email Yvonne Valles: yv8@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194582": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Public Health Research Center (PHRC) - Dr. Abdishakur Abdulle", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Public Health Research Center (PHRC) Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE  The Public Health Research Center (PHRC) at New York University Abu Dhabi is seeking to hire two Post-Doctoral Associates in Epidemiology/ public health to work with the UAE Healthy Future Study, a cohort of more than 15,000 Emirati nationals investigating the lifestyle, behavioral, and clinical determinants of chronic and complex diseases. The successful candidate will lead and contribute to a large-scale epidemiological analysis, with a focus on chronic disease risk factors and health outcomes. PHRC offers a multidisciplinary research environment with access to clinical, lifestyle and behavioral data. Applicants must hold a PhD in epidemiology, public health, biostatistics, population health, or in other related field, with no more than five years post receipt of this degree, with a proven record of scientific publications and demonstrable experience in analyzing large-scale epidemiological or population-based datasets. Experience with longitudinal data analysis, statistical modeling in Stata, SPSS, R, Python, or related statistical software is highly desirable. Terms of employment include a competitive salary and benefits. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree/ copy of highest degree, research statement, and contact information of at least two professional references who can provide letters of recommendation. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. For any questions, please reach out to Dr. Abdishakur Abdulle aa192@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/159944": {"title": "Visiting Clinical Faculty in Computer Science (open rank) for Fall 2025", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The Computer Science Department of the Courant Institute of Mathematical Sciences, New York University, invites applications for a full-time Visiting Clinical Faculty position beginning in September 2025. The position is a 1 year contract that is potentially renewable for up to a total of 3 years.\u00a0 The appointment will be at the rank of\u00a0Visiting Clinical Assistant Professor, Visiting Clinical Associate Professor or Visiting Clinical Professor.\u00a0The\u00a0position\u00a0is non-tenured and non-tenure-track. The successful applicant will teach three courses during each of the fall and spring semesters in the department's undergraduate and/or graduate program and is invited to participate in\u00a0curricular development, program administration, and other educational activities. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary (for 9-month) range for this position is $80,000 - $120,000.\u00a0 New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. NYU Courant is committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming environment. Applications by members of all underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged. MS or Ph.D. in Computer Science or a related field. Applicants must submit the following via Interfolio: A cover letter indicating how the position relates to your overall career plans A complete curriculum vitae Three confidential letters of recommendation are required. Please follow the prompts in the application. Once you submit your application,\u00a0an automated email with a link will\u00a0be generated to your recommenders asking them to upload their confidential letters of recommendation. For full consideration, we recommend that applicants apply by February 1, 2025, though we will continue to review applications past that date as needed."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/164909": {"title": "ADJUNCT FACULTY (Part-time) COMPUTER SCIENCE - October 1 - November 30, 2026", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The Department of Computer Science at New York University seeks highly motivated individuals to teach graduate and undergraduate level courses on a part-time basis. Courses could include introductory undergraduate courses; advanced Computer Science major courses; and graduate-level courses. Undergraduate introductory level teaching could include courses in programming with Python and Java, data structures, web design, and web-based programming.\u00a0 Advanced Computer Science major teaching could include courses in areas such as machine learning, computer security, and software engineering, among others. Graduate level teaching could include courses in areas such as scientific computing, web-based system design, software engineering, machine learning, data mining, and intelligent systems, among others. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $214.23 - $416.45. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902. Minimum Qualifications: For Undergraduate level teaching:\u00a0MS in Computer Science or a related field. For Graduate level teaching: PhD in Computer Science or a related field. Desired Qualifications: Work experience in related industry or teaching experience in higher education. Applicants should submit\u00a0a letter of interest / cover letter (please see instruction in the application), curriculum vita , and provide the names and contact information of at least three references via the contact reference required document in the application. All application materials should be uploaded through Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175537": {"title": "NYU Courant Computer Science Assistant Professor/Faculty Fellow | Fall 2026", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The NYU Courant Institute Department of Computer Science is recruiting for Assistant Professor / Faculty Fellow (non-tenure-track) positions in Computer\u00a0 Science. All areas of Computer Science are considered. The department has strengths in theoretical computer science and cryptography, distributed computing and networking, machine learning and data science, computer vision and graphics, scientific computing and optimization, and verification and programming languages. The department has extensive collaborations with other NYU schools, centers, and departments, as well as industrial research labs in New York, including Facebook, Google, IBM Watson Research Center, etc. The appointments are for two or three years, and carry a teaching load of one course per semester.\u00a0 For full consideration, applications should be submitted by February 1, 2026. The Search Committee will begin reviewing applications immediately. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary of this position is $98,325. Applicants must have (or expect to have by September 30, 2026) a Ph.D. in Computer Science, or related field (e.g. Mathematics, Statistics, or Engineering). Applicants must submit their CV, research statement, teaching statement, and arrange for at least three (3) reference letters to be submitted directly (follow instructions within the application). Please submit your application via Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/178940": {"title": "NYU Postdoctoral Associate -  TAUR Lab with Greg Durrett", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Working with PI Greg Durrett and his students as part of the TAUR Lab at NYU ( https://cims.nyu.edu/taur/ ), you are expected to collaborate within the lab, contributing to existing projects and helping to lead new ones, to produce strong research in the form of published papers, code releases, and blog posts. Specific topics will be decided on in consultation between you, the PI, and the PI\u2019s PhD students. Broad topics of potential projects include LLM reasoning, LLM creativity, limitations of current scaling paradigms, AI for science, and more. You will have access to compute resources in PI Durrett\u2019s lab, including the NYU Torch and Greene clusters, Empire AI, cloud providers, and APIs for running inference with frontier models. As part of the broader NYU community, you will have opportunities to collaborate with other faculty, postdocs, PhD students, MS students, and undergraduate students. The appointment is initially for one year, with an expected renewal for a second year based on performance. This is an in-person position located at NYU's Washington Square campus in New York City. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $95,000-$105,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Required qualifications: PhD in Computer Science, Data Science, or a related field Strong publication record in NLP, ML, or related areas Preferred qualifications: Strong engineering skills, including the modern LLM infrastructure stack Strong communication, teamwork, and leadership skills. Apply by Feb 1, 2026, for priority consideration. This position will remain open until it is filled. Required Documents to be sumitted via Interfolio: Cover letter describing (1) why you are interested in this position and (2) how your skills and experience match the position and qualifications described above. Curriculum Vitae, including a list of publications (a link to webpage / Google Scholar is acceptable in place of a list) Research statement summarizing past work and current/future research interests List of three references who could write a letter of recommendation on your behalf (letters themselves not needed)"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189284": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Postdoctoral Associate | Computer Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of research in computer science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Computer Science department welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Completion of a PhD in Computer Science, or other discipline aligned with faculty research, by the start date. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189285": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Part-Time Assistant Research Scientist | Department of Computer Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Part-time Assistant Research Scientist in the Department of Computer Science for a maximum of 27 hours per week. Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of research in computer science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Computer Science department welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the minimum hourly rate for this position is $18. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A qualified candidate should have a bachelor's degree in a relevant field with some prior experience in research and writing in a relevant field at a level of quality and rigor. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189286": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Research Scientist | Computer Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of research in computer science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Computer Science department welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Minimum qualification: Completion of a PhD in Computer Science, or other discipline aligned with faculty research by the start date. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189287": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Assistant Research Scientist | Department of Computer Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Assistant Research Scientist in the Department of Computer Science. Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of research in computer science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Computer Science department welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current annual base salary range for this position is $58,656 - $110,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Minimum qualification: Completion of a Master's degree in disciplines aligned with faculty research by the start date. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189288": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Junior Research Scientist | Department of Computer Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Junior Research Scientist in the Department of Computer Science. Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of research in computer science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Computer Science department welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current annual base salary range for this position is $58,656 - $110,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A qualified candidate should have a bachelor's degree in a relevant field with some prior experience in research and writing in a relevant field at a level of quality and rigor. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192092": {"title": "ADJUNCT FACULTY (Part-Time) COMPUTER SCIENCE - Sept. 2, 2026 - August 31, 2027", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The Department of Computer Science at New York University seeks highly motivated individuals to teach graduate and undergraduate level courses on a part-time basis. Courses could includeintroductory undergraduate courses; advanced Computer Science major courses; and graduate-level courses. Undergraduate introductory level teaching could include courses in programming with Python and Java, data structures, web design, and web-based programming. Advanced Computer Science majorteaching could include courses in areas such as machine learning, computer security, and software engineering, among others. Graduate level teaching could include courses in areas such as scientific computing, web-based system design, software engineering, machine learning, data mining, and intelligent systems, amongothers. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is\u00a0$235.61 - $457.99. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902. Minimum Qualifications: For Undergraduate level teaching: MS in Computer Science or a related field. For Graduate level teaching: PhD in Computer Science or a related field. Desired Qualifications: Work experience in related industry or teaching experience in higher education. Applicants should submit a letter of interest / cover letter (please see instruction in the application), curriculum vita , and provide the names and contact information of at least three references via the contact reference required document in the application. All application materials should be uploaded through Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193352": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate - AI/ML for Social Good", "description": "Posted: Sep 10, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Candidates will be responsible\u00a0for working on multiple research projects under the supervision of Profs. Daniel Neill and Emily Black at NYU Courant Institute, Department of Computer Science,\u00a0in the general area of AI and machine learning for social good.\u00a075% of the candidate's effort will be supported by the NIH-funded project, \" Precision Prevention Strategies for Mental Health \" (PRISM), and will focus on developing novel machine learning approaches for (i) identifying heterogeneity in risk, (ii) causal inference, (iii) assessment and reduction of disparities across multiple data dimensions, and (iv) optimization of targeted health interventions, and applying these methods to mental health data in collaboration with other PRISM investigators.\u00a0The remaining 25% of effort will support other research projects jointly directed by Profs. Neill and Black.\u00a0While this portion of effort is flexible,\u00a0many of our joint projects involve theoretical and empirical investigation of the properties of large language models, including stability, fairness, and model multiplicity. Specific\u00a0tasks may include developing and extending novel machine learning methodology, synthesizing literature, collecting experimental data, writing code, analyzing data, working on publications, and assisting with grant proposals. The candidate will be part of both Dr. Neill's Machine Learning for Good Lab and Dr. Black's Impact-Driven Evaluation for AI (IDEA) Lab and will actively participate in both labs' activities, including attending lab meetings and mentoring more junior lab members. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. We encourage applicants to submit all of the requested materials by November 1, 2026 for full consideration. Candidates should have a PhD in Computer Science, Machine Learning, or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Preference will be given to candidates with demonstrated ability to develop novel machine learning methodology supported by rigorous theory, as evidenced by publications in top venues such as NeurIPS, ICML, and JMLR. We are particularly looking for individuals with expertise in the following subareas of machine learning: causal inference, algorithmic fairness, and theoretical properties of large language models (e.g., stability, fairness, and model multiplicity). Experience in working with complex data (such as health and urban data) is a plus, as is a strong interest in applying methods to impactful public health problems. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications; a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position; copies of up to three relevant scientific papers; names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194584": {"title": "NYU Courant Computer Science Assistant Professor/Faculty Fellow for Fall 2027", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Computer Science - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The NYU Courant Institute Department of Computer Science is recruiting for Assistant Professor / Faculty Fellow (non-tenure-track) positions in Computer\u00a0 Science. All areas of Computer Science are considered. The department has strengths in theoretical computer science and cryptography, distributed computing and networking, machine learning and data science, computer vision and graphics, scientific computing and optimization, and verification and programming languages. The department has extensive collaborations with other NYU schools, centers, and departments, as well as industrial research labs in New York, including Facebook, Google, IBM Watson Research Center, etc. The appointments are for two or three years, and carry a teaching load of one course per semester.\u00a0 For full consideration, applications should be submitted by February 1, 2027. The Search Committee will begin reviewing applications immediately. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary of this position is set at $101,766. Applicants must have (or expect to have by September 30, 2027) a Ph.D. in Computer Science, or related field (e.g. Mathematics, Statistics, or Engineering). Applicants must submit their CV, research statement, teaching statement, and arrange for at least three (3) reference letters to be submitted directly (follow instructions within the application). Please submit your application via Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/159782": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty Mathematics 2025", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The Department of Mathematics at New York University seeks highly motivated individuals to teach graduate and undergraduate-level courses on a part-time basis at their Washington Square and Brooklyn campuses. Courses could include introductory undergraduate courses; advanced Mathematics major courses; and graduate-level courses. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is in the range of $214.24 - $341.72. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902. Minimum Qualifications: For Undergraduate level teaching: \u00a0MS in Mathematics or a related field. For Graduate level teaching: PhD in Mathematics or a related field. Desired Qualifications: Work experience in related industry or teaching experience in higher education. Please provide in your application: A cover letter indicating how the position relates to your overall career plans A complete curriculum vitae Provide the names and contact information of at least two\u00a0references via the contact reference required document in the application. All application materials should be uploaded through Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/162502": {"title": "Clinical Assistant Professsor in Mathematics for Fall 2025", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The Courant Institute of Mathematical Sciences at NYU seeks to fill Clinical Assistant Professor faculty positions in Mathematics at its Washington Square and Brooklyn campuses. These are non-tenure track appointments. The contract term is typically 3-years and potentially renewable.\u00a0 The primary responsibilities are: teaching and coordinating entry-level courses through multivariable calculus and linear algebra training and evaluating new instructors and teaching assistants curriculum development and assessment other responsibilities related to the undergraduate program In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary (for 9-month) range for this position is $80,000 - $120,000.\u00a0 New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. NYU Courant is committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming environment. Applications by members of all underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged. Applicants must have a strong background in advanced Mathematics, including a Ph.D. in Mathematics or a mathematics-related field, as well as experience in and knowledge about undergraduate teaching, learning, and curricular issues. Please provide the following materials as part of your application: A cover letter indicating how the position relates to your overall career plans A complete and up-to-date curriculum vitae Three confidential letters of recommendation, including one that focuses on your teaching. Please follow the prompts in the application. Once you submit your application, an automated email with a link will be generated to your recommenders asking them to upload their confidential letters of recommendation. A 1-2 page teaching statement A teaching portfolio.\u00a0 The portfolio may include a list of courses taught, sample teaching materials, evaluations of teaching, certifications, or other supporting documents. (preferred, but optional) A 15-minute video of your teaching.\u00a0 Upload your video to a streaming site (such as YouTube or Vimeo) and submit the URL with your application. Applications received before February 28, 2025, will be given full consideration."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175532": {"title": "Tenure/Tenure-track Faculty Positions in Mathematics | Fall 2026 | NYU Courant", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The Department of Mathematics at the Courant Institute is calling for applications for faculty positions in applied mathematics and mathematics to begin in September 2025 at the Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Full Professor level. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, Applied mathematics for Physical and Life Sciences, Combinatorics, Algebra/Number Theory, and Scientific computing. The Department of Mathematics at Courant offers an outstanding scholarly environment for mathematical and interdisciplinary research. It has a long and world-renowned tradition in both pure and applied mathematics and in bridging between them. Mathematics at Courant benefits from the proximity of the Computer Science department within the Courant Institute and the cross-disciplinary Center of Data Science at NYU. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $100,000 - $150,000 for the rank of Assistant Professor, $120,000 - $180,000 for the rank of Associate Professor, and $165,000 - $265,000 for the rank of Full Professor. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, and market and organization considerations. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Mathematics or a related field. Applications received before November 15 will be given full consideration. Application Materials Required: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement Publication List Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers) Please apply via Interfolio here:"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183889": {"title": "Postdoctoral researcher in AI for Climate -  Surrogate Multiscale Modeling (NYU Courant Institute School)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY We are seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Researcher to join our team in building next-generation Foundation Models for Earth System Modeling at NYU Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science.\u00a0 In collaboration with Profs Carlos Fernandez-Granda, Joan Bruna, Laure Zanna,\u00a0 the successful candidate will focus on developing methods to capture and understand generalization in AI emulators, in particular for unseen regimes, including rare events. The postdoc will be expected to lead independent research directions while collaborating with a team comprising domain scientists, experts in climate physics and modeling, and AI experts, as part of the international project M 2 LInES (https://m2lines.github.io) . You can read about some of our recent work here: Dheeshjith et al. 2025 , Duncan et al. 2025 , Pedersen et al. 2025 , Perezhogin et al. 2025 . The position, available immediately, is a full-time appointment, initially for one year, with the possibility of renewal for 2 years, subject to satisfactory performance and available funding. In compliance with NYU's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $94,000. NYU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer quality, as well as market and organization considerations. Qualifications: Education: Ph.D. in machine learning, computer science, engineering, physical science or related technical discipline. Experience: Expertise in developing and training AI models Proficiency in Python Experience with HPC (GPUs preferred) Related Skills and Other Requirements Ability to work at the interface of AI and science/engineering problems Ability to lead, develop, and contribute to multiple projects in a fast-paced environment; Ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of an interdisciplinary team; Outstanding written and oral communication skills Technical and scientific curiosity A solid record of relevant publications and software appropriate to their career stage For full consideration, applicants should submit via Interfolio, the following by April 20th ( Earlier applications are strongly encouraged) : a cover letter (no more than 2 pages) detailing 1) their research experience, 2) future research plans and how they would fit our\u00a0 project, and 3) available start date, a Curriculum Vitae (no more than 2 pages) A list of publications and software repositories 3 confidential letters of recommendation. Applications are reviewed only if you have fulfilled the requirements above, and submitted to Interfolio. For additional information, please contact m2lines@nyu.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183900": {"title": "Postdoctoral researcher in AI for Science in M2LInES - Generative Models for Dynamical Systems (NYU Courant Institute School)", "description": "Posted: Mar 26, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY M 2 LInES is seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Researcher to join our team at NYU Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. In collaboration with Profs Carlos Fernandez-Granda, Joan Bruna, Laure Zanna, the successful candidate will focus on developing generative machine learning models for complex dynamical systems for probabilistic forecasts. The postdoc will be expected to lead independent research directions while collaborating with a team composed of domain scientists, experts in climate physics and modeling, and AI, as part of the international project, M 2 LInES https://m2lines.github.io . The position, available immediately, is a full-time appointment, initially for one year, with the possibility of renewal for 2 years, subject to satisfactory performance and available funding. In compliance with NYU's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $94,000. NYU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer quality, as well as market and organization considerations. Qualifications: Education: Ph.D. in machine learning, computer science, engineering, physical science or related technical discipline. Experience: Expertise in developing and training AI models Proficiency in Python Experience with HPC (GPUs preferred) Related Skills and Other Requirements Ability to work at the interface of AI and science/engineering problems Ability to lead, develop, and contribute to multiple projects in a fast-paced environment; Ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of an interdisciplinary team; Outstanding written and oral communication skills Technical and scientific curiosity A solid record of relevant publications and software appropriate to their career stage For full consideration, applicants should submit via Interfolio, the following by April 20th ( Earlier applications are strongly encouraged) : a cover letter (no more than 2 pages) detailing 1) their research experience, 2) future research plans and how they would fit our project, and 3) available start date, a Curriculum Vitae (no more than 2 pages) A list of publications and software repositories 3 confidential letters of recommendation. Applications are reviewed only if you have fulfilled the requirements above, and submitted to Interfolio. For additional information, please contact m2lines@nyu.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189308": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Postdoctoral Associate | Department of Mathematics | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of mathematics. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Department of Mathematics welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Completion of a PhD in Mathematics, or other discipline aligned with faculty research, by the start date. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189313": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Part-Time Assistant Research Scientist | Department of Mathematics | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Part-time Assistant Research Scientist in the Department of Mathematics for a maximum of 27 hours per week. Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of mathematics. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Department of Mathematics welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current minimum hourly rate for this position is $18. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A qualified candidate should have a bachelor's degree in a relevant field with some prior experience in research and writing in a relevant field at a level of quality and rigor. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189309": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Research Scientist | Department of Mathematics | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of mathematics. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Department of Mathematics welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Minimum qualification: Completion of a PhD in Mathematics, or other discipline aligned with faculty research by the start date. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189310": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Assistant Research Scientist | Department of Mathematics | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Assistant Research Scientist in the Department of Mathematics. Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of mathematics. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Department of Mathematics welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current annual base salary range for this full-time position is $58,656 - $110,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Minimum qualification: Completion of a Master's degree in disciplines aligned with faculty research by the start date. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189311": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Junior Research Scientist | Department of Mathematics | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY Junior Research Scientist in the Department of Mathematics. Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in all areas of mathematics. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Department of Mathematics welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $58,656 - $110,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A qualified candidate should have a bachelor's degree in a relevant field and some prior experience in research and writing at a level of quality and rigor. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192797": {"title": "ADJUNCT FACULTY (Part-Time) MATHEMATICS - Sept. 2, 2026 - August 31, 2027", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The Department of Mathematics at the New York University Courant Institute seeks highly motivated individuals to teach graduate and undergraduate level courses on a part-time basis. Courses could includeintroductory undergraduate courses; advanced Mathematics major courses; and graduate-level courses. Undergraduate introductory level teaching could include courses in programming with Python and Java, data structures, web design, and web-based programming. Advanced Computer Science majorteaching could include courses in areas such as machine learning, computer security, and software engineering, among others. Graduate level teaching could include courses in areas such as scientific computing, web-based system design, software engineering, machine learning, data mining, and intelligent systems, amongothers. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is\u00a0$235.61- $375.81. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902. Minimum Qualifications: For Undergraduate level teaching: MS in Mathematics or a related field. For Graduate level teaching: PhD in Mathematics or a related field. Desired Qualifications: Work experience in related industry or teaching experience in higher education. Applicants should submit a letter of interest / cover letter (please see instruction in the application), curriculum vita , and provide the names and contact information of at least three references via the contact reference required document in the application. All application materials should be uploaded through Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193211": {"title": "Simons Center Postdoctoral Fellows in Computational Climate at NYU", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York, NY The Simons Center for Computational Geophysical Flows , housed at the Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science, is a new research and training hub, pioneering next-generation computational methods to address a range of problems in ocean, atmosphere, and ice dynamics. We are seeking full-time Postdoctoral Fellows who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the center and the broader Earth and Planetary Sciences at NYU. Candidates are expected to drive cutting edge research in the following areas of interest: multiscale interactions, geophysical and boundary layer turbulence, coupled atmosphere-ocean-ice dynamics, physics of rare events and climate risks, such as extreme precipitation and flooding, marine heatwaves. Candidates are expected to make advances in both methods and applications to further our understanding of complex multiscale flows on Earth and other planets. The Postdoctoral Fellows will be mentored by one or more of the Center\u2019s core faculty (Carlos Fernandez-Granda, Sara Shamekh, Jonathan Weare, Laure Zanna, Yi Zhang) and are expected to lead independent research. These positions, available immediately, are full-time appointments, initially for one year, with the possibility of renewal for two years, subject to satisfactory performance and available funding. These positions are also connected to NYU's expansion in Global Science and Tech, which includes the launch of the Earth Systems Institute . In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current annual base salary range for this position is $62,500\u2013$125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer quality, as well as market and organization considerations. \u25cf Education: Ph.D. in machine learning, computer science, engineering, physical science, applied mathematics, atmosphere/ocean science, or related technical discipline. \u25cf Experience: \u25cb Expertise in computational science \u25cb Proficiency in Python \u25cb Experience with high-performance computing (HPC), with GPUs preferred \u25cf Related Skills and Other Requirements \u25cb Ability to work at the interface of AI and physics-based science/engineering problems \u25cb Ability to lead, develop, and contribute to multiple projects in a fast-paced environment \u25cb Ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of an interdisciplinary team \u25cb Outstanding written and oral communication skills \u25cb Strong technical and scientific curiosity \u25cb A solid record of relevant publications and software development appropriate to the applicant\u2019s career stage For full consideration, applicants should submit the following via Interfolio by December 1st (earlier applications are strongly encouraged): \u25cf Cover letter (maximum one page) detailing research experience and indicating an available start date, and possible primary mentor(s) in the Center and secondary mentor(s) at NYU (e.g., in the Earth Systems Institute). \u25cf Research statement (maximum two pages) detailing future research plans and how they align with the center\u2019s goals. \u25cf Curriculum Vitae (maximum two pages) \u25cf List of publications and software repositories \u25cf Three letters of recommendation"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193868": {"title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor Appointments in Mathematics for Fall 2027", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York The Department of Mathematics at the Courant Institute School for Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science is calling for applications for Assistant Professor positions in Mathematics, beginning in September 2027. The Courant Institute School's Math Department offers an outstanding scholarly environment for mathematical and interdisciplinary research. It has a long and world-renowned tradition in both pure and applied mathematics and in bridging between them. Mathematics at Courant benefits from the proximity and interdisciplinarity of the school's Computer Science and Data Science (CDS) departments. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position (Assistant Professor) is $100,000 - $150,000.\u00a0 New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, and market and organization considerations. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Mathematics or a related field. Applications received before November 15 will be given full consideration. Application Materials Required: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement Publication List Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers). One of the reference letters must address your teaching ability. If you wish to upload additional reference letters, click on \"Additional Documents -> Add File -> Request a recommendation\"."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194459": {"title": "Associate and Full Professor Appointments in Mathematics for Fall 2027", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York The Department of Mathematics at the Courant Institute School for Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science is calling for applications for Associate Professor and Full Professor positions in Mathematics, beginning in September 2027. The Courant Institute School's Math Department offers an outstanding scholarly environment for mathematical and interdisciplinary research. It has a long and world-renowned tradition in both pure and applied mathematics and in bridging between them. Mathematics at Courant benefits from the proximity and interdisciplinarity of the school's Computer Science and Data Science (CDS) departments. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $120,000 - $180,000 for the rank of Associate Professor and $165,000 - $265,000 for the rank of Full Professor. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, and market and organization considerations. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Mathematics or a related field. Applications received before November 15 will be given full consideration. Application Materials Required: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement Publication List Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers). One of the reference letters must address your teaching ability.\u00a0If you wish to upload additional reference letters, click on \"Additional Documents -> Add File -> Request a recommendation\"."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195189": {"title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor Appointments in Mathematics and Atmosphere Ocean Science for Fall 2027", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Mathematics - NYU Courant Institute Location: New York The Department of Mathematics at the Courant Institute School for Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science is calling for applications for Assistant Professor positions in Mathematics and Atmosphere Ocean Science, beginning in September 2027. The Courant Institute School's Math Department offers an outstanding scholarly environment for mathematical and interdisciplinary research. It has a long and world-renowned tradition in both pure and applied mathematics and in bridging between them. Mathematics at Courant benefits from the proximity and interdisciplinarity of the school's Computer Science and Data Science (CDS) departments. We welcome applicants with exceptional quantitative skills and are open to all areas of research at the intersection of mathematics and atmosphere ocean science, with particular interest in scientific machine learning, modern data assimilation, and ocean-ice interaction. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position (Assistant Professor) is $100,000 - $150,000.\u00a0 New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, and market and organization considerations. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Mathematics, Physics, or a related field. Please submit your application via Interfolio using this application link: https://apply.interfolio.com/195189 Applications received before November 15 will be given full consideration."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/172964": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Full-Time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Institute of Human Development and Social Change Location: New York, NY Job Summary : The NYU Program of Nutrition at Steinhardt and the Institute of Human Development and Social Change (IHDSC) are seeking a Postdoctoral Associate to support Dr. Angela Trude and the OPT-FRESH research team. This role will provide comprehensive involvement in all aspects of the project, including protocol development, data collection and analysis, manuscript preparation, and dissemination of study findings. The Postdoctoral Associate will devote 12 person-months of effort each year to the project, Data Management and Sharing activities, assisting the Data Manager with data curation and documentation. Project Overview : OPT-FRESH is a 5-year NIH-funded intervention trial addressing gaps in healthy food access among low-income families. Using the multiphase optimization strategy (MOST), the study tests three intervention components designed to reduce barriers to online healthy food purchases: 1) weekly text messages to improve trust in online grocery services; 2) weekly match of up to $10 as a financial incentive for purchasing FVL online; and 3) weekly instant shoppable grocery lists of tailored meal suggestions using SNAP-Ed recipes. OPT-FRESH will provide a unique opportunity to identify effective, scalable, and efficient strategies that could be used to reach the over 44 million people in the U.S.,\u00a0 including six million children, experiencing food insecurity, thus addressing important social determinants of health that explain health disparities related to cardiovascular disease. Specific Responsibilities : Support the PI and research team in all aspects of study design, protocol development, and implementation. Conduct data collection, management, and analysis, including qualitative and quantitative methods. Assist the Data Manager with Data Management and Sharing activities (e.g., data curation, documentation, compliance with NIH data-sharing requirements). Contribute to manuscript writing, grant proposals, and dissemination of findings through academic presentations, policy briefs, and community reports. Facilitate communication and collaboration among investigators, research staff, and community partners. Support supervision and mentoring of graduate research assistants and student volunteers. Participate in regular team meetings, workshops, and events related to the project. Working Conditions In-person: meetings, recruitment, and data collection will be in-person. Meetings will be conducted in the NYU Washington Square Campus, where the Postdoctoral Associate will have a designated work-desk and computer. Recruitment and data collection will occur in the Bronx. Public transportation costs related to recruitment and data collection are reimbursed. Time Frame One-year position with potential renewal for up to five years. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500-$65,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Per NYU's remote-work policy, this position is expected to be completely in-person. This position will have some supervisory responsibilities, assisting the Project Coordinator in supervising at least 4 graduate student researchers and student volunteers. Qualifications and Competencies : PhD in Nutrition, Public Health, Food Policy, or a related field. Strong background in research methods, including study design, data collection, and analysis. Experience with Data Management and Sharing practices, including data curation and documentation. Demonstrated record of scientific writing and dissemination. Experience with community-engaged research, interventions, or public health programs preferred. Strong organizational, interpersonal, and collaborative skills. Sensitivity to racial, cultural, and economic diversity; familiarity with NYC communities preferred. Willingness to travel locally within New York City for recruitment and data collection. The position will begin October 1, 2026 . Qualified candidates should submit a one-page cover letter (please include future career goals, reason you are interested in the position, and specific examples that make you qualified for this position) and resume/CV, a sample of writing material (peer-reviewed publication, grant proposal), and the names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three references via Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192042": {"title": "Program Coordinator - ROSES Advocacy Coordinator (Full-Time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Institute of Human Development and Social Change Location: New York, NY Drs. Shabnam Javdani and Erin Godfrey are seeking a Program Coordinator who is excited to implement a strong intervention, and to help shape and strengthen a growing program committed to changing the systems and conditions that constrain young people's lives. The candidate will coordinate and support the Resilience, Opportunity, Strength, Education, Safety (ROSES) Advocacy Program. Currently implemented in collaboration with the New York Community Trust, ROSES is a community-based program pairing girls and LGB/TGNC+ youth experiencing school pushout or who have had police, probation, or court contact with a highly trained advocate. ROSES advocates work with young people in their communities free of charge to support them in their week, for 10-15 weeks, working with clients by providing access to resources and education, helping girls and young people define individual goals, supporting the development of self-efficacy skills, encouraging girls' and LGB/TGNC+ youths' engagement with multiple social contexts (school, family, peer, etc.), and promoting contextual competence to reduce school pushout, and child welfare and legal system involvement. The Program Coordinator is responsible for the day-to-day leadership and implementation of the ROSES program. This position combines program management, personnel supervision, community partnership development, and youth advocacy within the juvenile legal and child welfare systems. The Program Coordinator trains and supervises a team of 10-15 undergraduate and/or graduate advocates with strong model fidelity, ensuring high-quality and timely program implementation. The Program Coordinator also manages relationships with community partners and legal system stakeholders, collaborating across settings and systems within and outside of NYU, and supports youth and families through these complex systems. Lastly, the Program Coordinator will be expected to monitor and track program implementation and fidelity, including overseeing and carrying out day-to-day administrative tasks within NYU's infrastructure. ROSES is grounded in feminist, anti-racist, LGBTQIA+ affirming, trauma-informed, and community-based approaches that recognize and address the structural conditions that contribute to the criminalization of young people. We seek candidates who are committed to these principles and who approach their work with humility, curiosity, and a strong desire to partner with young people in pursuit of their self-determined goals. As such, the ideal candidate is both deeply committed to social justice and highly organized. They will have a strong interest in community-based outreach and interventions that support individuals from minoritized and often-excluded racial and ethnic backgrounds, as well as working knowledge or a strong desire to learn about the needs of youth in child welfare and juvenile legal settings in New York City with a focus on girls and LGBT+ and gender expansive youth. The role is well-suited for someone who is passionate about advancing equity and who is equally comfortable discussing structural racism and inequity as they are overseeing project tasks, providing constructive and strengths-based feedback to others, and trouble-shooting implementation challenges. The successful candidate will be excited to build, train, and directly supervise advocates in their work with young people while coordinating multiple moving pieces across the program. They will be comfortable providing weekly supervision and written feedback to advocates, providing on-call individualized support to advocates, clients, and families, and collaborating closely with ROSES leadership and supervisors and community partners, to ensure high-quality implementation of the intervention program. The Program Coordinator should be an effective project manager who can balance competing priorities, anticipate challenges, and develop thoughtful, responsive solutions. They will demonstrate sound judgement, adaptability, and attention to detail in complex and evolving situations. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, dedication to social equity, and clear organizational and project management skills are required. Specific responsibilities of the Advocacy Coordinator will include: Develop and maintain mastery of the ROSES advocacy model with support from study PIs. Build, train, lead, and supervise the advocacy team (around 10-15 advocates), composed of both undergraduate and graduate students at NYU, including developing and implementing training activities that foster and maintain critical consciousness and advocacy mastery among the intervention team. Supervise the advocacy team during multiple weekly supervision sessions; maintain advocacy calendars; develop and implement organizational structures and implement adaptive and creative responses to address shifting team needs. Develop, manage and maintain strong working relationships with a broad range of stakeholders in the child welfare and juvenile legal systems to promote their active engagement with the ROSES program. Coordinate recruitment of advocates and referrals of young people; work closely with the supervision staff and PIs, ensure smooth interactions between NYU departments, referring agencies, young people and their families. Develop, and utilize data management systems to track advocacy needs and acitivites, adherence or deviation from program framework. Research and compile community-based resources that can be organized into guides that advocates can utilize in their interventions. Establish feedback mechanisms to maintain responsiveness towards advocates, young people and families, in coordination with study PIs and the advocacy team. Devise swift troubleshooting mechanisms for emergent issues that affect the quality and sustainability of the program. Synthesize and translate insights across interventions into accessible communication materials that educate and inform referring agencies, young people and families about the program and the challenges of school pushout and legal system involvement. Perform research administration coordination tasks, including expense processing, procurement, and grant reporting. The Advocacy Program Coordinator position offers a number of excellent training opportunities, including collaboration with senior and junior members of the research team, exposure to university-community research partnerships, and experience with community-based intervention research. This position also allows for professional development opportunities, including opportunities for accruing intervention hours, conference presentations, and publications that support building a strong professional portfolio. Access to a rich array of NYU seminars, and special training workshops in research methods and statistics, and in areas of social justice, intervention/evaluation, implementation science, and gender-informed approaches are also available. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $60,000-$75,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Per NYU's remote-work policy, this position is expected to be fully in-person. This position has supervisory responsibilities, responsible for supervising 10-15 undergraduate and graduate students. The candidate must have at least a Bachelor's degree in psychology, sociology, criminology, public health, intervention or prevention science or a related field. Minimum two years experience supervising, mentoring, or coordinating the work of employees, students, interns, voluneets, or community advocates. Demonstrated experience managing projects, programs, research studies, or community-based initiatives and familiarity with office and/or research administration. Commitment to transformational change and advancing equity for young people and families through community-based, relationship-centered work, and a shared belief that effective advocacy is rooted in anti-racism, feminism, LGBTQIA+ affirmation, and respect for young people's autonomy, strengths, and self-determined goals. Excellent interpersonal communication/rapport-building skills, and ability to work with and coordinate across diverse individuals, stakeholders, and organizations. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Excellent organizational skills, attention to detail, leadership, and follow-through. Comfort and capacity to work in a fast-paced team-oriented environment. Ability to work well independently and collaboratoively on a multidisciplinary team. Proven ability to adapt and respond quickly to changing needs, manage multiple conflicting priorities, overcome challenges, and lead with solutions. Comfort \"managing up\" and \"managing down\" as needed to accomplish advocacy goals. Available to work outside regular office hours, when needed. Competence with Microsoft Suite (e.g. Word, PowerPoint, Excel) and Google Drive. Familiarity with project management tools (e.g. Airtable, Trello, Asana, etc.) is a plus. The review of applicants will begin immediately. To apply, send a cover letter including statement of interest, CV/resume, unofficial undergraduate transcript, and names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three references via Interfolio."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/186117": {"title": "Part-Time Tutor-Mentor (Liberty Partnerships Program)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Metropolitan Center for Research and Equity and the Transformation of Schools Location: New York, NY Position Description The Liberty Partnerships Program (LPP) at New York University\u2019s Metropolitan Center for Research on Equity and the Transformation of Schools is seeking a Part-Time Tutor-Mentor to support academic services at partner high schools in Manhattan for the remainder of the New York City Department of Education academic year. Funded by the New York State Education Department and sponsored by NYU since 1989, LPP is a college access and dropout prevention program that promotes academic resilience, high school completion, postsecondary persistence, and successful career transitions. Position Summary Tutor-Mentors will support students at Harvey Milk High School and Richard R. Green High School by providing academic, mentoring, and socio-emotional support. This role focuses on assisting students who may be struggling academically and/or emotionally to ensure grade promotion and high school graduation. Tutor-Mentors: Work with small groups of students identified by school staff Provide individualized academic support and mentoring Utilize school and Metro Center resources to support student success Participate in the school community, including meetings when possible Maintain documentation of student sessions and submit required data Engage in ongoing professional development About the NYU Metropolitan Center The NYU Metropolitan Center supports equity-focused, evidence-based innovations in education through: Technical assistance to schools and districts Professional learning for educators Applied research and evaluation Scholarly research Youth development and college readiness programming Principal Responsibilities 30% : Plan and implement tutoring lessons for small groups 20% : Meet weekly with students to assess needs and progress 30% : Communicate student progress with guidance counselors and staff; support college application processes for seniors 20% : Manage and submit program documentation Institutional Impact Serve approximately 10\u201350 high school students across the academic year and summer programming. Supervisory Responsibilities This position does not include any supervisory responsibilities. The Tutor-Mentor will not oversee or manage staff and will work collaboratively with school personnel and NYU LPP team members. Additional Information Ongoing collaboration with school staff, guidance counselors, and LPP team Participation in professional development opportunities Focus on both academic and socio-emotional student support Compensation In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly pay range for this position is $28-35/hour. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Equal Opportunity Statement All interested individuals\u2014including people of all races, national origins, ages, religions, abilities, gender identities, and sexual orientations\u2014are encouraged to apply. Required: Minimum of 2 years experience working with high school students Experience in tutoring and/or mentoring Strong communication and interpersonal skills Preferred: Bachelor\u2019s degree (BA) Experience providing Math or English tutoring, including Regents exam preparation Please submit resume and cover letter, for consideration.\u00a0For questions, please contact: Yolanda Fordham yf9@nyu.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168419": {"title": "Part-time Associate Research Scientist: Music and Mental Health in Sickle Cell Disease (SCD)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Music and Audio Research Laboratory Location: New York City, NY We are seeking a Part-Time Associate Research Scientist to contribute to a multidisciplinary project investigating the impact of music-based interventions on mental health and adherence\u00a0to medication\u00a0in individuals living with Sickle Cell Disease (SCD). Job responsibilities will include designing, conducting, and analyzing experiments related to music, mental health and adherence to pharmaceutical interventions; collecting and processing behavioral, physiological, and public health data; reviewing relevant literature; preparing manuscripts and contributing to publication; design interventions and recruiting participants; developing communication materials including for the web and social media. The position will not include any supervisory responsibilities. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the hourly salary range for this position is $28.00 - $32.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. The employee who takes on this role can expect to work 15 hours per week. Hybrid remote/on site: The job duties for this position can be done partially remote, but requires some in-person interactions with research collaborators. Progress Evaluation: Evaluation will be done on a weekly basis via in person and/or online meetings Required: Ph.D (completed or near completion) in Public Health, Music, Arts-based interventions for mental health, or related field. Demonstrated knowledge of Sickle Cell Disease and its impact on mental health Experience in collecting public health and qualitative/quantitative data, (e.g., focus groups, interviews).and using digital tools for data collection. Proficiency in Stata and SPSS Strong Scientific writing skills, including experience with peer-reviewed publications Ability to conduct thorough literature reviews and synthesize evidence Prefered: Experience working with mental, behavioral and digital health initiatives in Non-Communicable Diseases Skills in community-based research or participatory research design Skills in health communication and community and stakeholder engagement Please submit a cover letter and CV to be considered for this position."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188570": {"title": "Part-time Associate Research Scientist: Music and Mental Health in Sickle Cell Disease (SCD)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Music and Audio Research Laboratory Location: New York City, NY We are seeking a Part-Time Associate Research Scientist to contribute to a multidisciplinary project investigating the impact of music-based interventions on mental health and adherence\u00a0to medication\u00a0in individuals living with Sickle Cell Disease (SCD). Job responsibilities will include designing, conducting, and analyzing experiments related to music, mental health and adherence to pharmaceutical interventions; collecting and processing behavioral, physiological, and public health data; reviewing relevant literature; preparing manuscripts and contributing to publication; design interventions and recruiting participants; developing communication materials including for the web and social media. The position will not include any supervisory responsibilities. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the hourly salary range for this position is $28.00 - $32.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. The employee who takes on this role can expect to work 10 hours per week. Hybrid remote/on site: The job duties for this position can be done partially remote, but requires some in-person interactions with research collaborators. Progress Evaluation: Evaluation will be done on a weekly basis via in person and/or online meetings Required: Ph.D (completed or near completion) in Public Health, Music, Arts-based interventions for mental health, or related field. Demonstrated knowledge of Sickle Cell Disease and its impact on mental health Experience in collecting public health and qualitative/quantitative data, (e.g., focus groups, interviews).and using digital tools for data collection. Proficiency in Stata and SPSS Strong Scientific writing skills, including experience with peer-reviewed publications Ability to conduct thorough literature reviews and synthesize evidence Prefered: Experience working with mental, behavioral and digital health initiatives in Non-Communicable Diseases Skills in community-based research or participatory research design Skills in health communication and community and stakeholder engagement Please submit a cover letter and CV to be considered for this position."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195036": {"title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Healthy Aging and Longevity (Prof. Bei Wu)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Arts and Sciences Location: Shanghai, China  NYU Shanghai is committed to the development of the next generation of scientists and is seeking to fill full-time postdoctoral fellow roles with anticipated start dates around September 2027. Each position is initially awarded as a two-year appointment and is renewable annually thereafter based on performance and availability of funding. The position involves postdoctoral research on healthy aging and longevity, especially how artificial intelligence (AI) and other digital technologies can support longevity and resilience among older adults, and the biological mechanisms that underlie healthy aging. Applicants with backgrounds in AI and data science, as well as in the biological sciences, are encouraged to apply. Potential research directions include developing or evaluating AI-enabled and digital tools that support cognitive health, functional independence, and well-being in later life; and investigating how genetic and epigenetic factors, the microbiome, and immune aging and inflammation relate to cognitive decline and age-related disease. Experience in research on cognitive health, dementia, or the biology of aging is also desirable. The postdoctoral fellow will engage in conducting data analyses, reporting study results, publishing in peer-reviewed journals, presenting at conferences, and grant writing, in addition to advancing their own area of research. The postdoctoral fellow will be under the mentorship and direction of Prof. Bei Wu, Provost and Distinguished Global Professor of Public Health at NYU Shanghai, and will receive professional development and academic networking opportunities across NYU\u2019s Global Network. The position is based in Shanghai, China. The start date is flexible and, for international applicants, subject to immigration regulations. Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions. Dissertation completed in the last three years.\u00a0 If you have not yet completed your dissertation at the time of application, please provide verification in writing from your dissertation chair confirming that your degree will be conferred before the start of your position. Earned PhD or equivalent in computer science, data science, engineering, biology, genetics, bioinformatics, microbiology, immunology, or other related fields. As part of its selection process, our selection committee looks for evidence of a strong track record of scholarly achievement (publications, honors, awards), grant writing experience, and the promise of continued achievement toward independence as a researcher. Strong research skills relevant to the applicant\u2019s background, such as quantitative or computational methods (e.g., longitudinal data analysis, causal inference, machine learning, natural language processing, or large language models) or biological and bioinformatic methods (e.g., genomic, epigenomic, microbiome, or immunological analyses). Proficiency in at least one statistical or programming language, such as R, Python, Stata, or SAS, is preferred. Excellent written and oral communication skills in English and the ability to work independently and collaboratively in an interdisciplinary, international team. Proficiency in Mandarin Chinese is an advantage but not required. Please upload the following materials. 1. Curriculum vitae 2. A personal statement detailing the reasons for applying for the position, the applicant\u2019s research interests and career goals, and how the proposed research fits with the research program of the mentor, Prof. Bei Wu (maximum one page, single-spaced) 3. One research paper that is most representative of the candidate\u2019s work (preferably first-authored by the candidate) 4. Names and contact information for two references Applications will be reviewed as they are received and until the positions are filled. Shortlisted applicants will be invited for an interview. Incomplete submissions will not be accepted. Applications should be submitted through Interfolio at [link to be added]. For questions about the application process, please contact the NYU Shanghai Office of Faculty Recruitment at shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184158": {"title": "Associate Research Scientist (Part Time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Researchers_AP Location: New York, NY Q.U.E.S.T. is a full-time, 10-week summer program designed to expand access for historically underrepresented and under-resourced undergraduates who are dedicated to interrogating systems of power, addressing structural inequities impacting Communities of Color and marginalized identities, and developing evidence-based strategies for systemic change. Q.U.E.S.T. Research Coordinator - The Q.U.E.S.T. Research Coordinator plays a vital role in guiding the research curriculum throughout the summer and directly supporting the development of underrepresented students committed to systemic change. Duties: The job duties for this position are highly local and require on-site attendance for June and July. Facilitate a 1-hour weekly check-in meeting with Q.U.E.S.T. students Meet with Q.U.E.S.T. participants individually and with mentors to work on research papers and presentations for conferences Organizing and presiding over the AP Dept Quest presentation day Lead QUEST online-writing bootcamp in August Leads program evaluation efforts The Associate Research Scientist will supervise the Summer Program Assistant. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly salary range for this position is $28-$38. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Relevant experience in mentoring and managing \u201cstudent-affairs programming\u201d demonstrates your ability to make a meaningful difference in students' lives, which can inspire candidates to see their potential impact. Detail-oriented and demonstrate logistical and administrative skills Possess superb people skills and a sense of humor, be a creative problem-solver, and have sound judgment and emotional maturity Be willing to hold students, faculty, and volunteers to a high standard of excellence and professionalism Completed at least one year of a PhD program in the applied sciences Please submit a cover letter and resume by April 24th to be considered for this opportunity."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190518": {"title": "Speech Language Pathologist (Part-Time):NYU Swallowing Lab", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Researchers_CSD Location: 665 Broadway Seeking a part-time Speech Language Pathologist to assist with biomechanical analysis of swallowing data on modified barium swallow studies for a fully onsite position. The candidate must have completed all masters-level coursework and degree requirements in speech language pathology and have completed the student training version of the Modified Barium Swallowing Impairment Profile with at least 80% mastery. Previous research experience preferred. Successful applicants will be hired to assist with aspects of a randomized clinical trial focused on exercise for the muscles used in swallowing. Job duties will include database management, measurement of videofluoroscopy swallowing studies, assisting with data extraction, assisting with data analysis and dissemination. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly pay range for this position is $30.00-$35.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Qualified applicants must have completed their graduate education in Speech Language Pathology or Communicative Sciences and Disorders and hold an active Certificate of Clinical Competence in Speech Language Pathology (CCC-SLP) and at least one year of clinical work experience in adult swallowing disorders. Applicants with MBSImP(TM) or DIGEST(TM) training are preferred."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195458": {"title": "Part-Time Researcher", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Researchers_MPAP Location: New York, NY The Jameel Arts and Health Lab (the Lab), housed in the NYU Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development, seeks a Part-Time Researcher for the Artistic Justice Initiative, a program focusing on the integration and evaluation of arts interventions in prisons and justice-affected communities. The Part-Time Researcher will work under the direction of Dr. Nisha Sajnani , Founding Co-Director of the Lab, and will serve as the primary coordinator for the Artistic Justice Initiative. This is a part-time position of 18 hours per week, and requires in-person presence in New York City. The position is expected to begin in early November 2026 and last through June 30, 2027. K ey responsibilities include: Contribute to the design, implementation, and evaluation of the Artistic Justice Initiative's arts-based programs. Serve as the primary point of contact for scheduling and coordinating all program and research activities, including workshop cycles, programming calendars, staff training, artistic showcases, and documentation activities. Collaborate with Dr. Sajnani on participant recruitment and cohort development efforts, including managing outreach and orientation materials, coordinating application and interview processes, and maintaining adherence to institutional and IRB-approved protocols where applicable. Contribute to the collection, organization, and quality assurance of quantitative and qualitative data, including participant surveys, interviews, observational data, and creative outputs, applying standardized procedures to maintain data integrity. Analyze data across behavioral, psychosocial, and program evaluation datasets using appropriate qualitative and quantitative methodologies. Collaborate on the development of manuscripts for publication, and support the dissemination of findings through public-facing channels. Support the ongoing refinement and expansion of arts and health programming within correctional settings by contributing to strategic planning, program innovation, and evidence-building efforts. The Part-Time Researcher will have opportunities to contribute to conference presentations, publications, and a broad range of interdisciplinary activities through the Jameel Arts & Health Lab, New York University, and the Artistic Justice Initiative's national and international partnerships. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly pay range for this position is $60.00-$64.00. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Master's degree required, Ph.D. preferred, in Creative Arts Therapies, Criminal Justice, Arts in Health, Arts Administration, Education or a related field. Minimum of five years of experience working on interdisciplinary research teams, preferably within arts and health, criminal legal systems, public health, community-based research, or creative arts therapies. Minimum of ten years of experience working with incarcerated people and correctional staff in prisons, jails or other correctional spaces. Experience coordinating complex, multi-site projects involving diverse stakeholders, including artists, researchers, community organizations, and institutional partners. S trong organizational skills, exceptional attention to detail, and the demonstrated ability to manage multiple concurrent priorities and timelines. Excellent verbal, written, and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to effectively communicate with both specialized and general audiences. D emonstrated expertise in quantitative and qualitative data analysis preferred. Ability to work independently while fostering collaborative relationships across interdisciplinary teams and institutional settings. Please apply via Interfolio by uploading: (a) CV or resume; (b) cover letter clearly detailing how you meet the above qualifications and why you are interested in the position; and (c) names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three references. The review of applicants will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174114": {"title": "Research Scientist/Program Officer, Advocacy", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the Provost: Scholars at Risk Location: New York, NY ABOUT THE ORGANIZATION: Scholars at Risk (SAR) is an international network of over 600 higher education institutions in more than 42 countries. SAR\u2019s mission is to promote academic freedom and defend the human rights of scholars and their communities worldwide. SAR meets this mission through three primary activity areas: (1) protection services for threatened scholars and other at-risk individuals; (2) advocacy and related research, reporting, and campaigning for scholars or other victims of attacks on higher education; and (3) research and training on academic freedom and its constituent elements. In furtherance of all of these activities, SAR organizes multiple events, trainings, courses, and publications, including a biennial SAR Global Congress\u2014an international gathering for SAR\u2019s global movement and prospective new partners to share experience, discuss critical issues, and shape SAR\u2019s research and advocacy strategies for the coming years. SAR\u2019s staff is organized into teams for each of these programming areas, along with a membership team to research and develop services and activities for network member institutions, faculty and students, and related development and administration roles. Information at www.scholarsatrisk.org . POSITION SUMMARY: Scholars at Risk (SAR) seeks a detail-oriented, research-focused individual with excellent analytical and writing skills to join SAR\u2019s advocacy team as a Program Officer to lead the research, drafting, and publication of SAR\u2019s annual Free to Think report; to contribute research and writing to SAR\u2019s Academic Freedom Monitoring Project and related reports; and to manage a fellowship program. Through the Monitoring Project, SAR conducts research and reporting on attacks on higher education communities worldwide; the data is summarized and analyzed in SAR\u2019s annual Free to Think report and in other topical reports. SAR organizes regular cohorts of academics to further research on academic freedom as SAR/Mellon Fellows. SAR seeks a Program Officer to contribute to these projects. The position requires excellent research, analysis, writing, and project management skills, as well as a practiced attention to detail and judgment. It is ideal for a proactive, thoughtful individual who takes initiative, enjoys the research process, has excellent writing skills, and likes to work on a small team. The position requires timely and professional communication, including with partners, SAR and NYU staff, academia, and the public. The role is based in New York City and reports to SAR\u2019s Senior Program Officer, Advocacy. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & DUTIES: Research, analyze, write incident reports for SAR\u2019s Academic Freedom Monitoring Project, which identifies and registers attacks on the higher education sector worldwide (50%) Using SAR\u2019s research methodology, conduct research on attacks on the higher education community, as reported in open sources; and draft, edit, verify, and publish summary reports. Collaborate on efforts to assess project efficiency and propose and implement improvements, including assessing the project\u2019s database, use of partner data, and AI. Ensure appropriate and timely distribution of the project\u2019s incidents and key findings, including over social media, in newsletters, and in submissions to stakeholders. Regularly share key incidents and trends with SAR staff and make recommendations as to appropriate responses. Research, analyze, write, and produce reports that highlight trends in attacks on higher education, and develop response tools (30%) Lead the research and writing process for SAR\u2019s annual Free to Think report, drawing on Monitoring Project data. Lead the production and distribution of Free to Think, liaising with communications staff. Support research, writing, and production of other topical reports produced by SAR. Support development of training modules based on reports\u2019 recommendations, and of similar advocacy responses to the problem of attacks on higher education. Present report data and recommendations to academic and general audiences; and lead or co-lead related trainings. Organize SAR/Mellon Fellowship program (20%) Coordinate research cohorts of SAR/Mellon Fellows, including organizing regular fellows\u2019 meetings, commenting on research, and encouraging research on underfunded/understudied topics. Administer the annual call for proposals, including text in SAR\u2019s communications, responding to inquiries, conducting an initial review of proposals, and coordinating logistics for the review committee. Organize in-person fellows\u2019 meetings. Ensure timely payments to fellows. Undergraduate degree and 3-5 years\u2019 experience, or similar, relevant experience. Excellent research, writing, and editorial skills; demonstrated ability to synthesize and distill complex information for general audiences. Exceptional communication skills and proven proficiency in delivering impactful presentations. Exceptional attention to detail, initiative, time-management, and problem-solving abilities. Experience in report writing, production, distribution, and strategy preferred. Experience in data-driven decision making. Experience in project management, especially in managing competing priorities. Commitment to human rights, academic freedom, and diversity, equity, and inclusion. Ability to manage complex political and cultural factors, difficult information, including human rights abuses, and tensions between long- and short-term goals. Proficiency in wordprocessing and spreadsheet software (e.g., Word/Google Docs, Excel/Sheets) and website content management systems (e.g., WordPress). COMPENSATION & BENEFITS: This is a full-time, at-will position on an open-ended term, subject to program priorities, exemplary performance, and availability of funds. The position offers a salary range of $55,000 - 70,000, comparable to similar positions at peer organizations in the human rights/NGO field. Scholars at Risk is an independent not-for-profit corporation hosted at New York University (NYU). SAR staff are NYU employees seconded to SAR and are eligible for NYU employee benefits, including generous vacation leave and holidays; medical, dental, vision and life insurance options; and retirement benefits (all subject to terms established by NYU as described at https://www.nyu.edu/employees/benefit/full-time/professional-research-staff.html ). Apply online at https://www.scholarsatrisk.org/category/career-opportunities/ by uploading the requested materials in one combined PDF: (1) a letter of interest, (2) resume, (3) list of references or letters of reference, and (4) a brief, unedited writing sample. DEADLINE FOR INITIAL APPLICATION REVIEW IS October 12, 2025. Applications received after the deadline will be reviewed on a rolling basis, until the position is filled. No phone calls or email inquiries, please. (Regrettably, SAR is not able to sponsor work-eligible visas.)"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/166371": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate (P.I.: Dr. Brian L. Schmidt)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery: Translational Research Center Location: New York, NY Postdoctoral position in cancer pain neurobiology The New York University Oral Cancer Center seeks Postdoctoral Research Fellows with a background in cancer and/or neuroscience to study the reciprocal interaction between oral cancer and sensory nerves. Research projects will investigate the mechanisms by which mediators released from oral cancers activate sensory neurons to induce pain. Work will focus on the development and characterization of a new cancer model. Studies will use cell culture, animal models of oral cancer, genetically engineered mice, in vivo calcium fluorimetry, rodent behavior, imaging, molecular biology, and analysis of oral cancer specimens. Applicants need not have experience in all areas, as expertise is available in our group. We encourage postdoctoral fellows to broaden their skills and experience during their postdoctoral. The position offers competitive salary and provides opportunities for career development and exposure to a rich scientific environment at NYU and the New York City area. Positions open immediately and for up to five years. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $65,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Applicants should have a PhD, MD or DDS degree and experience in cancer, neuroscience or related disciplines. Candidates should have the ability and motivation to work independently and to manage their research projects. Individuals are encouraged to apply, who have outstanding publication records from their graduate or postgraduate work and a passion to better understand cancer-neural interactions that can have potential to translate into treatments to stop cancer and alleviate cancer pain. Candidates should apply in Interfolio with a CV, three references and short letter of intent by going to the:\u00a0 \"Apply Now\" button located on the right."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168824": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist  (P.I.: Dr. Brian L. Schmidt)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery: Translational Research Center Location: New York The New York University Translational Research Center seeks a Junior Research Scientist with a background in bioengineering to study calcium signaling in preclinical oral cancer pain models. The researcher must have experience with in vivo calcium imaging as well as in dissociated neurons and Schwann cells. The researcher will perform experiments independently as well as under guidance, utilize and maintain a variety of laboratory equipment/devices, perform behavioral and molecular experiments, and compile data and generate reports. Primary responsibilities include: Design experiments, set up microscopy for imaging, generate preclinical cancer models, analyze data and write results. Record, analyze and format experimental data; assist in manuscript preparation, grant application, and meeting presentations. Order and catalog animals, supplies, and reagents, maintain chemical and tissue sample inventory. Maintain cleanliness of work area and quality and safety standards; ensure disposal of hazardous and infectious materials; adhere to infection control procedures; prepare required documents for Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee applications for various protocols. Assist with student research projects. The position offers competitive salary and provides opportunities for career development and exposure to a rich scientific environment at NYU and the New York City area. Position is open immediately. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $50,000 - $58,500. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Applicants must hold a BA/BS degree in Biology, Chemistry, or a closely related field. Applicants must have at least 2 years of related experience, be proficient with a variety of laboratory equipment, and have experience working with animals/animal studies. Candidates should have the ability and motivation to work independently and to manage their research projects. The candidate must possess excellent oral communication and scientific writing skills in English. Applications must include: (1) a CV, (2) a contact information for at least three references and (3) a cover letter with a brief statement concerning research experience and interests. To access the application, please click the \"Apply Now\" button located on the right."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192764": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Climate Finance) Volatility Institute at NYU Shanghai", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: VINS Location: Shanghai, China NYU Shanghai is seeking a two-year Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join the Volatility Institute at NYU Shanghai (VINS). The candidate is expected to join VINS starting from Spring 2027, and the position will remain open until a suitable candidate is found. The appointment start date for non-Chinese applicants is subject to immigration regulations. The ideal candidate should have a strong background in Climate Finance, and a particular interest in the research related to the China market. The position offers opportunities to work alongside top-tier scholars, including Nobel Laureates, and have access to valuable and unique databases provided by VINS through its close collaborations with top financial institutions and data providers in China. The successful candidate is expected to participate in research projects within VINS and joint research projects between VINS and other financial institutions and schools. The position is renewable upon mutual agreement. Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions. Responsibilities Independently work on research projects aiming at top journal publications Work on VINS research projects, including data collection, cleaning, analysis, and writing reports Participate in joint research projects between VINS and other financial institutions/schools Assist the executive director on academic research studies including literature review,data processing, and analysis Help organize VINS conferences and workshops Any additional responsibilities related to Institute resources and activities that fall under the purview of the Research Fellow Qualified candidates must have recently received their Ph.D./ABD in Finance, Statistics or Econometrics Genuinely interested in research and capable of executing research projects with little supervision Have proven ability to use sophisticated econometric tools to analyze complex financial data on a large scale Be able to conduct research and write research papers/reports in both English and Mandarin with a high level of fluency Be familiar with Chinese financial markets, and have a strong background in finance and statistics Applications must include: Cover letter explaining applicant\u2019s interest in the position Curriculum Vitae Two letters of reference (must include committee chair or members for ABDs) Brief statement of academic or intellectual interests and research (including specific research topics, research purpose and significance, research methods and content framework, research innovation, etc.) Recent 2-3 papers/working papers Application Process The review of applications will begin immediately, and will continue until the position is filled. If you have any questions on the position, please contact vins@nyu.edu . For questions regarding the application process, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . About VINS The Volatility Institute at NYU Shanghai (VINS), located in the Qiantan (the New Bund) neighborhood of Pudong District, near to Shanghai\u2019s new hub of finance, technology and other key industries. VINS aims to create opportunities for research focused on both the Chinese financial markets and markets around the world. It also seeks to facilitate collaboration and community-building among market participants and academic researchers within China and abroad, as well as help improve global financial markets by providing timely financial information and analysis to academics, practitioners, regulators and policy makers through innovative technology platforms and services. The Volatility Institute at NYU Shanghai operates in close partnership with the NYU Stern Volatility and Risk Institute, the expansion of the Stern\u2019s Volatility Institute. Under the guidance of Robert Engle, a NYU Stern Emeritus Professor and 2003 Nobel Laureate in Economics, the institute members are developing cutting-edge research and providing comprehensive analyses on global financial markets\u2019 risks, and therefore contributes in a meaningful way to international financial policies. VINS is dedicated to advancing China\u2019s financial reform and development, particularly in the field of climate finance. It provides policy recommendations to government and regulatory authorities on the impact of climate change on the financial system and supports industry participants in achieving sustainable economic development while addressing climate change challenges. For more Information, please visit our website: https://research.shanghai.nyu.edu/vins . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191796": {"title": "Assistant Research Scientist in AI+Physics/Climate (NYU Courant Institute)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: NYU Courant Math and NYU Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY We are seeking a full-time Assistant Research Scientist to join a frontier research collaboration developing next-generation AI methods for scientific applications, particularly physics and climate, at the NYU Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. The successful candidate will contribute to building and evaluating physics-constrained AI foundation models for climate predictions, including generalization to unseen climate forcings, and extreme events. The candidate will be primarily advised by Prof. Laure Zanna and co-advised by Prof. Carlos Fernandez-Granda, as part of the M 2 LInES collaboration . They will also have the opportunity to collaborate with leading scientists in the field, which in addition to M 2 LInES scientists, include Jesse Rusak and Alex Merose from Open Athena , Ai2 Climate Modeling , and Prof. Richard Turner (Cambridge), on creating a new generation of AI foundation and world models for Climate. This will build on a suite of major developments led by the team in developing AI emulators for the ocean, sea-ice, and atmosphere, and the full climate science system, with 1000x speedups over the original models. The expansion of these works includes exploring multiple resolutions, multimodality, and the grand challenges of learning physics on multi-century timescales for climate projections. The position, available immediately, is a full-time appointment, initially for one year, with the possibility of renewal for two additional years, subject to satisfactory performance and available funding. Some examples of recent work (more at M 2 LInES ): Multi-resolution ocean AI model, Samudra 2: https://doi.org/10.1029/2025GL119340 Coupled ocean-atmosphere AI model, SamudrACE: https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.02610 A conservative sea-ice AI model, FloeNet: https://doi.org/10.1029/2026GL122981 In compliance with NYU's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $58,656 - $110,000. NYU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer quality, as well as market and organization considerations. Candidates with particularly relevant or specialized knowledge may be considered for compensation above the higher end of the range. Education: Bachelor's degree in computer science, machine learning, applied mathematics, engineering, physical science, or a related technical discipline. Master's degree is a plus. 2+ years of research experience Expertise or strong demonstrated skill in deep learning and computational science Experience or interest in developing methods for scientific applications (not necessarily climate-related) Proficiency in Python Experience with HPC (GPUs preferred) is a plus Experience with agentic programming is a plus Related Skills and Other Requirements: Ability to work at the interface of AI and science/engineering problems Ability to contribute to multiple projects in a fast-paced environment Ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of an interdisciplinary team Outstanding written and oral communication skills Technical and scientific curiosity A record of relevant projects, publications, or software appropriate to their career stage For full consideration, applicants should submit via Interfolio, the following by October 20th 2026 (earlier applications are strongly encouraged): A cover letter (no more than 2 pages) detailing 1) research/technical experience, 2) how you would fit our project, and 3) available start date. A Curriculum Vitae (no more than 2 pages) A list of relevant projects, publications, and/or software repositories (including GitHub repos) 2 confidential letters of recommendation Applications are reviewed only if you have fulfilled the requirements above, and submitted to Interfolio. For additional information, please contact m2lines@nyu.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191798": {"title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in AI for Science (NYU Courant Institute)", "description": "Posted: Aug 19, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: NYU Courant Math and NYU Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY We are seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Researcher to join a frontier research collaboration developing next-generation AI methods for scientific applications, particularly physics and climate, at the NYU Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. The successful candidate will contribute to building and evaluating physics-constrained AI foundation models for climate predictions, including generalization to unseen climate forcings, and extreme events. The candidate will be primarily advised by Prof. Laure Zanna and co-advised by Prof. Carlos Fernandez-Granda, as part of the M 2 LInES collaboration . They will also have the opportunity to collaborate with leading scientists in the field, which in addition to M 2 LInES scientists, include Jesse Rusak and Alex Merose from Open Athena , Ai2 Climate Modeling , and Prof. Richard Turner (Cambridge), on creating a new generation of AI foundation and world models for Climate. This will build on a suite of major developments led by the team in developing AI emulators for the ocean, sea-ice, and atmosphere, and the full climate science system, with 1000x speedups over the original models. The expansion of these works includes exploring multiple resolutions, multimodality, and the grand challenges of learning physics on multi-century timescales for climate projections. The position, available immediately, is a full-time appointment, initially for one year, with the possibility of renewal for two additional years, subject to satisfactory performance and available funding. Some examples of recent work (more at M 2 LInES ): Multi-resolution ocean AI model, Samudra 2: https://doi.org/10.1029/2025GL119340 Coupled ocean-atmosphere AI model, SamudrACE: https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.02610 A conservative sea-ice AI model, FloeNet: https://doi.org/10.1029/2026GL122981 The position, available immediately, is a full-time appointment, initially for one year, with the possibility of renewal for an additional year, subject to satisfactory performance and available funding. In compliance with NYU's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $94,000. NYU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer quality, as well as market and organization considerations. Expertise or strong demonstrated skill in deep learning and computational science Experience or interest in developing methods for scientific applications (not necessarily climate-related) Proficiency in Python Experience with HPC (GPUs preferred) is a plus Experience with agentic programming is a plus Related Skills and Other Requirements Ability to work at the interface of AI and science/engineering problems Ability to contribute to multiple projects in a fast-paced environment Ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of an interdisciplinary team Outstanding written and oral communication skills Technical and scientific curiosity A record of relevant projects, publications, or software appropriate to their career stage For full consideration, applicants should submit via Interfolio, the following by October 20th 2026 ( Earlier applications are strongly encouraged) : A cover letter (no more than 2 pages) detailing 1) research/technical experience, 2) how you would fit our project, and 3) available start date. A Curriculum Vitae (no more than 2 pages) A list of relevant projects, publications, and/or software repositories (including GitHub repos) 3 confidential letters of recommendation. Applications are reviewed only if you have fulfilled the requirements above, and submitted to Interfolio. For additional information, please contact m2lines@nyu.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191803": {"title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Ocean/Climate Dynamics (NYU Courant Institute School)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: NYU Courant Math and NYU Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY We are seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Researcher to join our team working on ocean and climate variability and predictability at NYU Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Working primarily with Prof. Laure Zanna, the successful candidate will explore what drives extreme ocean surface temperatures and build tools to analyze and predict them. In particular, we expect the candidate to explore the onset and termination of extreme ocean temperature conditions using theory, numerical, and AI models; no experience in all areas is necessary, but we expect candidates to engage with different methods. The postdoc will be expected to lead independent research directions while collaborating with a team of domain scientists, experts in ocean and climate physics and modeling, and computational/AI experts, as part of a collaboration between the international M 2 LInES collaboration . The position, available immediately, is a full-time appointment, initially for one year, with the possibility of renewal for an additional year, subject to satisfactory performance and available funding. In compliance with NYU's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $94,000. NYU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer quality, as well as market and organization considerations. Education : Ph.D. in oceanography, atmospheric science, climate science, applied mathematics, physics, engineering, or a related technical discipline. Experience : Expertise in at least one area among the following: ocean, atmosphere, sea-ice dynamics, and/or climate science. Demonstrated skill in, or strong interest in developing, computational methods for studying the ocean/climate system Proficiency in Python Experience with HPC is a plus Experience with agentic programming is a plus Related Skills and Other Requirements Ability to lead, develop, and contribute to multiple projects in a fast-paced environment Ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of an interdisciplinary team Outstanding written and oral communication skills Technical and scientific curiosity A solid record of relevant publications and software appropriate to their career stage For full consideration, applicants should submit via Interfolio, the following by October 20th, 2026 ( Earlier applications are strongly encouraged) : A cover letter (no more than 2 pages) detailing 1) research/technical experience, 2) how you would fit our project, and 3) available start date. A Curriculum Vitae (no more than 2 pages) A list of relevant projects, publications, and/or software repositories (including GitHub repos) 3 confidential letters of recommendation. Applications are reviewed only if you have fulfilled the requirements above, and submitted to Interfolio. For additional information, please contact m2lines@nyu.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/164213": {"title": "Polymathic AI Research Scientist", "description": "Posted: Feb 26, 2025 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY Join Polymathic AI: Pioneering the Future of AI-Driven Scientific Discovery! Polymathic AI is looking for full-time Research Scientists to join our team in building next-generation Foundation Models for Science at NYU CDS. Recent breakthroughs in Large Language Models and diffusion-based generative models have propelled AI into new frontiers. At Polymathic AI, we are harnessing these advances to develop powerful, interpretable AI models with transformative applications across scientific disciplines\u2014from astrophysics and biology to solar physics and material sciences. As part of our team, you'll collaborate with leading researchers from diverse scientific domains, pushing the boundaries of AI to solve some of the most pressing scientific challenges. With access to competitive computational resources, we offer an exciting multidisciplinary environment where machine learning meets real-world scientific impact. What You\u2019ll Do: Conduct cutting-edge research at the intersection of AI and science Develop large-scale deep learning models for scientific discovery Work closely with experts in machine learning, domain sciences, and AI Contribute to the development of interpretable and high-performing AI models Develop and train large AI models for science Collaborate and/or mentor other students, postdocs, Software Engineers, Research Analysts and Research Scientists. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the full-time annual compensation range for the Research Scientist level is $120,000 - $250,000 depending on experience. Why Join Us? Collaborate with a world-class, interdisciplinary team Access state-of-the-art compute capabilities for large-model training Be part of a mission to redefine how AI advances scientific understanding This full-time, fixed-term position is based on-site at NYU\u2019s Center for Data Science in New York City, with an initial one-year appointment and potential for extension. If you\u2019re passionate about shaping the future of AI for science, we\u2019d love to hear from you! Deadline Applications will be reviewed starting April 2, 2025 and we will fill the positions on a rolling basis. Earlier applications are strongly encouraged. Education: Ph.D. in machine learning, computer science, engineering or related technical discipline. Experience: Experience in developing novel ML architectures Expertise in developing and training large AI models is preferred Research expertise in one major area of computational science is preferred. Proficiency in Python and either PyTorch or JAX Experience with HPC, GPU is preferred Related Skills and Other Requirements Ability to collaborate on multidisciplinary research in a collegial environment Ability to lead, develop and contribute to projects Effective communication skills Ability to work both independently and collaboratively Technical and scientific curiosity Professional communication skills REQUIRED APPLICATION MATERIALS CV including publication list and software repositories A research statement highlighting previous research and future research agenda Three (3) letters of recommendation submitted confidentially by the letter writer via Interfolio application."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/164954": {"title": "Polymathic AI Junior Research Scientist", "description": "Posted: Mar 13, 2025 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY NYU CDS seeks Junior Research Scientists as part of its new initiative, Polymathic AI, Building Foundation Models for Science. Recent advances in machine learning, including Large Language Models and diffusion based generative models have driven significant advances in Artificial Intelligence. Polymathic AI seeks to leverage these ideas to develop the next generation of AI-powered scientific analysis tools, seeking more powerful\u00a0 and more interpretable models with broad applications to many scientific disciplines. Our group includes researchers from multiple scientific disciplines ranging from machine learning, to astrophysics, biology, neuroscience and quantum computing. The selected candidates will work closely with a group of computer scientists, domain scientists, and AI experts in developing software and leveraging large language models and large AI models to solve pressing scientific challenges. Polymathic AI has access to computational resources allowing training of large AI models to support this new initiative. We offer an exciting multidisciplinary context for researching AI and its applications. The Junior Research Scientist position will take place in-person at NYU CDS office in New York City. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS/RESPONSIBILITIES Develop and train large AI models for pressing scientific questions Curate large datasets pertinent to the initiative Develop, implement, maintain, and support software relevant to the initiative. Engage in foundation models for science research Collaborate with other software engineers, Flatiron Research Fellows, Research scientists Perform any other duties or tasks as required by the initiative Help build, improve, and support high-quality, open-source software libraries relating to efforts within the group In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $80,000. Education B.S., M.S. in machine learning, computer science, engineering or related technical discipline. REQUIRED APPLICATION MATERIALS Curriculum vitae with bibliography 3 references (names and emails)"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174891": {"title": "Software Engineer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY Center for Data Science at NYU seeks a full-time software engineer position as part of its new initiative, Polymathic AI, Building Foundation Models for Science.\u00a0 Recent advances in machine learning, including Large Language Models and diffusion based generative models have driven significant advances in Artificial Intelligence. Polymathic AI seeks to leverage these ideas\u00a0 to develop the next generation of AI-powered scientific analysis tools, seeking more powerful\u00a0 and more interpretable models with broad applications to many scientific disciplines. Our group\u00a0 includes researchers from multiple scientific disciplines ranging from machine learning, to\u00a0 astrophysics, biology, neuroscience and quantum computing. The selected candidates will work closely with a group of computer scientists, domain scientists,\u00a0 and AI experts in developing software and leveraging large AI models to solve pressing scientific challenges. The hired engineer will have access to computational resources allowing training of large AI models. We offer an exciting multidisciplinary context for researching AI and its\u00a0 applications. The Software Engineer will report to Shirley Ho, Senior Research Scientist and is a full-time fixed term position with a duration of one year, with possible renewals. This position is based on site at the Center for Data Science at NYU. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS/RESPONSIBILITIES Develop, implement, maintain, and support software relevant to the initiative. Engage in research on foundation models for science Collaborate with other Software Engineers, Flatiron Research Fellows, Research Analysts. Lead and improve open-source software practices SPECIFIC FUNCTIONS/RESPONSIBILITIES In addition to the essential functions listed above, the applicant should also be able to undertake one of the two sets of functions specific to the two focus areas for this position: Research facing Develop and train large AI models for science Curate large datasets pertinent to the initiative Optimize model performance Deployment facing Packaging research codes into deployable units Developing and deploying end-user facing applications for AI models Maintain deployed services In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $100,000-$250,000. Minimum Qualifications Education B.S., M.S. or Ph.D. in machine learning, computer science, engineering or related technical discipline. Experience At least three years\u2019 experience with software development in a research setting Expertise in developing and training large AI models. Proficiency in Python and either PyTorch, JAX, or Tensorflow Experience with HPC, GPU, or code profiling is required Knowledge of software engineering practices for working in groups, including software development life cycles, coding standards, code review, and version control Related Skills and Other Requirements Ability to collaborate on multidisciplinary research in a collegial environment Ability to lead, develop and contribute to software projects Effective communication, documentation, and user-support skills Ability to work both independently and collaboratively Technical and scientific curiosity Professional communication skills REQUIRED APPLICATION MATERIALS CV including possibly previous projects, software repositories, publications Description of previous research/engineering experience (not to exceed 2 pages, including figures). Three names for references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/178449": {"title": "Postdoctoral Researcher at Polymathic AI", "description": "Posted: Dec 01, 2025 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY The Center for Data Science at New York University (NYU) invites applications for postdoctoral researchers as part of the Polymathic AI initiative, focused on generalization and transfer across scientific disciplines , as well as mechanistic interpretability of scientific foundation models . The rush to build foundation models has led to the development of large machine learning models in Astrophysics, fluid dynamics, biology, weather prediction, solar prediction \u2026 At the core of their ability is the capacity to generalize unseen data and to be applied as pretrained models to different domains. However, these properties remain only partially understood, exploring and advancing them, both during training and at test time, is crucial for developing the next generation of pan-scientific models. The selected postdoctoral candidates will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary team based in New York, spanning NYU and the Flatiron Institute, composed of machine learning researchers, engineers, and domain scientists. This collaborative environment offers a unique opportunity to work on cutting edge AI models and advance AI for scientific discovery. Expected skills/functions: Engage in research and train large AI models for science Generate new large scientific datasets Develop and maintain complex codebases Read and integrate research across AI and scientific domains literature In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $80,000-$100,000. Ph.D. in a relevant field Please follow instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: Your curriculum vitae Your research statement (1-2 pages) Two (2) contact reference"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180888": {"title": "Assistant Professor Tenure-Track Positions for Fall 2026,  Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science", "description": "Posted: Jan 30, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY The Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science at New York University, seeks candidates for two (2) assistant professor faculty positions, anticipated to begin in September 2026. Faculty members are expected to be outstanding scholars and to participate in teaching at all levels, from undergraduate to doctoral. The aim of this position is to expand in the area of ML/AI for science, with a particular focus on the physical sciences. The ideal candidate will be firmly engaged in computational science, AI methodology,\u00a0 and scientific applications. The Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science furthers NYU\u2019s longtime preeminence in mathematics, applied mathematics, data science, computer science, and engineering. The school includes the Department of Mathematics, the Center for Data Science , and the Department of Computer Science. These positions may include affiliations with the Flatiron Institute Center for Computational Mathematics or the Tandon School of Engineering. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $100,000 - $150,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, and internal peer equity, as well as market and organization considerations. For full consideration,\u00a0 applications should be submitted by Feb 15. Applications will be considered until the positions are filled. Doctoral degree in a related field. Candidates should submit a curriculum vitae, a statement of research interest, and three reference letters."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184211": {"title": "[EOI][SPRING 2026] Polymathic AI Postdoctoral Researcher", "description": "Posted: Apr 01, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY The Center for Data Science at New York University (NYU) invites applications for postdoctoral scholars as part of the Polymathic AI initiative Polymathic AI is inviting applications for postdoctoral researchers in our team. Our research focuses on building and understanding foundation models for science, and in particular modeling complex interconnected systems from astronomy, biology, fluid dynamics to solar physics. For this position, we are looking for postdoctoral researchers that will contribute to our research on interpretability, generalization and general understanding of foundation models. The overarching objective is to create open access tools that enable researchers to reach meaningful understanding of the complex model quickly thus speeding up the pace of scientific discovery. Fulfilling this objective requires creativity to combine core-principles of machine learning to the practical tools of deep learning, now available through modern foundation models. For the theory part, the selected candidate will work in close collaboration with the Adaptive Bayesian Intelligence group headed by Dr. Emtiyaz Khan at RIKEN-AIP (in Tokyo, Japan) and TU Darmstadt (in Germany). The candidate is expected to make the best use of adaptive-learning methods to develop practical tools to the most pressing scientific challenges in science. The collaboration may require some amount of travelling to Japan and Germany. The selected candidates will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary team based in New York, spanning NYU and the Flatiron Institute, composed of machine learning researchers, engineers, and domain scientists. This collaborative environment\u00a0 at Polymathic AI offers a unique opportunity to work on cutting edge AI models and advance AI for scientific discovery. Several positions are available. The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the positions are filled. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $80,000-$110,000. We are seeking highly motivated individuals with backgrounds in computer science, machine learning, and science to join our group in a quest to perform breakthrough research. An ideal candidate has strong research skills in relevant areas: machine learning, foundation models, computational sciences. A Ph.D. degree is required. Significant experience with programming, project management, and large-scale model training is a plus. To apply, please send us your application and reference letters: The application should include: A 1-2 page research statement, please make sure to address the following: the specific areas you want to work on, how they fit with research in Polymathic, how they build upon your previous work, and your preferred start date; Your current CV with research background and a list of your publications; Copies of your two representative publications. Submit the application to LINK. We encourage you to also include links to any software that you have developed. Arrange for two letters of reference from your previous research supervisors and collaborators to be sent directly within 2 weeks of your submission."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184213": {"title": "[EOI][SPRING 2026] Polymathic AI Research Scientist", "description": "Posted: Apr 01, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY The Center for Data Science at New York University (NYU) invites applications for research scientists as part of the Polymathic AI initiative Polymathic AI is inviting applications for researchers in our team. Our research focuses on building and understanding foundation models for science, and in particular modeling complex interconnected systems from astronomy, biology, fluid dynamics to solar physics. For this position, we are looking for researchers that will contribute to our research on interpretability, generalization and general understanding of foundation models. The overarching objective is to create open access tools that enable researchers to reach meaningful understanding of the complex model quickly thus speeding up the pace of scientific discovery. Fulfilling this objective requires creativity to combine core-principles of machine learning to the practical tools of deep learning, now available through modern foundation models. For the theory part, the selected candidate will work in close collaboration with the Adaptive Bayesian Intelligence group headed by Dr. Emtiyaz Khan at RIKEN-AIP (in Tokyo, Japan) and TU Darmstadt (in Germany). The candidate is expected to make the best use of adaptive-learning methods to develop practical tools to the most pressing scientific challenges in science. The collaboration may require some amount of travelling to Japan and Germany. The selected candidates will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary team based in New York, spanning NYU and the Flatiron Institute, composed of machine learning researchers, engineers, and domain scientists. This collaborative environment\u00a0 at Polymathic AI offers a unique opportunity to work on cutting edge AI models and advance AI for scientific discovery. Several positions are available. The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the positions are filled. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $80,000-$200,000. We are seeking highly motivated individuals with backgrounds in computer science, machine learning, and science to join our group in a quest to perform breakthrough research. An ideal candidate has strong research skills in relevant areas: machine learning, foundation models, computational sciences. A PhD degree is required. Significant experience with programming, project management, and large-scale model training is a plus. To apply, please send us your application and reference letters: The application should include: A 1-2 page research statement, please make sure to address the following: the specific areas you want to work on, how they fit with research in Polymathic, how they build upon your previous work, and your preferred start date; Your current CV with research background and a list of your publications; Copies of your two representative publications. Submit the application to LINK. We encourage you to also include links to any software that you have developed. Arrange for two letters of reference from your previous research supervisors and collaborators to be sent directly within 2 weeks of your submission."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185110": {"title": "[EOI] Postdoctoral Researcher at Polymathic AI: Solar Physics", "description": "Posted: Apr 17, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY The Center for Data Science at New York University (NYU) invites applications for postdoctoral researchers as part of the Polymathic AI initiative, focused on applications of AI to solar physics. Our research focuses on building and understanding foundation models for science, and in particular modeling complex interconnected systems from astronomy, biology, fluid dynamics to solar physics. For this position, we are looking for postdoctoral researchers that will contribute to applying our research capabilities to solar physics. The overarching objective is to build large scale models of solar phenomena and apply them to important applications in solar physics and space weather research. The selected postdoctoral candidates will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary team based in New York, spanning NYU and the Flatiron Institute, composed of machine learning researchers, engineers, and domain scientists. This collaborative environment offers a unique opportunity to work on cutting edge AI models, with a focus on generalization and interpretability, and advance AI for scientific discovery. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Ph.D. in a relevant field Expected skills/functions: Experience with solar physics is an advantage, but is not a requirement Engage in research and train large AI models for science Generate new large scientific datasets Develop and maintain complex codebases Read literature and integrate research across AI and scientific domain Please follow instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: CV Two publications Two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185111": {"title": "[EOI] Postdoctoral Researcher at Polymathic AI: Generalization and Transfer in Scientific AI", "description": "Posted: Apr 17, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY The Center for Data Science at New York University (NYU) invites applications for postdoctoral researchers as part of the Polymathic AI initiative, focused on generalization and transfer across scientific disciplines , as well as interpretability of scientific foundation models . The rush to build foundation models has led to the development of large machine learning models in Astrophysics, fluid dynamics, biology, weather prediction, solar prediction \u2026 At the core of their ability is the capacity to generalize unseen data and to be applied as pretrained models to different domains. However, these properties remain only partially understood. Exploring and advancing these properties, during both training and at test time is crucial for developing the next generation of pan-scientific models. The selected postdoctoral candidates will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary team based in New York, spanning NYU and the Flatiron Institute, composed of machine learning researchers, engineers, and domain scientists. This collaborative environment offers a unique opportunity to work on cutting edge AI models, with a focus on generalization and interpretability, and advance AI for scientific discovery. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Ph.D. in a relevant field Expected skills/functions: Engage in research and train large AI models for science Generate new large scientific datasets Develop and maintain complex codebases Read literature and integrate research across AI and scientific domains Please follow instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: CV Two publications Two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189297": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Postdoctoral Associate | Center for Data Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Posted: Sep 01, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in Data Science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Center for Data Science (CDS), a department within the Courant Institute School welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Completion of a PhD in Data Science, Computer Science, Mathematics, or other discipline aligned with faculty research by the start date. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189298": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Part-Time Assistant Research Scientist | Center for Data Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY Part-time Assistant Research Scientist in the Center for Data Science for a maximum of 27 hours per week. Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in Data Science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Center for Data Science (CDS), a department within the Courant Institute School welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current minimum hourly rate for this position is $18. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A qualified candidate should have a bachelor's degree in a relevant field with some prior experience in research and writing in a relevant field at a level of quality and rigor. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189299": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Research Scientist | Center for Data Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Posted: Sep 01, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in Data Science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Center for Data Science (CDS), a department within the Courant Institute School welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Minimum qualification: Completion of a PhD in Data Science, Computer Science, Mathematics, or other discipline aligned with faculty research by the start date. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189301": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Assistant Research Scientist | Center for Data Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY Assistant Research Scientist in Data Science. Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in Data Science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Center for Data Science (CDS), a department within the Courant Institute School welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the current annual base salary range for this position is $58,656 - $110,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Minimum qualification: Completion of a Master's degree in disciplines aligned with CDS faculty research by the start date. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189302": {"title": "[EOI] [FALL 2026] Junior Research Scientist | Center for Data Science | Courant Institute School", "description": "Posted: Sep 01, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY Junior Research Scientist in Data Science. Seeking \u201cexpression of interest\u201d from researchers interested in Data Science. In November of 2025, New York University formally established the new Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Uniting the three academic departments allows faculty to focus educational programming and maximize research efforts. The Courant School is located in the heart of New York City\u2014an international hub of technology, education, finance, and healthcare\u2014which gives our community exclusive access to academia and industry. The Center for Data Science (CDS), a department within the Courant Institute School welcomes researchers across a wide range of domains who will conduct deep and impactful research and contribute to the creative growth of the Courant Institute School community. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $58,656 - $110,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A qualified candidate should have a bachelor's degree in a relevant field and some prior experience in research and writing at a level of quality and rigor. Please follow the instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter Your curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193498": {"title": "Faculty Fellow Appointment in Data Science Fall 2027", "description": "Posted: Sep 15, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York The Center for Data Science (CDS) at New York University (NYU) invites applications for its highly prestigious CDS Faculty Fellow positions. Alumni of the Data Science Faculty Fellow program have secured top-level academic positions and industry jobs. For instance, our former Fellows obtained faculty positions here at NYU, University of Cambridge, American University, and Johns Hopkins University to list just the most recent ones. Given the prestigious nature of the position, we offer a generous compensation package which may include NYU faculty housing as well as funds to support research and travel. The Center for Data Science (CDS) is New York University\u2019s focal point for university-wide efforts in Data Science and Artificial Intelligence. The Center was established in 2013 to advance NYU\u2019s goal of creating a world-leading training and research facility serving researchers, industry professionals, and students at the forefront of Data Science, Machine Learning, and Artificial Intelligence. As of 2026, CDS became a department in the newly-formed Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. Our vibrant community of experts collaborates across disciplines to drive innovation and solve real-world challenges. Today, CDS counts 28 jointly appointed interdisciplinary faculty and 4 clinical faculty housed on three floors of our modern 60 5th Avenue building, one of New York City\u2019s historic properties. It is home to a top-ranked MS in Data Science program, one of the first PhD programs in Data Science, and an undergraduate program in Data Science, as well as a lively Fellow and Postdoctoral program. It has over 90 associate and affiliate faculty from 25 departments in 9 schools and units. With cross-disciplinary research and innovative educational programs, CDS is shaping the fields of Data Science and Artificial Intelligence. The CDS Faculty Fellow will be expected to work at the boundaries between computational methods and domain sciences. They are also encouraged to develop collaborations with faculty at CDS and NYU. They will lead original research projects of their choosing with impact in one or more scientific domains and in one or more methodological domains (machine learning, computer science, statistics, and applied mathematics). Fellows will also teach courses for CDS students. We seek outstanding interdisciplinary researchers doing CDS-aligned work in the following areas: Core Machine Learning/AI Methods Machine Learning Theory AI for Science Computational Social & Political Sciences Responsible AI Building and understanding robust AI systems for applications across science and society remains a challenge. Successful candidates will bring a research agenda that can take advantage of the unique intellectual opportunities afforded by NYU, and will have experience in working with researchers across different fields. This is a full-time, non-tenure position, with a teaching load of one (1) CDS course per year as the primary instructor. You will also be expected to participate in service activities of the Center for Data Science (such as helping review admissions applications, committee work, etc.). Appointments will be for two years. Fellows will be offered a competitive salary and benefits, with funds to support research and travel. A start date of September 1, 2027 is planned. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary of this position is a set amount of $101,766. Applicants must possess a doctorate degree or expect to have one by the start date. Applicants should have a strong publication record and demonstrated experience teaching quantitative topics. Please submit your cover letter, curriculum vitae, a brief statement of research interests (no longer than 4 pages; including how you see your work in relation to CDS), and at least 2 confidential letters of recommendation via the Faculty Fellow Interfolio application page. Your cover letter should indicate in bold which of the topic areas bulleted above your research pertains to (please try to choose only one). The statement of research interests should mention the names of one or more faculty members associated with the NYU Center for Data Science who would have substantial intellectual overlap with the applicant\u2019s interests and likely program of research. More information about relevant faculty members can be found on our Faculty page . Please submit your application by November 24, 2026 for full consideration. Any questions may be directed to cds@nyu.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193509": {"title": "Research Scientist in AI for Computational Fluid Dynamics @ Polymathic AI", "description": "Posted: Sep 14, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY Join Polymathic AI: Pioneering the Future of AI for Computational Fluid Dynamics! The Center for Data Science at New York University (NYU) invites applications for an exceptional Research Scientist as part of the Polymathic AI initiative, focused on foundation models for computational fluid dynamics (CFD). As part of our team, you'll collaborate with leading researchers from diverse scientific domains, pushing the boundaries of AI to solve some of the most pressing scientific challenges in CFD. With access to competitive computational resources, we offer an exciting multidisciplinary environment where machine learning meets real-world scientific impact. The selected candidate will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary team based in New York , spanning NYU and the Flatiron Institute, composed of machine learning researchers, engineers, and domain scientists. This collaborative environment offers a unique opportunity to work on cutting edge AI models. What You'll Do: Conduct cutting-edge research applying AI methods to science Develop large-scale deep learning models for CFD Work closely with experts in AI and CFD Contribute to the development of interpretable and high-performing AI models Develop and train large AI models for CFD Collaborate and/or mentor other students, postdocs, Software Engineers, Research Analysts and Research Scientists In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the full-time annual compensation range for this position is $120,000 - $250,000, depending on experience. Why Join Us? Collaborate with a world-class, interdisciplinary team Access state-of-the-art compute capabilities for large-model training Be part of a mission to redefine how AI advances scientific applications This full-time, fixed-term position is based on-site at NYU's Center for Data Science in New York City, with an initial one-year appointment and potential for extension. Ph.D. in a relevant field Expected skills/functions: Engage in research and train large AI models for science Generate new large scientific datasets Develop and maintain complex codebases Read literature and integrate research across AI and scientific domains REQUIRED APPLICATION MATERIALS CV including publication list and software repositories A research statement highlighting previous research and future research agenda Three (3) letters of recommendation submitted confidentially by the letter writer via Interfolio application Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and the position will remain open until filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193510": {"title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in AI for Computational Fluid Dynamics @ Polymathic AI", "description": "Posted: Sep 14, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY Join Polymathic AI: Pioneering the Future of AI for Computational Fluid Dynamics! The Center for Data Science at New York University (NYU) invites applications for an exceptional Postdoctoral Researcher as part of the Polymathic AI initiative, focused on foundation models for computational fluid dynamics (CFD). As part of our team, you'll collaborate with leading researchers from diverse scientific domains, pushing the boundaries of AI to solve some of the most pressing scientific challenges in CFD. With access to competitive computational resources, we offer an exciting multidisciplinary environment where machine learning meets real-world scientific impact. The selected candidate will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary team based in New York , spanning NYU and the Flatiron Institute, composed of machine learning researchers, engineers, and domain scientists. This collaborative environment offers a unique opportunity to work on cutting edge AI models. What You'll Do: Conduct cutting-edge research applying AI methods to science Develop large-scale deep learning models for CFD Work closely with experts in AI and CFD Contribute to the development of interpretable and high-performing AI models Develop and train large AI models for CFD Collaborate and/or mentor other students, postdocs, Software Engineers, Research Analysts and Research Scientists In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the full-time annual compensation range for this position is $62,500 - $125,000, depending on experience. Why Join Us? Collaborate with a world-class, interdisciplinary team Access state-of-the-art compute capabilities for large-model training Be part of a mission to redefine how AI advances scientific applications This full-time, fixed-term position is based on-site at NYU's Center for Data Science in New York City, with an initial one-year appointment and potential for extension. Ph.D. in a relevant field Expected skills/functions: Engage in research and train large AI models for science Generate new large scientific datasets Develop and maintain complex codebases Read literature and integrate research across AI and scientific domains REQUIRED APPLICATION MATERIALS CV including publication list and software repositories A research statement highlighting previous research and future research agenda Three (3) letters of recommendation submitted confidentially by the letter writer via Interfolio application Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and the position will remain open until filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195745": {"title": "Empire Postdoc Fellow in Self Improving AI", "description": "Posted: Oct 15, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Center for Data Science Location: New York, NY The Department of Data Science at New York University invites applications for a postdoctoral associate position hosted jointly by Qi Lei and Pavel Izmailov . The appointment will be for two years, with a flexible start date at any time in 2027. The successful candidate will develop and study AI systems that improve their reasoning through learning, search, and feedback, with a central focus on AI for mathematics. The position connects the hosts\u2019 interests in learning theory and generalization, reinforcement learning and post-training, and reliable mathematical problem solving. Potential research directions include: \u2022 Foundations of self-improvement and reasoning. Understand how pretraining, supervised fine-tuning, and reinforcement learning shape reasoning capabilities; when self-supervised training generalizes to harder problems; and what limits sustained improvement. \u2022 Learning from weak and verifiable feedback. Study weak-to-strong generalization, model-generated supervision, and verification-guided learning, with attention to reasoning faithfulness, reward exploitation, and preservation of existing capabilities. \u2022 Data for sustained improvement. Design synthetic-data generation, selection, curricula, and replay methods that turn computation into useful learning signals while limiting bias, overfitting, forgetting, and model collapse. \u2022 Applications in AI for mathematical discovery. Develop methods for proof search, conjecture generation, abstraction, counterexample search, and constructive mathematical problems. Combine learned models with symbolic tools and automated verification, where available, to learn reusable problem-solving strategies. The Fellow will use Empire AI for large-scale, reproducible experiments that connect these questions to practical algorithms. The work will include efficient adaptation, compute allocation across generation, verification, and training, and criteria for stopping or rolling back harmful updates. Expected contributions include research publications and reusable, open-source pipelines, evaluated for both reasoning performance and improvement per GPU-hour. The research agenda will be developed jointly with the candidate and may emphasize theoretical foundations, algorithm design, or empirical discovery. The candidate will collaborate with both hosts and participate in the NYU research community. Annual base salary: starting salary range: $62,500-92,500. The Empire AI fellowship provides salary and fringe support for two years. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in mathematics, computer science, machine learning, statistics, data science, or a related discipline by the appointment start date. We seek researchers with a strong record in machine learning, optimization, or a closely related area; the ability to conduct rigorous computational experiments; and strong communication and collaboration skills. Relevant experience in one or more of the following areas is particularly welcome: \u2022 Reinforcement learning, language-model post-training, planning, or search. \u2022 Learning theory, optimization, generalization, or learning from limited or imperfect supervision. \u2022 Synthetic-data generation or selection, efficient adaptation, or reproducible GPU experimentation. \u2022 AI for mathematics, automated theorem proving, verification, reasoning evaluation, or AI for scientific discovery. Applicants with either a theoretical or an empirical research emphasis are encouraged to apply. Experience across all of these areas is not required. Please submit a single PDF through the Interfolio application link containing: \u2022 A curriculum vitae, including a publication list. \u2022 A research statement outlining previous work, future directions, and connections to this position. Please indicate your preferred start date in 2027-2028 academic year. If needed, the start date can be modified to earlier 2027. \u2022 Up to three representative publications or preprints. \u2022 Contact details for three referees. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181404": {"title": "Part-time Instructor - Theme Park and Experiential Entertainment Marketing", "description": "Department: New York University: Global Programs: NYU Los Angeles Location: NYU Los Angeles Program NYU's Los Angeles Program is seeking a local, part-time instructor to teach an undergraduate course focused on the global business of immersive experiences. The course will be under the sponsorship of NYU Stern School of Business. This is a shortened intensive course that is expected to be taught over the span of three weeks in the Summer 2026 semester (May - July) and will be held in the NYU LA academic center in the Fairfax/La Brea area of Los Angeles. Course Description This course will take students on a thrilling ride into the global business of fun and immersive experiences. This course will provide an introduction to experiential and location-based entertainment businesses, including: - global theme parks, - location-based, travel and leisure entertainment, - immersive experiences, including concerts, festivals, and live events. This course provides an overview and framework for understanding the key marketing, economic, and strategic issues in these businesses. We will explore how experiential entertainment is created, produced, and consumed by consumers. We will also examine the unique aspects of experiential and location-based entertainment, opportunities, issues, and how these businesses are evolving. We will look at the business models and where these businesses may be heading, including the key players, current trends and new opportunities. We will look at how the trends influence both the business models and the consumer experience About NYU and NYU Los Angeles: Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 11 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU Los Angeles is one of NYU\u2019s 12 study away programs where students can participate to study away while earning credits towards their degree. If you would like to learn more about the program, please visit the NYU Los Angeles program website . A strong candidate will have a Ph.D., DBA, MBA, or equivalent terminal training in a relevant field, as well as experience teaching courses at the undergraduate level within a degree-granting program. They must demonstrate a mastery of the experience economy, specifically regarding guest journey mapping and brand storytelling within physical or immersive environments. Extensive industry experience in theme parks, themed attractions, location-based entertainment, or experiential brand activations would be a plus factor for qualified candidates. All applications must be made online through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/181404 . Applications must include (1) a cover letter, (2) an updated CV, (3) list of references, teaching materials/list of topics (500 word max, including some of the reading and other materials to be covered), and (4) course evaluation summaries (if available). In the cover letter, please highlight any relevant experience you have in teaching and describe your teaching philosophy. Since this position will require the instructor to critically evaluate the established syllabus and bring their own unique perspective to the course, candidates are strongly encouraged to arrive at the interview prepared to discuss potential syllabus ideas. Candidates are also welcome to submit sample draft syllabi during the application process. Consideration for applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed jointly by senior NYU LA leadership and colleagues in Stern School of Business. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189984": {"title": "Part-time Instructor: Interactive Media Arts", "description": "Department: New York University: Global Programs: NYU Los Angeles Location: NYU Los Angeles NYU is seeking a local, part-time lecturer to teach an undergraduate course, Cyborgs to Siri: Gender, Technology, and Media under the sponsorship of NYU Shanghai\u2019s department of Interactive Media Arts (IMA). IMA combines practice-based work with critical scholarship in the exploration of the social, cultural and philosophical dimensions\u00a0 of emerging technologies. The course is to be taught in-person for the upcoming Spring 2027 academic semester (January - May). The instructor role includes all preparation, delivery, and grading. Some administrative assistance is available to support the preparation of course materials. Courses are to be intellectually challenging in content, and rigorous student assessment is required. Course Description: In this course, we will examine the humanoid robots, cyborgs, and AIs imagined in film, television, and advertising, as well as the dialogue between these fictional creations and their real-life counterparts, from historical automata to the current development of robotic and virtual assistants and companions. How does techno-fantasy inform material reality, and vice versa, in the construction of these figures? What roles do we ask them to play in both realms? What social, cultural and historical conditions give rise to, and shape, their appearance? What cultural hopes and fears do they reveal? In answering these questions, we will examine issues around gender, sexuality, and race, while drawing from a wide range of critical discourses, including technoculture studies, cyborg feminism, and posthumanism. About NYU: Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private university in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 15 global academic centers and research programs in more than 25 countries. NYU programs combine highly focused academic programming with substantial interdisciplinary and global perspectives. Education : Applicants should hold a Ph.D., M.F.A., or other terminal degree in Interactive Media, Media Studies, Gender and Sexuality Studies, Science and Technology Studies (STS), Film and Television Studies, or a closely related field from an accredited institution. Experience : The ideal candidate will have prior experience teaching undergraduate courses at the intersection of media, design, technology, and society within a degree-granting program. Expertise : Demonstrated academic engagement with critical theory, visual culture, digital media, or feminist science and technology studies is strongly preferred. All applications must be made online through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/189984. Applications must include: A cover letter An updated CV A Portfolio A list of references Teaching materials such as a sample syllabus and Course Evaluations if available In the cover letter, please highlight any relevant experience you have in teaching interdisciplinary media/technology courses and describe your teaching philosophy. Consideration for applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed jointly by senior NYU leadership and departmental colleagues in the Office of Global Programs. Terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. Please direct any questions to nyula.academics@nyu.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/167100": {"title": "Research Assistant", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work: Office of Research Location: New York, NY (Hybrid) Job Opportunity/Position Title: Research Assistant Description of Location: Hybrid Commitment: May - August Salary Range/Hourly Pay: $23.00 per hour, 10-15 hours per week Project Title: NYC Communities for Health (NYCC4H) Project Description: NYC Communities for Health (NYCC4H) is a prevention-based and SAMHSA-funded program housed at NYU Silver School of Social Work. Our mission is to prevent STIs, underage drinking, and substance use through community prevention education, harm reduction resources, and referrals to social and clinical services to ensure safer cities and healthier futures for Bronx youth ages 13-24. By addressing historical health injustices for marginalized groups (e.g., Black and Latinx communities), we aim to minimize health disparities and empower community members to enact change in their daily lives. Related to these goals, NYCC4H also provides overdose prevention and response training with fentanyl test strips (FTS) and naloxone (NARCAN\u00ae) to address substance use disparities in communities in need of such services. Duties and Responsibilities: The Research Assistant will specialize primarily in Community Outreach and Engagement: Outreach: Conducting outreach with community organizations in NYC to create and carry out a community engagement plan. Data Collection: Facilitating surveys to gather community insights. Service Delivery: Conduct engaging and informative training sessions on opioid overdose prevention and response using fentanyl test strips (FTS) and naloxone (NARCAN\u00ae). Training will be in English/Spanish, conducted in person at events throughout NYC and remotely via Zoom. Resource Identification: Identifying resources to help participants overcome care barriers and implementing referrals. Navigation Support: Assisting participants with accessing and staying in care for substance use disorders and STI/HIV treatment, including offering accompaniment when needed. Supply Preparation: Manage supplies for outreach and community events, as well as assist with inventory management. Required Education, Experience, and Skills: Bachelor's Degree Familiarity with the Bronx and NYC. Flexible schedule\u2013some evening and weekend work required. Trustworthiness and empathy, particularly when working with people at risk for substance use disorders and HIV. Cultural competence in working with Hispanic/Latino and Black/African American communities is required. Preferred Education, Experience, and Skills: Bilingual (English and Spanish) preferred. Please submit a Cover Letter, Resume/CV, and 3 professional references contact information."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/167814": {"title": "Lab Coordinator", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work: Office of Research Location: New York, NY (Hybrid) NYU SILVER SCHOOL OF SOCIAL WORK JOB OPPORTUNITY: Lab Coordinator Location: New York PART-TIME: 8 hours/week Hybrid in New York Salary: $20/hour (45 weeks) Study Title: Youth Mental Health Treatment Cascade Principal Investigator: Michelle Munson Job Description: Support all of the projects of the Youth and Young Adult Mental Health Group at New York University. Duties and Responsibilities Collaborate on designing project materials Do literature reviews Assist with grant development and writing Attend team meetings Provide administrative support to Drs. Munson and Narendorf Performance Evaluation Procedures The staff member will meet with the Co-Directors and other team members, as needed, on a regular basis to review completed tasks and plan next activities. During these contacts the staff member will also discuss professional development goals. Required Education, Experience, and Skills MSW Required Some experience with community-based programs Strong relational skills High level of attention to detail, flexibility, ability to work on a team"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/169180": {"title": "Research Assistant", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work: Office of Research Location: New York, NY (Remote) Position Description: The research assistant will meet with the principal investigator on a weekly basis to discuss completed tasks and plan activities for the following week. Duties and responsibilities include coding qualitative data from study project, performing literature review searchers, write and draft manuscript for publication, as well as other related activities as required. Job Opportunity/Position Title: Research Assistant / Cultural Broker Research Project Description of Location: Remote Commitment: Temporary \u2013 Part-Time (10 hours/week) Start date: June 30, 2025 End date: August 15, 2025 Salary Range/Hourly Pay: $29/hour Project Title: Cultural Brokers: engaging refugees in mental health services and treatment Project Description: The purpose of the study is to explore the role of Cultural Brokers in engaging refugees in mental health services and treatment. A Cultural Broker is a person who is familiar with both mainstream U.S. culture and refugee culture and uses this shared understanding to help refugees reduce cultural and structural barriers and facilitate mental health treatment. Duties and Responsibilities: Perform literature review searches Code qualitative data from study project Write and draft manuscripts for publication Perform other related activities as required Required Education, Experience, and Skills: Undergraduate degree in social work, public health, psychology, or a related field Excellent organizational skills Knowledge of Microsoft Office, particularly Word and Excel Ability to learn new software such as Nvivo Experience with conducting literature reviews Experience with qualitative analysis Must have a high level of attention to detail, flexibility, ability to work on a team, and punctuality Preferred Education, Experience, and Skills: Interest in mental health research with diverse immigrant and refugee communities Graduate degree in social work, public health, psychology, or a related field When applying, please include a Cover Letter and your Resume/CV."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170009": {"title": "Training Coordinator", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work: Office of Research Location: Partially Remote and Partially in New York, NY Job Description The Training Coordinator will mainly be responsible for managing federally funded training programs and events/conferences as well as providing technical support for grant submissions and progress reports. This includes scheduling meetings with PI and program collaborators and taking meeting minutes, making travel and other administrative arrangements research training fellows in the program, maintaining the training websites, and assisting with assembling administrative components of grant applications. The coordinator will also support recruitment of trainees, intake process, and providing ongoing communication and support during their appointment period. The position requires the ability to function with a high level of organizational skill to manage schedules, projects, and events. Duties and Responsibilities Manage Training Programs and Participants : \u00b7 Work with the Program Directors and collaborators to fulfill the goals and objectives of the training programs \u00b7 Provide support for recruitment of new trainees and manage the application process, including serving as the point of contact for program applicants \u00b7 Manage trainee travel arrangements \u00b7 Manage the trainee database in REDCap \u00b7 Coordinate communication and facilitate updates to trainees and alumni \u00b7 Support grant reporting process, including intake of trainee reports, and aggregate training program outputs \u00b7 Work on marketing materials and maintain training program websites as needed \u00b7 Generate documentation for program activities, including the preparation of manuals, education materials, committee notes, reference materials, admission letters and trainee handbooks. \u00b7 Contribute to program reports, newsletter content, and other products and tasks as needed Required Education, Experience, and Skills \u25cf Bachelor\u2019s required, Master\u2019s preferred in social work, public health, psychology, or other social/behavioral science discipline \u25cf Excellent communicator \u2013 demonstrated interpersonal, written, and oral communication skills to effectively communicate, collaborate, establish and maintain good working relationships with a diverse group of multi-disciplinary researchers, staff, and partners \u25cf Ability to exercise sound judgment in establishing priorities \u25cf Excellent organization skills with ability to balance multiple competing deadlines \u25cf Excellent computer skills including knowledge of Microsoft Office and the ability to learn new software applications such as REDCap and Qualtrics Must have a high level of attention to detail, flexibility, adaptability, punctuality, and ability to work on a team In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is 75K - 80K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170249": {"title": "Research Manager", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work: Office of Research Location: New York, NY NYU SILVER SCHOOL OF SOCIAL WORK JOB OPPORTUNITY: RESEARCH MANAGER Location: 1 Washington Square North, NY, NY 10003 PART TIME (5 days/week -30 hours) position through August 31st Date of posting: July 10, 2025 VIRTUAL Salary: $30/hour Study Title: Ebony in the Ivory Principal Investigator: Dr. Kirk \u2018Jae\u2019 James Duties and Responsibilities Organize and maintain recruitment spreadsheets and documents Monitor project emails Track participants Project Planning Strategic development Team Supervision IRB Duties Perform other related activities as required Required Education, Experience, and Skills Current or past student in social work, psychology, public health, or other social/behavioral science Experience working on a research study. Experience recruiting participants for research. Experience with qualitative and/or quantitative research methods. Interest in research with POC populations Strong project management skills (related to research preferred) Excellent organizational skills Leadership Skills High level of attention to detail, flexibility, ability to work on a team, and punctuality Commitment to social justice and racial and socioeconomic equity"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170378": {"title": "Research Assistant", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work: Office of Research Location: New York, NY (Hybrid) NYU Silver School of Social Work JOB OPPORTUNITY: Research Assistant Location: 1 Washington Square North, NY, NY 10003 PART TIME: 3-8 hours/week Expected Starting Date: ASAP Contact : si880@nyu.edu Compensation: $30/hour In-person and virtual Project title : Deconstructing School Pushout (DSP) (Principal Investigator: Sireen Irsheid, PhD, LCSW, MSW) Project Overview: DSP project aims to address knowledge gaps in the understanding of the nonlinear pathways and factors that act as facilitators to the school pushout phenomenon (e.g., suspensions/expulsions and engagement with the legal system). This study is built on a growing body of social justice-oriented research concerned with the systemic influences that shape the experiences for young people. Specifically, this study aims to qualitatively examine how young people who have been suspended, expelled, or interacted with the legal system understand, make meaning, and navigate the structural factors related to neighborhood and school disinvestment and access to resources and supports, and their risk for school pushout. Furthermore, schools as political, cultural, and ideologically reproductive spaces often serve as sites of resistance and can support Black and Brown students who are impacted by educational inequity. This study also aims to explore the perceptions of school staff which will aid in the understanding of organizational practices and the day-to-day interactions between school staff and students designated as \u201chigh risk\u201d for school pushout. Please see DSP for full description of study. Duties and Responsibilities \u25cf Assist in refining interview protocols, consent forms, and other materials as needed and input them on Qualtrics for electronic versions. \u25cf Assist PI in submitting IRB applications and modifications, as needed. \u25cf Assist in participant recruitment and enrollment efforts and track participant incentives for study participation. \u25cf Conduct interviews with participants. \u25cf Assist in monitoring progression of key research activities, such as recruitment, screening, enrollment, and follow-up of study participants. \u25cf Assist in maintaining a data management system and/or data collection database . \u25cf Carry out assessments of participants in Qualtrics or REDCap. \u25cf Assist in data collection activities including in-depth semi-structured interviews. \u25cf Assist in data coding and analysis using MAXDQ, SPSS, and STATA. Required Education, Experience and Skills \u25cf Background in social work, psychology, sociology, anthropology, or other social/behavioral science. \u25cf Prior experience or training in qualitative and quantitative research methods. \u25cf Interest in school pushout, school-prison nexus, racial equity, and/or structural racism and structural violence. \u25cf Prior experience or training facilitating interviews or focus groups with young people. \u25cf Strong organizational skills with attention to detail and accuracy. \u25cf Proficiency using or willingness to obtain working knowledge of computer software programs including REDCap, MS Office, Adobe, Qualtrics, and data management platforms. \u25cf Proficiency using STATA, SPSS, R and other statistical softwares. \u25cf Demonstrate deep commitment to racial justice, social justice, and social equity. \u25cf Ability to communicate in a clear, efficient manner as a member of a collaborative team. \u25cf Ability to work autonomously, organize daily tasks and meet required deadlines on multiple concurrent tasks. Preferred Education, Experience and Skills \u25cf Prior experience with research and/or academic research setting. \u25cf Prior experience in data ana lysis techniques and related data management platforms, i.e., MAXQDA, STATA, SPSS, descriptive statistics, and thematic analysis. \u25cf Prior experience supporting planning, designing, and implementing projects. \u25cf Training and experience in motivational interviewing theory and techniques. \u25cf Knowledge of IRB protocols and procedures to modify protocols. Performance Evaluation Procedure \u25cf The RA will meet with the PI on a weekly basis to discuss completed tasks and plan activities for the following week. During these meetings the RA will also discuss professional development goals."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/164473": {"title": "Research Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  FULL TIME RESEARCH ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering Biomechatronics and Intelligent Robotics Lab led by Prof. Hao Su (http://haosu-robotics.github.io) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Associate to work on mechatronics design, control, and reinforcement learning for wearable robots, surgical robots, image-guided intervention, and humanoid robots. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Department of Biomedical Engineering department at NYU Tandon focuses on connecting engineering and technology with medicine. Therefore, many groups have a substantial translational component in their research. Strong connections with world-class faculty at the NYU Grossman School of Medicine and the College of Dentistry offer unparalleled opportunities for research across boundaries through interdisciplinary collaborations. A detailed description of the laboratories and centers in BME can be found at https://engineering.nyu .edu/academics/departments/biomedical-engineering The Biomechatronics and Intelligent Robotics Lab conducts translational research, publishes high-caliber papers, and offers entrepreneurship opportunities. Our work was published in IEEE Transactions on Robotics, Nature, Nature Machine Intelligence, Science Robotics. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for mechatronics design of robots, or control and reinforcement learning for robotics. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background (as exemplified with a publication record) in one of the following areas, soft robots, legged robots, mechanism design and machine design, actuator design, electric motors, motor controller, embedded system, electronics, firmware, biomechanics, IMU sensors, Nvidia Isaac Gym, Simulator. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $48,000 - 65,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is immediately. This position is expected to be for at least a 6 moths appointment. Candidates should have a Bachelors or Masters Degree in Mechanical, Electrical, Biomedical Engineering, or Computer Science or a related field. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: hao.su@nyu.edu and [use an NYU email Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled. Expected start date is March 15, 2025. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: August 2024"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168245": {"title": "Researcher (Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Part time Research Scholar Biomedical Engineering New York University Faculty in the Biomedical Engineering department ( https://engineering.nyu.edu/ ) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a part-time Research Scholar to participate in exciting projects in signal processing, estimation, control and\u00a0optimization for biomedical engineering applications. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, is a fast-growing Engineering school with a focus on nanotechnology, biotechnology, urban engineering, and wireless technologies. NYU Tandon Biomedical Engineering department leads multidisciplinary centers in medical imaging and data analysis, tissue engineering and regenerative medicine, synthetic and systems bioengineering, robotic and rehabilitation engineering, nano- and micro-bioengineering, neuro-engineering, and cell and immuno-bioengineering. NYU Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Biomedical Engineering department is at the center of a high-tech start-up culture where student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship activities are supported and nurtured in Brooklyn, New York City, and across the NYU Global Network. We are seeking candidates with a B.S. or M.S. degree(s) in electrical, biomedical, mechanical, computer, or aerospace engineering, mathematics, computer science, or related fields. The successful candidate will: Be driven, creative and team-oriented. Have demonstrated writing and programming skills in MATLAB, Python, or R. Have training in statistical signal processing, machine learning Be capabile to apply various mathematical algorithms for biomedical engineering applications Be able to conduct human subject research including physiological health data collection and subsequent data analysis. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $26.37 per hour\u00a0 for up 20 - 30 hours per week. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific funding source and the terms Applicants should submit a cover letter, a current CV and a list of three references with complete contact information. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168246": {"title": "RESEARCH SCHOLAR", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, Ny  Research Scholar (Full-time) Biomedical Engineering New York University Faculty in the Biomedical Engineering department ( https://engineering.nyu.edu/ ) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a full time Research Scholar to join in exciting projects in signal processing, estimation, control and\u00a0optimization for biomedical engineering applications. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, is a fast-growing Engineering school with a focus on nanotechnology, biotechnology, urban engineering, and wireless technologies. NYU Tandon Biomedical Engineering department leads multidisciplinary centers in medical imaging and data analysis, tissue engineering and regenerative medicine, synthetic and systems bioengineering, robotic and rehabilitation engineering, nano- and micro-bioengineering, neuro-engineering, and cell and immuno-bioengineering. NYU Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Biomedical Engineering department is at the center of a high-tech start-up culture where student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship activities are supported and nurtured both in New York City, Brooklyn and across the NYU Global Network University. SALARY: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $48,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific funding source and the terms We are looking for candidate with a B.S. and/or M.S. degree in electrical, biomedical, mechanical, computer, or aerospace engineering or mathematics or computer science. The successful candidate will be driven, creative and team-oriented with excellent writing and programming skills in MATLAB, Python, or R and training in statistical signal processing, machine learning, and capability in applying various mathematical algorithms for biomedical engineering applications, and prepared for conducting human subject research including physiological health data collection and subsequent data analysis. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a current CV and a list of three references with complete contact information. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170952": {"title": "RESEARCH SCHOLAR", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, Ny  Part-Time Research Scholar Biomedical Engineering New York University Biomechatronics and Intelligent Robotics Lab led by Prof. Hao Su (http://haosu-robotics.github.io) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Scholar (Non-PhD) to work on mechatronics design, control, and reinforcement learning for wearable robots, surgical robots, and humanoid robots. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. NYU Tandon fosters innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Biomedical Engineering department is at the center of a high-tech start-up culture where student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship activities are supported and nurtured both in New York City, Brooklyn and across the NYU Global Network University. NYU Tandon Biomedical Engineering department leads multidisciplinary centers in medical imaging and data analysis, tissue engineering and regenerative medicine, synthetic and systems bioengineering, robotic and rehabilitation engineering, nano- and micro-bioengineering, neuro-engineering, and cell and immuno-bioengineering. Biomechatronics and Intelligent Robotics Lab focuses on use-inspired fundamental research, publishes high-caliber papers in Nature, Science Robotics, Nature Machine Intelligence, IEEE Transactions on Robotics, IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics, etc., and offers entrepreneurship opportunities. E xpectations: Candidates will be responsible for working with lab\u2019s PhD students or postdoc on mechatronics design, reinforcement learning-based robot controller development, conducting experiments and data processing. The candidates will be expected to: Conduct mechatronic system design, integration, and testing Develop and evaluate models in machine learning and reinforcement learning Publish papers in top robotics and AI conferences and journals Collect and process data from human experiments and sensor measurements Conduct human-subject experiments in medical robot applications The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in one or more of the following areas, soft robots, legged robots, mechanism design and machine design, actuator design, electric motors, motor controller, embedded system, electronics, firmware, biomechanics, IMU sensors, Nvidia Isaac Gym, Simulator, as exemplified by a strong publication record. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is immediately. This position is expected to be for 6 months. Salary In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the hourly rate for this position is $27.00 per hour for up 20 hours per week. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific funding source and the terms. Tandon faculty and students are at the forefront of the high-tech start-up culture in New York City and have access to NYU\u2019s Global Network University. The NYU Tandon School of Engineering is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. We are looking for candidate with a B.S. and/or M.S. degree in electrical, biomedical, mechanical, computer, or aerospace engineering or mathematics or computer science. The successful candidate will be driven, creative and team-oriented with excellent writing and programming skills in MATLAB, Python, or R and training in statistical signal processing, machine learning, and capability in applying various mathematical algorithms for biomedical engineering applications, and prepared for conducting human subject research including physiological health data collection and subsequent data analysis. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a current CV and a list of three references with complete contact information. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: hao.su@nyu.edu and su.lab.robotics@gmail.com . Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/172003": {"title": "Post Doctorate Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering Biomechatronics and Intelligent Robotics Lab led by Prof. Hao Su (http://haosu-robotics.github.io) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering\u2019s Biomedical Engineering (BME) Department is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work on mechatronics design, control, and reinforcement learning for wearable robots, surgical robots, image-guided intervention, and humanoid robots. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Department of Biomedical Engineering department at NYU Tandon focuses on connecting engineering and technology with medicine. Therefore, many groups have a substantial translational component in their research. Strong connections with world-class faculty at the NYU Grossman School of Medicine and the College of Dentistry offer unparalleled opportunities for research across boundaries through interdisciplinary collaborations. A detailed description of the laboratories and centers in BME can be found at https://engineering.nyu.edu/academics/departments/biomedical-engineering The Biomechanics and Intelligent Robotics Lab conducts translational research, publishes high-caliber papers, and offers entrepreneurship opportunities. Our work has been published in IEEE Transactions on Robotics, Nature, Nature Machine Intelligence, Science Robotics. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for mechatronics design of robots, or control and reinforcement learning for robotics. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background (as exemplified with a strong publication record) in one of the following areas, soft robots, legged robots, mechanism design and machine design, actuator design, electric motors, motor controller, embedded system, electronics, firmware, biomechanics, IMU sensors, Nvidia Isaac Gym, Simulator. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is no less than $62,500 and commensurate with experience and expertise depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Mechanical, Electrical, Biomedical Engineering, or Computer Science or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, research statement, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: hao.su@nyu.edu. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/172017": {"title": "Post Doctorate Researcher", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The de L\u00e1zaro Lab (NanoBioEngineering for Tissue Reprogramming and Regeneration, https://www.delazarolab.com ) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work in with Dr. Lazaro. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Our laboratory is a diverse mix of biomedical and mechanical engineers, biologists, and pharmacists. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for working with a unique transgenic mouse model that enables specific cardiomyocyte reprogramming (details here ) and will utilize molecular biology (including multi-omics), histological and functional (including echocardiography) techniques to investigate cell reprogramming and its effects on regeneration. Candidates will also work closely with rodent surgeons at the CVRC to establish different cardiac injury models and will be expected to contribute to other cell reprogramming projects in the laboratory. Previous experience in a wide variety of molecular biology and histological techniques is a must, prior experience working with mice is advantageous but not strictly required. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is between $62,500.00 and $70,000.00 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Tandon faculty and students are at the forefront of the high-tech start-up culture in New York City and have access to\u00a0NYU\u2019s Global Network University. The NYU Tandon School of Engineering is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Tech4Health Institute is NYU Langone\u2019s research and development hub for new biomedical instruments and technologies. The Tech4Health Institute connects engineers and physical scientists with clinicians and life scientists across NYU to address key unsolved problems and unmet needs in biomedicine. Candidates should have a PhD in Biology, Stem Cell Biology, Regenerative Medicine, Biomedical Engineering, or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to : irene.delazaro@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is between September and November 2025."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/171921": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor for BE-GY 6473 Applied Math and Statistics Course, Department of Biomedical Engineering, New York University", "description": "Posted: Aug 11, 2025 Location: Brooklyn, NY The Department of Biomedical Engineering at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for part-time, non-tenured instructors of BE-GY 6473 Applied Math and Statistics for Biomedical Engineering course in Fall 2025 semester, with a start date of September 2, 2025 , and an end date of December 21, 2025 . The Department of Biomedical Engineering at the NYU Tandon manages the graduate level course BE-GY 6473 Applied Math and Statistics for Biomedical Engineering, which provides a foundation for understanding and applying statistical methods to analyze biological and medical data. Topics covered include data visualization, descriptive statistics, random behavior modeling, normal distribution, statistical inference, analysis of variance, regression analysis, correlation analysis, non-parametric statistics, and survival analysis. The course includes hands-on computer lab sections for practical application of these techniques to real biological and medical datasets. By the end of this course, students should be able to understand and apply various statistical methods for data summarization and visualization in biological and medical contexts, choose appropriate statistical techniques for estimation, hypothesis testing, regression, and analysis of variance, select the correct statistical method based on data type and study design, and utilize statistical software tools to analyze, describe, and display data effectively. A typical teaching load is 37.5 hours for the whole semester, which includes 14 lectures + computer labs (2.5 hours for each week\u2019s lecture and lab session. More exactly, the lecture and lab date / time is every Tuesday, 6 pm \u2013 8:30 pm), and 1 final exam section (2.5 hours). We presently seek an adjunct instructor who is eager to provide a solid foundation for our BME graduate students upon understanding and applying statistical methods to analyze biological and medical data in this BE-GY 6473 Applied Math and Statistics for Biomedical Engineering course . The adjunct instructor\u2019s primary responsibility will be teaching. The responsibility is fulfilled by: Delivering the lecture and supervising the lab session every week Evaluating individual assignments and group-based lab reports, and send back feedbacks to students in time (typically within a week period) Grading quizzes and exam(s) in time (typically within a week period) Constructing a course project and arranging the key steps well for students to follow up and complete the project Answering students\u2019 questions relevant to course content, assignments, labs, and course project in time We invite applicants with expertise in relevant engineering or science fields. Applicants should ideally fulfill the following qualified requirements: Ph.D. in engineering, STEM, or related fields A documented record of university teaching in graduate level engineering statistics courses. Prior experience in course instruction, student supervision or tutoring on graduate level engineering statistics courses with excellent student evaluation ratings. Proficiency in engineering statistical key concepts, principles, and methods, such as data visualization, descriptive statistics, random behavior modeling, normal distribution, statistical inference, analysis of variance, regression analysis, correlation analysis, non-parametric statistics, and survival analysis Profound knowledge and experience in the application of engineering statistics towards biomedical engineering fields. Please submit the following materials electronically through the \"Apply Now\" button : Cover letter Contact References Current CV (emphasize on teaching experience) Teaching statement describing experience and teaching philosophy Sample teaching portfolio for the engineering statistics or relevant topic courses (e.g. syllabus, sample homework set, computational lab instruction, sample quizzes/exams, and course project guidance, etc.) Recent teaching evaluations A statement of your experience with or knowledge of inclusion, diversity, equity, and belonging\u00a0 efforts and your plans for incorporating them into your teaching and mentoring process Names and contact information for at least two references We will review applications starting Aug 10, 2025 and will continue until we fill the position; we encourage you to submit early. Should you have any questions please contact the program directors, Dr. Xiao Ma ( xm8@nyu.edu ) and Dr. Andreas Hielscher ( ahh4614@nyu.edu ). About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. NYU Tandon is committed to substantially increase the proportion of our faculty from historically underrepresented groups in STEM and we encourage candidates from such groups to apply. We aspire to create a climate where diversity and inclusion are not only appreciated but considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for a culturally diverse environment. We take pride in our high numbers of female students and students who are the first in their family to go to college. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $222.27. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/172424": {"title": "Post Doctorate Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Truong Lab for \"Programmable Regenerative Immunity\" ( truonglab.com ) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to lead experimental & intellectual efforts in Synthetic Immuno-Engineering and Regenerative Immunology. This is an opportunity to define the next generation of human cell engineering and cell-based therapeutics. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Truong lab, located in midtown Manhattan, is part of the rapidly growing Department of Biomedical Engineering and the Health Engineering initiative. The lab integrates synthetic biology, genome engineering, and stem cell differentiation to design programmable, interoperable human cells. We have developed a genome engineering platform (REWRITE) that enables scarless, large-scale rewriting (10\u2013100+ kb) of native loci in human iPSCs. These systems are being used to construct synthetic immune consortia from the ground up. Why Join Us? (1) The lab is interdisciplinary, high-momentum, and deeply collaborative, with strong translational & entrepreneurial ambitions. (2) Access state-of-the-art infrastructure for genome engineering, stem cell culture, and single-cell profiling. (3) Obtain opportunities for fellowship support, project independence, and technology translation (including spinout support). (4) Collaborate across NYU Tandon School of Engineering, Grossman School of Medicine, and Arts & Sciences. The primary project for this position is SynThyTune, our synthetic thymus platform for modeling central tolerance and T-cell education. This work supports applications in autoimmunity, transplantation, and cancer immunotherapy. A secondary opportunity is available for candidates with strong interest and expertise in macrophage biology and regenerative neuroimmunology, contributing to our REPHRESH program in Alzheimer\u2019s disease. Responsibilities Candidate will be responsible for: (1) Designing and assembling synthetic DNA 10\u2013100 kb in size. (2) Genetic engineering and manipulation of human iPSCs. (3) Direct reprogramming & differentiation of human iPSCs into thymic epithelial cells, T-cell precursor, and myeloid lineages. (4) in vitro modeling of TCR selection and immune education. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $65,000\u2013$70,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualifications Candidates should have a PhD in Biomedical Engineering, Bioengineering, Cell & Molecular Biology, Immunology or a related field. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated expertise in at least two of the following: (1) Genome Engineering & Synthetic Biology, (2) Stem Cells & Differentiation, (3) Immunology or Neuroscience, (4) Transcriptomics or Systems Biology, as exemplified by a strong publication record and scientific communication skills. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, names and contact information for 2\u20133 references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is open to discussion.question"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175595": {"title": "Post Doctorate Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Maimon lab (https://www.maimonlab.com) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering department of Biomedical Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work in the area of neural stem cell biology, brain regeneration, neurodegeneration (specifically Huntington\u2019s Disease) and neuro-engineering. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The mission of the department of Biomedical Engineering and particularly the Maimon lab (located in Manhattan) is to pioneer regenerative strategies for neurodegenerative diseases by unlocking the potential of in vivo cellular identity conversion. Through bold experimentation and interdisciplinary thinking, the Maimon lab explores how dormant neural stem cells can be reawakened and guided to form new glial and neurons, with the ultimate goal of inducing brain regeneration in aging and disease. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for leading and executing in vivo and ex vivo studies focused on gene editing\u2013based approaches to treat neurodegenerative diseases, particularly Huntington\u2019s Disease (HD), using mouse models. Responsibilities include designing and conducting experiments, performing behavioral and molecular phenotyping, and optimizing delivery and safety of CRISPR/Cas9-based tools. In addition, candidates will contribute to the analysis of single-cell and spatial transcriptomics datasets, including MERFISH and spatial proteomics. Familiarity with computational pipelines for single-cell data integration and interpretation is highly valued. Expertise in advanced microscopy (e.g., confocal, spinning disk, live imaging) and imaging-based analysis of neural tissues is strongly preferred. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in CRISPR, as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience with in vivo genome editing, AAV delivery systems, Antisense Oligonucleotides (ASOs), live cell imaging, neuronal co-cultures, neurodegeneration models, spatial omics, and computational biology is desired. Salary: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $62,500-$70,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in neuro cell biology, tissue engineering or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a cover letter that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three (or more) references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: roy.maimon@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: March/April 2026"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/178472": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor for Bioinformatics - BI-GY-7733", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn The Department of Biomedical Engineering at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for part-time, non-tenured instructors, with a start date of January 20, 2026 . The department of Biomedical Engineering offers MS degrees in Biomedical Engineering and Bioinformatics , and PhD degrees in Biomedical Engineering . We invite applicants to teach one graduate course. In particular, we presently seek instructors for BI-GY 7733: Translational Genomics and Computational Biology. You should be an excellent teacher. You should have a Ph.D. in Bioinformatics, Computational Biology, Biostatistics, Genomics, or a closely related discipline and a record of teaching experience in these areas. Please submit the following materials electronically through the \"Apply Now\" button. Cover letter Current CV Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Teaching statement describing experience and teaching philosophy Names and contact information for at least two references (if appropriate) We will review applications starting as soon as possible and will continue until we fill the position. Should you have any questions please contact: Mgavi E. Brathwaite meb549@nyu.edu About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. We aspire to create a climate where inclusion is not only appreciated but also considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for, and who possess an interest in, working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $222.27. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180875": {"title": "RESEARCH SCHOLAR", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, Ny  FULL TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Maimon lab (https://www.maimonlab.com) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Scholar to work in neural stem cell biology, brain regeneration, neurodegeneration (specifically Huntington\u2019s Disease) and neuro-engineering. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The mission of the Maimon lab (located in Manhattan) is to pioneer regenerative strategies for neurodegenerative diseases by unlocking the potential of in vivo cellular identity conversion. Through experimentation and interdisciplinary thinking, the Maimon lab explores how dormant neural stem cells can be reawakened and guided to form new glial and neurons, with the ultimate goal of inducing brain regeneration in aging and disease. Expectations We are seeking a highly motivated researcher to join our lab\u2019s mission to advance regenerative neurobiology and therapeutic genome editing for neurodegenerative diseases. This is a unique opportunity to contribute to both high-impact research and the early development of a new lab at NYU. Key Responsibilities : Design and perform in vivo and ex vivo studies in mouse models of Huntington\u2019s Disease (HD), focused on gene editing using CRISPR/Cas9, AAV, and ASO-based approaches. Conduct behavioral and molecular phenotyping, and optimize delivery and safety of therapeutic tools. Lead or support single-cell and spatial transcriptomics experiments, including MERFISH, immunofluorescence, spatial proteomics, and live-cell imaging. Analyze and integrate single-cell datasets using established computational pipelines. Assist in lab setup and daily operations, including ordering supplies, preparing reagents and buffers, and maintaining lab documentation. Help establish protocols, track inventory, and maintain a well-organized and efficient research environment. Expected start date is: March 2026. This position is expected to be for 12 months. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $48,000-$50,000 for full time depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have at least a BS in Neuro Cell Biology or a related field and 1 year of experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: roy.maimon@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181319": {"title": "RESEARCH SCHOLAR", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY F ULL TIME RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Computational Medicine Laboratory at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Scholar to work on exciting projects in signal processing, estimation, control and\u00a0optimization for biomedical engineering applications, with Dr. Rose T. Faghih, Associate Professor, Department of Biomedical Engineering. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. NYU Tandon Biomedical Engineering department leads multidisciplinary research in medical imaging and data analysis, tissue engineering and regenerative medicine, synthetic and systems bioengineering, robotic and rehabilitation engineering, nano- and micro-bioengineering, neuro-engineering, and cell and immune bioengineering. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for developing statistical signal processing, machine learning, and various mathematical algorithms for biomedical engineering applications conducting human subject research including physiological health data collection data analysis The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in mathematical algorithms for biomedical engineering applications as exemplified by a strong publication record, and will be driven, creative and team-oriented with excellent writing and programming skills in MATLAB, Python, or R .\u00a0 Previous experience in human subjects research is a plus. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this full time position is expected to be $48,000 at 35 hours per week. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is February 2026 with a duration of 3 months, with possible additional extension up to 6 months. Candidates should have at least a BS in electrical, biomedical, mechanical, computer, or aerospace engineering or mathematics or related field . Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references with complete contact information. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: Computational_Medicine_Laboratory@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187725": {"title": "RESEARCH SCHOLAR", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, Ny  RESEARCH SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Ramadi Lab for Advanced Neuroengineering and Translational Medicine at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Scholar to work with Professor Ramadi. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. The Ramadi Lab develops minimally invasive, engineering-driven technologies to modulate neural activity across the body, combining electrical, mechanical, materials, and bioengineering approaches. The translational work focuses on creating novel diagnostics and therapies for neurologic, metabolic, and immune disorders. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for supporting and leading in vivo studies, including designing experiments, assisting with animal procedures, and overseeing data collection and analysis. This includes contributing to animal handling and experimental workflows, as well as assisting with Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC) protocol preparation, documentation, and compliance. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $48,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is : June 18th. This position is expected to be for 12 months . Candidates should have at least a BS in biomedical engineering, bioengineering, biology, neuroscience, physiology, or a related field and at least 1 year of experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in biomedical, bioengineering, neuroscience, physiology, or related life sciences research as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on animal handling and in vivo experimentation is desired. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. Other additional information needed (e.g., research statement, publications) All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: Khalil Ramadi <kbr5930@nyu.edu> Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189975": {"title": "Post Doctorate Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Gibbons Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work in computational microbiome science and precision medicine. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Gibbons Lab ,(beginning September, 2026)\u00a0 will be housed within the new Institute for Engineering Health (IEH), an interdisciplinary center that integrates engineering, medicine, and the biological sciences to advance healthcare discovery, prevention, and treatment. Prof. Sean Gibbons is an international leader in the development of computational and experimental tools for studying the ecology, evolution, and function of host-microbiome systems, with applications in precision nutrition and personalized medicine. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for the design, execution, and dissemination of research projects spanning computational modeling of gut microbial communities, multi-omic data integration, bioinformatic method development, and translational human studies. Specific activities may include: building and validating community-scale metabolic models of the human gut microbiome using tools such as MICOM; analyzing large-scale metagenomic, metabolomic, transcriptomic, and clinical datasets from human cohorts contributing to the design and analysis of dietary and drug/treatment intervention trials in collaboration with clinical partners at NYU Langone investigating within-host evolutionary dynamics of gut commensal bacteria and the health consequences of these dynamics exploring gut-brain axis mechanisms mediated by microbiome-derived metabolites; developing and maintaining open-source bioinformatic tools for the microbiome research community; preparing manuscripts for peer-reviewed publication and presenting findings at national and international conferences actively contributing to lab meetings, journal clubs, and the broader NYU scientific community. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $65,000\u2013$80,000, depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualifications. Candidates should have a PhD in computational biology, bioinformatics, microbiology, genomics, systems biology, bioengineering, evolutionary biology, ecology, biophysics, applied mathematics, or a related quantitative field. Strong programming skills in Python and/or R are required. Experience with the analysis of multi-omic datasets (e.g., metagenomics, metabolomics, transcriptomics) and familiarity with gut microbiome research are expected. Familiarity with constraint-based metabolic modeling (e.g., flux balance analysis) and/or machine learning is a plus. Strong written and oral communication skills, interdisciplinary curiosity, a collaborative spirit, and a demonstrated commitment to reproducible and open science are essential. Candidates for the gut-brain axis track should have a strong background in behavioral neuroscience, neuroendocrinology, or enteric nervous system biology. Experience with gut\u2013brain signaling pathways, including those mediated by microbial metabolites derived from the diet, such as short-chain fatty acids, branch-chain fatty acids, or protein fermentation byproducts, is a plus. Familiarity with non-human animal model systems (e.g., germ-free mouse studies, behavioral assays) or human behavioral studies (e.g., behavior assessed from wearables or questionnaires) is desired. An interest in bridging mechanistic findings with translational human cohort and clinical intervention data is strongly valued. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: sgibbons@isbscience.org Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is : September 1, 2026, with flexibility for the right candidate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189980": {"title": "Post Doctorate Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Gibbons Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work in computational microbiome science and precision medicine. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Gibbons Lab , will be housed within the Institute for Engineering Health (IEH), an interdisciplinary center that integrates engineering, medicine, and the biological sciences to advance healthcare discovery, prevention, and treatment. Dr. Gibbons is an international leader in the development of computational and experimental tools for studying the ecology, evolution, and function of host-microbiome systems, with applications in precision nutrition and personalized medicine. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for the design, execution, and dissemination of research projects spanning computational modeling of gut microbial communities, multi-omic data integration, bioinformatic method development, and translational human studies. Specific activities may include: building and validating community-scale metabolic models of the human gut microbiome using tools such as MICOM; analyzing large-scale metagenomic, metabolomic, transcriptomic, and clinical datasets from human cohorts; contributing to the design and analysis of dietary and drug/treatment intervention trials in collaboration with clinical partners at NYU Langone; investigating within-host evolutionary dynamics of gut commensal bacteria and the health consequences of these dynamics; exploring gut-brain axis mechanisms mediated by microbiome-derived metabolites; developing and maintaining open-source bioinformatic tools for the microbiome research community; preparing manuscripts for peer-reviewed publication and presenting findings at national and international conferences; actively contributing to lab meetings, journal clubs, and the broader NYU scientific community. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $65,000\u2013$80,000, depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualifications. Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD in computational biology, bioinformatics, microbiology, genomics, systems biology, bioengineering, evolutionary biology, ecology, biophysics, applied mathematics, or a related quantitative field. Strong programming skills in Python and/or R are required. Experience with the analysis of multi-omic datasets (e.g., metagenomics, metabolomics, transcriptomics) and familiarity with gut microbiome research are expected. Familiarity with constraint-based metabolic modeling (e.g., flux balance analysis) and/or machine learning is a plus. Strong written and oral communication skills, interdisciplinary curiosity, a collaborative spirit, and a demonstrated commitment to reproducible and open science are essential. Candidates for the within-host evolution track should have a strong background in population genomics, comparative genomics, or evolutionary biology. Experience with variant calling, metagenome-assembled genome (MAG) reconstruction, or strain-resolved metagenomic analyses is expected. Familiarity with evolutionary theory as applied to microbial populations\u2014including natural selection, genetic drift, and horizontal gene transfer\u2014is essential. Experience with single-cell approaches and longitudinal sampling designs is a plus. An interest in linking microbial evolutionary dynamics to host health phenotypes in human cohort data is strongly valued. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: sgibbons@isbscience.org Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is : September 1, 2026, with flexibility for the right candidate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191891": {"title": "Post Doctorate Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Post Doctoral Associate New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Maimon lab (https://www.maimonlab.com) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Postdoctoral Associate to work in the area of neural stem cell biology, brain regeneration, neurodegeneration (specifically Huntington\u2019s Disease) and neuro-engineering. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The department of Biomedical Engineering and particularly the Maimon lab mission is to pioneer regenerative strategies for neurodegenerative diseases by unlocking the potential of in vivo cellular identity conversion. Through bold experimentation and interdisciplinary thinking, the Maimon lab explores how dormant neural stem cells can be reawakened and guided to form new glial and neurons, with the ultimate goal of inducing brain regeneration in aging and disease. Expectations We are seeking a highly motivated Post Doctoral Associate to join our lab\u2019s efforts in advancing regenerative neurobiology and therapeutic genome editing for neurodegenerative diseases. This is a unique opportunity to contribute to both high-impact research and the early development of a new lab at NYU. Key Responsibilities: Design and perform in vivo and ex vivo studies in mouse models of Huntington\u2019s Disease (HD), focused on gene editing using CRISPR/Cas9, AAV, and ASO-based approaches. Conduct behavioral and molecular phenotyping, and optimize delivery and safety of therapeutic tools. Lead or support single-cell and spatial transcriptomics experiments, including MERFISH, immunofluorescence, spatial proteomics, and live-cell imaging. Analyze and integrate single-cell datasets using established computational pipelines. Help establish protocols, track inventory, and maintain a well-organized and efficient research environment. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $90,000-$100,000 for full time depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is: January 2027. This position is expected to be for 12 months. Candidates should have PhD in Neuro Cell Biology or a related field and 3 years of postdoc experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: roy.maimon@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194738": {"title": "RESEARCH SCHOLAR", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, Ny  Research Scholar New York University Tandon School of Engineering Biomedical Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a full-time Research Scholar to work with Irene de Lazaro. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Our laboratory \u2013 a diverse mix of biomedical engineers, biotechnologists, and pharmacists \u2013 is a core member of the Department of Biomedical Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering . We are also part of the Institute for Engineering Health , the NYC Center for Microplastics Solutions and the NanoBioX Initiative at NYU Tandon, and of the Cardiovascular Research Center at NYU Grossman School of Medicine and NYU Langone Health . Responsibilities Candidates will support a number of projects that involve experimentation with cardiac organoids. Specific responsibilities will include: \u25cf Maintenance of human induced pluripotent stem cell (hiPSC) cultures. \u25cf Generation, characterization, and maintenance of cardiac organoids following established protocols. \u25cf Development and validation of protocols to generate cardiac organoids modeling specific cardiac diseases. \u25cf Working closely with other researchers from the de Lazaro lab and collaborating research groups who utilize cardiac organoids in their projects. \u25cf Supporting the principal investigator in the generation of preliminary data for funding applications. \u25cf Maintaining excellent research records (electronic lab-book and appropriate data storage). \u25cf Training new lab members in established research protocols. \u25cf Regularly attending and presenting research advances at lab meetings. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $50,000.00 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is 10/16/26 Expected end date is 10/15/27 The full-time position would require a work schedule of approximately 35 hours per week . Candidates should have at least a Bachelor's in Biomedical Engineering, Biology, Biotechnology, or a related field. Prior experience in mammalian cell culture is a must, prior experience in organoid or 3D cell culture is advantageous but not strictly required. The successful candidate will be self-motivated and driven, creative, team-oriented and able to work efficiently in a multidisciplinary environment. They will be expected to collaborate very closely with basic scientists and clinical investigators from the Cardiovascular Research Center and to contribute to the mentoring of junior researchers. We welcome applications from recent graduates, individuals seeking additional training to prepare for graduate or medical school, and individuals with industry experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: irene.delazaro@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195099": {"title": "RESEARCH SCHOLAR", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY RESEARCH SCHOLAR de L\u00e1zaro Lab: NanoBioEngineering for Tissue Reprogramming and Regeneration New York University The de L\u00e1zaro Lab (NanoBioEngineering for Tissue Reprogramming and Regeneration, https://www.delazarolab.com ) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Research Scholar to participate in exciting research focused on the development and testing of lipid nanoparticles to deliver mRNA to different organs and tissues . Our laboratory \u2013 a diverse mix of biomedical engineers, biotechnologists and pharmacists \u2013 is a core member of the Department of Biomedical Engineering at NYU Tandon School of Engineering ( https://engineering.nyu.edu/academics/departments/biomedical-engineering ). We are also part of the Institute for Engineering Health ( https://engineering.nyu.edu/research/centers/institute-engineering-health ), and the NanoBioX Initiative ( https://nanobiox.nyu.edu/ ) at NYU Tandon, and of the Cardiovascular Research Center at NYU Grossman School of Medicine and NYU Langone Health ( https://med.nyu.edu/departments-institutes/medicine/divisions/cardiology/research/cardiovascular-research-center ). Responsibilities: Candidates will support a number of projects that involve the generation, in vitro and in vivo testing of lipid nanoparticles delivering mRNA (LNP-mRNAs) to different organs and tissues. Specifically, the successful candidate with work closely with a Postdoctoral Fellow, team lead of the Nanomedicine Sub-group at the de Lazaro lab, in the following tasks: Formulation and physicochemical characterization of lipid nanoparticles. Generation and quality control of mRNA stocks. In vitro testing of LNP-mRNAs, including viability assays and the evaluation of transfection efficiency and protein expression. Assistance on in vivo studies (in mice). Processing of tissue samples for molecular biology and histological assays. Other responsibilities will include: Supporting the principal investigator in the generation of preliminary data for funding applications. Maintaining excellent research records (electronic lab-book and appropriate data storage). Training new lab members in established research protocols. Regularly attending and presenting research advances at lab meetings. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $50,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualifications. We are looking for candidates with a BS or MS in Biomedical Engineering, Chemical Engineering, Pharmacy, Biology, Biotechnology, or a related field. Demonstrable prior laboratory experience is a must, prior experience in nanoparticle formulation, cell culture and/or mice handling is advantageous but not strictly required. The successful candidate will be self-motivated and driven, creative, team-oriented and able to work efficiently in a multidisciplinary environment. We welcome applications from\u00a0recent graduates, individuals seeking additional training to prepare for graduate or medical school, and individual with industry experience. Eligible candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a current CV and a list of three references with complete contact information. All application materials should be submitted electronically through Interfolio. Enquiries about the positions should be directed to Dr. Irene de Lazaro at irene.delazaro@nyu.edu. The post is available immediately, but the starting date can be negotiated. Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195716": {"title": "Research Scholar", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY Researc h Scholar New York University Tandon School of Engineering Biomedical Engineering \u00a0at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a full-time Research Scholar to work with Rose T. Faghih. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. NYU Tandon Biomedical Engineering department leads multidisciplinary centers in medical imaging and data analysis, tissue engineering and regenerative medicine, synthetic and systems bioengineering, robotic and rehabilitation engineering, nano- and micro-bioengineering, neuro-engineering, and cell and immune bioengineering. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for: Developing statistical signal processing, machine learning, and other mathematical algorithms for biomedical engineering applications Conducting human subjects research involving physiological data collection and analysis, or building hardware. Various aspects of administrative duties and service, laboratory activities, grant development, organization and preparation of documentation and other materials, as well as other related activities as needed. Managing access to confidential information; the candidate is expected to maintain strict confidentiality at all times Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $48,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is 11/10/26. Expected end date is 11/09/27 The full-time position would require a work schedule of approximately 35 hours per week . Candidates should have at least a Bachelor's in electrical, biomedical, mechanical, computer, or aerospace engineering, mathematics, or a related field. Proficiency in signal processing, probability, and MATLAB is required; E xperience with Python or R is a plus. Candidates should be driven and team-oriented, with strong organizational and time-management skills, attention to detail, and excellent written and verbal communication skills. They should be able to follow instructions accurately, work independently and collaboratively, and use common word processing, spreadsheet, presentation, and cloud-based file management applications. Relevant coursework, projects, or research experience may demonstrate a background in mathematical algorithms for biomedical engineering applications. Prior research and scientific writing experience is a plus. Publications and human subjects research experience are preferred year (s) of experience required. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: Computational_Medicine_Laboratory@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192194": {"title": "Adjunct Clinical Instructor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: College of Dentistry: General Dentistry and Comprehensive Care: International Programs Location: New York, NY NYU College of Dentistry seeks a dynamic clinician-educator to teach post-doctoral students in the diagnosis, treatment planning, and delivery of digitally driven restorative dentistry. The successful candidate will bring current chairside experience across the complete digital workflow, including intraoral scanning, facial scanning and digital smile design, CAD/CAM design in Exocad or equivalent platforms, 3D printing and milling workflows, guided implant surgery and implant restoration, and removable prosthodontics. Experience with dynamic jaw motion capture, digital occlusion, and laboratory communication in a fully digital workflow is highly desirable. Beyond chairside supervision, the candidate will contribute to the didactic and hands-on components of the program, supporting fellows as they develop competence in digital design, treatment planning, and case documentation. Familiarity with implant education frameworks such as the ITI Academy curriculum is an asset. As the third oldest and the largest dental school in the United States, NYU Dentistry educates nearly 10 percent of our nation\u2019s dentists and endeavors through its faculty, students, and alumni to improve the health of the highly diverse populations in New York City and around the world. The Operative and Digital Dentistry program sits within the Advanced Clinical Fellowships and operates one of the most comprehensively equipped digital dentistry environments in dental education, including intraoral and facial scanning, multiple 3D printing and milling systems, and dedicated design stations. Adjunct faculty at NYU Dentistry benefit from: Affiliation with NYU. Regular interaction with top dental professionals in NYC. Contributions to the future of the dental profession using the latest technology. Access to a fully equipped digital dentistry laboratory and the opportunity to work with current scanning, design, printing, and milling technologies. Opportunities to receive continuing education credit while learning from renowned faculty and researchers. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the salary for this part-time is $43K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as, market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Candidates must possess a DDS or DMD degree and a New York State Dental License. Knowledge of prosthodontic laboratory and digital procedures and evidence of previous teaching experience (academia or industry) are required. Demonstrated proficiency with digital design software and additive or subtractive manufacturing workflows is strongly preferred. Postgraduate training in prosthodontics, operative dentistry, or a related discipline is an asset. Interested candidates please send your cover letter, curriculum vitae and two (2) letters of professional recommendation to: Dr. Pablo Atria via Interfolio by clicking the \"apply now\" button."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/99758": {"title": "Adjunct Faculty - Seminar in Composition: Advanced Scoring Toolkit", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Adjuncts: Adjuncts_MPAP Location: New York, NY NYU Steinhardt Department of Music and Performing Arts Professions (MPAP), Screen Scoring, seeks qualified candidates to teach the Seminar in Composition: Advanced Scoring Toolkit for the Spring 2022 semester. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's Degree At least 5 years of experience as a Screen Composer Experience as an instructor or as a presenter in professional workshops, masterclasses, or college level classes PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Proficient in DAWs and technology Masters Degree Submit the following via Interfolio (emailed materials will NOT be considered): Cover letter addressing teaching philosophy and diversity, equity and\u00a0inclusion practices in the classroom and/or other academic work Resume/CV Names and contacts of two references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192184": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor - Research Methods II", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Adjuncts: Adjuncts_AP Location: New York  The Department of Applied Psychology is seeking an Adjunct Instructor to lead the primary lecture for APSY-UE 1137: Research Methods II on Mondays & Wednesdays from 9:30-10:45 AM for the upcoming Fall 2026 semester. This position is responsible for delivering core curriculum lectures, guiding undergraduate students through quantitative and qualitative research designs, and supervising attached Course Assistants and recitation leaders. Key Responsibilities Primary Instruction: Prepare and deliver engaging, high-quality weekly lectures on advanced research methodologies, experimental designs, and data analysis. Curriculum Management: Maintain and update the Brightspace course site, syllabus, assignment rubrics, and exams in alignment with departmental learning objectives. Instructional Leadership: Direct and collaborate with assigned Course Assistants (CAs) and Recitation Instructors to ensure consistent grading and lab section alignment. Student Mentorship: Hold dedicated weekly office hours to support undergraduate students in mastering course content and research design projects. Qualifications PhD in Psychology, or a related field preferred Demonstrated expertise in research methods Prior college-level teaching experience as an instructor of record or primary TA is strongly preferred. Commitment to inclusive teaching practices and supporting a diverse undergraduate student body Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit the following documents via Handshake: Current Curriculum Vitae (CV) A brief cover letter outlining your teaching philosophy and relevant research methods background In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $235.61. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/123835": {"title": "Director of Youth Suicidal Behaviors Research, AI Hub", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Silver School of Social Work: Office of Research: McSilver Location: New York The NYU McSilver Institute for Poverty Policy and Research is committed to creating new knowledge about the root causes of poverty, developing evidence-based interventions to address its consequences, and rapidly translating research findings into action through policy and best practices. We are looking to hire a talented and motivated individual to serve as the Director of Youth Suicidal Behaviors\u00a0 Research with our Artificial Intelligence Hub (AI Hub). The AI Hub at NYU McSilver Institute addresses how AI can impact the lives of people in marginalized communities. The Director of Youth Suicidal Behaviors Research will play a crucial role within the AI Hub at McSilver by investigating how AI-driven systems can be used to equitably address challenges at the intersection of youth suicidal behaviors, poverty, race, and public health. Reporting to the Executive Director of NYU McSilver, the Director of Youth Suicidal Behaviors Research will lend their subject matter expertise in youth suicidal behaviors, mental health concerns, and behavioral health. They will also supervise the Assistant Director of Research and the AI Hub team, including graduate research assistants and consultants. The Director will also be responsible for study oversight, proposal development and implementation, manuscript preparation, grant writing for national and foundational opportunities, coordination with the AI data analytics team, and strategic planning for the continued growth and expansion of research and evaluation projects. This role combines proficiency in youth suicidal behaviors research to cultivate the creation of inventive solutions and visionary insights on public health challenges facing young people impacted by poverty. Additionally, the Director will spearhead initiatives to forecast youth suicide rates and behaviors by race, geography, income, and other demographic factors, with further groundbreaking public health research and interventions. Additionally, the Director will work with staff and students on research projects and will be responsible for the pursuit, development, and expansion of research-oriented activities with strategic partners and funders. Responsibilities: Youth Suicidal Behaviors Research : Spearhead, oversee, and execute pioneering research initiatives in the realm of youth suicidal behaviors. Develop research methodologies, data collection strategies, and analytical frameworks to begin to answer some of the gaps present in the field. Additionally, take the lead in research endeavors aimed at predicting suicide rates and related behaviors across various demographic variables such as race, geography, and income. Working with the AI hub staff members to leverage AI and data analytics to guide the development of preventive strategies and intervention programs to reduce suicidal behaviors in youth. Equitable AI Integration : Collaborate with AI experts to design, develop, and implement AI-driven systems/models that aim to address poverty, race-related challenges, and public health disparities that are associated with youth suicidal behaviors. Ensure ethical considerations, fairness, transparency, and bias mitigation in AI models and systems. Thought Leadership : Contribute to the development of a strategic vision for the AI Hub, positioning the director as a thought leader in the field. Publish research findings, whitepapers, presentations and reports to disseminate knowledge and influence public discourse concerning the use of AI in the field of youth suicidal behaviors. Team Management : Mentor, lead, and manage interdisciplinary research teams by working hand in hand with the AI Hub team to employ advanced AI methodologies to create novel solutions for youth suicide prevention. Cultivate collaboration and synergy between AI experts and mental health professionals. Foster a collaborative and inclusive work environment that encourages innovation and diversity of thought. Partnership Building : Establish strategic partnerships with relevant organizations, governmental bodies, and experts in the youth mental health field to ensure the AI hub's research efforts have a meaningful and positive impact on addressing critical mental health challenges youth and their families face. Resource Mobilization : Lead the planning and execution of strategic funding proposals and encompassing the cultivation of internal and external strategic partnerships essential for bolstering funding proposals. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Clinical Psychology, Public Health, Developmental Psychology, or related field with a focus on youth suicidal behaviors, youth behavioral health, and or/ public health equity lens. 5-10  years of experience in youth suicidal behaviors, youth mental health program development, policy, and/or research, with a preference for health-related expertise. Strong commitment to social justice, health equity, and experience in advancing the rights of historically excluded populations. Proven track record of being awarded funding for competitive, multiple investigator-initiated or large studies Proficiency in qualitative and quantitative research methods and statistical analysis within the field of youth suicidal behaviors including but not limited to: standard inferential statistics, survey analysis, multivariate regression, prevalence/risk analysis, and trend analysis. Expertise developing and defining appropriate methods for data collection and processing and appropriate statistical analyses for research questions. Strong leadership skills, including managing research teams, fostering cross-disciplinary collaborations, and demonstrating thought leadership. Experience in building and maintaining partnerships with external stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to secure research funding from diverse sources to support youth suicidal behaviors research. Adaptability, strong organizational skills, and the ability to work independently and as part of a diverse, multidisciplinary team. Extensive record of scholarly publications, especially as a first or senior author. Extensive record of presentations at leading conferences in the field of youth suicide research and prevention Preferred Qualifications: Knowledge of ethical AI principles and fairness considerations in AI systems. Proficiency in AI methodologies, with a deep understanding of AI's application in mental health and suicide prevention research. Some demonstrated experience in machine learning or predictive analytics, data science, and/or algorithm development, MLOps, and conducting research using high-risk or restricted data Please include a CV and cover letter with this application. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $150,000 - $185,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Please note: NYU McSilver is currently operating on a hybrid schedule of 3-days a week in-person. This is a grant funded position with the opportunity for ongoing renewal reviewed on an annual basis. This position does not qualify for visa sponsorship. NYU is an EOE/AA/Minorities/Females/Vet/Disabled/Sexual Orientation/Gender Identity employer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194028": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Science (Computer Science) - Dr. Talal Rahwan", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Data Science and AI Lab at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD), led by Associate Professor Talal Rahwan, invites applications for a Postdoctoral Associate in Computational Social Science and Artificial Intelligence. The lab models, simulates, and analyzes social phenomena using computational, experimental, and theoretical methods. We pursue consequential questions at the intersection of artificial intelligence, human behavior, social networks, institutions, and large-scale digital systems. Recent projects span the societal effects and governance of AI, online-platform audits, misinformation and political communication, science of science, scientific inequality, collective behavior, human-machine cooperation, algorithmic privacy, and new methods for discovering social heterogeneity. We seek a creative, technically strong researcher who can turn an important question into a rigorous study and lead it from conception to publication. The position is deliberately broad: applicants do not need to match every topic or method listed below. We value deep expertise in at least one area, intellectual range, scientific ambition, and the ability to collaborate across computer science and the social and behavioral sciences. Research directions: Societal, behavioral, and political consequences of AI and large language models, including bias, fairness, safety, governance, and human decision-making; Audits of recommender systems, search systems, generative AI, and online platforms, including misinformation, political communication, and digital influence; Science of science, including scientific careers, collaboration, inequality, credit, access, and institutional decision-making; Social networks, collective intelligence, group behavior, cooperation, strategic interaction, and human-machine collectives; New computational, experimental, or theoretical methods for measuring, explaining, and improving social outcomes at scale. The successful candidate will have substantial freedom to propose new directions that fit the lab\u2019s broader mission. We are particularly interested in projects with clear conceptual novelty, rigorous identification, broad interdisciplinary relevance, and the potential to influence both scholarship and practice. Role and responsibilities: Formulate original research questions and lead ambitious projects from design through publication; Design and execute observational studies, platform audits, computational analyses, online or field experiments, or formal models, as appropriate to the question; Build reproducible data-collection and analysis pipelines and document methods, code, and research decisions clearly; Write manuscripts for highly selective interdisciplinary or disciplinary venues and present findings at major conferences; Collaborate across disciplines, mentor junior researchers, and contribute to proposals or shared research infrastructure when relevant. Candidate profile: Applicants must have completed all requirements for a PhD before the start date and be less than 5 years post-receipt of the doctoral degree. Relevant fields include computer science, information science, data science, network science, computational social science, economics, political science, psychology, sociology, communication, human-computer interaction, statistics, applied mathematics, or a closely related discipline. Required Qualifications: Evidence of the ability to define and complete original, rigorous research, including at least one substantial output for which the applicant can explain a leading contribution Strong quantitative and computational skills, including reproducible programming in Python, R, or an equivalent research language Excellent written and spoken English, intellectual independence, and the ability to collaborate effectively across disciplines; A commitment to ethical, transparent, and reproducible research Methodological fit Applicants should bring deep expertise in at least one of the following. No candidate is expected to cover the full list: Large-scale data collection, data engineering, statistical modeling, or computational text and image analysis; Machine learning, natural-language processing, large language models, or evaluation and auditing of AI and online platforms Causal inference, natural experiments, panel-data methods, or quasi-experimental design Survey, online, laboratory, or field experiments involving human participants Network science, game theory, agent-based modeling, or computational models of collective behavior Especially welcome Candidates who combine strong technical methods with serious engagement with social-science theory, or whose training crosses conventional disciplinary boundaries; Researchers who have built original datasets, experimental platforms, or research software, especially for multilingual, cross-cultural, or globally comparative work How applications will be evaluated: We will evaluate research quality, originality, independence, methodological rigor, clarity of thought, and the potential to lead projects of broad scientific and societal importance. Publication records will be assessed relative to career stage and disciplinary norms. Applicants from different research traditions are encouraged to apply; venue labels alone will not substitute for evidence of a strong personal contribution. Why join the Data Science and AI Lab? Substantial intellectual freedom to develop new projects in consultation with the principal investigator; A lab culture oriented toward ambitious questions, methodological rigor, and publication in leading general-science and disciplinary venues; An interdisciplinary community spanning computer science, psychology, economics, political science, social research, engineering, and the humanities; High-performance computing, GPU-enabled workflows, large-scale data processing, research storage, and technical support; Opportunities across NYU\u2019s global network, mentoring experience, professional development, and an active postdoctoral community. Learn more: Data Science and AI Lab | Talal Rahwan | Postdoctoral research at NYUAD Applicants should submit the following through the official NYU recruitment posting: A one-page cover letter A current curriculum vitae and complete publication list Two representative research papers or working papers Transcript/ copy of latest degree A research and fit statement of no more than two pages, following the instructions below Names and contact information for three academic or professional references Research and fit statement The statement should be concrete and evidence-based. It must: Explain the applicant\u2019s personal intellectual, methodological, and technical contributions to the two submitted papers; Identify two or three specific connections between the applicant\u2019s prior work and the lab\u2019s research questions or methods; Propose one ambitious research question the applicant could realistically lead at NYUAD, including the core method or data source and the expected scientific contribution; Explain why the proposed question matters beyond a narrow subfield. Reference letters will be requested only from shortlisted candidates. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. The start date is flexible; appointment and renewal terms will be specified in the formal offer and governed by NYUAD policies. Questions may be directed to: talal.rahwan@nyu.edu About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188601": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Center of Interacting with Urban Networks (CITIES) - Dr. Monica Menendez", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Interacting Urban Networks (CITIES) Location: Abu Dhabi The Center for Interacting Urban Networks, New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit a Post-doctoral associate to contribute to interdisciplinary research centered on the Mobility theme. The theme is closely linked to one of NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s key research impact areas outlined in the Excellence 2030 Strategic Framework: Urban Systems. Specific research areas within the mobility theme include monitoring, modeling, and controlling the operations of multimodal transportation systems, new vehicle technologies, urban mobility, research at the intersection of traditional traffic flow theory and data science, the impact of cybersecurity on transportation systems, and more. Resposibilities: To undertake independent high-level scientific research. To publish and present the results of her/his research in high-quality conferences and journals (at least two journal papers are expected based on one year's work) To provide additional support to the research group as needed. To provide support and assistance in the planning of research projects, preparation and writing of proposals, search for open calls, etc. (a leading role in at least one proposal is expected during a year). To provide support and assistance with the execution of new and/or ongoing proposals/projects to achieve proposed tasks within the established time frames. To provide assistance in the preparation of classes, such as collecting materials, preparing exercises for courses, and assisting with evaluation of assignments. To assist in teaching specific courses/sessions, including conducting labs, teaching lectures, holding office hours, reviewing and grading exercises/exams, etc. To provide mentoring and support to students. Applicants must have received a PhD in engineering, computer science, urban science, applied mathematics, or a related field, with less than five years post recieving the PhD. Experience in transportation, in particular related to urban science, is required. Additionally, excellent communication skills in English, strong quantitative skills, great computational and data management skills, as well as scientific creativity are essential. Applicants with strong mathematics training are particularly encouraged to apply. The position will also require the ability to collaborate with other researchers on other center themes, such as Fairness and Uncertainty, and contribute to a comprehensive understanding of urban systems. For consideration, applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of research interests, transcript and contact information for referees, who may be contacted through the selection process. The research statement should outline the specific project(s) that the candidate would like to lead within CITIES and potential collaborators with the Center. If you have any questions, please email us at nyuad.cities@nyu.edu Evaluations will start immediately until the role is filled. The earliest start date for this position is as soon as possible. About The Center for Interacting Urban Networks (CITIES): The NYUAD\u2019s Center for Interacting Urban Networks (CITIES \u2013 \ufee3\ufeaa\u0646 ) is an interdisciplinary research center dedicated to advancing urban science and promoting cutting-edge research that is translated into practical, real-world solutions for the benefit of society. Our mission is to establish a vibrant and globally recognized knowledge hub for urban networks, bridge the gap between scientific significance and practical value, and further develop the human capital to propel the city of Abu Dhabi and the UAE into a more prosperous and sustainable future. CITIES leverages the expertise of faculty members from the four Divisions of NYUAD and world-renowned scientific collaborators from multiple institutions within and beyond the NYU Global Network, as well as local stakeholders and private companies across the UAE and abroad. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/166558": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Engineering (Electrical Engineering \u2013 Applied Interactive Multimedia) - Dr. Mohamad Eid", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Engineering: AD_Electrical Engineering Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE  The Applied Interactive Multimedia laboratory in the Division of Engineering, New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit a motivated research or postdoctoral associate to lead the design, development, and validation of material, control systems, and algorithms for next-generation soft haptic actuators and experiences. Note that the research involves significant interactions with collaborators in material science. The developed technologies will be utilized to develop digital twin and soft robot-assisted simulations in the areas of brain machine interaction, wearable haptics, and rehabilitation. The successful applicant will have the following technical experience in: PhD degree in Electrical, Mechanical, or Material Engineering (or related field) OR 3-5 years of industry experience (Research and Development). Significant experience in haptic technologies. Hands-on Lab Building and Rapid Prototyping of Novel Proof of Concepts (Extensive on-site lab work). Critical thinking and problem-solving skills. Team player with good communication skills. Proficiency in English. Still within the first 5 years since receiving the PhD For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with full publication list, statement of research interests, one letter of reference, and a transcript, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email Prof. Mohamad Eid at mohamad.eid@nyu.edu. The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. Please visit our website at http://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/about/careers/faculty-positions.html for instructions and information on how to apply. If you have any questions, please email: mohamad.eid@nyu.edu. About NYUAD: NYU Abu Dhabi is a degree-granting research university with a fully integrated liberal arts and science undergraduate program in the Arts, Sciences, Social Sciences, Humanities, and Engineering. NYU Abu Dhabi, NYU New York, and NYU Shanghai, form the backbone of NYU\u2019s global network university, an interconnected network of portal campuses and academic centers across six continents that enable seamless international mobility of students and faculty in their pursuit of academic and scholarly activity. This global university represents a transformative shift in higher education, one in which the intellectual and creative endeavors of academia are shaped and examined through an international and multicultural perspective. As a major intellectual hub at the crossroads of the Arab world, NYUAD serves as a center for scholarly thought, advanced research, knowledge creation, and sharing, through its academic, research, and creative activities. EOE/AA/Minorities/Females/Vet/Disabled/Sexual Orientation/Gender Identity Employer UAE Nationals are encouraged to apply"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/159987": {"title": "Urban Science Faculty Fellows", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Center for Urban Science and Progress Location: Brooklyn, NY Urban Science Faculty Fellows (2025) NYU Center for Urban Science and Progress Center for Urban Science and Progress New York University\u2019s Center for Urban Science and Progress (CUSP) is seeking faculty fellows with a strong academic record. CUSP is an interdisciplinary research center located in Downtown Brooklyn and is dedicated to the application of science, technology, engineering, and mathematics in the service of urban communities across the globe. Using New York City (NYC) as our laboratory and classroom, we strive to develop novel data- and technology-driven solutions to advance our understanding of cities and help address the complex challenges they face . CUSP\u2019s research and educational initiatives focus on high-impact areas such as climate change and resiliency, improving public health, designing modern urban infrastructure, and promoting the ethical and equitable design, collection, and deployment of data and technology in cities . CUSP strives to benefit NYC and other partners with research that is both mission- and impact- oriented. We develop and integrate data analytics and technologies for the common good. Our interdisciplinary research teams bring together experts in computer and data science; civil, electrical, environmental, biomedical, and mechanical engineering; design and interactive technologies; and the social sciences . Expectations Successful applicants are expected to work on interdisciplinary projects to contribute to both methods and foundational knowledge in their core\u00a0disciplines, as well as to novel data- and technology-driven applications. Ideal candidates should meet the following criteria: Evidence of or interest in conducting multi-disciplinary research with potential broad impact for urban communities Strong publication record Experience and/or interest in teaching and mentoring Interest in writing grants and establishing a record of externally funded research Outreach experience (with stakeholders, city agencies, community) and transitioning research into practice is encouraged, but not required. Applicants must identify at least two CUSP core or affiliated faculty members who would serve as mentors. The applicants\u2019 proposed research must span areas of expertise. Successful applicants will be expected to start new projects and collaborations and/or engage in existing projects with faculty. CUSP\u2019s strategic areas of focus are public health; climate change and resiliency/sustainability; modern infrastructure; and fairness, privacy and equity in urban tech and data science. Our methodological areas of interest include but not limited to (a) data science, (b) ML/AI and robotics; (c) urban sensing and IoT; and (d) modeling and complex systems. We encourage applications\u00a0in this wide range of areas and in other relevant areas in urban science domains. Responsibilities: Conduct groundbreaking research both independently and as part of\u00a0interdisciplinary teams; contribute to existing projects under the supervision of sponsoring\u00a0faculty, and start new projects and research collaborations as appropriate; participate in the\u00a0development of funded research proposals; build bridges between CUSP and other\u00a0Tandon/NYU labs, groups, and centers; publish in top venues in smart cities, urban science,\u00a0and/or their fields of specialization; contribute to scholarly life at CUSP through the organization\u00a0of seminars, mentoring of students and other service; and teach a maximum of two courses per\u00a0academic year. Salary range: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $75k/year with competitive benefits, plus funds for travel and professional development. The expected start date is January 15, 2025. Urban Science Faculty Fellows are full-time, fixed-term positions, with initial one-year appointment, and additional annual appointment renewal opportunity based on satisfactory performance. Candidates should have a PhD in a relevant field (granted by the start of the position), and an active agenda of research and publication with a strong focus on smart cities, urban science, or other areas with potential for direct application to cities. A record of transitioning research into practice, particularly as applied to critical urban challenges, is desirable. Applicants should submit the following as part of the application package: Cover letter (1 page) Curriculum vitae Personal statement (1 page): The document should describe how this position contributes to the applicant\u2019s future career. It should also describe your commitment to diversity, and teaching and mentoring style. Statement of research interests (two pages): This document should describe your research interests and plans at CUSP. It should also highlight how the applicant\u2019s previous research prepared the applicant to conduct the proposed investigation. The statement should also mention the names of one or more core or affiliated faculty members\u00a0from CUSP. Contact information for two references; the search committee will reach out to recommendation letter writers at a later date. Applications received by December 31, 2024, will receive full consideration. Any questions may be directed to Qi Sun."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/162151": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Center for Urban Science and Progress Location: Brooklyn, NY POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Dynamical Systems Laboratory (DSL) at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Post Doctoral Associate to work in cutting-edge research focused on human-swarm interaction, virtual reality, and robotics. We seek independent, motivated, and creative individuals to conduct research in human-computer interaction, human behavior, and virtual reality. The research will specifically focus on developing intuitive user interfaces to enable non-expert operators to control robotic swarms during disaster response. The DSL was established 16 years ago by Prof. Maurizio Porfiri (Institute Professor at Tandon School of Engineering and Director of the Center for Urban Science and Progress, with appointments at the Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, the Department of Biomedical Engineering, the Department of Civil and Urban Engineering, and the Center for Urban Science and Progress). The DSL is home to a highly collaborative, vibrant group of young scholars with complementary expertise across theory, experiments, and computations. The director, Prof. Porfiri, is the author of more than 350 journal publications and the recipient of numerous professional awards (Wikipedia/Maurizio_Porfiri). New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for leading the design and testing of virtual reality systems, mentoring undergraduate, master\u2019s, and doctoral students, coordinating outreach activities, and supporting proposal writing and grantsmanship. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in designing and implementing human-computer or human-robot interfaces; as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on virtual reality simulations (e.g., Unity, C#, VR headsets) or user-interface prototyping for testing usability and interaction is desired. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $62,500 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. This is a one-year position with an opportunity for renewal. Candidates should have a PhD in computer engineering, computer science, human-centered technology, innovation & design, robotics, neuroscience, or related fields. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: gl2861@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Expected start date is: February 1, 2025."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/176250": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor in Engineering Design - General Engineering, New York University", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: General Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY, United States The Department of Undergraduate Academics at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for part-time, non-tenured instructors, with a start date of January 20, 2026 . The instructor\u2019s primary responsibility will be teaching EG-UY 1004. A cornerstone design course, EG-UY 1004 introduces students to the engineering design process through the completion of an open-ended semester-long design project.\u00a0 This course is a 4-credit course that consists of weekly lectures, labs, and recitation components and is team-taught by a group of faculty and undergraduate peer mentors. The part-time instructor will co-teach recitation sections alongside a writing lecturer.\u00a0\u00a0 The instructor\u2019s responsibilities are fulfilled by: Mentoring teams of 3-4 students through completion of their semester-long design project. Supporting lab exercises that introduce students to engineering tools and skills, such as computer aided design, circuitry, coding, and manufacturing techniques. Evaluating students on their technical communication skills Delivering short lectures introducing students to various engineering disciplines and skills. We invite applicants with expertise in engineering, engineering education, or related fields. Applicants should ideally have: M.S. or Ph.D. in engineering, engineering education, computer science, or related field. A demonstrated interest in STEM education and mentorship of undergraduate students. Strong written and verbal technical communication skills. Experience working with engineering software and tools (for example, CAD software, Arduino, Raspberry Pi, 3D printers, laser cutters, and hand tools). Please submit the following materials electronically through the \"Apply Now\" button : Cover letter Current CV Teaching statement Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Names and contact information for three references. We will review applications beginning on November 10, 2025 and will continue until the position is filled. We encourage you to submit early. Should you have any questions please contact the program director, Ingrid Paredes (ijparedes@nyu.edu). About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion\u00a0 and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. We aspire to create a climate where inclusion is not only appreciated but also considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for, and who possess an interest in, working in broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $235.46. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/178060": {"title": "Adjunct Writing Lecturer - General Engineering, New York University", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: General Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY, United States The Department of Undergraduate Academics at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for part-time, non-tenured instructors, with a start date of January 20, 2026 . The General Engineering Program in Undergraduate Academics manages the first-year engineering course EG-UY 1004 Introduction to Engineering and Design, which is required for all engineering, computer science, and business and technology majors at NYU Tandon. This course consists of weekly lectures, labs, and recitation sections and is team-taught by a group of faculty who are supported by a large team of teaching assistants. The Lecturer will co-teach recitation sections alongside an engineering faculty member. We invite applicants to teach one or more undergraduate courses. In particular, we presently seek writing instructors for EG-UY 1004 Introduction Engineering and Design . We are seeking a dedicated faculty member eager to support STEM students in developing core skills in technical communication and writing. The writing instructor\u2019s primary responsibility will be teaching. Their responsibility is fulfilled by: Evaluating weekly individually- and group-written assignments Delivering short lectures on technical and professional communication Hosting in-class, one-on-one appointments with students to address writing issues We invite applicants with expertise in all areas of technical writing. Applicants should ideally have one of the following: MFA in writing field M.S or Ph.D. in engineering, STEM, communications, journalism, or related technical writing fields Bachelor\u2019s degree in STEM, communication, or related field with minimum of 10 years of relevant experience in technical writing Industry experience and/or teaching in the areas of technical report writing, scientific literacy, composition and rhetoric, and/or academic communication A documented record of university teaching in technical communication and/or composition and rhetoric Prior experience working with students through tutoring or supporting academic writing centers Please submit the following materials electronically through the \"Apply Now\" button : Cover letter Current CV Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Teaching statement describing experience and teaching philosophy Names and contact information for at least two references (if appropriate) We will review applications starting December 8, 2025, and will continue until we fill the position; we encourage you to submit early. Should you have any questions please contact the program director, Ingrid Paredes (ijparedes@nyu.edu). About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by a historic commitment investment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and new perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. We aspire to create a climate where inclusion is not only appreciated but also considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for, and who possess an interest in, working in broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $222.29. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with Local 7902."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/179164": {"title": "Industry Assistant Professor, General Engineering", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: General Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for a full-time, non-tenured, renewable faculty position in General Engineering, at the level of Industry Assistant Professor, with a start date of August 17, 2026. This appointment will be for three academic years. The instructor\u2019s primary responsibility will be teaching sections of EG-UY 1004 Introduction to Engineering and Design. A cornerstone design course, EG-UY 1004 introduces students to the engineering design process through the completion of an open-ended semester-long design project.\u00a0 This course is a 4-credit course that consists of weekly lectures, labs, and recitation components and is team-taught by a group of faculty and undergraduate peer mentors. The instructor\u2019s responsibilities are fulfilled by: \u25a0 Mentoring teams of 3-4 students through completion of their semester-long design project. \u25a0 Supporting lab exercises that introduce students to engineering tools and skills, such as computer aided design, circuitry, coding, and manufacturing techniques. \u25a0 Evaluating students on their technical communication skills \u25a0 Delivering short lectures introducing students to various engineering disciplines and skills. A typical teaching load is 5 sections per semester. The Industry Assistant Professor will also assist with the operations of the course. This includes: Collaborating with other faculty members and undergraduate peer mentors on curriculum development, including designing course material and developing assessment tools for the course Assisting with training peer mentors who support course delivery and grading We invite applicants with expertise in engineering, engineering education, or related fields. Applicants should ideally have: A Ph.D. in engineering, engineering education, computer science, or related field A demonstrated interest in STEM education and mentorship of undergraduate students Strong written and verbal technical communication skills Experience working with engineering software and tools (for example, CAD software, Arduino, Raspberry Pi, 3D printers, laser cutters, and hand tools) Please submit the following materials through the \" Apply Now \" button. Cover letter Current CV Teaching statement Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Names and contact information for three references. We will review applications beginning on February 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. We encourage you to submit early. Should you have any questions please contact the program director, Ingrid Paredes ( ijparedes@nyu.edu ). About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by a historic commitment investment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and new perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. We aspire to create a climate where inclusion is not only appreciated but also considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for, and who possess an interest in, working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by NYU Office of Work Life . Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary (9-month) range for this position is $85,000 - $135,000 for the Industry Assistant Professor rank.\u00a0 New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191283": {"title": "Part-Time Instructor in Engineering Design - General Engineering, New York University", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: General Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY, United States The Department of Undergraduate Academics at the NYU Tandon School of Engineering invites applications for part-time, non-tenured instructors, with a start date of September 1, 2026 . The instructor\u2019s primary responsibility will be teaching EG-UY 1004. A cornerstone design course, EG-UY 1004 introduces students to the engineering design process through the completion of an open-ended semester-long design project.\u00a0 This course is a 4-credit course that consists of weekly lectures, labs, and recitation components and is team-taught by a group of faculty and undergraduate peer mentors. The part-time instructor will co-teach recitation sections alongside a writing lecturer. The instructor\u2019s responsibilities are fulfilled by: Mentoring teams of 3-4 students through completion of their semester-long design project. Supporting lab exercises that introduce students to engineering tools and skills, such as computer aided design, circuitry, coding, and manufacturing techniques. Evaluating students on their technical communication skills Delivering short lectures introducing students to various engineering disciplines and skills. We invite applicants with expertise in engineering, engineering education, or related fields. Applicants should ideally have: M.S. or Ph.D. in engineering, engineering education, computer science, or related field. A demonstrated interest in STEM education and mentorship of undergraduate students. Strong written and verbal technical communication skills. Experience working with engineering software and tools (for example, CAD software, Arduino, Raspberry Pi, 3D printers, laser cutters, and hand tools). Please submit the following materials : Cover letter Current CV Teaching statement Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Names and contact information for three references. We will review applications beginning on August 17, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. We encourage you to submit early. Should you have any questions please contact the program director, Ingrid Paredes (ijparedes@nyu.edu). Additional Information In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the contact hour rate for this position is $249.59. This rate is based on the collective bargaining agreement with NYU Adjuncts - ACT-UAW Local 7902. About Us New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States with an unparalleled network of global campuses and academic centers. NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, located in the heart of Brooklyn, dates back to 1854, the founding year of both the New York University School of Civil Engineering and Architecture and the Brooklyn Collegiate and Polytechnic Institute. Backed by an historic commitment of $1 billion and under the new leadership of Juan de Pablo as the University\u2019s Executive Vice President for Global Science and Technology and Tandon\u2019s Executive Dean, the Engineering School is entering an exciting new phase to recruit world-class faculty conducting groundbreaking basic and applied research in key areas. We are home to a community of renowned faculty and undergraduate and graduate students united in a mission to understand and create technology that powers cities, enables worldwide communication, fights climate change, and builds healthier, safer, and more equitable real and digital worlds. The school\u2019s culture centers on encouraging rigorous, interdisciplinary collaboration and research; fostering inclusivity, entrepreneurial thinking, and diverse perspectives; and creating innovative and accessible pathways for lifelong learning in STEM. NYU Tandon is committed to substantially increase the proportion of our faculty from historically underrepresented groups in STEM and we encourage candidates from such groups to apply. We aspire to create a climate where diversity and inclusion are not only appreciated but considered an asset for creativity and innovation, and we seek faculty who have a real passion for a culturally diverse environment. We take pride in our high numbers of female students and students who are the first in their family to go to college. NYU belongs to the Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC), which assists with dual-career searches, and our faculty are supported by a range of work-life balance programs provided by the NYU Office of Work Life."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194365": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the Provost: NYU Africa House Location: New York, NY Africa House is seeking to hire a Junior Research Scientist to support the research of the Director, Professor Yaw Nyarko. Professor Nyarko\u2019s primary research interests include African economic development, artificial intelligence and technological adoption, and commodities markets. Primary duties will consist of conducting high-quality research suitable for publication in academic journals. Additional duties include providing broad support for ongoing projects, including: a multi-year study on the potential for blockchain to address market failures in rural credit markets; an international research consortium focused on scaling evidence-based technological innovations for agricultural systems, including livestock, using AI, digital advisory services, and weather forecasting to support smallholder farmers; a set of working papers focused on the economics and mathematics of large-language models; and research on various dimensions of the United Arab Emirates\u2019 economic history and structure. Our ideal candidate is a committed, responsible, highly motivated individual with a proven ability to work independently and effectively in a professional environment. We are looking for a problem-solver and a strategic, creative thinker with strong writing skills to be part of our interdisciplinary team. Key Responsibilities & Duties: Help conduct quantitative research on African commodities markets, AI and blockchain, and technological interventions for economic development Assemble, graph, and analyze data using Excel, Stata, or Matlab Manipulate economic data and/or create new variables using Excel, Stata, or Matlab Conduct quantitative and qualitative research Present information in clear, concise reports Clean and manage large datasets for field research project in Ghana, West Africa Conduct statistical analyses of data using Stata or Matlab (advanced knowledge required) Assist with data management for a research project on commodities markets, AI, and blockchain Prepare research reports for concept notes, academic papers, and presentations The ideal candidate exhibits the characteristics below and demonstrates a desire to be an important contributor to a collegial team. Required: Master\u2019s degree with concentration and course work in Economics, Mathematics, Statistics or a related field Strong analytical capability, including experience analyzing large data sets in Excel, Stata, Matlab, R, or similar software Exceptional academic record Excellent attention to detail Strong interest in economic development in emerging markets Excellent written and verbal communication skills Willingness to work as part of a global, diverse, multicultural team Excellent organizational and problem-solving skills Excellent interpersonal, communication, and customer service skills Ability to solve problems and interact with individuals at all levels Ability to work and communicate with diverse stakeholders Ability to complete detail-oriented tasks with accuracy and timeliness and perform multiple tasks in a fast-paced environment Willingness to take on assignments or tasks within short notice Previous work experience (2-3 years) in an office environment Preferred : Experience with analysis of complex survey data is highly desirable Knowledge of randomized control trials Prior field research or research assistant experience in the fields of development economics a plus In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the compensation for this position is $70,000-80,000 USD. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. This is a one-year fixed term position. Please submit your resume, cover letter, and two academic writing samples for consideration by November 30, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170594": {"title": "Attorney Advisor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law: Tax Law Center Location: See position description. The Tax Law Center at the NYU School of Law is seeking one or more full-time Attorney Advisors to work closely with the Executive Director to contribute to rigorous, high-impact tax legal work in the public interest. You are an attorney with experience practicing US tax law or working in US federal government processes of crafting, interpreting, or administering tax law, and are excited to work closely with academics, legal practitioners, and policymakers to advance racial and economic equity in taxation. You are an excellent writer of rigorous technical tax materials, such as reports, memos, briefs, comments, or legislation and regulations. You are a highly-organized project manager and a clear communicator, able to manage production of high-quality, timely written work, and exercise judgement about where to focus time and resources to have robust input in fast-moving tax law and policy processes. Responsibilities The Attorney Advisor, who will report to the Executive Director or another senior member of staff, will be responsible for duties including: In consultation with other senior staff, helping to shape an ongoing research agenda for the organization, and authoring a variety of technical tax written products called for in that agenda, including reports, briefs, and comments on regulations. Supervising a Research Associate in the production of a series of written products, ensuring the quality and timeliness of work from outside authors. Helping to design and implement systems to identify and prioritize opportunities for the Center to further its mission.\u00a0\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b Building strong, confidential relationships with high-level practitioners, academics, and other outside experts. Requirements JD\u00a0or equivalent and some US\u00a0experience in tax practice or federal tax policy making or administration. Strong legal research and writing skills, including the ability to express precise, highly technical information in a way that is clear and compelling. Excellent communication skills. A commitment to the mission of the Center. Prior experience managing staff is a plus but is not required, but you need to be excited to develop these skills. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $150,000 and $190,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. You will be an employee of New York University, with access to an excellent, comprehensive benefits package including generous paid time off and retirement contributions. The Tax Law Center has physical office space at NYU School of Law in New York, but also offers hybrid or remote work arrangements from within the US. You will be asked to upload a resume. Please do not upload a cover letter; instead, you will be asked to provide brief answers to short questions as part of the application form. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170595": {"title": "Senior Attorney Advisor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law: Tax Law Center Location: See position description. The Tax Law Center at the NYU School of Law is seeking one or more full-time Senior Attorney Advisors to work closely with the Executive Director to contribute to rigorous, high-impact tax legal work in the public interest. You are an attorney with significant experience in the federal tax policy making process and a deep commitment to advancing racial and economic equity through taxation. You are a clear, persuasive writer and communicator, and are excited to draft legislation, regulations, and comments in addition to briefs, technical reports, and less-technical white papers and blog posts. You have deep relationships in tax law and policymaking. Responsibilities The Senior Attorney Advisor, who will report to the Executive Director, will be responsible for: Helping to set the agenda for the Tax Law Center, including helping to lead the design and implementation of a system for identifying and prioritizing opportunities for the Center to further its mission. Playing a core role in driving forward the Center\u2019s ongoing regulatory work, coordinating and collaborating with outside experts, performing quality control, helping supervise the work of an Attorney Advisor and a Research Associate, and contributing your own original written work Interfacing with policymakers and staff, and connecting them with academic and practitioner experts. Providing strategic guidance to the Executive Director and other senior team members. Supervising and mentoring Tax Law Center staff. Requirements JD\u00a0or equivalent and significant experience working on US federal tax law and policy. A track record of developing and maintaining strong working relationships with tax technical experts, such as federal government tax staff and tax practitioners. Deep subject matter expertise. A commitment to advancing the mission of the Center. Good judgement about how to navigate and weigh complex and uncertain policy and procedural considerations. Prior experience managing staff is a plus but is not required, but you need to be excited to develop these skills. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $175,000 and $205,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. You will be an employee of New York University, with access to an excellent, comprehensive benefits package including generous paid time off and retirement contributions. The Tax Law Center has physical office space at NYU School of Law in New York, but also offers hybrid or remote work arrangements from within the US. You will be asked to upload a resume. Please do not upload a cover letter; instead, you will be asked to provide brief answers to short questions as part of the application form. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170596": {"title": "Legal Director", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law: Tax Law Center Location: See position description. The Tax Law Center at the NYU School of Law seeks a Legal Director to work closely with the Executive Director to lead the Center\u2019s rigorous, high-impact tax legal work in the public interest.\u202fAs the Center\u2019s most senior tax law specialist, the Legal Director will help shape and build the Center\u2019s program and public interest role in tax law, working with and further building an exceptional tax legal staff . You are a tax lawyer with extensive experience in US federal tax law or policymaking processes and a deep commitment to advancing equity through taxation. You are excited to help develop, lead, and supervise the Center\u2019s substantive work program across a broad range of issue areas spanning: legislation, regulation, and controversy aimed at shaping law and policy and strengthening the processes and institutions that make tax law. You have excellent tax legal skills and are a clear, persuasive writer and communicator. Responsibilities The Legal Director, who will report to the Executive Director, will be responsible for: Helping to set the Tax Law Center\u2019s strategy and craft its work agenda. This will include leading the design and implementation of systems for identifying and prioritizing projects that best further the Center\u2019s mission. Driving the Center\u2019s work forward. This includes contributing to original work, managing the Center\u2019s legal staff, and building collaborations with external partners. Ensuring that the Center\u2019s work is consistently rigorous, coherent, and mission-driven . The Legal Director will supervise program work to ensure that it meets the Center\u2019s high -quality standards, and is coherent and consistent with the Center\u2019s mission, body of work, and future plans. The Legal Director will be responsible for approving certain legal work, developing processes for quality control and evaluating impact, and ensuring this learning improves future work. Shaping and communicating the Center\u2019s positions . The Legal Director will shape the Tax Law Center\u2019s substantive positions; communicate with stakeholders including policymakers; build relationships with external experts and partners, the Center\u2019s advisory board, funders, and media; and review funding proposals. Depending on experience and background, the Legal Director would also be responsible for: Managing the Tax Law Center\u2019s legal staff (including Senior Attorney Advisors, Attorney Advisors and Fellows). The Center has already attracted exceptional legal staff in a number of areas. The Legal Director will identify and hire for needed skills and perspectives; supervise and mentor a collaborative and inclusive team; and strengthen internal practices for skill-building, professional development, and current awareness. Requirements A commitment to advancing the mission of the Center. JD or equivalent and extensive experience working on US federal tax law and policy. At least ten years of legal tax experience. Deep expertise in tax law, plus a desire and demonstrated ability to work widely across different tax issue areas (ranging from, for example, individual taxation to corporate taxation), and across different aspects of tax law (legislation, regulation, controversy, and processes/institutions).\u00a0 We do not expect candidates to be expert across all aspects of tax, but are looking for an interest in and demonstrated ability to: quickly understand issues outside of areas of your direct expertise; be briefed by and make judgments based on input from staff and external experts; and draw connections between work in different issue areas in order to execute a coherent work program. Excellent judgment about how to navigate and weigh complex and uncertain policy and procedural considerations.\u202fThis means the capacity for effective decision-making, as well as the ability to understand competing concerns and solve problems of substantial impact, sensitivity, and complexity. An extensive track record of developing and maintaining strong working relationships with tax experts, such as tax practitioners, tax academics, or government tax practitioners. Demonstrated skill in furthering equity in operational and substantive work. Excellent communication skills, including ability to draft and review a range of outputs including draft legislation, regulations, comments, briefs, and reports; and the ability to communicate with both technical expert audiences and those who are less familiar with a specific issue. Desired: These skills and experiences are a plus. If they are not part of your current skills or experience, you must be excited to develop knowledge and skill in these areas: A\u00a0track record in managing and developing staff, experience in hiring, and an ability to nurture a collaborative and inclusive work environment. Experience in tax controversy or considering administrative law issues and related trends in tax; and Experience across more than one aspect of government, private practice, tax academia, or other modes of tax research or policy development, or other experiences that provide deep insight into more than one set of institutions that play a role in tax law/policymaking. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is between $205,000 to $230,000 per year. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. You will be an employee of New York University, with access to an excellent, comprehensive benefits package including generous paid time off and retirement contributions. The Tax Law Center has physical office space at NYU School of Law in New York, but also offers hybrid or remote work arrangements from within the US.\u00a0 As a key leadership position, the Legal Director may be expected to travel on occasion. You will be asked to upload a resume. Please do not upload a cover letter; instead, you will be asked to provide brief answers to short questions as part of the application form. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191157": {"title": "Tax Law and Policy Fellow", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law: Tax Law Center Location: New York, NY Join our team at The Tax Law Center at NYU Law ! Our work includes offering technical input on tax policy ideas and legislation, submitting comments on tax regulations, and engaging in tax litigation, with the goals of protecting and improving the integrity of the tax system, saving and raising revenues, and advancing equity. Position Description The Tax Law Center at the New York University School of Law is seeking at least one full-time Tax Law and Policy Fellow/s to support Tax Law Center staff with research, writing, and project management. The Fellowship is for two years. The Fellow/s will work across subject matter areas in tax. The Fellow/s will be able to work with senior staff to focus on any area(s) of specialty in their second year if there is specific subject matter interest and as work needs allow. You are a recent law school graduate with a strong interest in advancing justice through the tax code, and gaining experience working with experts including academics, policymakers, and practitioners to solve difficult legislative, regulatory, litigation, and tax administration issues. This is a two-year Fellowship with case-by-case flexibility on exact start and end dates, with the general expectation that students graduating in 2027 will begin in late summer 2027. The two-year span of this Fellowship is anticipated to fall across an eventful period in tax law and policy, with the possibility of significant tax legislation; major regulatory and other administrative actions including implementation of the 2025 tax law; and increasing administrative and constitutional law controversies in the courts with potential impacts across the tax system. Over this period, the Tax Law Center will continue to counter extraordinary efforts to undermine the integrity of the tax system and advance its project to create an executable vision for rebuilding and remaking the federal tax administration system. Responsibilities The Tax Law and Policy Fellow will be responsible for a wide range of activities in an established Center including: Researching and drafting comments on proposed administrative action, legal briefs, legislative language, and policy memos, with guidance from senior members of staff. Tracking important legislative and regulatory activities and key court cases. Building productive working relationships with outside experts and policymaking staff. Working alongside Center staff to help build out and improve organizational processes. The fellow will work across subject matter areas in tax. This may include work on tax legislation, regulations or other administrative decision-making, and controversy, and in varied subject matter areas including: individual and corporate taxation, climate tax credits, administrative law, information reporting and financial transparency, emerging markets, and foundational statutes protecting taxpayer privacy and preventing political interference in tax administration. While you will get to work with a range of subject matter experts and colleagues, you will report to a single supervising attorney who will act as your main point of contact for workflow and professional development. Requirements A JD degree or LLM with coursework in taxation Excellent research and writing skills Excellent communication skills A high degree of organization and attention to detail A commitment to advancing the Center\u2019s goals Please feel free to email taxlawcentercareers@nyu.edu with any questions that you may have about the position. For equity purposes, responses to questions will also be anonymized and cross-posted to this page as well: https://www.taxlawcenter.org/careers/fellows. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $105,000 to $110,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. You will be an employee of New York University, with access to an excellent, comprehensive benefits package including generous paid time off and retirement contributions. Alongside NYU benefits, the Tax Law Center offers flexible policies and support for early career lawyers, including access to professional development, time off support for desired relevant Volunteer Income Tax Assistance or Low Income Tax Clinic volunteering, and membership to professional and bar associations. The Tax Law Center has physical office space at NYU School of Law in New York, but also offers hybrid or remote work arrangements from within the US. You will be asked to upload a resume. Please do not upload a cover letter; instead, you will be asked to provide brief answers to three short questions as part of the application form. This questionnaire will be assessed in a semi anonymized process, which means that the Tax Law Center will review your responses without connecting them to your name, resume details, or other personal information at the time of assessment. We will have a priority deadline for applicants who submit their questionnaire by September 7, 2026, guaranteeing review of your initial application by early October. After September 30, applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195416": {"title": "Lab Manager (Full-Time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Center of Health and Rehabilitation Research (CoHRR) Location: New York, NY The Center of Health and Rehabilitation Research (CoHRR) at New York University is seeking a full-time Lab Manager to work with Dr. Si On Yoon in the Department of Communicative Sciences and Disorders at NYU Steinhardt. The Lab Manager will contribute primarily to an NIH/NIA-funded R01 project, CLEAR (Cognitive-Linguistic Engagement in Aging and Recall), which examines how conversational engagement influences memory in healthy older adults and individuals with mild cognitive impairment and Alzheimer\u2019s disease. The project investigates the cognitive and linguistic mechanisms underlying memory for information encountered during conversation and examines how different communication supports and strategies may help older adults retain important information from everyday interactions. The Lab Manager will play a central role in the day-to-day coordination and implementation of the project, and will work closely with the PI, research staff, students, and collaborators to ensure that study activities are conducted efficiently and in accordance with established research protocols. Responsibilities include: Coordinating the day-to-day implementation of the research project and supporting the overall operation of the research lab; Recruiting, screening, scheduling, and communicating with research participants; Coordinating with collaborating clinics, community organizations, and other recruitment partners; Preparing for and conducting research study visits in accordance with established protocols; Administering behavioral, cognitive, and language-based study procedures; Maintaining accurate participant records, study documentation, and regulatory materials; Coding, cleaning, managing, and performing quality-control checks on research data; Training, scheduling, and supervising undergraduate and graduate research assistants; The ideal candidate will be highly organized, reliable, detail-oriented, and comfortable managing multiple aspects of a research project simultaneously. Strong interpersonal and communication skills are essential, as the position involves frequent interaction with older adults, individuals with cognitive impairment, caregivers, students, clinical collaborators, and community partners. This is a full-time, in-person position. The initial appointment is for one year, with the possibility of renewal contingent upon satisfactory performance and the availability of funding. In compliance with New York City\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $55,000-$60,000 per year. New York University considers factors including, but not limited to, the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in psychology, neuroscience, communication sciences and disorders, speech-language pathology, linguistics, cognitive science, or a related field; Prior experience conducting human-subjects research; Strong organizational and time-management skills; Excellent attention to detail and demonstrated reliability; Strong written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills; Ability to manage multiple tasks and priorities and work both independently and collaboratively; and Experience with research data collection, organization, and management. Preferred Qualifications Research experience with older adults and/or individuals with mild cognitive impairment, Alzheimer\u2019s disease, or other neurological conditions; Experience coordinating participant recruitment, screening, and scheduling; Experience training or supervising undergraduate research assistants; Familiarity with IRB procedures and human-subjects research regulations; Experience with research databases, data-entry systems, and research data management; Experience with quantitative data analysis and statistical software (e.g., R, SPSS, or similar programs); Strong technical and problem-solving skills; Experience with eye-tracking and/or EEG/ERP research, including data collection, preprocessing, or analysis, is preferred. To apply, please submit: A cover letter describing your relevant research experience, organizational and technical skills, and interest in the position A current CV or resume A mandatory one-page evidence-based fit statement Contact information for three academic or professional references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/104967": {"title": "Opportunity Programs Chemistry Adjunct Instructor & Tutor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the Provost: Opportunity Programs at NYU Location: New York City The Opportunity Programs at New York University seek an experienced chemistry adjunct instructor for the Summer 2022 Term. Employment Start Date: Summer 2022 Term Terms: Adjunct Reports To: Associate Director, Academic Services The Opportunity Programs\u2019 non-credit courses (NCCs) are designed to enhance and reinforce students\u2019 understanding of their credit-bearing course material. Responsibilities Confident in teaching a wide range of Chemistry and science courses (Strong preference for General Chemistry I & II, Organic Chemistry I & II, etc, additional courses may include\u00a0Biology I & II,\u00a0Anatomy & Physiology, Physics, Molecular & Cell, Pathophysiology) Please include relevant courses in your resume or cover letter. Work with the Academic Services team to design common objectives aligned with the credit-bearing course and participate in developing and improving delivery of course instructional content. Familiarize self with credit-bearing courses to ensure that subject area support is appropriate in the NCC. On a weekly basis, report all student absences and tardies from courses and/or the NCC to the Associate Director, Academic Services and the appropriate OP student counselor. At least twice a semester, evaluate and communicate progress of assigned students and report to Associate Director, Academic Services. If applicable to adjunct appointment, maintain office hours that meet the students\u2019 needs. If applicable, provide academic tutoring to students individual or in groups. Work with Associate Director, Academic Services and Assistant Director, Counseling as well as OP Instructional Specialists to develop a uniform approach for solving student problems and facilitating the type of instruction that will maximize the students\u2019 academic achievement. Develop with the Academic Services team (Associate Director, Academic Services and OP Instructional Specialists) uniform standards for grading students. Refer disciplinary concerns to the Associate Director, Academic Services and the Assistant Director, Counseling. When individual and/or group meetings with students occur, keep records in the platform specified by the Opportunity Programs, i.e. NYU Connect Highly encouraged to participate in the Opportunity Programs\u2019 required staff meetings, activities, and special events. Work Evaluation Work in this role will be evaluated each semester by course evaluations completed by students at the end of the semester. Tutoring is evaluated on a bi-weekly basis, based on student feedback.\u00a0Classes may be visited by the Associate Director of Academic Services for observation and review purposes. Faculty meetings are held regularly throughout the academic year. Pay Rate Individual tutoring sessions rate is $52.02 per hour.\u00a0Rate varies for Adjunct Faculty hours and group tutoring sessions Position Period Successful applicants may begin immediately. Successful applicants may be renewed each semester Requirements Minimum of enrolled/completed Master\u2019s degree in a science field or current enrollment in a PhD program. Instructional experience of at least 2 years preferred. Past experience in instructing a college level course with similar content. Significant professional experience may substitute this requirement. Experience in teaching on-site and in remote settings. Have a significant history of maintaining a high standard of professionalism, i.e. regularly prepared for class with course material, ready to engage students in a safe learning environment, punctuality, etc. Have knowledge and experience working with diverse student populations and in diverse settings and/or working with traditionally underrepresented students. Preferred experience working in a higher education setting. Available to teach and tutor in person on campus at NYU. Applicants must submit a resume and cover letter. Please include any teaching/tutoring experience as well as relevant course work/work experience\u00a0in your cover letter."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/104968": {"title": "Opportunity Programs Pathophysiology Adjunct Instructor & Tutor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the Provost: Opportunity Programs at NYU Location: New York City The Opportunity Programs at New York University seek an experienced science adjunct instructor for the Summer 2022 Term. Employment Start Date: Summer 2022 Term Terms: Adjunct Reports To: Associate Director, Academic Services The Opportunity Programs\u2019 non-credit courses (NCCs) are designed to enhance and reinforce students\u2019 understanding of their credit-bearing course material. Responsibilities Confident in teaching a wide range of science courses, primarily Pathophysiology and Microbiology (additional courses may include General Chemistry I & II, Biology I & II, Organic Chemistry I & II, Anatomy & Physiology, Physics, Molecular & Cell.) Please include relevant courses in your resume or cover letter. Work with the Academic Services team to design common objectives aligned with the credit-bearing course and participate in developing and improving delivery of course instructional content. Familiarize self with credit-bearing courses to ensure that subject area support is appropriate in the NCC. On a weekly basis, report all student absences and tardies from courses and/or the NCC to the Associate Director, Academic Services and the appropriate OP student counselor. At least twice a semester, evaluate and communicate progress of assigned students and report to Associate Director, Academic Services. If applicable to adjunct appointment, maintain office hours that meet the students\u2019 needs. If applicable, provide academic tutoring to students individual or in groups. Work with Associate Director, Academic Services and Assistant Director, Counseling as well as OP Instructional Specialists to develop a uniform approach for solving student problems and facilitating the type of instruction that will maximize the students\u2019 academic achievement. Develop with the Academic Services team (Associate Director, Academic Services and OP Instructional Specialists) uniform standards for grading students. Refer disciplinary concerns to the Associate Director, Academic Services and the Assistant Director, Counseling. When individual and/or group meetings with students occur, keep records in the platform specified by the Opportunity Programs, i.e. NYU Connect Highly encouraged to participate in the Opportunity Programs\u2019 required staff meetings, activities, and special events. Work Evaluation Work in this role will be evaluated each semester by course evaluations completed by students at the end of the semester. Tutoring is evaluated on a bi-weekly basis, based on student feedback.\u00a0Classes may be visited by the Associate Director of Academic Services for observation and review purposes. Faculty meetings are held regularly throughout the academic year. Pay Rate Individual tutoring sessions rate is $52.02 per hour.\u00a0Rate varies for Adjunct Faculty hours and group tutoring sessions Position Period Successful applicants may begin immediately. Successful applicants may be renewed each semester Requirements Minimum of enrolled/completed Master\u2019s degree in a science field or current enrollment in a PhD program. Instructional experience of at least 2 years preferred. Past experience in instructing a college level course with similar content. Significant professional experience may substitute this requirement. Experience in teaching on-site and in remote settings. Have a significant history of maintaining a high standard of professionalism, i.e. regularly prepared for class with course material, ready to engage students in a safe learning environment, punctuality, etc. Have knowledge and experience working with diverse student populations and in diverse settings and/or working with traditionally underrepresented students. Preferred experience working in a higher education setting. Available to teach and tutor in person on campus at NYU. Applicants must submit a resume and cover letter. Please include any teaching/tutoring experience as well as relevant course work/work experience\u00a0in your cover letter."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/105300": {"title": "Opportunity Programs Biology Adjunct Instructor & Tutor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the Provost: Opportunity Programs at NYU Location: New York City The Opportunity Programs at New York University seek an experienced biology adjunct instructor for the Summer 2022 Term. Employment Start Date: Summer 2022 Term Terms: Adjunct Reports To: Associate Director, Academic Services The Opportunity Programs\u2019 non-credit courses (NCCs) are designed to enhance and reinforce students\u2019 understanding of their credit-bearing course material. Responsibilities Confident in teaching a wide range of Biology and science courses (Strong preference for Molecular & Cell, Biology I & II, Anatomy & Physiology, etc, additional courses may include General Chemistry I & II, Organic Chemistry I & II, Physics, Pathophysiology) Please include relevant courses in your resume or cover letter. Work with the Academic Services team to design common objectives aligned with the credit-bearing course and participate in developing and improving delivery of course instructional content. Familiarize self with credit-bearing courses to ensure that subject area support is appropriate in the NCC. On a weekly basis, report all student absences and tardies from courses and/or the NCC to the Associate Director, Academic Services and the appropriate OP student counselor. At least twice a semester, evaluate and communicate progress of assigned students and report to Associate Director, Academic Services. If applicable to adjunct appointment, maintain office hours that meet the students\u2019 needs. If applicable, provide academic tutoring to students individual or in groups. Work with Associate Director, Academic Services and Assistant Director, Counseling as well as OP Instructional Specialists to develop a uniform approach for solving student problems and facilitating the type of instruction that will maximize the students\u2019 academic achievement. Develop with the Academic Services team (Associate Director, Academic Services and OP Instructional Specialists) uniform standards for grading students. Refer disciplinary concerns to the Associate Director, Academic Services and the Assistant Director, Counseling. When individual and/or group meetings with students occur, keep records in the platform specified by the Opportunity Programs, i.e. NYU Connect Highly encouraged to participate in the Opportunity Programs\u2019 required staff meetings, activities, and special events. Work Evaluation Work in this role will be evaluated each semester by course evaluations completed by students at the end of the semester. Tutoring is evaluated on a bi-weekly basis, based on student feedback.\u00a0Classes may be visited by the Associate Director of Academic Services for observation and review purposes. Faculty meetings are held regularly throughout the academic year. Pay Rate Individual tutoring sessions rate is $52.02 per hour.\u00a0Rate varies for Adjunct Faculty hours and group tutoring sessions Position Period Successful applicants may begin immediately. Successful applicants may be renewed each semester Requirements Minimum of enrolled/completed Master\u2019s degree in a science field, preferably Biology, or current enrollment in a PhD program. Instructional experience of at least 2 years preferred. Past experience in instructing a college level course with similar content. Significant professional experience may substitute this requirement. Experience in teaching on-site and in remote settings. Have a significant history of maintaining a high standard of professionalism, i.e. regularly prepared for class with course material, ready to engage students in a safe learning environment, punctuality, etc. Have knowledge and experience working with diverse student populations and in diverse settings and/or working with traditionally underrepresented students. Preferred experience working in a higher education setting. Available to teach and tutor in person on campus at NYU. Applicants must submit a resume and cover letter. Please include any teaching/tutoring experience as well as relevant course work/work experience\u00a0in your cover letter."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/105961": {"title": "Opportunity Programs Python Adjunct Instructor & Tutor", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the Provost: Opportunity Programs at NYU Location: New York City The Opportunity Programs at New York University seek an experienced Python adjunct instructor for the Summer 2022 Term. Employment Start Date: Summer 2022 Term Terms: Adjunct Reports To: Associate Director, Academic Services The Opportunity Programs\u2019 non-credit courses (NCCs) are designed to enhance and reinforce students\u2019 understanding of their credit-bearing course material. Responsibilities Confident in teaching a wide range of Python and coding\u00a0courses (Strong preference for\u00a0Programming & Problem Solving, Intro to Coding,\u00a0Object-Oriented Programming, etc) Please include relevant courses in your resume or cover letter. Work with the Academic Services team to design common objectives aligned with the credit-bearing course and participate in developing and improving delivery of course instructional content. Familiarize self with credit-bearing courses to ensure that subject area support is appropriate in the NCC. On a weekly basis, report all student absences and tardies from courses and/or the NCC to the Associate Director, Academic Services and the appropriate OP student counselor. At least twice a semester, evaluate and communicate progress of assigned students and report to Associate Director, Academic Services. If applicable to adjunct appointment, maintain office hours that meet the students\u2019 needs. If applicable, provide academic tutoring to students individual or in groups. Work with Associate Director, Academic Services and Assistant Director, Counseling as well as OP Instructional Specialists to develop a uniform approach for solving student problems and facilitating the type of instruction that will maximize the students\u2019 academic achievement. Develop with the Academic Services team (Associate Director, Academic Services and OP Instructional Specialists) uniform standards for grading students. Refer disciplinary concerns to the Associate Director, Academic Services and the Assistant Director, Counseling. When individual and/or group meetings with students occur, keep records in the platform specified by the Opportunity Programs, i.e. NYU Connect Highly encouraged to participate in the Opportunity Programs\u2019 required staff meetings, activities, and special events. Work Evaluation Work in this role will be evaluated each semester by course evaluations completed by students at the end of the semester. Tutoring is evaluated on a bi-weekly basis, based on student feedback.\u00a0Classes may be visited by the Associate Director of Academic Services for observation and review purposes. Faculty meetings are held regularly throughout the academic year. Pay Rate Individual tutoring sessions rate is $52.02 per hour.\u00a0Rate varies for Adjunct Faculty hours and group tutoring sessions Position Period Successful applicants may begin immediately. Successful applicants may be renewed each semester Requirements Minimum of enrolled/completed Master\u2019s degree in a computer science/coding/programming\u00a0field or current enrollment in a PhD program. Instructional experience of at least 2 years preferred. Past experience in instructing a college level course with similar content. Significant professional experience may substitute this requirement. Experience in teaching on-site and in remote settings. Have a significant history of maintaining a high standard of professionalism, i.e. regularly prepared for class with course material, ready to engage students in a safe learning environment, punctuality, etc. Have knowledge and experience working with diverse student populations and in diverse settings and/or working with traditionally underrepresented students. Preferred experience working in a higher education setting. Available to teach and tutor in person on campus at NYU. Applicants must submit a resume and cover letter. Please include any teaching/tutoring experience as well as relevant course work/work experience\u00a0in your cover letter."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194765": {"title": "Postdoctoral Fellowship in Operations Management (Prof. Chen Chen)", "description": "Posted: Sep 30, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Business: Operations & Business Analytics Location: Shanghai, China  Prof. Chen Chen , Assistant Professor of Operations and Business Analytics at NYU Shanghai, invites applications for the postdoctoral research fellow position in operations management beginning in Summer 2027 or Fall 2027. The Fellow will work under the supervision of Prof. Chen and Prof. Jiawei Zhang , Michael Armellino Professor in Business and Professor of Information, Operations and Management Sciences at New York University's Leonard N. Stern School of Business. The Fellow is expected to demonstrate a strong commitment to research excellence and to contribute to a broad portfolio of operations management projects led by the two faculty members. These projects span a wide range of topics, with primary emphasis on\u2014and intersections among\u2014market and mechanism design, revenue management and resource allocation, stochastic modeling, optimization, and data-driven methods. The Fellow\u2019s responsibilities include the following: Collaborate on impactful theoretical and practical projects and publish papers in top outlets; Present research at academic forums; Collect data and conduct empirical/numerical analysis; Engage in the intellectual environment of NYU Shanghai through workshops, presentations, seminars, and collaboration with faculty and other researchers; Fulfill other duties as determined by the supervisor. The position is for an initial appointment of one year, with the possibility of extension for another year contingent upon satisfactory performance . The position will be affiliated with NYU Shanghai and based in Shanghai, China. Opportunities for research visits and collaboration in New York will also be available. The appointment start date for non-Chinese applicants is subject to immigration regulations. Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions. Candidates should have (or be close to completing) a Ph.D. in operations management, operations research, computer science, economics, statistics, applied mathematics, or a closed related field by the start date, and have a strong theoretical background and record of accomplishment in research on similar topics; Candidates should have strong research potential and evidence of scholarly excellence; Candidates should have received solid methodological training (theory and/or data-driven methods, as appropriate) and are able to collaborate effectively and communicate research clearly; Candidates must be within\u00a0three\u00a0years of their PhD degree conferral when their fellowship starts at NYU Shanghai. Interested applicants are asked to submit a curriculum vitae, cover letter addressing research and academic interests, a research statement (1-2 pages), and electronic copies of one or more research papers (published or in progress). Additionally, applicants will be prompted to enter the names and email addresses of at least two referees. Each referee will be contacted to upload a reference letter through Interfolio. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you have any questions on application, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . For further questions on the position details, please contact Prof. Chen Chen at cc8029@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194860": {"title": "Tenured/Tenure-Track Positions in Technology, Operations, and Statistics", "description": "Posted: Oct 01, 2026 Deadline: Nov 15, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Business: Operations & Business Analytics Location: Shanghai, China NYU Shanghai is currently inviting applications for positions at the rank of Assistant Professor in Technology, Operations, and Statistics, expected to start in the Fall Semester of 2027. For further details about the research centers and institutes at NYU Shanghai, please visit this webpage . Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions with respect to research start-up funds and compensation, and they include housing subsidies and educational subsidies for children. Faculty may in certain cases have the opportunity to spend time at NYU New York and other sites of the NYU Global Network, engaging in both research and teaching. We seek candidates who have completed a Ph.D. in Information Systems, Operations Management, Operations Research, Statistics, or a closely related discipline and demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in scholarly research and teaching. Associate and full professor applicants should have a proven track record of exceptional scholarship in Technology, Operations, or Statistics as well as of institution building and service. We invite applications from candidates in any field and have a special interest in those with expertise in healthcare and in AI for business. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of research, statement of teaching interests (no more than three pages each), and at least one writing sample via Interfolio. Applicants are asked to identify one of the following as their main research area on the provided cover letter: (1) Information Systems, (2) Operations Management, or (3) Statistics. Additionally, applicants will be prompted to enter the names and email addresses of at least three evaluators. Each referee will be contacted to upload a confidential letter of recommendation through Interfolio. Applications may be received until November 15, 2026 . Members of the search committee will be available to meet with prospective applicants during the INFORMS Annual Meeting in San Francisco, CA, between November 1-4, 2026. Prospective applicants who are interested in meeting with the search committee are encouraged to complete their applications by October 20, 2026. If you have any questions, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/172947": {"title": "Legal Fellow (s)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law: Institute for Policy Integrity Location: New York, New York About the Institute for Policy Integrity Housed at the New York University School of Law, the Institute Policy Integrity (Policy Integrity) is a non-partisan think tank dedicated to improving the quality of government decisionmaking. Our principal focus is U.S. energy and environmental policy, with a particular emphasis on climate change. We produce original scholarly research in the fields of economics, law, and regulatory policy. We also advocate for reform before courts and agencies. Policy Integrity\u2019s interdisciplinary mission focuses on the rational use of economic analysis in government decisionmaking and how tools like cost\u2010benefit analysis and distributional analysis can improve policy design. Policy Integrity\u2019s docket covers a range of issue areas, with a particular emphasis on federal environmental protections and federal\u2010 and state\u2010level electricity policies, as well as a steady stream of projects on public health and consumer protection. Policy Integrity\u2019s successful advocacy outcomes include ensuring that agencies are required to consider the lost social benefits and other harms of any deregulatory actions; guiding state utility regulators on the need to value environmental externalities in energy policy decisions; improving energy market designs and planning decisions; and convincing both federal and state courts to endorse the use of the social cost of carbon for monetizing avoided climate damages. Responsibilities Legal Fellows may be assigned projects across a range of substantive areas or may be tasked to specialize in a key area depending on the fellow\u2019s experience and Policy Integrity\u2019s priorities. Legal Fellows work with Policy Integrity\u2019s economists, lawyers, and policy analysts to: draft amicus briefs to the Supreme Court, D.C. Circuit, and other courts in major policy cases; draft regulatory comments, petitions for rulemakings, and other formal submissions to federal and state agencies including state Public Utility Commissions, the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission, the Environmental Protection Agency, the Department of Interior, and the Department of Health and Human Services; advise other advocacy organizations on how to navigate regulatory impact analyses; supervise the work of NYU Law School students in the Regulatory Policy Clinic; and publish academic articles and op\u2010eds. Policy Integrity is committed to a culture in which all people are welcomed, respected, supported, and valued to fully participate. We welcome and encourage applications from diverse applicants, and we consider multiple aspects of diversity. NYU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and is committed to a policy of equal treatment and opportunity in every aspect of its recruitment and hiring process without regard to age, alienage, caregiver status, childbirth, citizenship status, color, creed, disability, domestic violence victim status, ethnicity, familial status, gender and/or gender identity or expression, marital status, military status, national origin, parental status, partnership status, predisposing genetic characteristics, pregnancy, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, unemployment status, veteran status, or any other legally protected basis. Women, racial and ethnic minorities, persons of minority sexual orientation or gender identity, individuals with disabilities, and veterans are encouraged to apply for vacant positions at all levels. Benefits and Terms of Employment In compliance with New York City\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $88,000\u2013$104,000. NYU considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, applicant\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. NYU offers an excellent,\u202fcomprehensive benefits package\u202fincluding generous paid time off and retirement contributions.\u202f Information about benefits can be viewed here. Partial relocation assistance will be provided. Policy Integrity staff members also have access to academic enrichment opportunities at NYU School of Law, including some faculty workshops, colloquia, and other scholarly forums. Policy Integrity follows a hybrid schedule that requires three days in office per week. Policy Integrity is looking to hire at least one legal fellow who can start immediately or at some point during the 2025\u20132026 academic year; all others will have the option to start at the beginning of the next academic year in September 2026. Requirements and Qualifications J.D., with superior academic achievement; 0\u20133 years of legal experience; Clerkships, litigation experience, legislative experience, or advanced degrees in relevant disciplines are valued, but not necessary; and Applicants\u2019 written materials will be reviewed to assess their professionalism, independent thinking, and persuasive writing skills, as well as their ability and willingness to absorb new subject matters in law and policy. To Apply Applicants should submit: (1) a cover letter detailing your interest in the position and your relevant experience; (2) a resume; (3) law school and undergraduate transcripts; (4) two writing samples, with a preference for independently authored briefs, memoranda, articles, blog posts, public comments, reports, or other relevant writings (including excerpts) of no more than 20 pages in length; (5) a brief statement (less than one page) on how your background or experiences would enhance the diversity, inclusivity, and perspectives of Policy Integrity; and (6) the names and contact information of three references. Applicants should send all materials either to policyintegrity.jobs@gmail.com, with \u201cLegal Fellow\u201d in the subject line, or apply via this Interfolio post. Application review will begin November 1st, 2025, after this date applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until all open positions are filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192896": {"title": "Associate Professor of Finance, Tenure/Tenure-Track - Stern at NYUAD", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Stern at NYUAD: AD_Stern at NYUAD Finance Location: Abu Dhabi NYU Abu Dhabi , in partnership with NYU Stern , invites applications for a position at the rank of Associate Professor (tenured or tenure-track) in Finance for the Stern School of Business at NYU Abu Dhabi (Stern at NYUAD). This search is aimed at scholars at or near the tenure decision point in their careers \u2014 typically candidates who have recently been awarded tenure, are currently under tenure review, or have an equivalent publication record several years post-PhD. Candidates should have an established record of publication in top-tier finance and economics journals and should be positioned to mentor junior faculty and serve as a senior research presence within a growing department. Responsibilities include conducting research at the highest level, teaching and developing core and elective courses in Finance and related areas, and contributing to the research mentorship of junior colleagues. Stern at NYUAD offers world-class compensation and benefits packages, and a competitive teaching load. About Stern at NYUAD In 2023, NYU Abu Dhabi and NYU Stern entered into an agreement to create the Stern School of Business at NYU Abu Dhabi (Stern at NYUAD), an extension of NYU Stern in the emirate of Abu Dhabi. Stern at NYUAD will leverage NYU Stern\u2019s leadership in business education and research, and NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s established academic excellence and local expertise, to offer world-class business programming in the heart of the UAE that will reach all business centers of the world. About NYU Stern NYU Stern School of Business was established in 1900 as one of the first business schools in the United States. Located in Greenwich Village and deeply connected with the dynamic global business city for which it is named, NYU Stern is one of the world\u2019s premier management education schools and research centers. It has an internationally diverse faculty and student body, a broad portfolio of programs at the graduate, undergraduate, and executive levels, and a welcoming community that inspires its members to embrace and lead change in a rapidly transforming world. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching.Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for faculty/academic staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to faculty/academic staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. This position requires: A Ph.D. in Finance, Economics, or a related discipline. A demonstrated record of publication in top-tier finance journals (e.g., Journal of Finance, Journal of Financial Economics, Review of Financial Studies, or equivalent field-leading outlets), consistent with a strong tenure case at a research-intensive institution. Demonstrated excellence in graduate or MBA-level teaching. Evidence of, or clear potential for, mentoring junior faculty and contributing to departmental research leadership. To apply for this position, please submit the following items: Curriculum Vitae (CV), including a full publication list Cover Letter Teaching Statement Research Statement Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Three (3) representative publications, including at least one from a top-tier finance or economics journal Applications received before December 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. For questions about this position, please email faculty.affairs@sternad.nyu.edu . NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192897": {"title": "Open-rank Professor of Entrepreneurship, Tenure - Stern at NYUAD", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Stern at NYUAD: AD_Stern at NYUAD Management Location: Abu Dhabi NYU Abu Dhabi , in partnership with NYU Stern , invites applications for a tenured or tenure-track open-rank faculty position in the field of Entrepreneurship for the Stern School of Business at NYU Abu Dhabi (Stern at NYUAD). We seek seasoned scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to transformative teaching and innovative research, and who embody the global society in which we live. Associate and full professors with a proven track record of excellent scholarship and business school teaching in new venture creation, business models, and entrepreneurial finance will be given priority for this position. Responsibilities include conducting rigorous academic research and teaching/developing core and elective courses in entrepreneurship and related areas of expertise. A strong track record of partnership building with startup incubators, investors, and business leaders will be prefered. Stern at NYUAD offers world-class compensation and benefits packages, and a competitive teaching load. About Stern at NYUAD In 2023, NYU Abu Dhabi and NYU Stern entered into an agreement to create the Stern School of Business at NYU Abu Dhabi (Stern at NYUAD), an extension of NYU Stern in the emirate of Abu Dhabi. Stern at NYUAD will leverage NYU Stern\u2019s leadership in business education and research, and NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s established academic excellence and local expertise, to offer world-class business programming in the heart of the UAE that will reach all business centers of the world. About NYU Stern NYU Stern School of Business was established in 1900 as one of the first business schools in the United States. Located in Greenwich Village and deeply connected with the dynamic global business city for which it is named, NYU Stern is one of the world\u2019s premier management education schools and research centers. It has an internationally diverse faculty and student body, a broad portfolio of programs at the graduate, undergraduate, and executive levels, and a welcoming community that inspires its members to embrace and lead change in a rapidly transforming world. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching.Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for faculty/academic staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to faculty/academic staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. This position requires a PhD in Entrepreneurship, Management,\u00a0 Business Administration or a related field. 5-10+ years of academic experience are expected, along with a record of top academic publications. To apply for this position, please submit the following items: \u25cf\u00a0 Curriculum Vitae (CV) \u25cf\u00a0 Cover Letter \u25cf\u00a0 Teaching Statement \u25cf\u00a0 Research Statement \u25cf\u00a0 Recent teaching evaluations (if available) \u25cf\u00a0 Three (3) representative publications/writing samples The applications received by Dec 7, 2026 will receive full consideration. For questions about this position, please email faculty.affairs@sternad.nyu.edu. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195010": {"title": "Associate/Full Professor of Management - Stern at NYUAD", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Stern at NYUAD: AD_Stern at NYUAD Management Location: Abu Dhabi NYU Abu Dhabi , in partnership with NYU Stern , invites applications for a tenured or tenure-track position at the rank of Associate or Full Professor in Management for the Stern School of Business at NYU Abu Dhabi (Stern at NYUAD). We are seeking proven research scholars in any core area of management, including Strategy, Organizational Behavior, and Organizational Theory. Candidates should have an established record of publication in top-tier journals and should be positioned to mentor junior faculty and serve as a senior research presence within a growing department. Applicants without a substantial record of publication in leading journals will not be considered. Stern at NYUAD offers world-class compensation and benefits packages, and a competitive teaching load. About Stern at NYUAD In 2023, NYU Abu Dhabi and NYU Stern entered into an agreement to create the Stern School of Business at NYU Abu Dhabi (Stern at NYUAD), an extension of NYU Stern in the emirate of Abu Dhabi. Stern at NYUAD will leverage NYU Stern\u2019s leadership in business education and research, and NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s established academic excellence and local expertise, to offer world-class business programming in the heart of the UAE that will reach all business centers of the world. About NYU Stern NYU Stern School of Business was established in 1900 as one of the first business schools in the United States. Located in Greenwich Village and deeply connected with the dynamic global business city for which it is named, NYU Stern is one of the world\u2019s premier management education schools and research centers. It has an internationally diverse faculty and student body, a broad portfolio of programs at the graduate, undergraduate, and executive levels, and a welcoming community that inspires its members to embrace and lead change in a rapidly transforming world.\u00a0 NYU Stern Center for Sustainable Business manages a specialization and concentration in sustainable business for undergraduates and graduates, as well as executives and will be a resource to NYU Abu Dhabi. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching.Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for faculty/academic staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to faculty/academic staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. This position requires: A Ph.D. degree in Management-related fields, including, but not limited to, Psychology, Leadership, Organizational Behavior, Organizational Theory, Economics, and Strategy A demonstrated record of publication in top-tier journals Demonstrated excellence in graduate or MBA-level teaching. Evidence of, or clear potential for, mentoring junior faculty and contributing to departmental research leadership. Candidates without a top-tier publication record, or who are within the first years of their first academic appointment, are asked not to apply to this posting. To apply for this position, please submit the following items via Interfolio: Curriculum Vitae (CV), including a full publication list Cover Letter Recent teaching evaluations (if available) Three (3) representative publications, including at least one from a top-tier management, psychology or economics journal. Applications received before Nov 2, 2026 will receive full consideration. For questions about this position, please email faculty.affairs@sternad.nyu.edu . NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/154275": {"title": "Post Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: CUSP - Center for Urban Science  + Progress Location: Brooklyn, NY  FULL TIME RESEARCH ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering mLab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Full time Research Associate to work with Prof. Danny Yuxing Huang. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. mLab conducts cutting edge research on usable security and privacy; we have published in top tier conferences, such as SOUPS, CHI, IMWUT, and USENIX Security. Expectations Candidates will be responsible for conducting research in the field of usable security and privacy. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in systems, networking, usable security and privacy as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience on systems and HCI is desired. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $48,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is expected to be: September 16, 2024 This position is expected to be for 12 months. Candidates should have at least an MS in Computer Science and Engineering or a related field and at least a summer of related industry internship experience. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. A list of publications if any All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: dhuang@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/162700": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer: Food, Culture, and Globalization", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Tulsa Location: NYU Tulsa NYU Tulsa is seeking a local, part-time instructor to teach \u201cFood, Culture, and Globalization\u201d for Summer term, May 19 - July 16 2025. The course is a 2-credit offering sponsored by NYU\u2019s Steinhardt School of Culture, Education, and Human Development. The course is taught in English, and there is a 6 1/2 week condensed summer schedule. The instructor will design the course, prepare for all classes, teach, grade student work, and hold at least one office hour per week. Candidates should be able to demonstrate their teaching experience. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life at the NYU Tulsa campus. Course Description Food, Culture, and Globalization utilizes an interdisciplinary and transdisciplinary approach to explore Tulsa's vibrant cultural landscape and the city's current transformation in food systems. The course investigates how produce, people, and animals have interacted to sustain life in cities, including urban Indigenous communities such as Tulsa. It also examines how these interactions have evolved and how changes in food production, distribution, and preparation impact human health, knowledge systems, livelihoods, social relations, and the natural environment. Through lectures, readings, and activities beyond the traditional classroom, students will learn about Tulsa's unique food culture, which includes food sovereignty movements, racial history and inequality, farm-to-table community dinners, food trucks, and pop-up kitchens. Students will situate their experiences within broader theoretical and conceptual frameworks related to culture, food, and power, producing new knowledge together. About NYU and NYU Tulsa: Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private research university in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 13 additional global academic centers on 6 continents, as well as sponsored research projects in more than 100 countries as of 2024. NYU Tulsa is NYU's 13th global academic center. A dynamic and diverse city with a rich and complex history, Tulsa offers an abundance of opportunities for NYU students and faculty alike, whether as a destination for a study away semester, a summer internship, a short-term program, or research and creative work. If you would like to learn more about our program please visit NYU Tulsa . Applicants should have a PhD in Food Studies, Anthropology, Sociology, History, or a related field. We seek candidates who can integrate interdisciplinary perspectives in teaching the course. Ideally, we seek candidates with two to three years of experience teaching at the university level; experience teaching American undergraduates is helpful. In some instances, significant professional expertise and experience in the relevant field may be a suitable substitute for teaching experience. Candidates with extensive experience in food sovereignty, food journalism, culinary arts and food justice. Salary will be based on equivalent part-time faculty rates for the course and location\u2014other terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicants. Candidates should have the right to live and work in the United States prior to appointment. NYU's Global Programs & University Life organization is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to being a welcoming campus community that reflects and enacts the values of inclusion, diversity, belonging, equity, and accessibility that inform academic excellence. Employees in this organization are expected to contribute to diverse, equitable, inclusive, and accessible learning and working environments for our students, staff, and faculty. All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at apply.interfolio.com/162700 If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . Applications must include: (1) a CV, (2) a cover letter, (3) a sample syllabus for the course or list of topics, readings, and excursions. In the letter, please highlight any relevant experience you have in teaching and/or research relevant for the course. Please direct any questions to tulsa.academics@nyu.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/168608": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer: \"Across the Divide: Constructive Policymaking at the Subnational Level\"", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Tulsa Location: NYU Tulsa NYU Tulsa is seeking a local, part-time instructor to teach \u201cAcross the Divide: Navigating Polarization through Subnational Politics and Policymaking\u201d for Fall 2025 (August 27th - December 4th). The course is sponsored by NYU\u2019s College of Arts and Science and the Wilf Family Department of Politics. The course is taught in English, and there are 15 in-person instructional weeks. Students will be a mix of upperclassmen. The schedule will be discussed with the final candidates. The instructor will design the course, prepare for all classes, teach, grade student work, and hold at least one office hour per week. Candidates should be able to demonstrate their teaching experience. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life at the NYU Tulsa campus. Course Details Standoffs, crises, and dysfunction have become signature features of U.S. politics at the national level, fueled by increasingly acute partisan and ideological conflict. But in the American federal system, states, cities, and other subnational governments have long served as \u201claboratories of democracy\u201d and sites of policy innovation \u2014 a phenomenon that persists even in our sharply divided era, with complex and sometimes surprising results. This course introduces students to political science scholarship on the historical, cultural, economic, and media dynamics that shape political polarization in the United States, along with its implications for policymaking at the federal, state, and local levels. It then examines how those who seek to effect change navigate polarization at the subnational level, often in seemingly inhospitable environments. Emphasis is placed on practical approaches and strategies that conservatives, progressives, and others are using to successfully work across political divides. To the greatest extent possible, class workshops and projects will focus on the experiences of local officials, policy experts, advocates, and NGOs in Tulsa and Oklahoma to identify best practices for fostering constructive dialogue and compromise to address tough policy problems in challenging environments. About NYU and NYU Tulsa: Founded in 1831, New York University is the largest private research university in the United States. The University has degree-granting campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai and operates 13 additional global academic centers on 6 continents, as well as sponsored research projects in more than 100 countries as of 2024. NYU Tulsa is NYU's 13th global academic center. A dynamic and diverse city with a rich and complex history, Tulsa offers an abundance of opportunities for NYU students and faculty alike, whether as a destination for a study away semester, a summer internship, a short-term program, or research and creative work. If you would like to learn more about our program please visit NYU Tulsa . Applicants should have a terminal degree in Politics, or a related field. We seek candidates who can integrate interdisciplinary perspectives in teaching the course. Ideally, we seek candidates with two to three years of experience teaching at the university level; experience teaching American undergraduates is helpful. In some instances, significant professional expertise and experience in the relevant field may be a suitable substitute for teaching experience. Salary will be based on equivalent part-time faculty rates for the course and location, and other terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. The successful candidate should have the right to live and work in the United States prior to appointment. NYU's Global Programs & University Life organization is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to being a welcoming campus community that reflects and enacts the values of inclusion, diversity, belonging, equity, and accessibility that inform academic excellence. Employees in this organization are expected to contribute to diverse, equitable, inclusive, and accessible learning and working environments for our students, staff, and faculty All candidates must submit the following: (1) a cover letter; (2) a current C.V. and (3) sample syllabus or list of topics and readings All applications must be made online through Interfolio ByCommittee at apply.interfolio.com/168608. If you are new to using Interfolio Faculty Search you may find the following guide useful: Job Applicant's Guide to Interfolio Faculty Search . If you have any questions you may reach out to nyu.tulsa@nyu.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/169402": {"title": "Interactive Media Design Lecturer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Tulsa Location: NYU Tulsa NYU Tulsa is seeking a local, part-time instructor to teach \u201cIntroduction to Game Development\u201d in Spring 2026 (January 21 \u2013 May 8, 2026). The course is sponsored by the NYU Tandon School of Engineering and is part of the Integrated Design & Media curriculum. The course is taught in English, with 15 in-person instructional weeks. Students will include a mix of undergraduate and interdisciplinary majors. The course schedule will be determined in consultation with the final candidate. The instructor will be responsible for designing the course while following the NY version of the course, preparing and delivering all classes, grading student work, and holding at least one office hour per week. Candidates should have demonstrated experience teaching game design or interactive media. We are especially interested in candidates who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life at NYU Tulsa. Course Details Introduction to Game Development This course introduces students to the principles of 2D and 3D computer game design. Students will explore a wide range of game types and analyze their conceptual building blocks. Through a staged sequence of assignments, students will develop a fully worked-out design for an original game. Evaluation will be based on factors such as storyline, visual style, gameplay, and conceptual originality. For games with educational or instructional objectives, clarity and effectiveness for the target audience will also be assessed. Students are expected to build toward a final project through preparatory assignments over the course of the semester. We are seeking candidates with a mix of education/training and creative or professional practice as well as teaching/instructional experience. A terminal degree (PhD, JD, MFA, MPS, MBA) in Interactive Media Design, Game Design, Digital Media, Computer Science, or a related field, would be a plus for qualified candidates. Candidates who bring interdisciplinary and project-based approaches to teaching are encouraged to apply. Ideally, applicants will have two to three years of university-level teaching experience; experience working with undergraduates is especially valuable. In some cases, significant professional experience in the game design or interactive media industries may be considered in place of academic teaching experience. Salary will be based on part-time faculty rates for the course and location. Other terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. Candidates must be eligible to live and work in the United States at the time of appointment. All applications must be submitted via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/169402. All candidates must submit the following: cover letter current CV If you have any questions, please contact: tulsa.academics@nyu.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/169414": {"title": "Sustainability as Competitive Advantage Lecturer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Tulsa Location: NYU Tulsa NYU Tulsa is seeking a local, part-time instructor to teach \u201cSustainability as Competitive Advantage\u201d in Fall 2026 (August 31 \u2013 December 11, 2026). The course is sponsored by the NYU Stern School of Business and contributes to the Sustainable Business concentration , which prepares students to embed sustainability into core business strategy and create long-term value across economic, social, and environmental dimensions. The course is taught in English and includes 15 in-person instructional weeks. Students will include a mix of undergraduate business students and interdisciplinary majors. The course schedule will be determined in consultation with the final candidate. The instructor will be responsible for preparing and delivering all classes, (based on the Sustainability As Competitive Advantage syllabus, cases, and decks which will be provided), grading student work, and holding at least one office hour per week. Candidates should have demonstrated experience teaching or working in sustainable business strategy, corporate responsibility, environmental, social, and governance (ESG) metrics, or related areas. We are especially interested in candidates who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life at NYU Tulsa. Course Details This course explores how forward-thinking companies leverage sustainability not just as a compliance or ethical concern but as a strategic business advantage. Students will analyze how integrating environmental and social goals into a company\u2019s operations, value chain, and brand identity can drive innovation, enhance stakeholder trust, and improve financial performance. Topics may include circular economy, ESG reporting, stakeholder capitalism, sustainable supply chains, and climate risk. Through case studies, group projects, and applied analysis, students will learn to assess sustainability strategies and develop business solutions that balance profit with purpose. This course supports the Sustainable Business concentration within Stern\u2019s Business and Society Program, which offers over 20 electives focused on the role of business in social and environmental change. Qualifications We are seeking candidates with a blend of academic preparation, industry experience, and teaching ability. A terminal degree (MBA, PhD, JD, MPA, MPS, or related) in sustainable business, environmental studies, public policy, business strategy, or a relevant field is preferred. Candidates with professional experience in sustainability, CSR, ESG consulting, or sustainable innovation are strongly encouraged to apply. Applicants should demonstrate the ability to teach with an interdisciplinary and applied approach. Ideally, candidates will have two to three years of university-level teaching experience; experience working with undergraduate students is especially valued. In some cases, substantial industry experience may be considered in lieu of academic teaching experience. Salary will be based on part-time faculty rates for the course and location. Other terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. Candidates must be eligible to live and work in the United States at the time of appointment. All candidates must submit the following: A cover letter A current CV If you have any questions, please contact: tulsa.academics@nyu.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/173350": {"title": "Energy Industry Case Study Lecturer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Tulsa Location: NYU Tulsa NYU Tulsa is seeking a local, part-time instructor to teach \u201che American Clean Energy Industry: Case Study Tulsa\u201d in Spring 2026 (January 21 \u2013 May 8, 2026). The course is sponsored by NYU Abu Dhabi and is part of the Engineering curriculum. The course is taught in English, with 15 in-person instructional weeks. Students will include a mix of undergraduate and interdisciplinary majors. The course schedule will be determined in consultation with the final candidate. The instructor will be responsible for designing the course in close collaboration with NYU Abu Dhabi, preparing and delivering all classes, grading student work, and holding at least one office hour per week. Candidates should have demonstrated experience the energy industry. We are especially interested in candidates who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life at NYU Tulsa. Course Details This course explores the global and American energy industries and the shift from fossil fuels to green energy through scientific, economic, policy, and ethical lenses. Emphasizing Tulsa\u2019s significant role in the American energy industry, it covers the structure and history of the energy sector, renewable energy technologies, and the economics of transitioning to green energy. The course discusses how politics, public policy, international agreements, and ethical considerations shape the energy landscape, focusing on Tulsa as a case study to examine local impacts and strategies. In a major assignment for the class, the students will collaborate on a semester-long industry challenge with a local energy-focused company. We are seeking candidates with a mix of education/training and professional practice, as well as teaching/instructional experience. A terminal degree (PhD, JD, MPS, MBA) in Engineering, Energy Management, Sustainability, or a related field, would be a plus for qualified candidates. Candidates who bring interdisciplinary and project-based approaches to teaching are encouraged to apply. Ideally, applicants will have two to three years of university-level teaching experience; experience working with undergraduates is especially valuable. In some cases, significant professional experience in the energy industry may be considered in place of academic teaching experience. Salary will be based on part-time faculty rates for the course and location. Other terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. Candidates must be eligible to live and work in the United States at the time of appointment. All applications must be submitted via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/173350. All candidates must submit the following: cover letter current CV If you have any questions, please contact: tulsa.academics@nyu.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/186913": {"title": "Sustainability/Business Lecturer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: NYU Tulsa Location: NYU Tulsa NYU Tulsa is seeking a local, part-time instructor to teach \u201cSustainability for Competitive Advantage\u201d in Spring 2027. The course is sponsored by the NYU Stern School of Business and contributes to the Sustainable Business concentration, which prepares students to embed sustainability into core business strategy and create long-term value across economic, social, and environmental dimensions. Students will include a mix of undergraduate business students and interdisciplinary majors. The course schedule will be determined in consultation with the final candidate. The instructor will be responsible for preparing and delivering all classes, (based on the Sustainability forCompetitive Advantage syllabus, cases, and decks which will be provided), grading student work, and holding at least one office hour per week. Candidates should have demonstrated experience teaching or working in sustainable business strategy, corporate sustainability, environmental, social, and governance (ESG) metrics, or related areas. We are especially interested in candidates who can contribute to the diversity of intellectual life at NYU Tulsa. We are seeking candidates with a blend of academic preparation, industry experience, and teaching ability. Candidates should have extensive industry/professional experience in sustainable business, environmental studies, public policy, operations, management, finance, business strategy, or a relevant field and/or a relevant terminal degree (e.g., PhD, JD, MBA, MPA, MPS). Candidates with professional experience in sustainability, CSR, ESG consulting, or sustainable innovation are strongly encouraged to apply. Applicants should demonstrate the ability to teach with an interdisciplinary and applied approach. Ideally, candidates will have two to three years of university-level teaching experience; experience working with undergraduate students is especially valued. In some cases, substantial industry experience may be considered in lieu of academic teaching experience. Salary will be based on part-time faculty rates for the course and location. Other terms and conditions will be discussed with the successful applicant. Candidates must be eligible to live and work in the United States at the time of appointment. All application must be submitted via Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/186913. All candidates must submit the following: A cover letter A current CV Potential Syllabus Topics"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195395": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the Provost: Center for the Study of Antisemitism Location: New York, NY  Call for Applications The Center for the Study of Antisemitism at New York University invites applications for the position of Postdoctoral Associate in Antisemitism Studies beginning September 1, 2027. The fellowship is a one-year research position with the possibility of renewal for another year. Eligible applicants must have their Ph.D in hand by August 31, 2027, and must have completed their Ph.D within the last five years prior to the start date of September 1, 2027. Candidates whose research focuses on any aspect of historical or contemporary antisemitism and working within any of the relevant disciplines of the humanities or social sciences are welcome to apply. Candidates must have already demonstrated a record of original and compelling research in their area of specialization and an ability to work with the relevant languages and skill sets. We welcome applicants whose work opens up new vistas in the field whether by examining local case studies, regional or global trends, political or legal developments, new or underexplored source material, or novel theoretical approaches. Position Description The Postdoctoral Associate in Antisemitism Studies is a research position with no formal teaching duties. This is a hybrid position with 2-3 days in the office. The Associate will attend regularly scheduled meetings with Center staff and faculty. The Associate will also serve as a scholarly resource for undergraduate and graduate students across campus in support of student research as appropriate. The Associate will deliver a lecture or take a leading role in an event organized in the spring semester highlighting the research undertaken over the course of the academic year. The Associate will participate actively in the activities of the Center for the Study of Antisemitism and is encouraged to make full use of the scholarly resources available throughout the University, such as those in the Skirball Department of Hebrew and Judaic Studies, the Taub Center for Israel Studies, the Goldstein Goren Center for American Jewish History, or other relevant programs. Associates will be able to take advantage of research materials available at the NYU Libraries, the New York Public Library, the Center for Jewish History, and beyond. Compensation: In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $65,000 to $75,000 depending on the candidate's experience and qualifications. Must have received Ph.D by August 31, 2027, and or have completed their Ph.D within the last five years prior to the start date of September 1, 2027. Previous research experience focusing on any aspect of historical or contemporary antisemitism and working within any of the relevant disciplines of the humanities or social sciences Eligible to work in the United States Applications will be accepted no later than November 30, 2026 at 11:59pm EST. Applicants should submit the following as separate document through the Interfolio portal: Cover letter including a description of the research specialization and the specific research proposal to be undertaken at the Center for the Study of Antisemitism Curriculum vitae One sample of recent scholarly work such as a dissertation chapter or published article Applications must include two academic letters of recommendation and an optional third professional letter of recommendation. All materials (those submitted by the candidate and letters of recommendation submitted on the candidate\u2019s behalf) should be submitted via Interfolio"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/176086": {"title": "Dean of Social Science", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Provost Office Location: Abu Dhabi The Opportunity New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD) seeks an accomplished scholar and visionary leader to serve as its next Dean of the Division of Social Science. This is a rare opportunity to shape a dynamic academic division at a pivotal moment in the university\u2019s evolution. The Dean will lead a vibrant community of scholars who advance foundational and applied research on some of the most pressing issues of our time - ranging from inequality, governance, and social change - within a highly international and interdisciplinary environment. The Division The Social Science Division is home to over 80 full-time faculty, including a Nobel Laureate in Economics and a vibrant community of post-doctoral researchers in Economics, Political Science, Social Research & Public Policy, Business Organizations & Society, and Legal Studies. The Division offers five undergraduate majors as well as graduate programs. Faculty conduct world-class research that is both theoretically rigorous and globally engaged, with collaborations spanning every continent and close ties to policy and practice in the UAE and beyond. The Division\u2019s research strength is internationally recognized - Economics at NYUAD ranks 4th in Asia in Research Papers in Economics, and Political Science ranks 6th globally according to a recent study. Its faculty regularly publish in leading disciplinary and interdisciplinary journals. The Dean will have a unique opportunity to capitalize on this momentum: building on the Division\u2019s existing strengths in graduate education, interdisciplinary collaboration, and the global and local visibility of its research and teaching. About NYU Abu Dhabi Founded in partnership with New York University and the Emirate of Abu Dhabi, NYUAD is an extraordinary achievement in global higher education. Since opening in 2010, it has become one of the most selective universities in the world with a 2025 admit rate of 2.7 percent, enrolling students from over 120 countries, NYU Abu Dhabi has produced 24 Rhodes Scholars. Its more than 370 faculty members include 4 Nobel laureates. The faculty are situated across five divisions spanning Arts and Humanities, Science, Engineering, Social Science, and Stern at NYUAD.\u00a0 They are drawn from leading institutions worldwide. NYUAD\u2019s research and teaching are deeply integrated with NYU\u2019s global network, offering opportunities for collaboration and mobility across New York and Shanghai. The Role The Dean reports to the Provost and serves as a key member of NYUAD\u2019s senior leadership team. As the Division\u2019s chief academic officer, the Dean will provide strategic leadership for its faculty, curricula, and research agenda, championing excellence and innovation in both research and pedagogy. The role also carries significant responsibility for fostering an accessible and collaborative academic community and for representing the Division\u2019s strengths to partners within and beyond NYU. As part of the senior administration, the Dean must work effectively and collegially with other deans as well as academic and administrative leaders. In addition to engaging with members of the Division of Social Science, they must also be able to work with faculty across the institution. The Dean must be committed to two-way communication, to fostering trust, and to building community at the campus, national, and international levels. Specifically, the Dean will be a catalyst in addressing the following key challenges and opportunities for the Division: Recruiting and retaining an excellent, collaborative, and engaged faculty Enhancing interdisciplinary research, scholarship, and creative activity Delivering programs that maximize the excellence and societal impact of NYUAD Engaging the Abu Dhabi and UAE community Cultivating and promoting the values and vision of NYUAD and the NYU Global Network The Candidate Candidates should hold a PhD in a social science discipline and possess a record of research, publication, and teaching that merits appointment as a tenured full Professor at NYUAD. They will be internationally recognized scholars with proven leadership experience and intellectual breadth to guide a diverse division covering multiple disciplines. The successful candidate will combine academic distinction with sound judgment and the ability to inspire others around a shared vision, while demonstrating strong leadership in managing, supporting, and developing faculty and staff. NYUAD is being supported by the executive search firm Perrett Laver. For nominations or to learn more, please contact NYUAD@perrettlaver.com . Formal applications should be made via INTERFOLIO. Candidates are requested to submit the following documents: A letter of interest that addresses specifically the leadership priorities and themes described in the profile; A current curriculum vitae or resume; The search will continue throughout the 2025-2026 academic year until an appointment is made, with the expectation that the selected candidate will begin no later than September 2027. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the University\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194023": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Center for Behavioral Institutional Design (C-BID) - Dr. Nikos Nikiforakis & Dr. John Wooders", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Behavioral Institutional Design (CBID) Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Center for Behavioral Institutional Design , New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit several postdoctoral researchers interested in exploring the origins of human behavioral diversity, developing empirically-informed models of human behavior, and designing innovative institutions and policies for promoting social welfare. C-BID offers a vibrant and stimulating research environment, with opportunities to engage in highimpact projects and collaborations.\u00a0Post-Doctoral researchers join an active international community of scholars and fellow postdocs and benefit from a world-class seminar series. The Center also hosts numerous workshops, conferences, and other academic events that foster collaboration, idea exchange, and visibility, providing an exceptional platform for advancing researchers\u2019 work and academic careers. The ideal candidate is highly ambitious, well-trained, self-motivated, hard-working, equally capable of working alone and as part of a team, and holds a PhD in economics or a related field. The candidate should still be within their first 5 years since receiving the PhD. The position requires expertise in either experimental, empirical, or theoretical methods or, ideally, a combination of these methods. Excellent communication skills in English, the ability to work in multidisciplinary teams, and scientific creativity are essential. candidate is highly ambitious, well-trained, self-motivated, hard-working, equally capable of working alone and as part of a team, and holds a PhD in economics or a related field. The position requires expertise in either experimental, empirical, or theoretical methods or, ideally, a combination of these methods. Excellent communication skills in English, the ability to work in multidisciplinary teams, and scientific creativity are essential. Successful applicants are expected to publish research in leading peer-reviewed journals in social science or leading interdisciplinary journals, and work both individually and in close collaboration with the C-BID team: Olivier Bochet, Nikos Nikiforakis, and John Wooders. The position provides a salary higher than internationally competitive rates for postdoctoral research positions, in addition to substantial benefits (generous housing, transportation and travel allowances, educational subsidies for children), together with full health insurance and retirement contributions. The UAE does not levy income tax. Appointments will be for two years, renewable for at least one additional year conditional on a satisfactory evaluation of performance. Applications will be accepted on a rolling basis and candidates will be considered until the positions are filled. To be considered, applicants must submit their curriculum vitae and job market paper, a research statement highlighting past and current research experience, and at least three reference letters, all in PDF format. Note that this position is NOT located in the United States. You must be willing to relocate to Abu Dhabi, UAE. For any questions, please email: Nikos Nikiforakis ( nn30@nyu.edu )\u00a0or John Wooders ( john.wooders@nyu.edu ). About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/178219": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist in the Center for Interdisciplinary Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (CIDSAI) - Dr. Pierre Youssef", "description": "Posted: Jul 20, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Interdisciplinary Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (CIDSAI) Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Center for Interdisciplinary Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (CIDSAI) at NYU Abu Dhabi seeks to recruit a highly motivated researcher to work within its foundation cluster, led by Professors Pierre Youssef, Keith Ross, and Saurabh Ray. The position will contribute to CIDSAI\u2019s efforts to design AI systems that advance scientific and mathematical discovery, combining machine learning, reasoning, and human-AI collaboration. Successful candidates will work in a dynamic research environment at the intersection of data science, artificial intelligence, and mathematics, alongside faculty and researchers across disciplines. The successful candidate will contribute to one or more of the following components: Reinforcement Learning for Reasoning Formal Verification and Lean Automation AI-Assisted Collaboration and Problem Formulation Preference will be given to candidates with strong background in machine learning and mathematical reasoning and experience in one or more of the following: Large Language Models (LLMs), multi-agent systems, or reinforcement learning. Formal proof assistants (Lean, Coq, Isabelle, or similar). Programming. Strong mathematical maturity. Requirements: a Bachelor\u2019s degree in mathematics or computer science, with evidence of strong mathematical and computational skills. The appointment is for two years, with the possibility of renewal. The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree, a maximum three pages summary of research accomplishments and interests, and at least 2 letters of recommendation, all in PDF format. Further questions may be directed to Professor Pierre Youssef at yp27@nyu.edu . About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. UAE Nationals are encouraged to apply."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/178221": {"title": "Research Engineer in the Center for Interdisciplinary Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (CIDSAI) - Dr. Pierre Youssef", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Interdisciplinary Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (CIDSAI) Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Center for Interdisciplinary Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (CIDSAI) at NYU Abu Dhabi seeks to recruit a Research Engineer to support the design and implementation of interactive AI and data science platforms across the Center\u2019s research clusters. The successful candidate will collaborate with researchers and faculty across domains such as Foundations of Data Science, Enabling Technologies, Health, Education and Personalized Learning, and Bias, Ethics & Privacy, contributing to the development of prototypes, interfaces, and software infrastructure that translate research outputs into usable tools. Key Responsibilities: Design, develop, and maintain web-based and AI-powered platforms supporting CIDSAI research projects. Collaborate with faculty and postdocs on projects such as: Mathematical Collaboration Platform, Personalized Learning Systems and SmartClass AI Tutors,\u00a0 Health-focused conversational AI and multimodal interfaces, Bias detection and fairness-aware tools for ethical AI. Integrate machine learning models, visualization dashboards, and backend services. Contribute to data collection, testing, documentation, and dissemination of open-source resources. Qualifications: Bachelor\u2019s or Master\u2019s degree in Computer Science, Software Engineering, or a related field. Strong programming skills. Experience with machine learning frameworks and backend systems. Familiarity with cloud computing, API integration, data visualization, and database management. Ability to work in interdisciplinary research environments bridging theory, applications, and ethics. The appointment is for two years, with the possibility of renewal. The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree, a maximum three pages summary of research accomplishments and interests, and at least 2 letters of recommendation, all in PDF format. Further questions may be directed to Professor Pierre Youssef at yp27@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. UAE Nationals are encouraged to apply."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189802": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Center for Interdisciplinary Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (CIDSAI) - Dr. Pierre Youssef", "description": "Posted: Jul 28, 2026 Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Interdisciplinary Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (CIDSAI) Location: Abu Dhabi The Center of Interdisciplinary Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (CIDSAI) at NYU Abu Dhabi seeks to recruit a highly motivated Post-Doctoral Associate to work on topics in the theoretical foundations of data science and AI. Responsibilities: Conduct research on automated reasoning and proof checker, AI-assisted collaboration. Develop and analyze algorithms for learning and optimization. Participate in collaborative research projects and mentoring. Contribute to academic publications, reports and seminars The selected candidate will be part of the Foundations Cluster of CIDSAI and will collaborate closely with Professor Saurabh Ray, and Professor Pierre Youssef. We are particularly interested in candidates with a strong mathematical background and expertise in one or more of the following areas: High-dimensional probability and concentration/functional inequalities Markov processes and stochastic analysis Theoretical analysis of neural networks and deep learning Foundations of reinforcement learning and bandit algorithms Mathematical and algorithmic perspectives on large language models Statistical learning theory and complexity analysis Automated theorem proving and formal methods Random matrix theory and its applications in modern AI systems Requirements: PhD in Mathematics, Computer Science or related fields, with no more than five years post-receipt of PhD. The appointment is for two years, with the possibility of renewal. The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree, a maximum three pages summary of research accomplishments and interests, and at least 2 letters of recommendation, all in PDF format. Further questions may be directed to Professor Pierre Youssef at yp27@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193093": {"title": "Graduate Fellowship", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law: Center on Race, Inequality, and the Law Location: New York The Center on Race, Inequality, and the Law at NYU School of Law confronts and upends the array of American laws, policies, and practices that lead to racial oppression and injustice. The Center advances its work through interconnected approaches that shape its research, advocacy, and partnerships. Grounded in collaboration with communities, scholars, advocates, and policymakers, the Center translates insight into action through: Research & Thought Leadership: Challenging dominant narratives and producing original research and analysis that reveal how race shapes the law, contributing scholarship and public commentary that shift the field. Litigation & Advocacy: Pursuing strategies that disrupt harmful systems and promote justice through litigation, amicus advocacy, policy reform, and coalition work. Education & Leadership Development: Supporting the next generation of leaders working to transform and challenge the institutions they encounter through legal education, training, and mentorship. Convenings & Community Engagement: Bringing people together through public programs, dialogues, and partnerships that deepen relationships, strengthen movements, and support collective action. We are seeking applications for a Graduate Fellowship, a one-year position designed to support the professional development of current third-year law students, recent law school graduates, and early-career attorneys with no more than three years of post-graduate legal experience who are interested in pursuing or continuing a career focused on racial justice. The fellow will play an integral role in the Center's work, under the supervision of the Center's Faculty Director, Professor Vincent Southerland, and the Center's Executive Director, Patrice Sulton. The fellow will be expected to actively participate in the Center's research, public education, advocacy, organizing, litigation, events, and programming. Fellows have the opportunity to develop and produce research reports on a range of racial justice issues, draft amicus submissions in state and federal courts across the country, and engage in the Center's ongoing advocacy and organizing work with and on behalf of communities of color. The Fellow may also support, coordinate, or supervise the work of law students and other project contributors during the academic year. The fellow will be appointed for one year, but their term may be renewed for a second year, contingent upon the fellow's satisfactory performance and the availability of funding. The fellowship will begin after the successful candidate has completed law school and, for candidates sitting for a bar examination, after the applicable bar examination period. The fellowship start date is negotiable, based on the Center's priorities and the candidate's availability, although it generally will commence by either January or August 2027. The fellowship salary is commensurate with experience and includes full benefits. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $80,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Compensation includes excellent benefits , including medical, dental, and vision. To learn more about the Center on Race, Inequality, and the Law, visit http://www.law.nyu.edu/centers/race-inequality-law . Questions may be addressed to law.race.inequality@nyu.edu , with the subject line \"CRIL Graduate Fellowship Application Question.\" Eligibility and Qualifications: This fellowship is intended for current third-year law students (3Ls), recent law school graduates, and early-career attorneys. Applicants must have no more than three years of post-graduate legal experience as of the fellowship start date. Candidates should possess, or expect to receive before the fellowship start date, a JD, LLM, or equivalent legal training; distinguished analytical, research, and writing skills; and a demonstrated interest in racial justice. Please apply through Interfolio and submit the materials listed below through Interfolio: R\u00e9sum\u00e9 Writing sample Cover letter Short responses addressing each of the following prompts: Which focus area is of greatest interest to you, and why? Please select one: Race + Tech; Race + Punishment; Race + Place; or Race + Culture. If you could instantly add, repeal, or revise one law or policy, what would it be and why? List of three references, including email addresses References will not be contacted unless and until an applicant advances to a later stage of the selection process. The application period will open September 8, 2026, and close at 11:59 p.m. Eastern Time on January 15, 2027. Please ensure that you include a telephone number and/or an email address where we can contact you."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194721": {"title": "Operations Manager/Research Scholar", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: School of Law: Center on Race, Inequality, and the Law Location: New York The Center on Race, Inequality, and the Law at NYU School of Law confronts and upends the array of American laws, policies, and practices that lead to racial oppression and injustice. The Center advances its work through interconnected approaches that shape its research, advocacy, and partnerships. Grounded in collaboration with communities, scholars, advocates, and policymakers, the Center translates insight into action through: Research & Thought Leadership: Challenging dominant narratives and producing original research and analysis that reveal how race shapes the law, contributing scholarship and public commentary that shift the field. Litigation & Advocacy: Pursuing strategies that disrupt harmful systems and promote justice through litigation, amicus advocacy, policy reform, and coalition work. Education & Leadership Development: Supporting the next generation of leaders working to transform and challenge the institutions they encounter through legal education, training, and mentorship. Convenings & Community Engagement: Bringing people together through public programs, dialogues, and partnerships that deepen relationships, strengthen movements, and support collective action. We are seeking a dynamic, highly organized Operations Manager to lead the Center\u2019s day-to-day operations and strengthen the infrastructure that supports our work to advance racial justice. The Operations Manager will serve as the Center\u2019s primary administrator and provide operational and programmatic support for the Center\u2019s research, advocacy, education, and convening work. This role is ideal for someone with strong operational judgment, a demonstrated commitment to racial justice, and the ability to manage complex priorities in a fast-paced, mission-driven environment. The Operations Manager will report to the Center\u2019s Faculty Director, Professor Vincent Southerland, and Executive Director, Patrice Sulton. As the Center\u2019s operational team grows, the position may develop into a supervisory role. Responsibilities The Operations Manager is in charge of: Leading the Center\u2019s day-to-day operations, including developing, implementing, and improving systems, processes, and workflows. Serving as the Center\u2019s primary liaison with NYU\u2019s finance, human resources, information technology, and other administrative departments, and managing relationships with university partners. Managing operational aspects of the Center\u2019s financial administration, including expense tracking, reimbursements, vendor payments, and related documentation, in coordination with Center leadership and NYU finance staff. Coordinating logistics for Center meetings, programs, and events, including vendor coordination, participant communications, event materials, and on-site staffing. Supporting Center-hosted events and events hosted by partner organizations, including occasional early-morning, evening, and off-site commitments. Providing additional operational and programmatic support as needed to advance the Center\u2019s work. Compensation In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $70,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Compensation includes excellent benefits , including medical, dental, and vision. At least three years of relevant professional experience in operations, administration, project management, or a related role, preferably in a nonprofit, legal, academic, or other mission-driven setting. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple workstreams, establish effective systems, and complete accurate, timely work in a fast-paced environment. Strong organizational skills and meticulous attention to detail. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, sound judgment, discretion, and a proactive approach to problem-solving. Experience managing budgets, expense reporting, reimbursements, vendor relationships, event logistics, or comparable administrative processes is strongly preferred. Comfort learning and adapting to new platforms and technologies. The Center\u2019s current tools include Microsoft Office, Google Workspace, Canva, Notion, and Mailchimp. Commitment to racial justice and to the Center\u2019s mission. Flexibility to work outside standard business hours as needed for events and willingness to travel within New York City for Center activities. Please apply through interfolio. R\u00e9sum\u00e9 Cover letter describing your interest in the role and relevant experience List of two references, including email addresses References will not be contacted unless and until an applicant advances to a later stage of the selection process. The application period will close at 11:59 p.m. Eastern Time on October 16, 2026. Please ensure that you include a telephone number and/or an email address where we may contact you. To learn more about the Center on Race, Inequality, and the Law, visit http://www.law.nyu.edu/centers/race-inequality-law . Questions may be addressed to law.race.inequality@nyu.edu , with the subject line \"CRIL Operations Manager Application Question.\""}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187088": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist in the Division of Social Science (CITIES) - Dr. Etienne Wasmer", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Social Science: AD_Economics: AD_Research_Economics Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE  The Center for Interacting Urban Networks, New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit a Junior Research Scientist to support the project LANDMark on research on land economics, in any of the following areas: Measurement of land prices, with a focus on imputation techniques Land value determinants: public infrastructures (transport, health, and education services) Macroeconomic stability and instability in the presence of land as a fixed, non-produced asset Taxation of land wealth as an alternative to the consumption tax and the capital tax The political economy of land and its taxation Land, fixed resources, and political conflict The successful applicant will contribute to the research centered on any of these themes. The qualified candidate will have an MSc or an MA in economics or another quantitative field with no more than five years from receipt of the degree, and an appetite for working on interdisciplinary teams. This position requires: Research experience in the mentioned areas of land (in a broad sense, including housing, macroeconomics, labor, urban, spatial economics, political economy, and conflict). Excellent communication skills in English. Ability to work in teams Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a statement of research interests, a transcript or degree, and the contact information for 2-3 referees, who may be contacted during the selection process. The research statement should outline specific projects that the candidate would like to lead within CITIES, along with potential collaborators within the Center. If you have any questions, please email us at nyuad.cities@nyu.edu. Evaluations will begin immediately and continue until the role is filled. The earliest start date for this position is January 1st, 2027. About LANDMark (Land Assets, National Development and Markets): This project, developed by Etienne Wasmer, professor of Economics and program head in Economics at NYUAD, and funded for the next 6 years (2027-2032), brings together a group of researchers working on land all over the world. A team of 4 postdocs will constitute the project's core. Associations and collaborative research funding projects with researchers in Economics, Engineering, Computer Science, and Political Science are encouraged. The post-doctoral associate will be hosted in the Social Science Division, near the economics faculty, and can be affiliated with CITIES (infra). About The Center for Interacting Urban Networks (CITIES): The NYUAD\u2019s Center for InTeractIng urban nEtworkS (CITIES \u2013\u00a0\u0645\u062f\u0646) is an interdisciplinary research center dedicated to advancing urban science and promoting cutting-edge research that is translated into practical, real-world solutions for the benefit of society. Our mission is to establish a vibrant and globally recognized knowledge hub for urban networks, bridge the gap between scientific significance and practical value, and further develop the human capital to propel the city of Abu Dhabi and the UAE into a more prosperous and sustainable future. CITIES leverages the expertise of faculty members from the four Divisions of NYUAD and world-renowned scientific collaborators from multiple institutions within and beyond the NYU Global Network, as well as local stakeholders and private companies across the UAE and abroad. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189508": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Center Of Quantum and Topological Systems (CQTS) - Dr. Asif Equbal", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Quantum and Topological Systems (CQTS) Location: Abu Dhabi The Center for Quantum and Topological Systems (CQTS) at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD), UAE, invites applications for a Post-Doctoral Associate position in Experimental Quantum Science Instrumentation. The successful candidate will join the Quantum center and will focus primarily on the design, construction, and deployment of advanced instruments for quantum-optical and spin-based experiments. Applicants with a strong experimental background and a demonstrated interest in building new measurement platforms are especially encouraged to apply. The selected candidate will work in a highly collaborative research environment within CQTS. The central responsibility of the position is instrument building: conceiving, prototyping, assembling, integrating, calibrating, automating, and troubleshooting custom experimental systems. These instruments may combine optical components, laser and spectroscopic methods, spin-control or magnetic-resonance techniques, electronics, data-acquisition hardware, and software control. The candidate will use these platforms to advance research in optics, spin physics, and related areas of quantum science, while collaborating with faculty, researchers, and students across CQTS and NYUAD. The successful applicant will also participate actively in undergraduate training through research supervision, laboratory instruction, and hands-on mentoring in experimental design and scientific instrument development. Applicants should meet the following qualifications and possess relevant expertise: A PhD in Physics, Applied Physics, Electrical Engineering, or a closely related field, with a strong experimental research background. Demonstrated experience designing and building scientific instruments, including the integration of optical, electronic, mechanical, and computer-controlled subsystems. Hands-on expertise in one or more of the following areas: optical systems, lasers, spectroscopy, quantum optics, spin physics, magnetic resonance, precision measurement, electronics, data acquisition, automation, or experimental control software. Strong problem-solving and troubleshooting skills, a commitment to collaborative interdisciplinary research, and an interest in mentoring undergraduate students in laboratory research and instrument development. The appointment will be for a period of two years. The terms of employment include a very competitive salary and attractive additional benefits, including a generous housing allowance, medical insurance with access to world-class facilities, an annual home-leave allowance, and, if applicable, dependent private-school fees. Salaries are tax-free in the UAE (for U.S. tax purposes, the Foreign Earned Income Exclusion may apply). The postdoctoral researcher will have dedicated funds for travel, computing, publications, and other research needs, and will have access to excellent world-class research facilities. The preferred starting date is September 2026, although an earlier start may be possible depending on the selected candidate and the completion of the appointment process. Review of applications will begin soon after the position is posted and will continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, Transcript, publication list, a brief statement of research interests, and three letters of recommendation. In the cover letter and/or research statement, applicants are strongly encouraged to describe their experience in scientific instrument development and explain how they would contribute to interdisciplinary connections among the research areas represented within CQTS. Questions regarding this position may be directed to Prof. Asif Equbal at asif@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192992": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Center of Cyber Security", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_ Center for Cyber Security Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Center of Cyber Security, New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit a postdoctoral associate to work on state-of-art research on security and privacy. The researcher will be mainly based in New York University Abu Dhabi, UAE, and can closely collaborate with researchers from New York University, USA. The ideal candidate will have an excellent knowledge of cyber security and must hold a PhD degree or have submitted a PhD thesis. Responsibilities of the Position: Research Conduct world class research in systems security and privacy. Write research articles and publish work in top-tier conferences and journals. Work closely with collaborators (such as government entities and companies) to deliver ground breaking results. Supervision Mentor PhD, master, and/or bachelor students. Work with them to improve their research capability. Service Help the center in conducting workshops, conferences, competitions etc. by contributing technically and/or administratively. Essential Qualifications: PhD in computer science/engineering with expertise in a security or privacy research topic. Less than 5 years post-receiving the Doctoral degree. Excellent analytic, design, and coding skills. Excellent communication (both writing and oral) and interpersonal skills. Can work independently or in a team. Willingness to take initiative. Applicants to provide: Cover letter and Research statement. Cover letter must include which research topic is of interest. CV (Should include all publications) Three names of referees to contact Contact: Center For Cyber Security NYUAD: ccsad@nyu.edu The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193478": {"title": "Research Scientist in the Center For Cyber Security (CCS)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_ Center for Cyber Security Location: Abu Dhabi The Center of Cyber Security, New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit a Research Scientist to work on state-of-art research on security and privacy. The researcher will be mainly based in New York University Abu Dhabi, UAE, and can closely collaborate with researchers from New York University, USA. The ideal candidate will have an excellent knowledge of cyber security and must hold a PhD degree. Responsibilities of the Position: Research Conduct world class research in systems security and privacy. Write research articles and publish work in top-tier conferences and journals. Work closely with collaborators (such as government entities and companies) to deliver ground breaking results. Supervision Mentor PhD, master, and/or bachelor students. Work with them to improve their research capability. Service Help the center in conducting workshops, conferences, competitions etc. by contributing technically and/or administratively. Essential Qualifications: PhD in computer science/engineering with expertise in a security or privacy research topic At least six years of relevant research experience in computer security, excluding the years spent obtaining a PhD or equivalent terminal degree, along with a strong record of research and publications in well-known journals and conferences, or comparable research accomplishments. Excellent analytic, design, and coding skills. Excellent communication (both writing and oral) and interpersonal skills. Can work independently or in a team. Willingness to take initiative. Applicants to provide: Cover letter and Research statement. Cover letter must include which research topic is of interest. CV (Should include all publications) Three names of referees to contact Contact: Center For Cyber Security NYUAD: ccsad@nyu.edu The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195015": {"title": "Research Scientist in the Center for Cyber Security (CCS) - (One-Year Position)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_ Center for Cyber Security Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Center for Cyber Security at New York University Abu Dhabi seeks to recruit a Research Scientist for a one-year appointment to conduct state-of-the-art research in cybersecurity governance, law, policy, human factors, or the socio-behavioral dimensions of security. The researcher will be based primarily at New York University Abu Dhabi in the UAE and may collaborate closely with researchers at New York University in the United States. Ideal candidates will have excellent knowledge of human-centric cyber security, cybersecurity law, policy, or governance and must hold a PhD or equivalent terminal degree. The appointment will be for an initial fixed term of one year. Depending on performance, the contract may be extended. Responsibilities of the Position: Research Conduct world class research in systems security and privacy. Write research articles and publish work in top-tier conferences and journals. Work closely with collaborators (such as government entities and companies) to deliver ground breaking results. Supervision Mentor PhD, master, and/or bachelor students. Work with them to improve their research capability. Service Help the center in conducting workshops, conferences, competitions etc. by contributing technically and/or administratively. Essential Qualifications: PhD in Cybersecurity, Information Systems, Public Policy, Law, Psychology, Behavioral Sciences, Sociology, Business, or a related interdisciplinary field with expertise in a human-centric or policy/law-oriented cybersecurity research topic. At least six years of relevant research experience, excluding the years spent obtaining a PhD or equivalent terminal degree, along with a strong record of research and publications in well-known journals and conferences, or comparable research accomplishments. Excellent qualitative and/or quantitative research skills (e.g., policy analysis, empirical user studies, survey methodology, or behavioral experiments). Excellent communication (both writing and oral) and interpersonal skills. Can work independently and in a team. Willingness to take initiative. Applicants to provide: Cover letter and Research statement. Cover letter must include which research topic is of interest. CV (Should include all publications) Three names of referees to contact Contact: Center For Cyber Security NYUAD: ccsad@nyu.edu The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately, and candidates will be considered until both positions are filled. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195038": {"title": "Associate Research Scientist in the Center for Cyber Security (CCS) - (One-Year Position)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_ Center for Cyber Security Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Center for Cyber Security at New York University Abu Dhabi seeks to recruit an Associate Research Scientist for a one-year appointment to conduct state-of-the-art research in cybersecurity governance, law, policy, human factors, or the socio-behavioral dimensions of security. The researcher will be based primarily at New York University Abu Dhabi in the UAE and may collaborate closely with researchers at New York University in the United States. Ideal candidates will have excellent knowledge of human-centric cyber security, cybersecurity law, policy, or governance and must hold a PhD or equivalent terminal degree. The appointment will be for an initial fixed term of one year. Depending on performance, the contract may be extended. Responsibilities of the Position: Research Conduct world class research in systems security and privacy. Write research articles and publish work in top-tier conferences and journals. Work closely with collaborators (such as government entities and companies) to deliver ground breaking results. Supervision Mentor PhD, master, and/or bachelor students. Work with them to improve their research capability. Service Help the center in conducting workshops, conferences, competitions etc. by contributing technically and/or administratively. Essential Qualifications: PhD in Cybersecurity, Information Systems, Public Policy, Law, Psychology, Behavioral Sciences, Sociology, Business, or a related interdisciplinary field with expertise in a human-centric or policy/law-oriented cybersecurity research topic. Candidate with a PhD or equivalent terminal degree and at least three years of relevant experience in the field, excluding the years spent obtaining the PhD or equivalent terminal degree. Excellent qualitative and/or quantitative research skills (e.g., policy analysis, empirical user studies, survey methodology, or behavioral experiments). Excellent communication (both writing and oral) and interpersonal skills. Can work independently and in a team. Willingness to take initiative. Applicants to provide: Cover letter and Research statement. Cover letter must include which research topic is of interest. CV (Should include all publications) Three names of referees to contact Contact: Center For Cyber Security NYUAD: ccsad@nyu.edu The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately, and candidates will be considered until both positions are filled. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195263": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist in the Center for Cyber Security (CCS) - (One-Year Position)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_ Center for Cyber Security Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Center of Cyber Security, New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit a (Junior) Research Scientist to work on state-of-art research at the center. The successful applicant is expected to conduct research in cybersecurity policy, governance, law, or human/psychological aspects of cybersecurity. This includes, but is not limited to research, contributing to research communication, supporting relationship building with industrial and governmental entities, supporting activities of the Center for Cyber Security such as cybersecurity competitions.\u00a0The successful applicant will assist in research in the selected topic and expected to publish in top-tier conferences and journals. The candidate will work in a multidisciplinary environment consisting of PhD-level scientists, graduate students and undergraduate students. Applicants must have a BSc or MSc in Cybersecurity, Cyber Law, Societal Computing/HCI, Science and Technology Studies, Information Systems, Public Policy, or a related field. Candidates with an MSc degree must have less than 5 years of relevant experience. The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. To be considered, all applicants must submit: Cover letter stating why they think they are suitable for the position Curriculum vitae Transcript of degree(s) One-page summary of research accomplishments and interests, and at least 2 names of recommenders (letters will only be solicited if shortlisted), all in PDF format. If the cover letter does not mention the position the applicant is applying for and/or any of the documents are missing, the application will not be considered. Please visit our website at http://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/about/careers/faculty-positions.html for instructions and information on how to apply. If you have any questions, please email ccsad@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194580": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist in the Center for Brain and Health (CBH) - Dr. David Melcher", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Brain and Health (CBH) Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE  The Perception and Active Cognition Laboratory in the Center for Brain & Health, New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit a junior research scientist to work on the mechanisms underlying cognitive and perceptual Speed of Processing. The successful applicant will drive a fascinating project on the interactions between sleep, circadian rhythms, stress and Speed of Processing, in both healthy participants and in patients with Multiple Sclerosis (and potentially other health and mental health conditions). The Research Scientist will ensure the successful acquisition and analysis of experimental data, including behavioral and neuroimaging (EEG/MEG and/or MRI) data. These studies will be informed by computational modeling and will use cutting-edge data analysis methods. The project will build on recent work from the Melcher lab on the neural mechanisms underlying individual and clinical differences in visual temporal resolution and in classic measures of Speed of Processing such as the SDMT, as well as recent studies from our lab showing an influence of mood and stress on these measures. Applicants must have at least a Bachelor\u2019s degree in a field related to this project, or a Masters degree with less than 5 years of experience. Previous experience in similar research paradigms (behavioral and/or neuroimaging experiments with human participants) is required. Applicants with a strong background in coding (such as in Python or Matlab) are particularly encouraged to apply. For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with full publication list, statement of research interests, the name of three referees, and a transcript, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email Prof. Melcher at david.melcher@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195016": {"title": "Research Engineer In the Mubadala Arabian Center for Climate and Environmental Sciences (ACCESS) - Dr. John Burt", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Arabian Center for Climate and Environmental Sciences (ACCESS) Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Mubadala Arabian Center for Climate and Environmental Sciences (Mubadala ACCESS) at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD) is seeking a Research Engineer to lead the development of modern data engineering infrastructure supporting multidisciplinary environmental and climate research. This role offers an exciting opportunity to work with researchers across marine science, climate science, biodiversity, and environmental monitoring to build scalable data platforms that enable world-class research and evidence-based decision making. The successful candidate will design and maintain cloud-based data infrastructure, develop automated data pipelines, integrate diverse scientific datasets, and implement data governance and quality assurance frameworks. Working closely with faculty, researchers, software developers, and external collaborators, the Research Engineer will help develop analytical platforms that support environmental monitoring, artificial intelligence applications, geospatial analytics, and long-term research data management. Candidates should hold a Master\u2019s degree in Computer Science, Data Engineering, Software Engineering, Information Systems, or a related discipline, with demonstrated experience in Python, SQL, cloud computing, data engineering, and modern software development practices. Experience as a full-stack developer or system manager, particularly in work involving scientific data, geospatial information, environmental monitoring systems, or research infrastructure, is highly desirable. All applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree/ copy of highest degree, and a research statement, all in PDF format. Shortlisted candidates will provide at least 2 contact references, all in PDF format. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. If you have any questions, please reach out to Kelly Bradham email: kelly.bradham@nyu.edu About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188599": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Center for Sand Hazards and Opportunities in Resilience, Energy, and Sustainability (SHORES) Dr. Tarek Abdoun", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Hazards and Opportunities in Resilience, Energy, and Sustainability (SHORES) Location: Abu Dhabi  The Center for Sand Hazards and Opportunities in Resilience, Energy, and Sustainability (SHORES), together with the Division of Engineering, Civil and Urban Engineering Program, at NYU Abu Dhabi seeks to recruit a Post-doctoral Associate in the broad area of geotechnical engineering. Research Project: The successful candidate will contribute to a research project focused on the development, testing, and analysis of resilient geotechnical systems. The work will rely on centrifuge modeling, geotechnical laboratory testing, and complementary numerical simulations to improve understanding of soil behavior and geotechnical system performance for infrastructure, energy, and sustainability applications. The Post-Doctoral Associate will be expected to: Perform and interpret geotechnical laboratory tests to support the experimental program. Plan, design, conduct, and analyze centrifuge model experiments. Contribute to the design and development of experimental setups and apparatus, with SolidWorks experience considered an advantage. Publish research findings in high-quality peer-reviewed journals. Work collaboratively with faculty, postdoctoral researchers, students, and other members of a multidisciplinary research team. Required Qualifications: Applicants must hold a PhD in Geotechnical Engineering or a closely related field, with less than 5 years post-recieving the PhD, and with research experience in one or more of the following areas: Geotechnical laboratory testing, including soil characterization, sample preparation, mechanical testing, and experimental data analysis. Centrifuge experimental modeling, including test design, execution, instrumentation, data collection, and result interpretation. Preferred: Experience with CAD and modeling tools, such as SolidWorks, for experimental design and apparatus development. Post-Doctoral Associate Employment at NYUAD: The terms of employment include highly competitive salary, medical insurance, housing allowance, annual home-leave travel, and educational subsidies for children as well as other benefits. For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with full publication list, transcript, statement of research interests and contact information for at least two references, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email Dr. Tarek Abdoun at tarek.abdoun@nyu.edu About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189065": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Engineering (Computer Engineering) - Dr. Tuka Alhanai", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Engineering: AD_Computer Engineering: AD_Research_Computer Engineering Location: Abu Dhabi The Laboratory for Computer-Human Intelligence (CHI-lab, https://www.x-labs.xyz) in the Division of Engineering, New York University Abu Dhabi, is seeking a highly motivated Post-Doctoral Associate to advance cutting-edge research in machine learning (ML). Our lab explores the intersection of artificial intelligence, and human-computer interaction, striving to create technologies that amplify human potential. The successful candidate will engage in innovative research projects in ML, focusing on developing novel ML algorithms, enhancing human-AI collaboration, and exploring systems tailored to dynamic, human-centered environments. They may also work with diverse signal modalities, including vision, speech, images, and physiological signals. Preferred Experience: The lab highly values candidates with one or more of the following experiences: \u25cf Human-Centered Applications: Familiarity applying ML in areas like healthcare, education, neuro-robotics, and/or assistive technologies. Prior experience in physiological signal processing (e.g., EMG, EEG, ECG) is an advantage. Familiarity with HCI principles and frameworks, in particular, experience conducting usability studies and designing user- centric AI systems. \u25cf Assistive / Collaborative Robotics: Interest in developing robotic systems for rehabilitation, assistive technology, or neuro-prosthetics, leveraging machine learning to improve precision and adaptability in user interactions. Knowledge of deploying robots in shared workspaces, focusing on safety, cooperation, and efficiency in human-robot teams. \u25cf Multi-Modal ML: Expertise in working with diverse data types, such as vision, speech, images, and physiological signals. Experience integrating multiple modalities to build robust AI systems is an advantage. \u25cf Interdisciplinary Applications: Leveraging LLM / VLMs for interdisciplinary problems, such as: AI-driven scientific discovery, automating hypothesis generation in finance / natural sciences / physical sciences, enhancing collaborative workflows in complex organizational settings. Qualifications: \u25cf Applicants must have a PhD in Computer Science or related field, with\u00a0no more than five years post receipt of the PhD. \u25cf Experience in one or more ML domains, such as deep learning, reinforcement learning, or human-centered ML. \u25cfProficiency in programming languages (e.g., Python) and ML frameworks (e.g., TensorFlow, PyTorch), with evidence in the form of public (Github) repository. \u25cfExcellent abilities in communication, teamwork, and mentorship. \u25cfStrong publication record in top-tier conferences (e.g., NeurIPS, ICML, CHI, CVPR). For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with full publication list, transcript, statement of research interests and two letters of reference, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email Prof. Tuka Alhanai at tuka.alhanai@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195008": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist in the Division of Engineering (Computer Engineering) - Dr. Jiahao Yu", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Engineering: AD_Computer Engineering: AD_Research_Computer Engineering Location: Abu Dhabi The research group of Professor Jiahao Yu at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD) invites applications for full-time research positions at the Junior Research Scientist level in AI and Cybersecurity. The preferred start date is 1 February 2027, subject to completion of the appointment process. The group studies the security of artificial intelligence (AI) and the application of AI to cybersecurity. We welcome applicants working in one or more of the following areas: AI security; trustworthy machine learning and interpretability; or applying AI to software security, vulnerability detection, and other cybersecurity domains. Successful candidates will develop and evaluate methods in these research areas, implement experiments, analyze results, and contribute to publications. Junior Research Scientists will carry out research objectives defined with the principal investigator. Junior Research Scientist applicants must hold a bachelor's degree in computer science, computer engineering, or a related field; a master's degree with less than 5 years of experience is preferred. Applicants should have research experience in at least one of the areas above, strong programming and experimental skills, and clear written and oral communication. Experience with machine learning frameworks, security analysis tools, or both is desirable. Publications and well-documented research software are welcome evidence of research ability. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter indicating the position sought, curriculum vitae, transcript or copy of the highest degree, research statement, and contact details for at least two references or recommendation letters, all in PDF format, through the advertised Interfolio position. Applications will be accepted immediately and reviewed until the positions are filled. For questions, please contact Professor Jiahao Yu at jy5951@nyu.edu . About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195009": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Engineering (Computer Engineering) - Dr. Jiahao Yu", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Engineering: AD_Computer Engineering: AD_Research_Computer Engineering Location: Abu Dhabi The research group of Professor Jiahao Yu at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD) invites applications for full-time research positions at the Postdoctoral Associate level in AI and Cybersecurity . The preferred start date is 1 February 2027, subject to completion of the appointment process. The group studies the security of artificial intelligence (AI) and the application of AI to cybersecurity. We welcome applicants working in one or more of the following areas: AI security; trustworthy machine learning and interpretability; or applying AI to software security, vulnerability detection, and other cybersecurity domains. Successful candidates will develop and evaluate methods in these research areas, implement experiments, analyze results, and contribute to publications.\u00a0Postdoctoral Associates will develop research questions, lead studies, and mentor student researchers in collaboration with the principal investigator. Postdoctoral Associate applicants must hold a PhD in computer science, computer engineering, or a related field by the start of the appointment and demonstrate the ability to conduct independent research. The candidate must be within 5 years of receiving the Doctoral degree. Applicants should have research experience in at least one of the areas above, strong programming and experimental skills, and clear written and oral communication. Experience with machine learning frameworks, security analysis tools, or both is desirable. Publications and well-documented research software are welcome evidence of research ability. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter indicating the position sought, curriculum vitae, transcript or copy of the highest degree, research statement, and contact details for at least two references or recommendation letters, all in PDF format, through the advertised Interfolio position. Applications will be accepted immediately and reviewed until the positions are filled. For questions, please contact Professor Jiahao Yu at jy5951@nyu.edu . About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187146": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Engineering (Electrical and Computer Engineering) - Dr. Farah Shamout", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Engineering: AD_Electrical and Computer Engineering: AD_Research_Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Clinical Artificial Intelligence Lab at NYU Abu Dhabi seeks to improve patient care by developing new machine learning methodologies that tackle unique computational problems in healthcare applications. We use large real-world complex datasets, including data extracted from electronic health records and medical images, for applications pertaining to patient diagnostics and prognostics. We are seeking a Postdoctoral Researcher to join the team and make significant contributions to the field. The researcher is expected to have (i) strong machine learning skills to improve model performance and robustness, and (ii) exemplary passion and motivation to pursue multidisciplinary research at the intersection of computing and healthcare. Methodologies of interest include: multi-modal learning, foundation models, including large language models, agentic AI, multi-agent AI systems, transfer learning, self-supervised learning, and federated learning. The Postdoctoral Researcher will be primarily based at NYU Abu Dhabi. The researcher will report directly to Dr. Farah Shamout and work in close collaboration with other researchers, PhD students, and undergraduate research assistants. The researcher will engage with our regular collaborators across the NYU campuses and local medical institutions in the UAE. Key Responsibilities Research Support the supervisor in developing and implementing the research agenda; Conduct high-quality and innovative research primarily focused on ML methodology development for healthcare; Generate new high-impact ideas based on gaps and limitations of the state-of-the-art (SOTA); Design and implement experiments to compare proposed work with SOTA baselines; Publish research findings in high-impact journals and conferences; Communicate and present research findings at international academic gatherings; Create, maintain, and document high-quality research code for reproducibility; Maintain good practice in managing and accessing sensitive medical datasets; Assist the supervisor in the preparation of grant applications (as appropriate); And collaborate with scientists within the NYU Global Network and in Abu Dhabi. Training & professional development* Attend trainings and workshops for career development; Mentor PhD students and undergraduate research assistants (as appropriate); Actively participate in events and committees at NYU Abu Dhabi, such as the Postdoctoral Council Steering Committee; Gain experience in applying for local research grants (subject to eligibility); And transition to independence to pursue a career of choosing following the appointment. * The researcher will create a personalized training and development plan with the supervisor. Minimum Qualifications Currently has or is in the process of completing a PhD, MD/PhD, DPhil or equivalent terminal degree from a recognized institution (no more than 5 years since completing the doctoral degree) Doctoral research in the area of machine learning and artificial intelligence Bachelor\u2019s/ Master\u2019s degree in computer science, mathematics, computer engineering, or relevant technical field First-author peer-reviewed published papers (or under review) Proficient programming experience in Python and libraries (e.g., Pytorch, TensorFlow) with several years of practice Experience in maintaining high-quality code on Github Experience in running and managing experiments using GPUs Ability to visualize experimental results and learning curves Effective inter-personal and team-building skills Self-motivated with an ability to work independently and in a team to get the work done Excellent communication skills (oral and written communication) Willingness to learn and confront new challenges Preferred Qualifications Doctoral research conducted in the area of machine learning for healthcare and related topics Deep knowledge of multi-modal learning, transfer learning, foundation models, and self-supervised learning. Experience in dealing with large medical datasets (e.g., electronic health records data or medical images) Ability to use high performance computing cluster For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with full publication list, research statement (1-page), project proposal summary (1-page), a transcript or degree, and three letters of reference, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email Prof. Farah Shamout at farah.shamout@nyu.edu. The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189262": {"title": "Associate Research Scientist in the Division of Engineering (Electrical and Computer Engineering) - Dr. Mahmoud Rasras", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Engineering: AD_Electrical and Computer Engineering: AD_Research_Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Division of Engineering at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD) invites applications for a fully funded Associate Research Scientist position in integrated photonics and optoelectronic device development. The successful candidate will contribute to research on silicon photonics and optoelectronic devices based, with applications in optical communications, sensing, and photonic computing. Experience in silicon photonics is required, while expertise in plasmonics, nanophotonics, and 2D materials is a plus. The candidate will join a multidisciplinary team and have access to state-of-the-art facilities for photonic device design, fabrication, and characterization. Applicants should have a Ph.D. in science or engineering with at least 3 years of experience in the relevant field. For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with a full publication list, statement of research interests, a transcript or degree, and three letters of reference, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email Dr. Mahmoud Rasras at mr5098@nyu.edu . The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194119": {"title": "Associate Research Scientist in the Division of Engineering (Mechanical Engineering ) - Dr. Pradeep George", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Engineering: AD_Mechanical Engineering: AD_Research_Mechanical Engineering Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Division of Engineering at New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit a motivated Associate Research scientist to work under the supervision of Professor Pradeep George in process Optimization, with emphasis on Chemical vapor Deposition (CVD).\u00a0 The successful applicant must have a strong background in the area of Computational Fluid Dynamics. Familiarity with response surface modeling and optimization, is a plus. Experience using commercial codes like ANSYS-FLUENT is desirable. An Associate Research Scientist/Scholar typically holds a PhD or equivalent terminal degree with at least 3 years of relevant post-degree experience in their field. The candidate will work in a multidisciplinary environment consisting of PhD-level scientists and undergraduate students. In addition to conducting high-quality, innovative research, responsibilities include publishing research findings in scientific journals and conferences, mentoring undergraduate students, and participating in drafting research grant proposals. Applicants must have a PhD in mechanical engineering, or closely-related fields. For consideration, applicants need to submit the following in PDF format: Cover letter Curriculum vitae with full publication list Statement of research interests Contact details of at least three references Sample relevant publications (if available) Applications will be accepted immediately, and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. If you have any questions, please email Prof. Pradeep George, pg66@nyu.edu About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188424": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division Science (Biology) - Dr. Kenichiro Kamei", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Biology: AD_Research_Biology Location: Abu Dhabi  The Kamei Reverse Bioengineering Laboratory within the Divisions of Science and Engineering at NYU Abu Dhabi invites applications for a Post-Doctoral Associate position that advances the frontiers of developmental biology and disease modeling. The laboratory integrates stem-cell biology, fluorescence imaging, bioinformatics, and advanced nano- and micro-engineering to decode organogenesis and early embryogenesis, translating these insights into regenerative medicine, drug discovery, and precision therapeutics. The appointed fellow will design and fabricate nano- and micro-engineered platforms that interface with human stem cells, investigate cellular and molecular mechanisms governing development and pathology, lead experiments from conception through publication in high-impact journals, mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers, and contribute to grant writing and interdisciplinary collaborations. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in science, engineering, or a closely related field awarded before the start date, and is within the first five years post-receiving a PhD. Competitive candidates will demonstrate expertise in areas such as device design and fabrication, stem-cell culture and differentiation, high-resolution imaging, single-cell or bulk omics, or bioinformatics, together with a strong publication record, excellent communication skills, and a collaborative mindset. For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with full publication list, statement of research interests, transcript, and three letters of reference, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email kk4801@nyu.edu (Ken-ichiro Kamei, laboratory PI). About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192660": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist in the Division of Science (Biology) - Dr. Mohamed Al-Sayegh", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Biology: AD_Research_Biology Location: Abu Dhabi The Al-Sayegh Lab at New York University Abu Dhabi invites applications for a Junior Research Scientist position. The laboratory investigates the molecular and cellular mechanisms underlying embryonic stem cell differentiation, with interests in stem cell biology, cellular differentiation, genomics, and integrated molecular approaches. The successful candidate will contribute to ongoing stem cell and genomics research projects and will participate in experimental design, data generation, analysis, and interpretation. The Junior Research Scientist will work closely with the Principal Investigator and other members of the research team in developing and executing research projects. Responsibilities will include conducting molecular and cell biology experiments, maintaining mammalian cell cultures, supporting stem cell differentiation experiments, collecting and analyzing experimental data, maintaining accurate laboratory records, and contributing to the preparation of scientific presentations and manuscripts. The successful candidate will also contribute to the day-to-day operation of the laboratory, including maintaining laboratory organization, monitoring supplies and inventory, coordinating laboratory orders, and supporting compliance with laboratory procedures and research protocols. Applicants must hold a bachelor\u2019s degree in Biology, Bioengineering, Molecular Biology, Biotechnology, or a closely related field. Previous research experience in molecular biology, cell biology, stem cell biology, or a related area is highly desirable. Candidates should demonstrate strong organizational and communication skills, attention to detail, the ability to work independently and collaboratively, and a strong interest in scientific research. Experience with mammalian cell culture, molecular biology techniques, microscopy, quantitative data analysis, or computational analysis would be advantageous. Applicants with less than five years of professional experience are encouraged to apply. The appointment is anticipated to begin in Fall 2026, and will be for two years, subject to applicable NYU Abu Dhabi appointment policies. Competitive salary and benefits packages are available. Funding is also available for conference travel and collaborative research activities. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript, a publication bibliography where applicable, and the names and contact information of three referees. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188519": {"title": "Research Engineer/Technician in the Division of Science (Computer Science) - Dr. Nizar Habash", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Computer Science: AD_Research_Computer Science Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE  The New York University Abu Dhabi Computational Approaches to Modeling Language (CAMeL) Lab seeks to hire a new Research Engineer / Technician to work in any of the lab research areas, to be involved in the development of open-source tools and resources, and to work on publications related to their work.\u00a0 CAMeL's mission is research and education in artificial intelligence, specifically focusing on natural language processing, computational linguistics, and data science.\u00a0 The main lab research areas are Arabic natural language processing (orthography normalization, grammatical error correction, morphology tagging, syntax parsing, readability assessment), machine translation, and text analytics. Since its establishment in 2014, the CAMeL Lab has produced over 200 publications and 20 language resources and tools.\u00a0 The lab website is http://www.camel-lab.com/ .\u00a0 Google Scholar of the lab is at http://scholar.camel-lab.com/ . The position will target the rank of Research Engineer (MS), and Research Technician (BS , with at least 3-5 years of experience).\u00a0 A strong preference is for individuals with (a) computer science or computer engineering degrees including previous experience in natural language processing or computational linguistics/language technologies degrees; (b) strong programming and software engineering skills, and machine learning experience; and (c) some research publication experience. Knowledge of Arabic language is preferred, but not required.\u00a0 Previous work on Arabic NLP is preferred but not required. The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree, a one-page summary of research accomplishments and interests, and at least 2 letters of recommendation, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email: nizar.habash@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189259": {"title": "Senior Research Engineer in the Division of Science (Computer Science) - Dr. Azza Abouzied", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Computer Science: AD_Research_Computer Science Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Human Data Interaction Lab at NYU Abu Dhabi is seeking to appoint a Senior Research Engineer to support and lead the design, development, evaluation, and dissemination of novel systems and tools for decision-making support. The position is intended for a highly experienced researcher and systems builder with strong technical independence, excellent software engineering practices, and a demonstrated ability to translate research ideas into robust, reproducible, and usable tools. The successful candidate must be available to start immediately and should bring a minimum of six years of relevant research experience, together with at least a Master\u2019s degree in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Human-Computer Interaction, Artificial Intelligence, Data Systems, or a closely related field. HuDa Lab examines data and decision-making from a tooling and systems-building perspective. The lab designs and builds systems that support decision-making across a variety of domains, including disinformation mitigation, collaborative decision-making, interactive data exploration, and optimization problems in uncertain environments such as stock portfolio optimization. The lab does not specialize in applied data science projects or computational social science research. Candidates whose primary interest is applied data science rather than building tools, systems, and research prototypes for data-driven decision-making are encouraged to consider opportunities in other labs. The Senior Research Engineer will be expected to contribute intellectually and technically to ongoing and new research projects. This includes designing and implementing research-grade systems, developing algorithms and interfaces, conducting simulations and comparative performance evaluations, designing and running user studies, maintaining high-quality code and data artifacts, and preparing research outputs for publication and presentation. The candidate should be comfortable working independently, mentoring junior researchers or research assistants, and collaborating closely with faculty, students, and external partners. Key responsibilities include: Designing, building, and maintaining novel systems and tools for decision-making support. Developing research prototypes, interactive interfaces, data-processing pipelines, and experimental platforms. Evaluating systems through rigorous experiments, simulations, benchmarks, and user studies. Maintaining reproducible code, documentation, datasets, and research artifacts through GitHub and other shared repositories. Preparing technical reports, presentations, research manuscripts, and LaTeX documents. Contributing to grant-funded research outputs, conference submissions, and journal publications. Supporting project planning, milestone tracking, and technical coordination across the lab. Mentoring junior team members in software engineering, experimental design, reproducibility, and research communication. A successful candidate will demonstrate strong experience in systems and tool building, algorithmic thinking, research design, and reproducible experimentation. They should be able to move fluidly between software architecture, implementation, experimental evaluation, and research communication. Strong coding skills are essential, particularly in Python, C++, JavaScript/TypeScript, or comparable languages and frameworks. Experience with SQL, declarative programming, data management, databases, visualization tools, HCI methods, AI/ML systems, optimization, or interactive data systems is highly desirable. Applicants should have a strong record of research and technical output, which may include peer-reviewed publications, open-source projects, deployed systems, research prototypes, technical reports, or major software artifacts. Experience presenting research work clearly and concisely is essential, as the lab meets frequently for short status updates and holds regular technical one-on-one meetings to examine research problems in depth. Minimum qualifications: Master\u2019s degree in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, HCI, AI, Data Systems, or a closely related field. At least six years of relevant research, research engineering, or senior technical experience. Immediate availability to start the position. Strong record of designing and building systems, tools, platforms, or research prototypes. Excellent programming ability in Python, C++, JavaScript/TypeScript, or comparable languages. Experience with reproducible research practices, GitHub workflows, documentation, and artifact management. Ability to evaluate systems through experiments, simulations, benchmarks, or user studies. Strong written and oral communication skills, including experience with technical presentations and LaTeX documents. Ability to work independently while contributing effectively to a collaborative research environment. The position offers a competitive salary and attractive benefits, including housing allowance, medical insurance, annual home leave allowance, and, where applicable, dependent private school fees. The researcher will have access to excellent research facilities and dedicated support for travel, computing, publications, and research-related expenses. Applications will be evaluated immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of research interests, a transcript or degree, and two letters of reference, all in PDF format. Questions regarding specifics for this position may be directed to Prof. Azza Abouzied at azza@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189807": {"title": "Research Scientist in the Division of Science (Computer Science) - Dr. Nizar Habash", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Computer Science: AD_Research_Computer Science Location: Abu Dhabi The Computational Approaches to Modeling Language (CAMeL) Lab at New York University Abu Dhabi invites applications for a Research Scientist / Scholar position in natural language processing (NLP), computational linguistics, artificial intelligence, and related areas. The successful candidate will contribute to and lead research initiatives aligned with the lab's mission while developing an independent research program. The CAMeL Lab conducts research in NLP, computational linguistics, and data science, with a particular focus on Arabic language technologies. Current research areas include grammatical error correction, text generation, computational morphology, syntactic parsing, dialect and non-standard language modeling, pedagogical applications, machine translation, and text analytics. Since its establishment in 2014, the lab has produced over 200 publications and more than 20 language resources and tools. The lab website is http://www.camel-lab.com/ .\u00a0 Google Scholar of the lab is at http://scholar.camel-lab.com/ . The position will target the position of Research Scientist (open rank; more than 3 years of PhD). We seek candidates with a PhD degree with at least 6 years in computer science, computer engineering, computational linguistics, language technologies, artificial intelligence, or a closely related field, along with substantial research experience and a strong publication record in leading journals and conferences. Candidates should demonstrate research leadership, the ability to develop and manage independent research projects, and expertise in machine learning and NLP. The successful candidate will be expected to lead innovative research projects, contribute to the development of open-source tools and resources, mentor junior researchers and students, publish in top-tier venues, and participate in collaborative research and grant-development activities. Knowledge of Arabic and experience in Arabic NLP are desirable but not required. The terms of employment are very competitive and include housing and educational subsidies for children. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree, a one-page summary of research accomplishments and interests, and at least 2 letters of recommendation, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email: nizar.habash@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194026": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Science (Computer Science) Dr. Yasir Zaki", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Computer Science: AD_Research_Computer Science Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Communication Networks Lab at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD), led by Yasir Zaki, invites applications for a Post-Doctoral Associate in Internet Measurement and Equitable Web Systems. Our lab develops measurement-driven approaches to understanding and improving the Internet and the Web. Our research spans Internet access and digital inequality, cellular and satellite connectivity, web performance and accessibility, network measurement, mobile systems, and technologies for connecting underserved populations. We are seeking an outstanding researcher who already has a demonstrated record of publishing rigorous work in leading networking, systems, web, or Internet-measurement venues. This is not an entry-level research-training position: the successful candidate will be expected to formulate research questions, lead projects, build and evaluate systems or measurement studies, publish in highly selective venues, and help mentor junior researchers. The successful candidate will lead research in one or more areas such as: Large-scale Internet and Web measurements. LEO Satellites. Internet access and digital inequality in developing and underserved regions. Web performance, accessibility, and multilingual Web experiences. Mobile and networked systems. Measurement-driven design of more equitable Internet infrastructure. AI-assisted optimization of Web and network performance. Auditing the availability, quality, and affordability of global connectivity. The candidate will have significant freedom to propose new directions that align with the lab\u2019s broader goal of building a more accessible and equitable Internet. Mandatory publication requirement Applicants must have a strong peer-reviewed publication record in leading venues relevant to this position. Qualifying conferences include: ACM SIGCOMM ACM Internet Measurement Conference (IMC) ACM CoNEXT USENIX NSDI ACM MobiCom ACM MobiSys The Web Conference ACM SIGMETRICS. At minimum, applicants must identify one qualifying paper in which they were the first or co-first author, or for which they can document an equivalent leading intellectual and technical contribution. A competitive applicant will normally have multiple strong research outputs appropriate to their career stage. Applications that do not demonstrate a publication record in at least one of the venues listed above will not be considered. General interest in networking, completed coursework, technical skills, unpublished projects, or publications only in unrelated venues do not satisfy this requirement. For collaborative publications, applicants must explain their personal contribution. Authorship by itself will not be treated as evidence of research leadership. Candidate requirements Applicants must: Hold a PhD\u2014or have completed all PhD requirements before starting\u2014in computer science, electrical or computer engineering, information science, or a closely related discipline. L ess than 5 years post receiving the Doctoral degree. Meet the mandatory publication requirement above. Demonstrate the ability to define and complete original research Possess strong programming, experimental, and quantitative-analysis skills. Have experience designing reproducible systems or measurement studies. Communicate research clearly in writing and presentations Be able to lead projects while collaborating effectively with researchers from different backgrounds. Be committed to ethical and reproducible research. Relevant experience may include network measurement, mobile systems, browser instrumentation, distributed experimentation, statistical analysis, causal inference, large-scale data collection, active or passive measurements, or production-quality research software. Application materials Applicants must submit: A cover letter A current CV and complete publication list; Two representative publications; A mandatory one-page evidence-based fit statement; Contact information for three academic or professional references. Required one-page fit statement The statement must explain why the applicant is specifically qualified for the work of the Communication Networks Lab. It must include: Two or three concrete connections between the applicant\u2019s prior research and specific work, methods, datasets, systems, or research questions pursued by our lab The applicant\u2019s personal intellectual and technical contribution to the submitted publications Concrete evidence of relevant expertise\u2014for example, a measurement platform built, dataset collected, system implemented, experiment designed, methodology developed, or finding established One research question or project the applicant could realistically lead at NYUAD, including the proposed method and expected contribution. Statements that merely express enthusiasm for networking, connectivity, digital inclusion, or the lab\u2019s topics\u2014without concrete evidence from the applicant\u2019s record\u2014will be treated as nonresponsive, and the application will not be considered further. References will be contacted only for shortlisted candidates. Appointment Questions may be directed to Yasir Zaki at yasir.zaki@nyu.edu. Information about the lab is available at the Communication Networks Lab website. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194027": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Science (Computer Science) Dr. Yasir Zaki", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Computer Science: AD_Research_Computer Science Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Communication Networks Lab at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD), led by Yasir Zaki, invites applications for a Postdoctoral Associate in Responsible AI and Computational Social Science. Our research examines how artificial intelligence, online platforms, and the Web shape individuals, societies, and access to information. Recent themes include large-language-model safety and bias, political behavior in AI systems, misinformation and online harms, platform and recommendation-system audits, multilingual and culturally inclusive AI evaluation, and biases in digital and scientific ecosystems. We seek an outstanding researcher who already has a demonstrated record of publishing rigorous work in leading AI, NLP, Web, computational-social-science, human-computer-interaction, or interdisciplinary-science venues. The successful candidate will be expected to formulate research questions, lead empirical projects, develop rigorous computational or experimental methods, publish in highly selective venues, and help mentor junior researchers. The successful candidate will lead research in one or more areas such as: Safety, bias, fairness, and political behavior in large language models Multilingual and culturally inclusive AI evaluation Misinformation, toxicity, and online-harm measurement and mitigation Auditing recommendation systems, search systems, and online platforms AI-mediated political communication and public opinion Social and behavioral effects of generative AI Computational analysis of digital and scientific ecosystems Human-centered evaluation of AI systems Reproducible datasets, benchmarks, and auditing infrastructure for responsible AI. The candidate will have substantial freedom to develop new research directions aligned with the lab\u2019s goal of creating a more trustworthy and socially responsible Web. Mandatory publication requirement Applicants must have a strong peer-reviewed publication record in leading venues relevant to this position Qualifying conferences include: ACM Web Conference; ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency (FAccT) International AAAI Conference on Web and Social Media (ICWSM) ACL EMNLP NAACL COLING AAAI ACM CHI ACM CSCW Qualifying journals include: Nature or relevant Nature-family journals, including Scientific Reports; Science and Science Advances PNAS or PNAS Nexus; National Science Review; IEEE Transactions on Computational Social Systems; ACM Transactions on the Web ACM Transactions on data science Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction; Journal of Computational Social Science. At minimum, applicants must identify one qualifying paper in which they were the first or co-first author, or for which they can document an equivalent leading intellectual and technical contribution. A competitive applicant will normally have multiple strong research outputs appropriate to their career stage. Applications that do not demonstrate a publication record in at least one of the venues listed above will not be considered. General interest in responsible AI, coursework, use of machine-learning tools, unpublished projects, or publications only in unrelated venues do not satisfy this requirement. For collaborative publications, applicants must explain their personal contribution. Authorship by itself will not be treated as evidence of research leadership. Candidate requirements Applicants must: Hold a PhD\u2014or have completed all PhD requirements before starting\u2014in computer science, information science, data science, computational social science, electrical or computer engineering, or a closely related discipline and be less than 5 years post-receipt of the doctoral degree. Meet the mandatory publication requirement above Demonstrate the ability to define and complete original, rigorous research Possess strong programming, experimental-design, and quantitative-analysis skills Have experience working with large-scale data, machine-learning models, human-subject studies, platform audits, or computational experiments Communicate research clearly in writing and presentations Be able to lead projects while collaborating across technical and social-science disciplines Be committed to ethical, transparent, and reproducible research. Relevant expertise may include NLP, large language models, computational social science, social-network analysis, causal inference, survey or behavioral experiments, recommender systems, algorithmic auditing, machine-learning evaluation, or human-computer interaction. Experience conducting multilingual, cross-cultural, or Global South-focused research is particularly valuable. Application materials Applicants must submit: A cover letter A current CV and complete publication list; Two representative publications; A mandatory one-page evidence-based fit statement; Contact information for three academic or professional references. Required one-page fit statement The statement must explain why the applicant is specifically qualified for the work of the Communication Networks Lab. It must include: Two or three concrete connections between the applicant\u2019s prior research and specific work, methods, datasets, systems, or research questions pursued by our lab The applicant\u2019s personal intellectual and technical contribution to the submitted publications Concrete evidence of relevant expertise\u2014for example, a measurement platform built, dataset collected, system implemented, experiment designed, methodology developed, or finding established One research question or project the applicant could realistically lead at NYUAD, including the proposed method and expected contribution. Statements that merely express enthusiasm for networking, connectivity, digital inclusion, or the lab\u2019s topics\u2014without concrete evidence from the applicant\u2019s record\u2014will be treated as nonresponsive, and the application will not be considered further. References will be contacted only for shortlisted candidates. Appointment Questions may be directed to Yasir Zaki at yasir.zaki@nyu.edu. Information about the lab is available at the Communication Networks Lab website. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191928": {"title": "Research Scientist in the Division Of Science (Mathematics) - Dr. Taoufik Hmidi", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Mathematics: AD_Research_Mathematics Location: Abu Dhabi The research group of Prof. Taoufik Hmidi at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD) is seeking a Research Scientist to conduct research in partial differential equations (PDEs), mathematical fluid dynamics, and related areas of mathematical physics. We particularly encourage applications from candidates with expertise in nonlinear PDEs, fluid dynamics, KAM theory, hyperbolic systems, kinetic equations, and plasma physics. Terms of employment include a competitive salary and benefits. The research group works on a broad range of problems in nonlinear partial differential equations, mathematical fluid dynamics, and mathematical physics. Research interests include global well-posedness and long time behavior of solutions to nonlinear PDEs; coherent structures in vortex dynamics; KAM theory and dynamical systems, control theory for PDEs; hyperbolic systems; and mathematical problems arising in plasma physics. Responsibilities of the position include to conduct independent and collaborative research in PDEs, mathematical fluid dynamics, KAM theory, or closely related areas. Participate actively in research seminars, working groups, and other scientific activities. Candidates should have an excellent scientific background and a strong research record. In addition to holding a Ph.D. in Mathematics with at least six years of research experience. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree/ copy of highest degree, research statement, and at least 2 contact references/ recommendation letters, all in PDF format. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. If you have any questions, please reach out to Taoufik Hmidi email: th2644@nyu.edu About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194672": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Science (Mathematics) - Dr. Elena Beretta", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Mathematics: AD_Research_Mathematics Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE  The Mathematics Program in the Division of Science at New York University Abu Dhabi seeks to recruit a Post-Doctoral Associate to work with Professor Elena Beretta on inverse problems for nonlinear partial differential equations arising in the life sciences and medical imaging. The successful applicant will contribute to a research project at the intersection of inverse problems, nonlinear partial differential equations, numerical analysis, mathematical biology, medical imaging, and scientific machine learning. The project will focus on the recovery of unknown initial states, physical parameters, coefficients, sources, and geometric structures from indirect, partial, and noisy measurements. Particular emphasis will be placed on nonlinear PDE models arising in tumor growth, cardiac electrophysiology, and medical imaging. The project will build on recent work by Professor Beretta and collaborators on inverse problems for nonlinear phase-field and reaction-diffusion models of tumor evolution, inverse problems in cardiac electrophysiology, nonlinear conductivity equations, and computational reconstruction methods based on regularization and interpretable neural networks. In the context of tumor growth, possible research directions include the reconstruction of earlier tumor configurations from later-time measurements, the identification of biological parameters and nutrient distributions, and the analysis of nonlinear phase-field systems coupling tumor evolution, nutrient transport, chemotaxis, and biomarkers. In cardiac electrophysiology, the project may address the identification of ischemic or electrically inactive regions, the recovery of anisotropic and discontinuous conductivities, and reconstruction from localized or finitely many boundary measurements. Related questions in medical imaging may include electrical impedance tomography, inverse conductivity problems, and shape or interface reconstruction. The candidate will investigate both the mathematical foundations and the computational aspects of these inverse problems. The analytical component of the project may include the well-posedness of the forward problem, identifiability and uniqueness, conditional stability estimates, shape sensitivity, and regularization of nonlinear inverse problems. The computational component will involve the design and implementation of efficient reconstruction algorithms, including iterative regularization, PDE-constrained optimization, shape and topological derivative methods, and neural-network-based approaches. A central objective will be to develop scientific machine-learning techniques that preserve the structure of the underlying mathematical models. Possible directions include physics-informed or physics-constrained neural networks, neural operators, learned iterative methods, reduced-order models, and interpretable neural architectures in which trainable parameters are directly related to physical coefficients, initial states, sources, or interfaces. Particular attention will be devoted to the mathematical justification, stability, robustness, interpretability, and computational efficiency of the proposed algorithms. The candidate will work in a multidisciplinary research environment involving faculty members, postdoctoral researchers, graduate students, and undergraduate students in mathematics, scientific computing, data science, biology, and biomedical applications. The successful applicant will be encouraged to participate actively in seminars, workshops, and collaborative research activities within the Mathematics Program and the Division of Science. Applicants must have a PhD in Mathematics, Applied Mathematics, Computational Science, Scientific Computing, Mathematical Biology, Biomedical Engineering, or a closely related field, with no more than 5 ears post receipt of PhD. A strong background in partial differential equations, inverse and ill-posed problems, numerical analysis, scientific computing, or mathematical modeling is required. Candidates with experience in nonlinear elliptic or parabolic PDEs, phase-field models, reaction-diffusion systems, regularization methods, PDE-constrained optimization, shape reconstruction, or medical imaging are particularly encouraged to apply. Strong programming skills are required. Experience with Python, Julia, MATLAB, or C++, and with machine-learning frameworks such as PyTorch, JAX, or TensorFlow, is desirable. Previous experience with finite-element, finite-difference, spectral, or iso-geometric methods, as well as with physics-informed neural networks, neural operators, or interpretable machine-learning methods, would be an advantage. The successful applicant should have excellent written and oral communication skills and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively. For consideration, applicants need to submit a cover letter, a curriculum vitae including a complete publication list, a statement of research accomplishments and interests, one or two representative publications or preprints, and three letters of reference, all in PDF format. The research statement should explain how the applicant\u2019s previous work and future research interests relate to inverse problems, nonlinear PDEs, numerical analysis, mathematical biology, medical imaging, or scientific machine learning. Questions regarding the research project may be directed to Professor Elena Beretta at eb147@nyu.edu. The terms of employment are very competitive and include a comprehensive benefits package. Applications will be accepted immediately, and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. The anticipated starting date is 1 February 2027, and the initial appointment will be for one year. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195018": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Science (Mathematics) - Dr. Weiren Zhao", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Mathematics: AD_Research_Mathematics Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE The Division of Science, Mathematics Department at New York University Abu Dhabi is looking to recruit a postdoctoral associate to work with Weiren Zhao on subjects including (in no particular order): Stability/Instability of coherent structures in 2D/3D flows. Stability/Instability in geophysical fluids and plasma physics. Spectral theory for non-self-adjoint operators. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Mathematics and Applied Mathematics (or be close to completing one). The candidate must have received the doctoral degree within the past 5 years. Ph.D. holders with a strong publication record and a background in analysis and PDE are encouraged to apply. Applications will be accepted immediately, and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. To be considered, all applicants need to submit cover letter curriculum vitae with a full publication list statement of research interests representative publications three recommendation letters. The anticipated appointment start date is September 2027, and applications will be considered on a rolling basis. If you have any questions, please email wz19@nyu.edu . About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189429": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate/ Associate Research Scientist in the Division of Science (Biological Physics) - Dr. George Shubeita", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Science: AD_Physics: AD_Research_Physics Location: Abu Dhabi The Shubeita laboratory in the program of Physics, New York University Abu Dhabi, seeks to recruit a Post-Doctoral Associate or Associate Research Scientist to work on unravelling principles that drive cellular organization. The successful applicant will lead research projects to study the interplay between active cytoskeletal transport, cytoskeletal organization, and the physical driving forces of macromolecular crowding. Research will take place within a multidisciplinary group of Post-doctoral and Pre-doctoral researchers trained in physics, biology, and chemistry with whom the successful candidate will collaborate closely. Applicants must have a PhD in physics or biological physics. Excellent analytical skills are required and prior experience in soft matter or biological physics research is preferable. Prior experience in the experimental techniques used in the lab such as optical tweezers and quantitative imaging is advantageous but not required. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. The successful candidate will be appointed as a Postdoctoral Associate if they received their PhD within the past five years, or as an Associate Research Scientist if more than five years have elapsed since the degree was awarded. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree / copy of highest degree, a one-page summary of research accomplishments and interests and at least two letters of recommendation, all in PDF format. If you have any questions, please email:\u00a0nyuad.shubeitalab@nyu.edu About NYUAD: https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194581": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in the Division of Social Science (SSEL) - Dr. Andrzej Baranski", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Social Science: AD_Social Research and Public Policy: AD_Research_Social Research and Public Policy Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE  The Social Science Experimental Laboratory (SSEL) at New York University Abu Dhabi invites applications for a Post-Doctoral Associate position. We seek an ambitious, well-trained, and collaborative scholar with a PhD in economics or a related field and must be within 3 years of having received their PhD. Candidates must have expertise in experimental methods and prior experience conducting social science experiments (lab, lab-in-the-field, online, and/or field). Researchers working at the intersection of experiments and other methods (theory, empirical) as well those studying human behavior and artificial intelligence are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate is expected to: Conduct and publish research in leading journals in social science or interdisciplinary outlets. Collaborate with SSEL board members and affiliated faculty. Contribute to the smooth operation of the laboratory. Participate in the academic activities of the division. This position does not include teaching duties and the SSEL provides financial support for travel and research. The successful applicant will join a vibrant community of economists, political scientists, and sociologists, conducting cutting-edge experimental research on human behavior. The Social Science division holds active seminar series, as well as workshops and events year round. The SSEL also maintains close ties with the Center for Behavioral Institutional Design (CBID), thereby offering many opportunities for collaboration, idea exchange, and research visibility. The position offers a highly competitive salary, generous benefits (including housing, transportation, travel allowances, and educational subsidies for children), and full health insurance and retirement contributions. The UAE does not levy income tax. Appointments are for two years, renewable for at least one additional year subject to satisfactory performance. Applications will be accepted immediately. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcript of degree/ copy of highest degree, research statement, and at least 2 recommendation letters, all in PDF format. Please feel free to share this announcement with PhD candidates, recent graduates, and others who may be interested. For questions, please contact Andrzej Baranski ( a.baranski@nyu.edu ) or Nikos Nikiforakis\u00a0 (nn30@nyu.edu). About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/186097": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate | Quantum Institute", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the President: Office of Global Science and Technology Location: New York, NY Seeking \"Expression of Interest\" from postdoctoral researchers interested in Quantum Physics at the Quantum Institute at NYU. The Quantum Institute seeks to create a highly stimulating environment that engages the best and brightest faculty and students to conduct deep and impactful research. This project is a collaborative work with IBM Q researchers and NYU faculty in the Quantum Institute. It involves expertise in quantum algorithm and requires dilution refrigerator characterization of the quantum circuits.\u00a0 Areas of interest include: 1. Hamiltonian simulation e.g. time-dependent Schrodinger equation, eigenstates, thermal states; 2. Classical simulation of quantum circuits; 3. resource estimation of early fault tolerant algorithms. This project involves collaborating broadly with theoretical and experimental groups within and outside NYU and IBM. Team work, good communication skills and discipline in summarizing results are necessary. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $62,500 - $70,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Completion of PhD in Physics, Computer Science, Engineering, Mathematics, or other discipline aligned with Quantum Institute faculty research by the start date. Please follow instructions in the Interfolio application and upload the following documents: A brief cover letter (no more than one (1) page) Your curriculum vitae Your research statement (1-2 pages) One (1) sample publication Two (2) contact reference"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194708": {"title": "Program Officer, Africa Support Project", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Robert F. Wagner Graduate School of Public Service: Researchers Location: New York, New York Background The Center on International Cooperation (CIC) at New York University is launching a two-year project to strengthen African member states' engagement in multilateral negotiations at the United Nations. The project seeks to make available dedicated research, analytical, and convening capacity that will support African Member states, using the established framework of engagement within the Africa Group and the Permanent Observer Mission of the African Union. The focus for the first phase will be on three thematic areas: financing for development; global health governance; and AI and digital technologies. The Program Officer is the position responsible for driving this work day to day. Purpose of the Position The Program Officer will support the design and delivery of the project's core activities \u2014 policy briefs, expert convenings, deep dives, and the sequencing of New York discussions with African Union processes in Addis Ababa \u2014 in close, sustained coordination with the AU Permanent Observer Mission and in service of the UN Africa Group's negotiating priorities. The role combines substantive policy research with hands-on program management and diplomatic convening, and requires the judgment to operate effectively inside a sensitive, politically attentive multilateral environment. Key Responsibilities Serve as CIC's dedicated point of contact with the AU Permanent Observer Mission, Africa Group Member States Missions, and the African Union Commission building and maintaining the day-to-day working relationship on which the project depends. Support the research and drafting analytical policy briefs across the three thematic areas, timed to specific decision points on the UN negotiating calendar, in consultation with member states and experts. Design and organize convenings that bring Africa Group member state representatives together with subject-matter experts. Plan and facilitate expert deep dives on complex or emerging issues within each thematic area. Maintain a living map linking the New York negotiating calendar to the corresponding African Union policy calendar in Addis Ababa, and identify and sustain relationships with the relevant AU Commission department and contact person for each thematic area. Commission and manage inputs from short-term consultants and CIC's broader expert network where specialized expertise is required. Track and report on project indicators, contribute to funder reporting, and support the quarterly advisory group process. Represent CIC professionally and diplomatically in UN and AU-related settings at expert level, exercising discretion appropriate to the sensitivity of the diplomatic environment. Contribute to CIC's broader institutional knowledge on African multilateral engagement, including through occasional public-facing writing or events. Deliverables 4\u20136 policy briefs per thematic area per year (12\u201318 total annually), delivered on schedule against the UN negotiating calendar. 8\u201310 expert convenings per year, each producing a talking points document. 3\u20134 expert deep dives per year across the three thematic areas. A maintained NY\u2013AU linkage map, reviewed and updated quarterly. Quarterly updates to the project advisory group and contributions to annual funder reports. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $80,000 - $95,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Required Qualifications Advanced degree (Master's or equivalent) in international relations, public policy, economics, public health, law, or a related field. A minimum of 7 -10 years of relevant professional experience in multilateral diplomacy, international organizations, policy research, or a closely related field, ideally including direct experience with UN processes and/or African Union institutions. Strong substantive knowledge of at least one of the project's three thematic areas (financing for development, global health governance, or AI/digital governance), with the ability to learn quickly across the other two. Excellent analytical writing skills, with demonstrated ability to produce concise, negotiation-ready policy material under deadline pressure. Demonstrated experience convening or facilitating multi-stakeholder dialogues involving government officials, diplomats, or senior policymakers. Sound diplomatic judgment and cultural fluency in working with African diplomatic missions and multilateral institutions. Fluency in English required; working proficiency in French and/or Arabic strongly preferred given the composition of the UN Africa Group. Desired Qualifications Prior work experience with an African Union organ, a UN mission, a member state foreign ministry, or a research institution focused on African multilateral engagement. Existing professional networks among UN Africa Group missions, AU Commission departments, or relevant technical experts. Experience managing donor-funded programs and reporting to institutional or philanthropic funders."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/196033": {"title": "2027-2028 Open Rank Clinical Professor of Accounting (full-time, non tenure-track)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Leonard N. Stern School of Business: Accounting Department Location: New York, NY Posted: Oct 26, 2026 Deadline: Nov 24, 2026 The Department of Accounting invites applications for a full-time non-tenure-track, clinical faculty appointment (at the assistant, associate, or full levels) in the area of Financial Accounting, Managerial Accounting, Auditing, and Accounting Information Systems starting in September 2027. The NYU Stern School of Business is recognized internationally as being among the world\u2019s best graduate and undergraduate business schools. Located in the heart of Greenwich Village in New York City, Stern has an internationally diverse faculty and student body. More information on Stern can be found at www.stern.nyu.edu . In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is: Clinical Assistant Professor: $110,000 - $170,000 Clinical Associate Professor: $125,000 -\u00a0 $195,000 Clinical Professor: $140,000 - $230,000 This pay range represents base pay only (for 12 months) and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, clinical compensation, or other items. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. A candidate for a full-time clinical appointment must present evidence of an outstanding teaching career in academia or a distinguished career as a practitioner. Candidates are required to have a minimum of a Master\u2019s degree, have strong interpersonal and collaborative abilities, and show strong pedagogical skills including classroom performance, curriculum development, and teaching innovation in the areas related to Accounting.\u00a0 The candidate will be expected to teach various accounting courses, such as auditing, accounting information systems, and other financial and managerial accounting courses, at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Please submit your curriculum vitae, teaching and service statement, a list of three references, and any additional materials relevant to evaluating the candidate\u2019s potential via Interfolio no later than November 24th, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195832": {"title": "2027-2028 Assistant Professor of Economics (Full-Time, Tenure Track)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Leonard N. Stern School of Business: Economics Department Location: New York Posted: Oct 19, 2026 Deadline: Dec 01, 2026 The Economics Department at the Stern School of Business, New York University, has an opening for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Economics, in any field, to start in September 2027. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $150,000 - $250,000. This pay range represents base pay only (for 9 months) and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, clinical compensation, or other items. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Candidates should have a Ph.D. or must have successfully defended their doctoral dissertation prior to appointment. They are expected to be productive researchers and effective teachers at undergraduate, MBA, and PhD levels. The online, completed application should include a curriculum vitae, three letters of reference, published and working papers, as well as any additional materials relevant to evaluating the candidate\u2019s potential and should be received no later than December 1st, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195963": {"title": "Executive Director and Senior Research Scientist, NYU Shanghai Global Institute for Longevity and Resilience", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai Location: Shanghai, China Posted: Oct 19, 2026 General Introduction The Executive Director and Senior Research Scientist will work closely with the Founding Director to advance the academic and strategic development of the NYU Shanghai Global Institute for Longevity and Resilience. The Institute is a university-wide, interdisciplinary platform that brings together expertise across public health, social science, urban and environmental studies, computer and data science, engineering, business, economics, finance, and policy to promote healthy longevity and resilient societies. The Executive Director will provide both intellectual and executive leadership for the Institute, working with the Founding Director, Hub Leads, faculty, and external partners to advance high-impact interdisciplinary research, secure external research funding, and maximize the Institute\u2019s scientific and policy impact. The role will also focus on integrating research expertise and resources across NYU Shanghai, developing strategic partnerships, supporting faculty and research talent, managing the Institute\u2019s pilot research program, and strengthening its connections across Shanghai, China, Asia, and the NYU global network. The position has a flexible start date, with a preference for candidates who can begin in Spring 2027 . The appointment start date is subject to immigration regulations for the country of employment. Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to those at NYU New York and other U.S. institutions. Main Responsibilities Research and Funding Development Work with the Founding Director and Hub Leads to develop and implement the Institute\u2019s research strategy and interdisciplinary research initiatives. Identify and connect existing faculty and staff expertise, research facilities, and ongoing research programs across NYU Shanghai to facilitate resource sharing, strengthen collaboration across the four Hubs, and develop competitive joint research initiatives and funding proposals. Work closely with faculty to identify funding opportunities, develop interdisciplinary collaborations, and lead or support the preparation of competitive domestic and international grant proposals to secure external research funding. Develop and administer the Institute\u2019s pilot research funding program and help translate promising pilot projects into external grants, publications, partnerships, and larger-scale initiatives. Build a sustained pipeline of interdisciplinary and multi-institutional research proposals and projects. Faculty and Research Talent Development Support faculty, particularly junior faculty and early-career researchers, in developing research programs, collaborative networks, and external funding. Help recruit and engage faculty and researchers whose expertise strengthens the Institute and NYU Shanghai\u2019s research priorities. Develop opportunities for postdoctoral researchers, visiting scholars, students, and faculty exchanges across NYU and partner institutions. Strategic Partnerships and Resource Development Develop strategic relationships with universities, government agencies, healthcare organizations, foundations, industry, and other external partners. Work closely with the Founding Director and relevant University offices to identify, cultivate, and engage prospective donors and philanthropic partners. Help develop funding opportunities and materials for prospective donors and support philanthropic gifts, sponsored research, and other external resources. Strengthen partnerships across Shanghai, China, and Asia while leveraging the NYU global network. Research Translation and External Engagement Maximize the scientific, policy, and societal impact of the Institute\u2019s research by promoting high-quality scholarly publications, translating research findings into policy and practice, and engaging relevant academic, government, industry, and community stakeholders. Develop the Institute\u2019s annual conference and other scholarly convenings as platforms for research collaboration, partnership development, and broader impact. Represent the Institute, as appropriate, and strengthen its academic visibility and reputation in China and internationally. Institute Leadership and Operations Work with the Founding Director to develop annual priorities, work plans, budgets, and measures of progress. Coordinate Institute staff and resources and work with relevant NYU Shanghai offices to support research, grants, partnerships, communications, and operations. Support the Founding Director in coordinating the Advisory Board and track and report on the Institute\u2019s major activities and outcomes. Required Education PhD or other advanced degree in a field relevant to the Institute\u2019s mission. Required Experience Minimum of 8\u201310 years of research experience Significant experience in academic research, research leadership, research program development, or leadership of a university-based research institute or center. Demonstrated excellence in independent research, as evidenced by a strong record of scholarly publications, research impact, and other significant research accomplishments. Demonstrated success in securing external research funding, developing competitive grant proposals, and building interdisciplinary research collaborations. Experience working closely with faculty and supporting research and talent development. Demonstrated ability to develop relationships with academic institutions, government agencies, foundations and donors, healthcare organizations, industry, and other external partners. Experience working across disciplines and institutions; experience with China and international research partnerships is highly desirable. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Strong research-development, grant-writing, strategic planning, and project-management skills. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and ability to work effectively with faculty and diverse external stakeholders. Excellent written and oral communication skills in English; professional proficiency in Chinese strongly preferred. Ability to work effectively in a collaborative, interdisciplinary, and international academic environment. Applicants should submit a current curriculum vitae and personal statement via Interfolio. The personal statement should outline the candidate\u2019s vision for advancing the Institute\u2019s research, external funding, and impact, including how they would leverage NYU Shanghai\u2019s existing strengths and the NYU global network, and navigate the opportunities and challenges of operating within a Sino-US joint venture university. Additionally, applicants will be prompted to enter the names and email addresses of three referees. Each referee will be contacted to upload a reference letter through Interfolio. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you have any questions on the application process, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment at shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . For further questions on the position details, please contact Yvonne Liu at yvonne.j.liu@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the \ufb01rst higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr . About the Institute NYU Shanghai Global Institute for Longevity and Resilience is a university-wide, interdisciplinary, and globally connected platform that brings together NYU Shanghai\u2019s strengths in public health, the social sciences, psychology, neuroscience, urban and environmental studies, computer and data science, engineering, business, economics, finance, and public policy. Its mission is to generate interdisciplinary knowledge, support talent development, advance and evaluate responsible technologies, inform policy and practice, and build strategic partnerships that promote healthy longevity and resilience across the life course. Anchored in Shanghai, rooted in China, focused on Asia as a key region for comparative research and training, and globally connected through NYU, the Institute will organize its work through four interconnected research Hubs: Healthy Longevity and Life-course Resilience; Earth Systems and Urban Resilience; Longevity Finance, Silver Economy, and Social Resilience; and Health Technology. Through shared research questions, responsibly governed data and methods, real-world research settings in Pudong, Shanghai, and China, comparative work across Asia and the NYU global network, and translation into policy and practice, the four Hubs will jointly explore how people can live longer and healthier lives and how societies can become more inclusive, equitable, sustainable, and resilient."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195967": {"title": "Associate Professor or Professor of Economics, Tenured", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Social Science: AD_Economics Location: Abu Dhabi Posted: Oct 26, 2026 The Economics Program in the Division of Social Science Division at New York University Abu Dhabi (NYUAD) is inviting applications from economists with outstanding research records. Strong preference will be given to senior candidates for a position as Associate Professor or Professor of Economics (tenured). The Economics faculty at NYUAD is ranked among the top departments in Asia, and it offers a dynamic and diverse environment. The position comes with generous research support. NYUAD expects successful candidates to publish in high-quality, internationally refereed journals. The position features a standard teaching load of three courses per year. The terms of employment are very competitive and include generous start-up and ongoing research funds, as well as housing subsidies and educational subsidies for children. Appointment will begin on September 1, 2027 or September 1, 2028, subject to final budget approval. We will consider applicants with an active research agenda in all areas of economics. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for faculty/academic staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to faculty/academic staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. This position requires a Ph.D. in Economics. We seek individuals who are strong scholars with a substantial publication record. To apply for this position, please submit the following items: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover Letter Teaching Statement Research Statement Recent teaching evaluations (optional) Up to 5 representative publications or other writing samples Names and email addresses of 3 references (each will be contacted to upload their reference via Interfolio). Senior candidates may delay having references sent until they know if they are finalists. We will review applications on a rolling basis and continue until the position is filled. We anticipate that successful candidates can start the appointment and relocate to Abu Dhabi in the academic year 2027-2026, subject to budget approval. NYUAD is committed to equality and is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply For questions about this position, please email nyuad.academicrecruitment@nyu.edu . Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values inclusion, belonging, and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a globally diverse campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD #nyuadfacultycareers"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/196308": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate, Sensory Aging and Function", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Anthropology Department Location: New York, NY Posted: Oct 23, 2026 A Post-Doctoral Associate position is available in the New York University Department of Anthropology for a longitudinal study of the rhesus macaques of Cayo Santiago, Puerto Rico, as part of a new U19 award from the National Institute on Aging. The position is initially for one year, potentially renewable for up to five years. Post-Doctoral Associate A Post-Doctoral Associate is sought to undertake research on sensory aging and function across the lifespan in the Cayo Santiago rhesus macaques, using multi-omic measures to understand how social, environmental, and life-history factors shape sensory phenotypes. This PDA will work with Prof. James Higham, and in close collaboration with Prof. Amanda Melin, University of Calgary. The PDA will access and analyse samples in CBRU\u2019s long-term biobank, as well as new samples being collected both cross-sectionally and longitudinally. The available data provide an opportunity to investigate how individuals\u2019 sensory systems differ in molecular composition and putative function and health with age, and how these differences are shaped by their social and ecological environments. The position offers opportunities to collaborate on multiple ongoing and planned research studies, develop experience in new methods and techniques, and contribute to dissemination of findings through publications and conferences. The PDA will write manuscripts, and disseminate results via conference and public presentation. The PDA will spend time at NYU and UCalgary for training, sample processing and analyses. The position is available immediately, but later start dates are negotiable. Key Responsibilities Oversee the processing of biological sample (tissues, excreta and body fluids) data Work with PIs to organize timelines and analysis priorities Establish standard operating protocols for analysis Write and publish peer-reviewed manuscripts Disseminate results via public and academic presentations This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $60,000-65,000 per year. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A PhD in animal biology, biological anthropology, molecular ecology or a related field will be required by the onset of the position. Experience working with molecular techniques in a biosafety laboratory (e.g., DNA/ RNA extraction/ PCR/ Library Preparation/ Proteomics). Evidence of strong organizational and communication abilities. Track record of publishing papers in peer-reviewed journals. Applicants should submit a one-page cover letter, a CV, and the names and addresses of 3 referees, via Interfolio. We will begin reviewing applications immediately on receipt."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/196313": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Anthropology Department Location: New York, NY Posted: Oct 23, 2026 Post-Doctoral Associate A Post-Doctoral Associate (PDA) based at New York University (NYU) is required to work on topics related to longitudinal aging in the long-running (>80 years) study of rhesus macaque biology on Cayo Santiago, Puerto Rico. The position is funded by the National Institute on Aging, in collaboration with a multi-institution team of investigators. The study seeks to determine the impact of hurricanes on the pace of aging across different domains (physiological, physical, molecular, aging), and the impact of social buffering on mitigating these effects. The PDA will integrate data from these different aging domains, and will be expected to produce manuscripts on the impacts of hurricanes on the multidimensional components of aging, and the modulating impacts of social buffering on these effects. The position is available from November 1st 2026 for one year, renewable for further years depending on performance and the continued availability of funding. Later start dates are negotiable. This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. We welcome applications from international candidates; however, please note that NYU is not an E-Verify employer and therefore unable to support STEM OPT extensions. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $60,000-65,000 per year. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. A PhD in zoology, animal biology, biological anthropology, or a related field. Evidence of active outputs, such as peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations is strongly preferred. Previous experience working directly in nonhuman primate systems is an advantage but not essential. Applicants should submit a cover letter outlining their training, background, and suitability for the position, along with a CV and the names and addresses of 3 referees, via Interfolio. Referees will only be contacted for shortlisted applicants."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195994": {"title": "Laboratory Associate (Tian Lab)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Center for Neural Science Location: New York, NY Posted: Oct 20, 2026 This position is based in New York and the selected candidate will be expected to work onsite as of their effective start date. Laboratory of Cognitive Neural Systems (PI: Lucas Tian) Center for Neural Science & Department of Biomedical Engineering | New York University The Laboratory of Cognitive Neural Systems, based in NYU\u2019s Washington Square campus, is seeking a Laboratory Associate to assist with the day-to-day operations pertaining to animal care. These operations include maintenance of research animals/management/training, assistance with surgical procedures, ensuring regulatory compliance , managing/ordering lab supplies, and other related responsibilities. These operations will contribute to the lab\u2019s mission to understand the neuroscience of learning, abstraction, and generalization\u2014abilities critical for intelligence. In addition to these core responsibilities, there will be an opportunity to contribute across other diverse lab functions, including experiments and data analysis. This is expected to be a long-term position with the opportunity to help build a new lab and grow alongside it in a position of impact, including helping to build a lab culture of rigor, curiosity and collegiality. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $58,656 - $70,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Required qualifications: Bachelor\u2019s degree in Neuroscience, Biology or related field Diligent, responsible, and pays great attention to detail. Experience working with animals in a laboratory setting. Preferred qualifications: Licensed Veterinary Technician CV, brief description of experience and interests (1/2 page), contact information for 2-3\u00a0references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195400": {"title": "Assistant Professor in AI-Enabled Chemistry", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Arts and Science (A&S): Chemistry Department Location: New York Posted: Oct 12, 2026 The Department of Chemistry at New York University (NYU) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the area of AI-Enabled Chemistry , broadly defined. Particular attention will be given to candidates whose research develops and applies innovative approaches in machine learning/artificial intelligence to accelerate discovery in one or more areas of molecular sciences. Successful candidates must have a Ph.D. in chemistry or related field and demonstrate the potential to conduct pioneering research and teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The Department of Chemistry is continuing a significant growth plan, which includes the recent creation of the Simons Center for Computational Physical Chemistry and the Center for Chemical Biology Research; recently hired senior and junior faculty members add to the university's expansions in AI and quantum science and the creation of the Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. New faculty would also benefit from, and participate in, significant interdisciplinary programs within the Washington Square campus, as well as with NYU\u2019s Tandon School of Engineering, Langone School of Medicine, and NYU\u2019s Shanghai and Abu Dhabi campuses. Candidates should submit a curriculum vitae, a detailed description of research plans and interests, and a statement of teaching experience and interests. \u00a0The teaching statement may reflect plans to create an inclusive teaching and research mentoring environment. The application should also include three letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin on November 7, 2026, and will continue on a rolling basis. The earliest anticipated start date is September 1, 2027, subject to budgetary and administrative approval. Please submit applications through the following : apply.interfolio.com/195400 . Questions about this position can be sent by email to: chemistry.search@nyu.edu In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $110,000 to $130,000. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Arts & Science at NYU is at the heart of a leading research university that spans the globe. We seek scholars of the highest caliber who are sensitive to the needs of and possess an interest in working in a broad academic community with different viewpoints and backgrounds. NYU affirms the value of differing perspectives on the world as we strive to build the strongest possible university with the widest reach. To learn more about the Arts & Science commitment to inclusive excellence, please read here: https://as.nyu.edu/departments/facultyexcellence.html ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/196000": {"title": "Tenured/Tenure-Track Position in Political Science  (China)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Shanghai: Shanghai: Arts and Sciences: Political Science Location: Shanghai, China Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Deadline: Nov 30, 2026 NYU Shanghai is currently inviting applications for a position at the rank of assistant, associate, or full professor in Political Science, with a research focus on China. For further details about the research centers and institutes at NYU Shanghai, please visit this webpage . Terms of employment at NYU Shanghai are comparable to NYU New York and other U.S. institutions with respect to research start-up funds and compensation, and they include housing subsidies and educational subsidies for children. Faculty may in certain cases have the opportunity to spend time at NYU New York and other sites of the NYU Global Network, engaging in both research and teaching. We seek candidates who have completed or are close to completing a Ph.D. in Political Science or a closely related discipline and whose work demonstrates excellence in empirical research and high-quality classroom instruction. We invite applications from scholars across all subfields of political science whose research has a substantial focus on China, broadly conceived. We welcome a wide range of substantive interests and methodological approaches to empirical research. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to join a vibrant research community housed in the NYU Shanghai Social Sciences Area and its research centers . Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of research, statement of teaching interests, and at least one writing sample via Interfolio. Additionally, those applying at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor (without tenure) will be prompted to enter the names and email addresses of at least three evaluators. Each referee will be contacted to upload a confidential letter of recommendation through Interfolio. Applications may be received until Monday, November 30, 2026 . If you have any questions, please email the NYU Shanghai NY Office of Faculty Recruitment shanghai.faculty.recruitment@nyu.edu . About NYU Shanghai NYU Shanghai is the third degree-granting campus within New York University\u2019s Global Network. It is the first higher education joint venture in China authorized to grant degrees that are accredited in the U.S. as well as in China. All teaching is conducted in English. A research university with liberal arts and science at its core, NYU Shanghai resides in one of the world's great cities with a vibrant intellectual community. NYU Shanghai recruits scholars of the highest caliber who are committed to NYU's global vision of transformative teaching and innovative research and who embody the global society in which we live. NYU\u2019s Global Network includes degree-granting campuses in New York, Shanghai, and Abu Dhabi, complemented by thirteen additional academic centers across the world. Faculty and students may circulate within the Network in pursuit of common research interests and cross-cultural, interdisciplinary endeavors, both local and global. For people in the EU, click here for information on your privacy rights under GDPR: www.nyu.edu/it/gdpr."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195714": {"title": "Research Scholar ( Part-time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Posted: Oct 14, 2026 PART TIME RESEARCH \\SCHOLAR New York University Tandon School of Engineering The Riviere Robot Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Part Time Research Scholar \u00a0to work with Prof. Benjamin Riviere New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY,\u00a0is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. At the Riviere Robot Lab (RRL), we develop autonomous robotics research with theory, software, and experiments. Our methods are at the intersection of machine learning, discrete and continuous optimization, dynamical systems, and hardware systems. Our applications are focused in spacecraft robotics to enable new capabilities such as in-orbit servicing, assembly and manufacturing . Responsibilities The selected candidate will be responsible for: conducting research on autonomy algorithms with machine learning and classical methods. working on exploration problems in reinforcement learning applied to robotic manipulation. Working on mathematical formulation, algorithm development, and hardware implementation. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $30/hour depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is in the Fall of 2026\u00a0 for a period of 3 months.\u00a0 Renwal is available for Spring 2027 for an additional 3 months.\u00a0 This position is expected to be for 4-5 months for each term. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately 27 hours per week. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $30/hour depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification Expected start date and period of employment Expected start date is in the Fall of 2026\u00a0 for a period of 3 months.\u00a0 Renwal is available for Spring 2027 for an additional 3 months.\u00a0 This position is expected to be for 4-5 months for each term. Part time appointments would require a work schedule of approximately 27 hours per week. Candidates should have at least a BS in Computer Science or a related field. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated background in robotics, c omputer science and engineering as exemplified by a strong publication record. Previous experience in Computer Science is desired . Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit the following information: Cover letter up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, names and contact information for three references. All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: ert215@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195906": {"title": "Collaborative Arts, Associate/Full Professor, Department Chair", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tisch School of the Arts: Collaborative Arts Location: New York Posted: Oct 26, 2026 Deadline: Nov 24, 2026 The Department - The Department of Collaborative Arts at Tisch School of the Arts, New York University, seeks an accomplished, visionary Chair to lead its innovative Bachelor of Fine Arts program beginning in Fall 2027. This search is for an Associate Professor or full Professor tenure line (pending successful review) who will serve as Chair. The Chair position is a renewable 3-year term, upon successful review. The Department of Collaborative Arts is an innovative, rigorous, and ambitious 4-year undergraduate interdisciplinary arts BFA program founded to cultivate a community of artists eager to break boundaries across all art disciplines and imagine new possibilities for artistic expressions. At its core lies a powerful intersection of hands-on art making, community building, collaborative spirit, and interdisciplinary arts education at the world's first global network university. Department Overview & Philosophy - The Department of Collaborative Arts educates the next generation of artists and creative leaders through a unique interdisciplinary curriculum that encourages them to work across traditional boundaries of artistic practice. Students engage with a broad range of forms and disciplines, including performance, acting, dance, film and moving image, music and sound, visual art, writing, emerging media and technology, and critical and cultural studies. Rather than asking students to define themselves through a single discipline, Collaborative Arts provides them with the tools, experiences, and intellectual frameworks to develop artistic practices that move among forms, methodologies, technologies, and fields of knowledge. The curriculum combines hands-on artistic practice with historical, theoretical, and critical inquiry, culminating in advanced interdisciplinary work and a senior Capstone. Guided by experimentation, curiosity, and rigor, this program is far more than an amalgamation of all areas represented at Tisch, it is a pioneering area of study that promotes a bold vision for cross-disciplinary creation. Responsibilities - The Chair of Collaborative Arts will provide dynamic intellectual vision for the department\u2019s faculty, staff, and student body, driving a curriculum that prioritizes collaboration, experimentation, artistic inquiry, an d the c reation of original work. In their administrative capacity, the Chair oversees day-to-day operations, strategic procedures, budgetary initiatives, enrollment targets, and overall programmatic activity. Personnel management is a core focus, as the Chair directs faculty and staff recruitment, hiring, formal reviews, future tenure-line development, and ongoing mentorship. Additionally, the Chair leads the creation, organization, and implementation of curricular frameworks while strategically managing facilities, cutting-edge technologies, and physical resource allocation. Finally, the successful candidate will nurture and foster a collaborative practice through modeling collaboration with faculty and administrative staff, within the department, while actively connecting students to the extraordinary range of artistic, academic, and global study resources across Tisch, NYU\u2019s global network, and New York City.\u00a0 The Chair reports directly to the Dean of Tisch School of the Arts, NYU. In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for an Associate Professor position is $130,000 - $150,000\u00a0 and the annual base salary range for a full Professor position is $150,000 - $170,000. This pay range represents base pay only (for 9 months - paid over 12 months) and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, clinical compensation, or other items. The position will provide additional compensation for serving as Chair of the Department. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate\u2019s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. The successful candidate must qualify for an academic appointment as an Associate or Full Professor on a tenure line at NYU Tisch, supported by a distinguished national or international profile as a practicing artist, researcher, or creator with deep expertise in interdisciplinary practices, performance, media, or technology. This role requires proven administrative leadership experience, including demonstrated success in academic governance, creative direction, budget oversight, and faculty and staff management. The ideal Chair brings a track record of pedagogical excellence, with extensive experience teaching and mentoring undergraduate students and a steadfast dedication to nurturing individual artistic voices within a collaborative framework. Finally, the role demands a forward-looking vision for innovation, marked by a commitment to exploring new relationships among artistic disciplines, research, contemporary cultural practices, and emerging technologies. Candidates are required to submit a comprehensive CV and a professional portfolio and a one-page (500-word) personal statement articulating a philosophy of interdisciplinary collaborative practices in artistic work and education via https://tisch.nyu.edu/faculty/faculty-positions. Detail any past successes, priorities, strategies, etc. Application deadline is November 24, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195418": {"title": "Communications Program Assistant, Advocacy /Junior Research Scientist - Scholars at Risk", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Office of the Provost: Scholars at Risk Location: New York, New York Posted: Oct 09, 2026 Scholars at Risk (SAR) seeks a proactive, detail-oriented, research-focused individual with excellent analytical and writing skills to join SAR\u2019s advocacy and research teams as a Communications Program Assistant. This role is responsible for communications-related research and outputs for SAR\u2019s ongoing advocacy, research, and learning efforts. The role will research and draft communications to reach intended, wider audiences. In support of SAR\u2019s advocacy efforts responding to attacks on higher education (including via SAR\u2019s Free to Think reports and Scholars in Prison Project), the role will research media and press avenues through which to disseminate SAR\u2019s reports, outputs, and training materials; research individual scholars\u2019 stories that epitomize SAR\u2019s work and build storytelling-focused communications campaigns; write communications materials and press releases; and develop data-driven assessment tools to refine SAR\u2019s communications. The position requires excellent research, analysis, writing, and project management skills, as well as a practiced attention to detail and judgment, particularly under deadline and with competing priorities. It is ideal for a proactive, thoughtful individual who takes initiative, enjoys the research process, has excellent writing skills, and likes to work on a small team. The position requires timely and professional communication, including with partners, SAR and NYU staff, academia, and the public. This position is based in New York with a regular in-office presence expected. The role reports to SAR\u2019s Advocacy Director. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & DUTIES 40% - Research, develop, and deploy communications around attacks on higher education and at-risk scholars With an eye to cultivating wider audiences for SAR\u2019s messaging, research, develop, and deploy communications to articulate the connection between higher education, knowledge production, and the greater public good,, including: Analyzing ongoing media coverage, including through the compilation of SAR\u2019s Academic Freedom Media Review, US Bulletin, Advocacy Insider, and other e-newsletters; Highlighting policy/legal updates, Scholars in Prison or other case developments, and key issues of concern over social media; Maintaining relevant sections of the SAR website. Conduct research of\u00a0 outlets and creative communications methods that reach faculty, students, policymakers, media, and the public; and, based on this research, make recommendations for communications strategies and implementation options. 40% - Research, write, and produce communications materials Coordinate communications around SAR\u2019s advocacy and research materials, including the Monitoring Project\u2019s annual Free to Think report, white papers, country briefing papers, topical reports, Scholars in Prison campaigns, and letters of appeal, by: Developing graphics and other design elements in partnership with SAR\u2019s graphic design consultant; Designing and deploying social media campaigns; Developing and executing production timelines for all materials; Coordinating other staff contributing to these materials; Prioritizing storytelling in communications; Integrating discerning use of AI to increase efficiencies; Coordinating with SAR\u2019s Communications Officer on the media distribution of these materials, responding to press inquiries, and organizing interviews for SAR staff; and Presenting to academic and general audiences on the materials. Conduct an assessment of communications campaigns, especially for SAR\u2019s Free to Think reports, to improve SAR\u2019s impact. 10% - Create a data-driven communications structure Conduct a regular review of communications efforts, track specific benchmarks for success, analyze available data, and make recommendations for changes; Specifically, conduct an annual review of SAR\u2019s Free to Think communications; and Ensure that all advocacy and research communications data, including events, web stats, and press relationships, feeds into SAR\u2019s communications data. 10% - Ensure accurate implementation of SAR\u2019s communications tools Maintain website, socials, and EveryAction database; Support in-person and online events, including development of best communications practices at events; Offer communications-related administrative support, as needed; and Coordinate with other SAR staff to share lessons learned. This is a full-time, at-will position on an open-ended term, subject to program priorities, exemplary performance, and availability of funds. The position offers a salary range of $50,000 - 57,000, comparable to similar positions at peer organizations in the human rights/NGO field. Scholars at Risk is an independent not-for-profit corporation hosted at New York University (NYU). SAR staff are NYU employees seconded to SAR and are eligible for NYU employee benefits, including generous vacation leave and holidays; medical, dental, vision and life insurance options; and retirement benefits (all subject to terms established by NYU as described at https://www.nyu.edu/employees/benefit/full-time/professional-research-staff.html ). Undergraduate degree or similar, relevant experience. Excellent research and writing skills; demonstrated ability to synthesize and distill complex information for general, academic, and government audiences. Exceptional interpersonal communication skills, including cross-team collaboration, and proven proficiency in delivering impactful presentations. Exceptional attention to detail, initiative, time-management, and problem-solving abilities. Experience in developing and executing communications campaigns, including report distribution and strategy. Experience in digital communications; strong understanding of social media for nonprofit organizations, including outreach, media, and development. Experience in developing, refining, and assessing impact of social media content. Experience in data-driven decision making. Experience in project management, especially in managing competing priorities. Commitment to human rights, academic freedom, and diversity, equity, and inclusion. Ability to manage complex political and cultural factors, difficult information, including human rights abuses, and tensions between long- and short-term goals. Ability and commitment to work beyond a typical work day or work week when needed. Proficiency in word processing and spreadsheet software (e.g., Word/Google Docs, Excel/Sheets) and website content management systems (e.g., WordPress). HOW TO APPLY: Apply online at https://www.scholarsatrisk.org/category/career-opportunities/ by uploading the requested materials in one combined PDF: (1) a letter of interest, (2) resume, (3) list of references or letters of reference, and (4) a brief, unedited writing sample. DEADLINE FOR INITIAL APPLICATION REVIEW IS November 1, 2026 . Applications received after the deadline will be reviewed on a rolling basis, until the position is filled. No phone calls or email inquiries, please."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195991": {"title": "Faculty Position and Chair, Center for Data Science, NYU Courant Institute School", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science: Center for Data Science Location: New York Posted: Oct 20, 2026 The Center for Data Science (CDS) at New York University invites applications and nominations for the position of Department Chair. This appointment comes at a transformative juncture: CDS has recently been elevated to a full-fledged academic department within the newly established Courant Institute School of Mathematics, Computing, and Data Science. As the focal point for NYU\u2019s mission to advance the mathematical, statistical, and computational foundations of the field, the Center is entering a period of unprecedented expansion. Following its promotion to departmental status, CDS will be instrumental in reshaping Courant\u2019s vision through this period of unprecedented change. The incoming Chair will serve as a foundational leader, holding a strategic mandate to drive growth, oversee faculty recruitment, and solidify the Center\u2019s position at the global vanguard of data science and artificial intelligence. The successful candidate will both carry out her/his forward-thinking research vision, and lead CDS\u2019s vibrant research community dedicated to bridging deep methodological rigor with high-impact applications. This role offers a unique opportunity to shape the legacy of a premier academic department at one of the world\u2019s leading research universities, fostering a culture of excellence that integrates the core disciplines of mathematics, statistics, and computer science to solve the complex challenges of the 21st century. In compliance with NYC's Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for the rank of Professor is $200K - $300K. New York University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. This position will provide additional compensation for serving as Chair of the Department. PhD in a related field with a proven record of world-class research Qualifications and Criteria Strategic Leadership &Administration Experience in Research Center Leadership: A proven track record of establishing, managing, or directing large-scale research initiatives or centers within either an academic or industrial setting (e.g., NSF AI Institutes, major industrial research labs, or equivalent). Departmental Growth & Scaling: Demonstrated success in guiding the expansion of an academic department or research unit, with specific experience in scaling operations, faculty recruitment, and resource management to support organizational growth. Academic Vision & Institutional Alignment Commitment to Core Identity: A demonstrated alignment with the department\u2019s unique academic profile, specifically the convergence of rigorous foundations in mathematics, statistics, and computer science with broad, interdisciplinary application and collaboration. Vision for Interdisciplinary Excellence: An ability to articulate and execute a strategic vision that reinforces the department's position at the intersection of deep methodological research and cross-domain impact. Operational Expertise & Funding Knowledge of the U.S. Academic Landscape: Deep familiarity with the United States higher education system, including university governance structures, tenure and promotion processes, and administrative protocols. Research Funding Acumen: Extensive experience with U.S. funding ecosystems, including a track record of securing and managing grants from federal agencies (such as the NSF, NIH, DARPA) and industry partners. Please follow the instructions to submit your application via Interfolio. Applications received by February 1, 2027, will receive full consideration."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195715": {"title": "Post Doctorate Associate", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Tandon School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Brooklyn, NY  Posted: Oct 14, 2026 POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE New York University Tandon School of Engineering Biomedical Engineering Maimon Lab at NYU Tandon School of Engineering is seeking to hire a Postdoctoral Associate to work with Roy Maimon. New York University (NYU) is one of the top private universities in the United States, and the Tandon School of Engineering, located in Brooklyn, NY, is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and learning. Tandon fosters student and faculty innovation and entrepreneurship that make a difference in the world. The Maimon Lab in the Department of Biomedical Engineering seeks to pioneer regenerative strategies for neurodegenerative diseases by unlocking the potential of in vivo cellular identity conversion. Through bold experimentation and interdisciplinary thinking, the Maimon lab explores how dormant neural stem cells can be reawakened and guided to form new glial and neurons, with the ultimate goal of inducing brain regeneration in aging and disease. Responsibilities Candidates will be responsible for: Designing and performing in vivo and ex vivo studies in mouse models of Huntington\u2019s Disease (HD), focused on gene editing using Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats / CRISPR-associated protein 9, , Adeno-associated virus, and Antisense oligonucleotide-based approaches. Conducting behavioral and molecular phenotyping, and optimizing delivery and safety of therapeutic tools. Leading or supporting single-cell and spatial transcriptomics experiments, including MERFISH, immunofluorescence, spatial proteomics, and live-cell imaging. Analyzing and integrating single-cell datasets using established computational pipelines. Helping establish protocols, track inventory, and maintain a well-organized and efficient research environment. Salary range In compliance with NYC\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary for this position is $ 70,000 depending on the candidate\u2019s experience and qualification. Candidates should have a PhD in Neuro Cell Biology or a related field or a related field. We welcome applicants with recent PhDs and individuals seeking additional postdoctoral training. Candidates will be required to present eligibility to work in the United States. Applicants should submit, as one pdf, the following information: \u25cf an up-to-date CV that includes a complete list of publications, \u25cf a research statement that describes their interest and goals and how their research relates to this position, \u25cf copies of up to three relevant scientific papers, \u25cf names and contact information for three references All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio and to: roy.maimon@nyu.edu Review of applications will begin as soon as possible and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 The anticipated start date of this posion is April 1, 2027 with an expected end date of March 31, 2028.\u00a0 ed end date is 03/31/28"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195456": {"title": "Junior Research Scientist in the Center for Interacting Urban Networks \u2013 Dr. Azza Abouzied", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Interacting Urban Networks (CITIES) Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Deadline: Oct 27, 2026 We are looking to hire a Junior Research Scientist or engineer to help us design and build novel systems and tools for decision-making support. You can learn more about our research vision and work on the lab website (huda-lab.github.io). HuDa lab examines data and decision-making from a tooling and systems-building perspective1. You can learn more about the lab\u2019s PI, Prof Azza Abouzied, here (azzaabouzied.com). A successful candidate should be able to design and implement systems and tools as well as demonstrate their efficacy through comparative performance evaluations or user studies. They should be able to work independently or as part of a team, depending on the project's scope. As we publicly share all our code and data artifacts, good coding & reproducible experimentation practices are a must. We meet frequently to provide short status updates and have regular technical 1-1s to dive deep into research problems. Successful RAs or REs present their work, progress, and challenges concisely and clearly. They should be keen about publishing their work and findings and strive to meet deadlines. A bachelor\u2019s degree in Computer Science or Computer Engineering is required, or a master's degree and less than 5 years of research experience in the relevant field. The candidate should demonstrate experience in systems or tool building, algorithmic thinking, and research. Proficiency in Python, C++, and other programming frameworks is required. Familiarity with declarative programming, SQL, and data management is highly desirable. Advanced coursework or projects in systems, HCI, or AI/ML are also desirable. Employees receive a competitive salary with attractive benefits, including housing allowance, medical insurance, annual home leave allowance, and (if applicable) dependent private school fees. Salaries are tax-free in the UAE (for US tax, the Foreign Earned Income Exclusion can be used). The researchers will have dedicated funds for traveling, computing, publications, and access to excellent world-class research facilities. Applications will be evaluated immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To apply please submit the following: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Transcript, Statement of Research Interests Two Letters of Reference Please submit all documents in PDF format.\u00a0 Questions regarding specifics for this position may be directed to Prof. Azza Abouzied (azza@nyu.edu). About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195865": {"title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in Transportation Systems and Urban Science in the Center for Interacting Urban Networks (CITIES) - Dr. Monica Menendez", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Research Centers: AD_Center for Interacting Urban Networks (CITIES) Location: Abu Dhabi, UAE Posted: Oct 20, 2026 The Division of Engineering and the Center for Interacting Urban Networks (CITIES) at New York University Abu Dhabi are inviting applications for a Post-Doctoral Associate to join the research group of Prof. Monica Menendez. The group focuses on monitoring, modeling, and controlling the operations of multimodal transportation systems. Particular attention is paid to new vehicle technologies and data sources; as well as the combination of traditional traffic flow theory concepts with new empirically derived models and data science ideas. Applicants must have received a PhD in engineering, computer science, urban science, or a related field, and are still within their first 5 years since receiving their PhD. Experience in transportation, in particular related to urban science is required. Additionally, excellent communication skills in English and scientific creativity are essential. Salary is dependent upon qualifications. The terms of employment are very competitive and include a flexible working environment, annual home-leave travel allowance, generous housing and relocation benefits, medical insurance with access to world-class facilities, as well as educational subsidies for children. Salaries are tax-free in the UAE (for US tax, the Foreign Earned Income Exclusion can be used). The researchers will have dedicated funds for traveling, computing, publications, and access to excellent world-class research facilities. Applications will be accepted immediately and candidates will be considered until the position is filled. To be considered, all applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae with full publication list, transcript, statement of research interests and at least two reference letterst, all in PDF format. Please visit our website at https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/about/careers/postdoctoral-and-research.html for instructions and information on how to apply. If you have any questions, please email: monica.menendez@nyu.edu. About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for research staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to research staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195625": {"title": "Professor of Art History, Tenured/Tenure-Track - Open Rank", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - Global: Abu Dhabi: AD_Arts and Humanities: AD_Art and Art History Location: Abu Dhabi Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The Division of Arts and Humanities invites applications for a tenure-track/ tenured Professor of Art History (Open rank) specialising in Modern and Contemporary Arab Art History, in Academic Year 2027-28. We seek a scholar who can demonstrate a commitment to innovative research that is open to cross-disciplinary and cross-cultural perspectives. The successful candidate will help develop collaborations with other programs in the Arts & Humanities Division as well as with other divisions at NYUAD. Responsibilities include undergraduate and graduate teaching and advising in the Program, teaching in the Core Curriculum, and service to the university. The ideal candidate will have an established international publication record and corresponding teaching experience, including the expertise to supervise BA, MA, and PhD students. The ideal candidate will have a PhD in art history and will contribute to interdisciplinary programs in Middle East Studies, Global Studies, Curatorial Studies and Public Humanities. We seek a distinguished scholar whose research and teaching focus on the visual arts of the Middle East and North Africa, with particular interest in one or more of the following areas: Modern and contemporary Arab art Arab visual culture Global modernisms Cross-cultural artistic exchange Postcolonial and decolonial art histories Museum and curatorial studies Cultural heritage and identity formation Transnational art movements About NYU Abu Dhabi https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/ NYU Abu Dhabi is the first comprehensive liberal arts and research campus in the Middle East to be operated abroad by a major American research university. Times Higher Education ranks NYU among the top 30 universities in the world, making NYU Abu Dhabi the highest-ranked university in the UAE and MENA region. NYU Abu Dhabi has integrated a highly selective undergraduate curriculum across the disciplines with a world center for advanced research and scholarship. The university enables its students in the sciences, engineering, social sciences, humanities, and arts to succeed in an increasingly interdependent world and advance cooperation and progress on humanity\u2019s shared challenges. NYU Abu Dhabi\u2019s high-achieving students have come from over 120 countries and speak over 100 languages. Together, NYU's campuses in New York, Abu Dhabi, and Shanghai form the backbone of a unique global university, giving faculty and students opportunities to experience varied learning environments and immersion in other cultures at one or more of the numerous study-abroad sites NYU maintains on six continents. NYUAD is committed to upholding a culture of non-discrimination, anti-harassment, dignity, and mutual respect; providing equal access and opportunity; and fostering academic excellence in learning, research, and teaching. Students are drawn from among the world\u2019s best. They are bright, intellectually passionate, and committed to building a campus environment anchored in mutual respect, understanding, and care. The NYUAD undergraduate student body has garnered an impressive record of scholarships, graduate-school admissions, and other global honors. Graduate education is an area of growth for the University; the current graduate student population of over 100 students is expected to expand in the next decade as doctoral programs are developed. Working for NYUAD At NYUAD, we recognize that Abu Dhabi is more than where you work; it\u2019s your home. In order for faculty/academic staff to thrive, we offer a comprehensive benefits package. This starts with a generous relocation allowance; educational assistance for your dependents; access to health and wellness services; and more. NYUAD is committed to faculty/academic staff success throughout the academic trajectory, providing support for ambitious and world-class research projects and innovative, interactive teaching approaches. Support for dual-career families is a priority. Visit our website for more information on benefits for you and your dependents. Required: Ph.D. in Art History, Humanities, Middle East Studies, or a closely related field. Demonstrated record of scholarly publications. Evidence of successful university-level teaching. Expertise in modern and contemporary art of the Arab world. Preferred: Record of museum, curatorial, or exhibition experience. Experience securing external grants and research funding. Ability to teach interdisciplinary courses connecting art history, cultural studies, and cross-cultural scholarship. Knowledge of the Arabic language and culture. Responsibilities Teach at undergraduate and graduate levels. Supervise M.A. and Ph.D. students. Maintain an active research and publication program. Participate in departmental and university service. Foster collaborations with museums, archives, and cultural institutions. Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement Sample publications Contact information for three references The university is committed to fostering an inclusive academic environment and welcomes applications from scholars whose work advances understanding of diverse artistic traditions and global perspectives. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, beginning immediately. Shortlisted candidates are expected to be invited for campus visits in Abu Dhabi in Fall 2026. We anticipate that successful candidates can start the appointment and relocate to Abu Dhabi in the academic year 2027- 2028, subject to final budget approval. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to the excellence and vibrancy of our academic community. Applications are welcome from all qualified candidates. In line with UAE regulations, Emirati candidates are encouraged to apply. For questions about this position, please email nyuad.academicrecruitment@nyu.edu . Join NYU Abu Dhabi, an exceptional place for exceptional people. NYUAD values belonging and respect; such principles are fundamental to the university\u2019s commitment to excellence. NYUAD is an equal-opportunity employer. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates and seek individuals who will contribute to our vibrant, multidisciplinary research and teaching community. Multidisciplinary research and exceptional teaching in a global campus community are hallmarks of the University\u2019s mission. @WorkAtNYUAD #nyuadfacultycareers"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/196072": {"title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Full-Time)", "description": "Department: New York University: NYU - NY: Steinhardt School of Culture, Education and Human Development: Office of Research: Center of Health and Rehabilitation Research (CoHRR) Location: New York, NY Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The Center of Health and Rehabilitation Research (CoHRR) at New York University is seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Associate to work with Dr. Si On Yoon in the Department of Communicative Sciences and Disorders at NYU Steinhardt. The Postdoctoral Associate will contribute primarily to an NIH/NIA-funded R01 project, CLEAR (Cognitive-Linguistic Engagement in Aging and Recall), which examines how conversational engagement influences memory in healthy older adults and individuals with mild cognitive impairment and Alzheimer\u2019s disease. The project investigates the cognitive and linguistic mechanisms underlying memory for information encountered during conversation and examines how different communication supports and strategies may help older adults retain important information from everyday interactions. The Postdoctoral Associate will play an active role in the scientific development and implementation of the project, including study implementation, participant recruitment and data collection, data management and analysis, interpretation and dissemination of findings, manuscript preparation, and conference presentations. The successful candidate will receive ongoing mentorship and professional development opportunities to support their growth as an independent researcher, including opportunities to develop independent and collaborative research projects related to language, communication, memory, and cognitive aging. Responsibilities include: Contributing to the implementation and coordination of the CLEAR research project; Recruiting, screening, and scheduling research participants; Preparing for and conducting research study visits in accordance with established protocols; Administering behavioral, cognitive, and language-based study procedures; Recording, organizing, and maintaining language samples and study documentation; Coding, cleaning, managing, and analyzing research data; Contributing to the interpretation and dissemination of research findings; Preparing manuscripts, conference presentations, and other scholarly products; Collaborating with the PI, research staff, students, and other members of the interdisciplinary research team; and Contributing to the development of new research questions and independent or collaborative projects. The ideal candidate will have strong organizational and time-management skills and experience conducting human-subjects research. Candidates with research interests in language and communication, memory, cognitive aging, or related areas are particularly encouraged to apply. This is a full-time, in-person position. The initial appointment is for one year, with the possibility of renewal contingent upon satisfactory performance and the availability of funding. In compliance with New York City\u2019s Pay Transparency Act, the annual base salary range for this position is $67,000-$72,000 per year. New York University considers factors including, but not limited to, the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Minimum Qualifications PhD in psychology, neuroscience, speech-language pathology, linguistics, communication sciences and disorders, or a related field; Strong organizational and time-management skills; Experience conducting human-subjects research, including data collection and data management; and Experience with quantitative data analysis. Preferred Qualifications Research experience with older adults and/or individuals with mild cognitive impairment, Alzheimer\u2019s disease, or other neurological conditions; Research interests relevant to language, communication, memory, and/or cognitive aging; Strong attention to detail, reliability, and ability to work both independently and collaboratively; Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with evidence of scholarly productivity (e.g., peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations); Prior research experience in psychology, cognitive science, clinical research, rehabilitation, or speech and hearing sciences; Strong technical and problem-solving skills; Experience with research databases, data-entry systems, statistical software, and/or research data management; Experience with eye-tracking and/or EEG/ERP research, including data collection, preprocessing, or analysis, is preferred. To apply, please submit: A cover letter describing your research interests and relevant experience; A current CV and complete publication list; Two representative publications; A mandatory one-page evidence-based fit statement; Contact information for three academic or professional references."}}, "rows": {"https://apply.interfolio.com/149913": {"school_id": 970, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/149913", "job_title_in_post": "Adjunct Position in Marketing (non-tenure track, part-time, all ranks)", "position_type": "Lecturer", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "Marketing", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https